window.NPLEX_UNITS = {"CV-1":{"id":"CV-1","summary":"How a single heart tube folds, loops, and divides into four chambers with valves and outflow vessels, and how fetal circulation bypasses the lungs and liver. Exam questions link each embryonic structure to its adult derivative and to the congenital defect that results when a step fails.","notes":[{"h":"Heart tube and looping","points":["Heart forms from splanchnic mesoderm (cardiogenic field) in week 3.","Paired endocardial tubes fuse into one heart tube as the embryo folds.","Heart beats spontaneously by about day 22 (week 4); first functional organ.","Looping begins in week 4 and sets left-right position of the chambers.","Defective left-right dynein (primary ciliary dyskinesia, Kartagener syndrome) can cause dextrocardia with situs inversus."]},{"h":"Atrial septation","points":["Septum primum grows toward the endocardial cushions; the gap below it is the ostium primum.","Ostium secundum opens by apoptosis in the upper septum primum.","Septum secundum grows on the right side of septum primum and leaves an opening, the foramen ovale.","Septum primum acts as a one-way flap valve over the foramen ovale.","After birth, left atrial pressure rises above right and presses the flap shut; the scar is the fossa ovalis.","Failure of the two septa to fuse leaves a patent foramen ovale (about 1 in 4 adults)."]},{"h":"Ventricles, AV canal, and outflow tract","points":["Dorsal and ventral endocardial cushions fuse to split the AV canal into right and left channels.","Muscular interventricular septum grows up from the floor of the ventricle.","Membranous septum closes the last gap using endocardial cushion and outflow ridge tissue; most VSDs are membranous.","Neural crest cells form truncal and bulbar ridges that spiral to make the aorticopulmonary septum.","Mitral and tricuspid valves come from fused AV canal endocardial cushions; aortic and pulmonary valves come from outflow tract endocardial cushions."]},{"h":"Fetal circulation","points":["Umbilical vein carries the most oxygenated fetal blood from the placenta.","Ductus venosus lets most umbilical blood bypass the liver into the IVC.","IVC blood is directed through the foramen ovale to the left atrium, then to the head and upper body.","SVC blood goes RA to RV to pulmonary trunk, then through the ductus arteriosus to the descending aorta.","High fetal pulmonary vascular resistance drives the ductus shunt.","Two umbilical arteries (from the internal iliac arteries) return deoxygenated blood to the placenta."]},{"h":"Changes at birth","points":["First breaths drop pulmonary vascular resistance, so more blood returns to the left atrium.","Higher left atrial pressure closes the foramen ovale.","Rising O2 and falling prostaglandin E2 (placenta removed) constrict the ductus arteriosus.","Clamping the cord stops flow in the umbilical vessels and ductus venosus."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Embryonic heart structures and adult derivatives","cols":["Embryonic structure","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["Truncus arteriosus","Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"],["Bulbus cordis","Smooth outflow parts of both ventricles (RV conus arteriosus, LV aortic vestibule)"],["Primitive ventricle","Trabeculated parts of left and right ventricles"],["Primitive atrium","Trabeculated parts of both atria (auricles)"],["Left horn of sinus venosus","Coronary sinus"],["Right horn of sinus venosus","Smooth part of right atrium (sinus venarum)"],["Primitive pulmonary vein","Smooth part of left atrium"],["Right common and anterior cardinal veins","Superior vena cava"]]},{"title":"Fetal shunts and their adult remnants","cols":["Fetal structure","What it does before birth","Adult remnant"],"rows":[["Umbilical vein","Brings oxygenated blood from placenta","Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of liver)"],["Ductus venosus","Shunts umbilical blood past the liver to the IVC","Ligamentum venosum"],["Foramen ovale","Shunts RA blood to LA","Fossa ovalis"],["Ductus arteriosus","Shunts pulmonary trunk blood to the aorta","Ligamentum arteriosum"],["Umbilical arteries","Return blood to the placenta","Medial umbilical ligaments"]]},{"title":"Aortic (pharyngeal) arch artery derivatives","cols":["Arch","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["1st","Part of the maxillary artery"],["2nd","Stapedial and hyoid arteries"],["3rd","Common carotid artery and proximal internal carotid artery"],["4th","Left: part of the aortic arch. Right: proximal right subclavian artery"],["5th","Regresses"],["6th","Proximal pulmonary arteries; left side also forms the ductus arteriosus"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"1st arch is MAXimal, Second = Stapedial, C is the 3rd letter, 4 limbs = systemic, 6th = pulmonary","text":"Arch 1: maxillary artery. Arch 2: stapedial artery. Arch 3: common carotid. Arch 4: systemic vessels (aortic arch, right subclavian). Arch 6: pulmonary arteries and the pulmonary-to-systemic shunt (ductus arteriosus)."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"The 5 Ts of early cyanosis","text":"Truncus arteriosus, Transposition of the great arteries, Tricuspid atresia, Tetralogy of Fallot, TAPVR. The first, second, and fourth are outflow tract (neural crest) septation errors."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Follow the oxygen","text":"Trace one red cell from the placenta: umbilical vein, ductus venosus, IVC, foramen ovale, LA, LV, aorta to the brain. Every shunt exists to send the best blood to the brain and skip the airless lungs and busy liver."}],"highYield":["Neural crest failure in the outflow tract gives persistent truncus arteriosus, transposition, or tetralogy of Fallot.","22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) is linked to truncus arteriosus and tetralogy of Fallot.","Endocardial cushion defects (AV septal defect) are classic in Down syndrome.","Patent foramen ovale can let a venous clot cross to the systemic side (paradoxical embolism).","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the ligamentum arteriosum (6th arch) and aortic arch; the right hooks under the right subclavian artery (4th arch).","Most VSDs are in the membranous septum."],"pitfalls":["Ligamentum venosum comes from the ductus venosus; ligamentum arteriosum comes from the ductus arteriosus.","Medial umbilical ligaments are old umbilical arteries; the median umbilical ligament is the urachus (allantois), which is not a vessel.","Ostium secundum forms in septum primum; the foramen ovale is the hole left in septum secundum.","Failure to form the aorticopulmonary septum gives truncus arteriosus; failure of the septum to spiral gives transposition."],"dumps":[{"t":"Fetal circulation map","p":"Draw the fetal circulation from the placenta and back. Label the three shunts, mark where blood is most and least oxygenated, and write each shunt's adult remnant beside it.","l":["Umbilical vein carries the most oxygenated blood","Ductus venosus bypasses the liver to the IVC (ligamentum venosum)","Foramen ovale moves blood RA to LA (fossa ovalis)","Ductus arteriosus moves blood pulmonary trunk to aorta (ligamentum arteriosum)","High pulmonary vascular resistance explains why lungs are bypassed","Two umbilical arteries from internal iliacs return blood (medial umbilical ligaments)","Umbilical vein becomes ligamentum teres hepatis"]},{"t":"Atrial septum step by step","p":"Draw four panels showing how the atrial septum forms, from septum primum to the closed foramen ovale after birth. Label each opening and septum.","l":["Septum primum grows toward endocardial cushions","Ostium primum is the gap below septum primum, then closes","Ostium secundum forms by apoptosis in septum primum","Septum secundum grows to the right of septum primum","Foramen ovale is the opening in septum secundum","Septum primum serves as the flap valve","At birth LA pressure exceeds RA pressure and closes the flap (fossa ovalis)"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and Neonatal Physiology (section on circulatory readjustments at birth)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Embryology | Development of the Heart ❤️","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RjiPx6Xi-68"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Fetal Circulation | Embryology | Dr Najeeb","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYVGjbzmAtg"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Fetal circulation right before birth | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-IRkisEtzsk"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-18-G","CV-18-G2","CV-18-G3","CV-18-G4"]},"CV-2":{"id":"CV-2","summary":"The microscopic structure of the heart wall, cardiac muscle cells, and the different types of blood vessels. Questions ask you to match a wall feature to its function and to know which layer is affected in common vascular diseases.","notes":[{"h":"Layers of the heart wall","points":["Endocardium: endothelium plus thin connective tissue; the subendocardial layer holds Purkinje fibers, nerves, and small vessels.","Myocardium: thickest layer, made of cardiac muscle cells; thickest in the left ventricle.","Epicardium: visceral layer of serous pericardium (mesothelium) over fat that carries the coronary vessels.","Cardiac skeleton: dense connective tissue rings around the valves; electrically insulates atria from ventricles except at the AV bundle.","Valve leaflets: dense connective tissue core covered by endothelium on both sides."]},{"h":"Cardiac muscle cells","points":["Striated, branched cells with one or two central nuclei.","Intercalated discs join cells end to end: fascia adherens and desmosomes hold, gap junctions pass current.","Gap junctions let the heart contract as a functional syncytium.","T tubules sit at the Z lines and pair with one SR cistern (diads).","Very rich in mitochondria; the heart depends on aerobic metabolism.","Atrial muscle cells contain granules of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP).","Adult cardiac muscle barely regenerates; dead muscle is replaced by fibrous scar."]},{"h":"Purkinje fibers","points":["Larger and paler than working myocytes, with fewer myofibrils and lots of glycogen.","Lie in the subendocardium and conduct impulses fast."]},{"h":"Blood vessel wall","points":["Tunica intima: endothelium, basal lamina, subendothelial connective tissue; arteries add an internal elastic lamina.","Tunica media: smooth muscle and elastic tissue; thickest layer in arteries.","Tunica adventitia: collagen-rich connective tissue; thickest layer in large veins.","Vasa vasorum supply the outer wall of large vessels and are more plentiful in veins.","Limb veins have valves (folds of intima) that stop backflow."]},{"h":"Endothelial products","points":["Nitric oxide and prostacyclin relax smooth muscle and resist clotting.","Endothelin-1 is a strong vasoconstrictor.","Weibel-Palade bodies store von Willebrand factor and P-selectin."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vessel types under the microscope","cols":["Vessel","Key wall features","Examples","Main job"],"rows":[["Elastic artery","Media packed with fenestrated elastic laminae","Aorta, pulmonary trunk, their large branches","Stretch in systole and recoil in diastole to keep flow steady"],["Muscular artery","Thick smooth muscle media, clear internal elastic lamina","Coronary, radial, femoral arteries","Distribute blood to organs"],["Arteriole","Thin media of only a few smooth muscle layers","Inside every organ","Main site of vascular resistance; control capillary inflow"],["Capillary","Endothelium and basal lamina, with pericytes","All tissues","Exchange between blood and tissue"],["Postcapillary venule","Endothelium and pericytes; high endothelial venules in lymph nodes","All tissues","Main site where leukocytes leave blood"],["Medium and large vein","Thin media, thick adventitia, valves in limb veins","Great saphenous vein, IVC","Low-pressure return; hold most of the blood volume"]]},{"title":"Capillary types","cols":["Type","Structure","Where found"],"rows":[["Continuous","Unbroken endothelium and basal lamina, tight junctions","Muscle, lung, connective tissue, nervous tissue (blood-brain barrier)"],["Fenestrated","Endothelium with small pores","Kidney glomeruli, intestinal villi, endocrine glands"],["Sinusoidal (discontinuous)","Wide, irregular lumen with gaps in endothelium and basal lamina","Liver, spleen, bone marrow"]]},{"title":"Three muscle types compared","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Cell-to-cell links","No gap junctions between fibers","Intercalated discs with gap junctions","Gap junctions in single-unit smooth muscle"],["T tubule arrangement","Triads at A-I junctions","Diads at Z lines","No T tubules (caveolae instead)"],["Ca2+ binds to","Troponin C","Troponin C","Calmodulin"],["Regeneration","Limited, through satellite cells","Very little; heals by scar","Good; cells can divide"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Arteries flex, veins hold","text":"Arteries face high pressure, so their media is thick. Veins hold volume at low pressure, so their adventitia is the thickest layer and the lumen is wide."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leakiness matches the job","text":"Where cells and large proteins must pass (liver, spleen, marrow) capillaries are sinusoidal. Where filtration or hormone release happens (kidney, gut, endocrine) they are fenestrated. Everywhere else they are continuous."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Disc parts by direction","text":"In an intercalated disc, the parts that run across the cell end (fascia adherens, desmosomes) hold cells together; the parts along the side (gap junctions) carry the electrical signal."}],"highYield":["Gap junctions in intercalated discs make the myocardium a functional syncytium.","Infarcted myocardium heals by fibrous scar because cardiac muscle cells barely divide.","Arterioles set most of the systemic vascular resistance.","Postcapillary venules are where neutrophils leave the blood in acute inflammation.","Continuous capillaries with tight junctions form the blood-brain barrier.","Atherosclerosis starts in the intima; Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis affects the media of muscular arteries."],"pitfalls":["Cardiac T tubules sit at Z lines (diads); skeletal T tubules sit at A-I junctions (triads).","Purkinje fibers are larger and paler than working myocytes; their wide diameter helps them conduct fast.","The epicardium is the same thing as the visceral serous pericardium.","In arteries the media is the thickest layer; in large veins the adventitia is."],"dumps":[{"t":"Artery versus vein","p":"Draw a cross section of a muscular artery next to its companion vein. Label all three tunics in each and add the elastic laminae and a valve.","l":["Intima with endothelium in both","Internal elastic lamina prominent in the artery","Thick smooth muscle media in the artery","Thin media in the vein","Thick adventitia in the vein","Wider, often collapsed lumen in the vein","Valve drawn as a fold of intima in the vein"]},{"t":"Cardiac muscle close up","p":"Draw two cardiac muscle cells joined end to end. Label the nucleus, intercalated disc parts, T tubule, SR, and mitochondria, and write what each part does.","l":["Branched cells with one or two central nuclei","Fascia adherens anchors actin at the cell end","Desmosomes resist pulling apart","Gap junctions on the lateral part pass ions between cells","T tubule at the Z line forming a diad with SR","Many mitochondria between myofibrils","Striations from sarcomeres"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Circulatory System"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue (section on cardiac muscle)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Histology of Cardiovascular System 🧑🏻‍⚕️","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UiMHnNHmgHM"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Structures and Layers of the Heart","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dgAbpwp9gF8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Layers of the heart | Human anatomy and physiology | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bm65xCS5ivo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Arteries vs. veins-what's the difference? | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7b6LRebCgb4"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-11","CV-18-I3"]},"CV-3":{"id":"CV-3","summary":"Where the heart sits in the chest, which chambers form each surface and border, how the great vessels connect, and how the pericardium wraps it all. Exam questions use these relations to explain stab wounds, enlarged chambers, referred pain, and tamponade.","notes":[{"h":"Position","points":["Heart lies in the middle mediastinum inside the pericardium, about two thirds left of midline.","Apex sits behind the left 5th intercostal space at about the midclavicular line.","The plane through the sternal angle and the T4/T5 disc separates superior from inferior mediastinum.","Right ventricle is the most anterior chamber, right behind the sternum.","Left atrium is the most posterior chamber, right in front of the esophagus."]},{"h":"Pericardium","points":["Fibrous pericardium: tough outer sac fused to the central tendon of the diaphragm and to the adventitia of the great vessels.","Serous pericardium has a parietal layer (lines the fibrous sac) and a visceral layer (epicardium).","Pericardial cavity holds a thin film of serous fluid between the two serous layers.","Phrenic nerves (C3 to C5) carry pain from the fibrous and parietal layers; pain refers to the shoulder and supraclavicular skin.","Main blood supply is the pericardiacophrenic artery from the internal thoracic artery.","Transverse pericardial sinus lies behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of the SVC.","Oblique pericardial sinus is a blind pouch behind the left atrium, bounded by the pulmonary veins and IVC."]},{"h":"Great vessels","points":["SVC (formed by the two brachiocephalic veins) and IVC enter the right atrium; the azygos vein drains into the SVC.","Pulmonary trunk leaves the RV and divides into right and left pulmonary arteries under the aortic arch.","Usually four pulmonary veins enter the left atrium.","The coronary arteries are the only branches of the ascending aorta.","Aortic arch branches, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian.","Ligamentum arteriosum links the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch next to it."]},{"h":"Inside the chambers","points":["RA: crista terminalis separates smooth sinus venarum from pectinate muscle; fossa ovalis on the septum; coronary sinus opening.","RV: trabeculae carneae, three papillary muscles, smooth conus arteriosus leading to the pulmonary valve.","Moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) carries part of the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle.","LV wall is about two to three times thicker than the RV wall; two papillary muscles (anterior and posterior)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Heart chambers at a glance","cols":["Chamber","Receives blood from","Sends blood to","Key internal landmarks"],"rows":[["Right atrium","SVC, IVC, coronary sinus","RV through tricuspid valve","Crista terminalis, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, SA node near SVC opening"],["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Pulmonary trunk through pulmonary valve","Trabeculae carneae, moderator band, three papillary muscles, conus arteriosus"],["Left atrium","Pulmonary veins (usually four)","LV through mitral valve","Mostly smooth wall; pectinate muscles only in left auricle"],["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Ascending aorta through aortic valve","Thickest wall, two papillary muscles, smooth aortic vestibule"]]},{"title":"Which chamber forms each surface and border","cols":["Surface or border","Formed mainly by"],"rows":[["Anterior (sternocostal) surface","Right ventricle"],["Diaphragmatic (inferior) surface","Left ventricle, partly right ventricle"],["Base (posterior surface)","Left atrium, small part of right atrium"],["Apex","Left ventricle"],["Right border","Right atrium"],["Left border","Left ventricle, small part of left auricle"],["Inferior border","Right ventricle, small part of left ventricle"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive","text":"Phrenic nerve roots are C3 to C5. The same nerve carries pericardial pain, which is why pericarditis can be felt at the shoulder."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Front to back: RV first, LA last","text":"A knife through the sternum hits the RV. An enlarged LA pushes back on the esophagus (trouble swallowing) and can stretch the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Transverse sinus = finger behind the arteries","text":"Slide a finger behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the SVC. Surgeons pass a clamp or ligature through this sinus to stop arterial outflow during bypass."}],"highYield":["Left atrial enlargement (for example from mitral stenosis) can cause dysphagia and hoarseness.","Cardiac tamponade: fluid in the pericardial sac compresses the heart; Beck triad is hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds.","Pericardial pain refers to the shoulder because of the phrenic nerve.","Phrenic nerve runs anterior to the lung root on the pericardium; vagus runs posterior to the lung root.","The fibrous pericardium does not stretch quickly, so even a small fast bleed can cause tamponade.","A stab wound just left of the sternum most likely enters the right ventricle."],"pitfalls":["The base of the heart is its posterior surface (mostly LA), and the apex points anteroinferiorly to the left.","Epicardium equals visceral serous pericardium; it is part of the heart wall.","Transverse sinus is behind the great arteries; oblique sinus is behind the left atrium.","The right border on a chest X-ray is the right atrium, while the left border is mostly the left ventricle."],"dumps":[{"t":"Anterior view of the heart","p":"Draw the heart as seen from the front. Label each chamber, the borders, the apex, and the great vessels leaving the top.","l":["RV forms most of the anterior surface","RA forms the right border","LV forms the left border and apex","Inferior border mostly RV","SVC entering RA from above","Pulmonary trunk from RV","Ascending aorta and arch with its three branches"]},{"t":"Pericardium and its sinuses","p":"Draw a cross section through the heart and pericardium. Label the fibrous and serous layers, the cavity, and both pericardial sinuses, then add the nerve and artery that supply the pericardium.","l":["Fibrous pericardium outermost, fused to central tendon of diaphragm","Parietal serous layer lines the fibrous sac","Visceral serous layer is the epicardium","Pericardial cavity with thin fluid film","Transverse sinus behind aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of SVC","Oblique sinus behind LA, bounded by pulmonary veins and IVC","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) carries pain","Pericardiacophrenic artery supplies it"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, sections on the pericardium and the heart"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the superior mediastinum and great vessels"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Anatomy of the Heart | Heart Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jU9w6w8LwqM"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Introduction to Cardiac (Heart) Anatomy and the Chest Xray","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HYr2NiOvjZE"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-8","CV-18-F","CV-18-F2"]},"CV-4":{"id":"CV-4","summary":"Where the four heart valves sit, how pressure differences open and close them, and how the valve events line up with pressures, volumes, heart sounds, and the ECG in one cardiac cycle. This is the foundation for every murmur and valve disease question.","notes":[{"h":"Valve anatomy","points":["Tricuspid valve (RA to RV): anterior, posterior, and septal cusps.","Mitral valve (LA to LV): anterior and posterior cusps.","Chordae tendineae tie AV cusps to papillary muscles; papillary muscles tighten in systole so cusps do not flip into the atria.","Pulmonary valve: anterior, right, and left cusps.","Aortic valve: right, left, and posterior cusps; right and left aortic sinuses give rise to the coronary arteries.","All four valves open and close passively, driven by pressure differences."]},{"h":"Where to listen","points":["Aortic area: right 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border.","Pulmonic area: left 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border.","Tricuspid area: left lower sternal border.","Mitral area: apex, left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line."]},{"h":"Pressures and volumes","points":["Aortic valve opens when LV pressure rises above aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg).","Normal aortic pressure about 120/80 mmHg; pulmonary artery about 25/8 mmHg.","End-diastolic volume about 110 to 120 mL, stroke volume about 70 mL, end-systolic volume about 40 to 50 mL.","Ejection fraction = SV / EDV, normally about 55 to 70%.","Most ventricular filling is passive; atrial contraction adds about the last 20%.","Dicrotic notch (incisura) on the aortic pressure curve marks aortic valve closure."]},{"h":"Jugular venous pulse waves","points":["a wave: right atrial contraction.","c wave: RV contraction bulges the closed tricuspid valve into the RA.","x descent: atrial relaxation and downward pull of the tricuspid ring during ejection.","v wave: RA fills against the closed tricuspid valve.","y descent: RA empties into the RV after the tricuspid valve opens."]},{"h":"Splitting of S2","points":["Inspiration lowers chest pressure, raises venous return to the right heart, lengthens RV ejection, and delays P2.","Normal (physiologic) split widens on inspiration."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Phases of the cardiac cycle (left heart)","cols":["Phase","Valve status","LV volume","Markers"],"rows":[["Atrial contraction","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises to EDV","Follows the P wave; a wave; S4 if the ventricle is stiff"],["Isovolumetric contraction","All valves closed","Stays at EDV","Starts just after QRS onset with S1; highest myocardial O2 use"],["Ejection","Mitral closed, aortic open","Falls to ESV","During the ST segment and T wave"],["Isovolumetric relaxation","All valves closed","Stays at ESV","Starts with S2 (aortic closure); dicrotic notch"],["Rapid filling","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises quickly","S3 if present; y descent"],["Reduced filling (diastasis)","Mitral open, aortic closed","Rises slowly","Flat stretch before the next P wave"]]},{"title":"Heart sounds","cols":["Sound","Cause","Timing","What it means"],"rows":[["S1","Mitral and tricuspid closure","Start of systole","Normal; loudest at the apex (mitral area)"],["S2","Aortic (A2) then pulmonic (P2) closure","End of ejection","Normal; loudest at left upper sternal border; splits on inspiration"],["S3","Rapid early filling into a dilated or volume-loaded ventricle","Early diastole, just after S2","Normal in children and pregnancy; in adults over about 40 suggests heart failure or volume overload"],["S4","Atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle","Late diastole, just before S1","Stiff or hypertrophied ventricle; absent in atrial fibrillation"]]},{"title":"Abnormal S2 splitting","cols":["Pattern","What happens","Classic causes"],"rows":[["Wide split","P2 is delayed more than normal; split still widens on inspiration","Pulmonic stenosis, right bundle branch block"],["Fixed split","Split stays the same through the breathing cycle","Atrial septal defect"],["Paradoxical split","A2 is delayed past P2; split narrows on inspiration and widens on expiration","Aortic stenosis, left bundle branch block"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"All Physicians Take Money","text":"Listening areas in order from the right upper chest to the apex: Aortic, Pulmonic, Tricuspid, Mitral."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Kentucky and Tennessee","text":"S3 follows S2 with the rhythm of Kentucky (S1, S2, S3). S4 comes before S1 with the rhythm of Tennessee (S4, S1, S2)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Valves are doors pushed by pressure","text":"A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts when that reverses. Find which pressure line is on top in a Wiggers diagram and you know the valve status."}],"highYield":["Isovolumetric phases are the only times all four valves are closed.","Loss of the atrial kick in atrial fibrillation removes S4 and lowers filling in a stiff ventricle.","Fixed splitting of S2 points to an atrial septal defect.","Paradoxical splitting points to delayed LV emptying, such as aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block.","The c wave is the closed tricuspid valve bulging into the RA as the RV contracts; the x descent is atrial relaxation plus the tricuspid ring pulled down during ejection.","Papillary muscle rupture after MI lets mitral cusps prolapse and causes acute mitral regurgitation."],"pitfalls":["S1 marks the start of isovolumetric contraction, which comes before ejection; the aortic valve opens later.","Papillary muscles do not pull the AV valves open; they hold the cusps shut during systole.","S3 can be normal in a child or pregnant patient; the same sound in an older adult points to disease.","Wide split still varies with breathing; fixed split does not."],"dumps":[{"t":"Wiggers diagram","p":"Draw one cardiac cycle with aortic, LV, and LA pressure curves, the LV volume curve, the ECG, and heart sounds on a shared time axis. Mark when each valve opens and closes.","l":["Mitral closes where LV pressure crosses above LA pressure (S1)","Isovolumetric contraction with volume flat at EDV","Aortic valve opens where LV pressure crosses aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg)","Volume falls to ESV during ejection","Aortic closure at the dicrotic notch (S2)","Isovolumetric relaxation with volume flat at ESV","Mitral opens where LV pressure drops below LA pressure, then rapid filling","P wave before atrial kick; QRS before isovolumetric contraction; T wave near end of ejection"]},{"t":"Listening map","p":"Draw the front of the chest and mark the four auscultation areas. Next to each, write which valve it represents and which heart sound is loudest there.","l":["Aortic area at right 2nd intercostal space, sternal border","Pulmonic area at left 2nd intercostal space, sternal border","Tricuspid area at left lower sternal border","Mitral area at apex, left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line","S1 loudest at the apex","S2 loudest at the left upper sternal border"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the heart valves and areas of auscultation"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Cycle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xamYVlNF5Zo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Lub Dub | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-4kGMI-qQ3I"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Cardiac Cycle | Systole & Diastole | Dr Najeeb Part 1/8","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XbivIaFPoQI"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Mastering Second Heart Sound | S2 Splitting🩺","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IdRb3OjFAtQ"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-6","CV-18-D","CV-18-D1","CV-18-D2"]},"CV-5":{"id":"CV-5","summary":"How an action potential in a ventricular muscle cell triggers calcium release and contraction, and how calcium handling sets the strength and speed of each beat. Expect questions on ion channels by phase, calcium-induced calcium release, and how sympathetic input raises contractility.","notes":[{"h":"Ventricular action potential","points":["Resting potential is about -90 mV, held by inward rectifier K+ channels.","Upstroke comes from fast voltage-gated Na+ channels.","The plateau comes from L-type Ca2+ influx balanced by K+ efflux.","The action potential lasts about 0.2 to 0.3 s, far longer than in skeletal muscle.","The long refractory period lasts almost as long as contraction, so the heart cannot be tetanized."]},{"h":"Excitation-contraction coupling","points":["The action potential spreads into T tubules and opens L-type Ca2+ channels (dihydropyridine receptors).","Entering Ca2+ opens ryanodine receptors (RyR2) on the SR: calcium-induced calcium release.","Ca2+ binds troponin C, tropomyosin moves, and myosin-binding sites on actin are exposed.","Cross-bridges cycle using ATP until Ca2+ falls.","Cardiac muscle needs extracellular Ca2+ to contract; skeletal muscle does not."]},{"h":"Relaxation","points":["SERCA pumps most Ca2+ back into the SR.","Phospholamban brakes SERCA; when PKA phosphorylates phospholamban, the brake comes off.","The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (3 Na+ in for 1 Ca2+ out) removes Ca2+ from the cell.","A sarcolemmal Ca2+-ATPase removes a smaller share.","Relaxation uses ATP, so ischemia impairs relaxation as well as contraction."]},{"h":"Controlling force","points":["Every cell contracts with every beat, so force is graded by Ca2+ and sarcomere length, with no motor unit recruitment.","Sympathetic β1 stimulation raises cAMP and activates PKA.","PKA phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels (more Ca2+ in), phospholamban (faster SR uptake, bigger SR store), and troponin I (faster relaxation).","Stretch (Frank-Starling) improves filament overlap and raises troponin C sensitivity to Ca2+.","Blocking the Na+/K+-ATPase raises cell Na+, slows Ca2+ removal by the exchanger, and strengthens contraction (cardiac glycoside mechanism)."]},{"h":"Energy","points":["Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism and burns mostly fatty acids at rest.","It extracts a large share of delivered O2 even at rest, so more demand must be met by more coronary flow."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ventricular myocyte action potential phases","cols":["Phase","What happens","Main current"],"rows":[["0","Rapid depolarization","Na+ in through fast voltage-gated Na+ channels"],["1","Brief early repolarization","Na+ channels inactivate; transient outward K+ current"],["2","Plateau","Ca2+ in through L-type channels balanced by K+ out"],["3","Rapid repolarization","L-type Ca2+ channels close; delayed rectifier K+ out"],["4","Resting potential (about -90 mV)","Inward rectifier K+ channels; Na+/K+-ATPase keeps gradients"]]},{"title":"Skeletal versus cardiac muscle coupling","cols":["Feature","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"rows":[["What starts contraction","Motor neuron releases ACh at the neuromuscular junction","Action potential spreads from neighbor cells through gap junctions"],["DHPR to RyR link","Mechanical: DHPR directly pulls RyR1 open","Ca2+ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2"],["Needs extracellular Ca2+","No","Yes"],["Action potential length","A few milliseconds","About 200 to 300 ms"],["Tetanus possible","Yes","No (long refractory period)"],["How force is graded","Recruiting motor units and firing rate","Amount of Ca2+ released and sarcomere length"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Phase story: Na, K, Ca, K, rest","text":"Phase 0 Na+ rushes in, phase 1 K+ starts out, phase 2 Ca2+ holds the plateau, phase 3 K+ pulls it down, phase 4 rests."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Phospholamban is a brake","text":"Unphosphorylated phospholamban slows SERCA. PKA phosphorylation releases the brake, so Ca2+ is pumped back faster: quicker relaxation and a bigger store for the next beat."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Calcium is the volume knob","text":"Anything that raises cytosolic Ca2+ during systole (β1 stimulation, slower Ca2+ removal) turns up force. Anything that lowers it turns force down."}],"highYield":["Calcium-induced calcium release through RyR2 is the core of cardiac excitation-contraction coupling.","The plateau (phase 2) depends on L-type Ca2+ channels.","A refractory period nearly as long as contraction prevents tetany and lets the heart refill.","β1 stimulation raises contractility and speeds relaxation through PKA phosphorylation of L-type channels, phospholamban, and troponin I.","High extracellular K+ makes the resting potential less negative, inactivates some fast Na+ channels, and slows conduction.","Inhibiting the Na+/K+-ATPase raises intracellular Ca2+ through reduced Na+/Ca2+ exchange."],"pitfalls":["Ventricular myocytes use Na+ for the upstroke; SA and AV nodal cells use Ca2+ (covered in CV-7).","In skeletal muscle the DHPR is linked mechanically to RyR1; in cardiac muscle Ca2+ entry is the trigger.","Phosphorylating phospholamban speeds SERCA; it does not block it.","Calcium binds troponin C in both striated muscles; calmodulin is the smooth muscle partner."],"dumps":[{"t":"One beat inside a myocyte","p":"Draw a cardiac muscle cell with its T tubule and SR. Trace Ca2+ from the moment the action potential arrives to full relaxation, numbering each step.","l":["Action potential travels down the T tubule","L-type Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ enters","Ca2+ opens RyR2 and the SR releases more Ca2+","Ca2+ binds troponin C and tropomyosin shifts","Cross-bridge cycling shortens the sarcomere","SERCA pumps Ca2+ back into the SR (phospholamban regulates it)","Na+/Ca2+ exchanger moves Ca2+ out of the cell"]},{"t":"Action potential with labels","p":"Draw a ventricular myocyte action potential. Label phases 0 to 4, the voltage values, the ion moving in each phase, and the refractory period.","l":["Resting about -90 mV in phase 4","Steep phase 0 from fast Na+ influx","Small notch in phase 1 from transient K+ efflux","Long plateau in phase 2 from L-type Ca2+ in and K+ out","Phase 3 repolarization from delayed rectifier K+","Effective refractory period spans most of the action potential"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue (section on cardiac muscle)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Action potentials in cardiac myocytes | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rIVCuC-Etc0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Calcium puts myosin to work | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vv6WBeqw2Nc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Heart Physiology I (Cardiac Myocyte and Membrane Potential)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FlyKXrY5ak0"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Heart Physiology II (Muscle contraction and Pacemaker activity)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o7L0W3Xb5Eg"}],"related":["CV-2","CV-6","CV-7","CV-18-B"]},"CV-6":{"id":"CV-6","summary":"What sets cardiac output from beat to beat: heart rate plus the three stroke volume controls (preload, afterload, contractility), and the reflexes that adjust them. Exam questions ask you to predict how a change shifts stroke volume, ejection fraction, or a pressure-volume loop.","notes":[{"h":"Core equations","points":["Cardiac output (CO) = stroke volume (SV) x heart rate (HR); about 5 L/min in a resting adult.","Ejection fraction (EF) = SV / EDV.","Mean arterial pressure (MAP) = CO x total peripheral resistance.","MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one third of pulse pressure at resting heart rates.","Fick principle: CO = O2 consumption / (arterial O2 content minus venous O2 content).","Wall stress (Laplace) = pressure x radius / (2 x wall thickness)."]},{"h":"Preload","points":["Preload is ventricular stretch at end of diastole, best judged by end-diastolic volume.","Set mainly by venous return.","Frank-Starling: more filling gives a stronger beat and larger SV, so the heart pumps out what comes in.","Venous return rises with mean systemic filling pressure (about 7 mmHg) and falls as right atrial pressure rises."]},{"h":"Afterload","points":["Afterload is the wall stress the ventricle must overcome to eject.","For the LV it mostly tracks aortic pressure; it rises in hypertension and in aortic stenosis.","Higher afterload raises ESV and lowers SV.","Concentric hypertrophy thickens the wall and lowers wall stress back toward normal."]},{"h":"Contractility and heart rate","points":["Contractility is force at a given preload and afterload; it rises with sympathetic β1 input and falls with ischemia, acidosis, and hypoxia.","Intrinsic SA node rate after autonomic blockade is about 100 bpm, so resting rate shows vagal tone.","Fast heart rates shorten diastole much more than systole, which cuts filling time and LV coronary flow.","Myocardial O2 demand rises with heart rate, contractility, and wall stress."]},{"h":"Reflex control","points":["Carotid sinus baroreceptors signal through CN IX and respond to rises and falls in pressure.","Aortic arch baroreceptors signal through CN X, mainly to rises in pressure.","Both feed the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla.","Falling pressure lowers baroreceptor firing, so sympathetic output rises (faster, stronger beats, vasoconstriction) and vagal output falls.","Atrial stretch speeds heart rate (Bainbridge reflex)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"What each change does to the left ventricle","cols":["Change","EDV","ESV","Stroke volume","Pressure-volume loop"],"rows":[["Higher preload","Up","About the same","Up","Wider, shifts right"],["Higher afterload","About the same","Up","Down","Taller and narrower"],["Higher contractility","About the same","Down","Up","Wider, shifts left; steeper end-systolic line"]]},{"title":"Autonomic effects on the heart","cols":["Effect","Sympathetic (β1)","Parasympathetic (vagus, M2)"],"rows":[["SA node rate (chronotropy)","Faster: steeper phase 4","Slower: hyperpolarized cell, flatter phase 4"],["AV node conduction (dromotropy)","Faster; shorter PR interval","Slower; longer PR interval"],["Atrial contractility","Up","Down"],["Ventricular contractility (inotropy)","Up strongly","Small effect (vagal fibers go mainly to atria and nodes)"],["Relaxation (lusitropy)","Faster","Little direct effect"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Preload is volume, afterload is pressure","text":"Preload asks how full the ventricle is before it squeezes. Afterload asks how hard it must push to get blood out."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Read the loop by its corners","text":"The lower right corner is EDV (preload). The upper left corner is ESV and aortic pressure (afterload). The loop width is SV. Move one corner and see what happens to the width."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Baroreceptor direction","text":"Baroreceptors fire more when stretched. Low pressure means less firing, and the brain answers with more sympathetic and less vagal output."}],"highYield":["SV depends on preload, afterload, and contractility; CO = SV x HR.","Normal EF is about 55 to 70%; it falls when contractility falls.","Raising afterload raises ESV and lowers SV.","Tachycardia shortens diastole and reduces LV coronary perfusion.","Carotid sinus uses CN IX; aortic arch uses CN X.","Concentric LV hypertrophy is the adaptation that lowers wall stress in pressure overload."],"pitfalls":["Increasing preload raises SV with little change in ESV; increasing contractility raises SV by lowering ESV.","Contractility is independent of preload; a stronger beat from more filling is the Frank-Starling effect.","The vagus has little direct effect on ventricular force even though it slows the heart a lot.","MAP equals diastolic plus one third of pulse pressure only at normal resting rates."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three pressure-volume loops","p":"Draw a normal LV pressure-volume loop. Then overlay separate loops for higher preload, higher afterload, and higher contractility, and label the EDV, ESV, and SV changes.","l":["Normal loop with four labeled corners (mitral close, aortic open, aortic close, mitral open)","Width of the loop is SV","Higher preload loop extends right with larger SV","Higher afterload loop is taller with larger ESV and smaller SV","Higher contractility loop extends left with smaller ESV","End-systolic pressure-volume line steeper with higher contractility"]},{"t":"Baroreceptor reflex","p":"Trace the baroreceptor reflex after a sudden drop in blood pressure, from receptor to heart and vessels. Label each nerve and the brainstem center.","l":["Stretch receptors in carotid sinus and aortic arch","Less stretch means less firing","Carotid sinus afferents in CN IX","Aortic arch afferents in CN X","Nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla","Sympathetic output up: faster HR, more contractility, vasoconstriction","Vagal output down","Result: CO and TPR rise and MAP returns toward normal"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Output, Venous Return, and Their Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Nervous Regulation of the Circulation and Rapid Control of Arterial Pressure"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves (section on regulation of heart pumping)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Output","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0O3FfHPE9PU"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiovascular System Physiology - Cardiac Output (stroke volume, heart rate, preload and afterload)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hpQFToprlH8"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Cardiac Cycle | Cardiology | Systole & Diastole | Cardiovascular🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVvQaqFOJpY"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-5","CV-11","CV-12","CV-18-B"]},"CV-7":{"id":"CV-7","summary":"Where the heart's conduction system lies, how pacemaker cells fire on their own, how the autonomic nervous system speeds or slows them, and how the ECG records the result. Questions ask you to link a wave or interval to a cardiac event and a lead group to the wall and artery it views.","notes":[{"h":"Conduction pathway","points":["SA node: right atrial wall near the SVC opening, at the top of the crista terminalis.","AV node: lower interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus.","AV bundle (His) crosses the fibrous skeleton, the only normal electrical link from atria to ventricles.","Bundle splits into right and left bundle branches; the left has anterior and posterior fascicles.","Right bundle branch reaches the anterior papillary muscle through the moderator band.","Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the subendocardium."]},{"h":"Blood supply of the nodes","points":["SA nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 60% of people.","AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80% of people (right-dominant hearts)."]},{"h":"Pacemaker action potential","points":["No true resting potential; lowest point about -60 mV.","Phase 4: slow climb from the funny current (If, Na+ in through HCN channels), then T-type Ca2+.","Phase 0: slow upstroke from L-type Ca2+ channels once threshold (about -40 mV) is reached.","Phase 3: K+ efflux repolarizes. There is no phase 1 or 2.","The fastest pacemaker (normally the SA node) suppresses slower ones (overdrive suppression)."]},{"h":"Autonomic control","points":["Norepinephrine on β1 receptors raises cAMP, increases If and Ca2+ currents, and steepens phase 4.","Acetylcholine on M2 receptors lowers cAMP and opens K+ channels, hyperpolarizes the cell, and flattens phase 4.","AV node delay (about 0.1 s) lets the atria finish filling the ventricles; sympathetic input shortens it and vagal input lengthens it."]},{"h":"Reading the ECG","points":["Standard paper at 25 mm/s: small box 0.04 s, large box 0.20 s; 10 mm = 1 mV.","Heart rate is about 300 divided by the number of large boxes between R waves.","Depolarization moving toward a positive electrode gives an upward deflection.","Septum depolarizes first (left to right), then apex, then free walls from endocardium to epicardium; the base is last.","Repolarization runs epicardium to endocardium, so the T wave normally points the same way as the QRS.","Normal QRS axis is about -30° to +90°."]},{"h":"AV blocks","points":["First degree: PR over 0.20 s, every P wave conducts.","Second degree Mobitz I (Wenckebach): PR lengthens beat by beat until a QRS is dropped; usually AV node.","Second degree Mobitz II: QRS dropped without PR lengthening; usually below the AV node and can progress.","Third degree: P waves and QRS complexes beat independently; an escape rhythm drives the ventricles."]}],"charts":[{"title":"ECG waves and intervals","cols":["Component","What it shows","Normal value"],"rows":[["P wave","Atrial depolarization","Under 0.12 s"],["PR interval","Atrial depolarization plus AV nodal delay","0.12 to 0.20 s"],["QRS complex","Ventricular depolarization (hides atrial repolarization)","Under 0.12 s (usually about 0.06 to 0.10 s)"],["ST segment","Ventricles fully depolarized (plateau phase)","Flat at baseline"],["T wave","Ventricular repolarization","Same direction as QRS in most leads"],["QT interval","Ventricular depolarization plus repolarization","Shortens as heart rate rises; read as rate-corrected QTc"],["U wave","Small wave after T","Prominent in hypokalemia"]]},{"title":"Pacemakers and conduction speed","cols":["Site","Intrinsic rate","Conduction speed"],"rows":[["SA node","About 60 to 100 bpm","Slow (nodal tissue)"],["Atrial muscle","None normally","About 0.3 m/s"],["AV node","About 40 to 60 bpm (junctional escape)","Slowest, gives about 0.1 s delay"],["Purkinje fibers","About 20 to 40 bpm (ventricular escape)","Fastest, about 1.5 to 4 m/s"],["Ventricular muscle","None normally","About 0.3 to 0.5 m/s"]]},{"title":"Limb and augmented lead angles","cols":["Lead","Electrodes","Angle"],"rows":[["I","Right arm (-) to left arm (+)","0°"],["II","Right arm (-) to left leg (+)","+60°"],["III","Left arm (-) to left leg (+)","+120°"],["aVR","Augmented, right arm positive","-150°"],["aVL","Augmented, left arm positive","-30°"],["aVF","Augmented, left leg positive","+90°"]]},{"title":"Lead groups, wall, and usual artery","cols":["Wall","Leads","Usual artery"],"rows":[["Septal","V1, V2","LAD"],["Anterior","V3, V4","LAD"],["Lateral","I, aVL, V5, V6","Left circumflex (V5 and V6 may be LAD)"],["Inferior","II, III, aVF","RCA in most people"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Park AT VENtura AVenue","text":"Conduction speed from fastest to slowest: Purkinje, Atria, Ventricles, AV node."},{"kind":"trick","label":"300, 150, 100, 75, 60, 50","text":"Find an R wave on a thick line. Count the next thick lines as 300, 150, 100, 75, 60, 50. Where the next R wave lands is the approximate heart rate."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Leads II, III, aVF look at the floor","text":"All three have their positive pole on the left leg, so they view the inferior wall, which usually gets its blood from the RCA."}],"highYield":["The AV node is the slowest part of the conduction system; that delay lets the atria finish ventricular filling.","SA nodal artery from the RCA in about 60% and AV nodal artery from the RCA in about 80%, so inferior MI often causes bradycardia or AV block.","Phase 0 in nodal cells is Ca2+ driven, so Ca2+ channel blockade slows AV conduction.","Einthoven's law: lead II = lead I + lead III.","Lyme carditis classically causes third-degree AV block.","Mobitz II is more dangerous than Mobitz I because the block is usually below the AV node.","Prominent U waves suggest hypokalemia."],"pitfalls":["The PR interval is measured from the start of P to the start of QRS; the PR segment is only from the end of P.","Atrial repolarization has no visible wave because it is hidden in the QRS.","The T wave is upright even though repolarization is the opposite of depolarization, because it travels in the opposite direction (epicardium to endocardium).","Mobitz I has progressive PR lengthening; Mobitz II has a constant PR before the dropped beat."],"dumps":[{"t":"Conduction system and pacemaker potential","p":"Draw the heart's conduction system in an opened heart, then beside it draw a pacemaker cell action potential. Label each structure and each ion current.","l":["SA node near SVC at the top of the crista terminalis","AV node near coronary sinus opening in the interatrial septum","Bundle of His through the fibrous skeleton","Right bundle branch through moderator band; left bundle with two fascicles","Purkinje fibers in subendocardium","Pacemaker phase 4 slow rise from If (HCN) then T-type Ca2+","Phase 0 slow upstroke from L-type Ca2+","Phase 3 K+ efflux; no plateau"]},{"t":"Label one ECG beat","p":"Draw one normal ECG beat on grid paper at standard speed. Label each wave, segment, and interval with its normal duration and the cardiac event it represents.","l":["Small box 0.04 s and large box 0.20 s","P wave is atrial depolarization","PR interval 0.12 to 0.20 s includes AV nodal delay","QRS under 0.12 s is ventricular depolarization","Atrial repolarization hidden in QRS","ST segment is the plateau, normally flat","T wave is ventricular repolarization","QT interval spans depolarization plus repolarization"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Rhythmical Excitation of the Heart"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fundamentals of Electrocardiography"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, section on the conducting system of the heart"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Electrophysiology | Intrinsic Cardiac Conduction System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1kX6Tp8CWFw"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Electrophysiology | Extrinsic Cardiac Conduction System","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2L4FbEEsy1s"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Electrical system of the heart | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7K2icszdxQc"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Relationship of conduction system, ventricular contraction and ECG","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eOX_NmYLKOE"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"EKG or ECG Interpretation | Part 1/20","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ku96IY27CE0"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-6","CV-8","CV-18-C3","CV-18-K3"]},"CV-8":{"id":"CV-8","summary":"This unit covers where the coronary arteries arise, how they branch, which part of the heart each one feeds, and how the heart's veins drain back to the right atrium. The exam uses it to link a blocked artery to the wall that dies, the ECG leads that change, and the complications that follow.","notes":[{"h":"Origins and the left system","points":["Right coronary artery (RCA) arises from the right aortic sinus; left coronary artery (LCA) from the left aortic sinus.","Left main runs between the pulmonary trunk and left auricle, then splits into the LAD and the circumflex (LCx).","LAD (anterior interventricular artery) runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex.","LAD feeds the anterior LV wall, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the septum (septal perforators); diagonal branches feed the anterolateral wall.","LCx runs in the left atrioventricular (coronary) groove to the back; obtuse marginal branches feed the lateral LV wall."]},{"h":"The right system","points":["RCA runs in the right atrioventricular groove.","Right (acute) marginal branch feeds the right ventricle.","SA nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 60% of hearts and from the LCx in about 40%.","AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80% of hearts.","In a right-dominant heart the RCA gives the posterior descending artery (PDA), which feeds the posterior one-third of the septum and the inferior LV wall."]},{"h":"Dominance and papillary muscles","points":["Dominance is defined by which artery gives the PDA.","Most hearts are right dominant (estimates run from about 67% to 85%); the rest are left dominant (PDA from LCx) or codominant.","Anterolateral papillary muscle has a double supply (LAD and LCx).","Posteromedial papillary muscle depends on the PDA alone, so it is the one that ruptures after an inferior MI."]},{"h":"Venous drainage","points":["Coronary sinus lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the right atrium.","Great cardiac vein runs with the LAD, middle cardiac vein with the PDA, small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery; all three reach the coronary sinus.","Anterior cardiac veins drain the front of the RV straight into the right atrium.","Smallest cardiac veins (venae cordis minimae) open directly into the chambers."]},{"h":"How coronary flow works","points":["Resting coronary flow is about 225 mL/min, about 4 to 5% of cardiac output.","LV muscle is perfused mostly in diastole, because systole squeezes the intramyocardial vessels shut.","Tachycardia shortens diastole, so it cuts LV perfusion while raising demand.","The heart extracts about 70% of arterial oxygen at rest, so more demand must be met by more flow.","Adenosine and other local metabolites are the main coronary vasodilators.","The subendocardium has the highest wall stress and the least flow reserve, so it becomes ischemic first."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Coronary arteries and their territories","cols":["Artery","Course","Main territory","Typical infarct ECG leads"],"rows":[["LAD","Anterior interventricular groove to the apex","Anterior LV wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum","V1 to V4 (anterior and septal)"],["LCx","Left atrioventricular groove to the posterior heart","Lateral and posterolateral LV wall, left atrium","I, aVL, V5, V6 (lateral)"],["RCA","Right atrioventricular groove","Right atrium, most of the RV, SA node (about 60%), AV node (about 80%)","II, III, aVF (inferior), often with bradycardia or AV block"],["PDA (from RCA in right-dominant hearts)","Posterior interventricular groove","Posterior one-third of septum, inferior LV wall, posteromedial papillary muscle","II, III, aVF (inferior)"]]},{"title":"Cardiac veins","cols":["Vein","Runs with","Drains into"],"rows":[["Great cardiac vein","LAD","Coronary sinus"],["Middle cardiac vein","PDA","Coronary sinus"],["Small cardiac vein","Right marginal artery","Coronary sinus"],["Anterior cardiac veins","Over the anterior RV wall","Right atrium directly"],["Smallest cardiac veins","Within the myocardium","Heart chambers directly"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Who gives the PDA wins","text":"To name the dominant artery, ask only one question: which artery gives the posterior descending artery? Size of the vessel does not matter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Veins shadow arteries","text":"Pair each vein with its artery: great with LAD, middle with PDA, small with right marginal. All three empty into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Diastole feeds the left","text":"Anything that shortens diastole (fast heart rate) or lowers aortic diastolic pressure (aortic regurgitation, hypotension) starves the LV subendocardium first."}],"highYield":["LAD is the most commonly occluded coronary artery, followed by the RCA, then the LCx.","Inferior MI (II, III, aVF) usually means RCA occlusion and can come with bradycardia or AV block.","Posteromedial papillary muscle rupture after inferior MI causes sudden acute mitral regurgitation.","Coronary perfusion pressure is aortic diastolic pressure minus LV end-diastolic pressure.","Coronary sinus runs in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the right atrium.","Adenosine is the classic local metabolite that dilates coronary arterioles when demand rises."],"pitfalls":["Dominance is about who supplies the PDA; a large LCx can still sit in a right-dominant heart.","The SA node is usually fed by the RCA (about 60%), but in about 40% it comes from the LCx, so an LCx lesion can still cause sinus node problems.","The anterolateral papillary muscle rarely ruptures because it has two arteries; the posteromedial one has only the PDA.","Anterior cardiac veins and smallest cardiac veins bypass the coronary sinus, so the coronary sinus does not carry all cardiac venous blood."],"dumps":[{"t":"Map the coronary arteries","p":"Draw the heart from the front and from below. Label the left main, LAD, diagonal branch, circumflex, obtuse marginal, RCA, right marginal, and PDA. Shade and name the wall each artery feeds.","l":["RCA from the right aortic sinus, LCA from the left aortic sinus","Left main splits into LAD and circumflex","LAD in the anterior interventricular groove reaching the apex","LAD territory: anterior LV wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum","Circumflex in the left atrioventricular groove feeding the lateral wall","RCA in the right atrioventricular groove with the right marginal branch to the RV","PDA in the posterior interventricular groove feeding the inferior wall and posterior one-third of septum","Note that the PDA source defines dominance"]},{"t":"Trace coronary blood","p":"Trace a red cell from the aorta through the LAD, across the anterior wall capillaries, and back to the right atrium. Name each vessel and say when in the cardiac cycle the flow is highest.","l":["Left aortic sinus to the left main coronary artery","Left main to LAD, then septal or diagonal branches","Capillaries of the anterior LV wall","Great cardiac vein running back up beside the LAD","Coronary sinus in the posterior atrioventricular groove","Opening into the right atrium","LV flow is highest in diastole because systole compresses intramyocardial vessels","About 70% of the oxygen is extracted on the way through"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax chapter, section on the heart: arterial supply and venous drainage"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on ischemic heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Coronary Circulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TEcPhovaAg4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Anatomy of the Heart and Coronary Circulation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ERVxf5eD7k"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiology - Coronary Blood Supply","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3wpT-4bSmoU"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-7","CV-18-C","CV-18-C3"]},"CV-9":{"id":"CV-9","summary":"This unit covers how arterial blood reaches the head, limbs, and abdominal organs, how venous blood returns, and how the portal system connects the gut to the liver. Exam questions name a vessel or a vertebral level and ask what it supplies, or describe a blockage and ask what tissue suffers.","notes":[{"h":"Aortic arch and thoracic aorta","points":["Arch branches, right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian.","Brachiocephalic trunk splits into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries.","Common carotid divides at about the level of C4 (upper border of the thyroid cartilage) into internal and external carotids.","Descending thoracic aorta gives posterior intercostal, bronchial, and esophageal branches."]},{"h":"Abdominal aorta","points":["Enters the abdomen through the aortic hiatus at T12 and bifurcates into the common iliac arteries at L4.","Unpaired gut arteries: celiac trunk (T12), superior mesenteric artery (L1), inferior mesenteric artery (L3).","Paired branches: inferior phrenic, middle suprarenal, renal (about L1), gonadal (about L2), and four pairs of lumbar arteries.","Celiac trunk gives the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","Splenic flexure sits between SMA and IMA territories, a watershed zone for ischemic colitis."]},{"h":"Brain and limbs","points":["Brain gets anterior flow from the internal carotids and posterior flow from the vertebral arteries (branches of the subclavians), which join as the basilar artery.","Circle of Willis links them: anterior communicating, anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral arteries.","Upper limb: subclavian becomes axillary at the lateral border of the first rib, then brachial at the lower border of teres major; brachial splits into radial and ulnar in the cubital fossa.","Lower limb: external iliac becomes femoral under the inguinal ligament, then popliteal at the adductor hiatus; popliteal splits into anterior tibial and the tibiofibular trunk (posterior tibial and fibular).","Dorsalis pedis is the continuation of the anterior tibial artery."]},{"h":"Veins and the portal system","points":["SVC forms from the two brachiocephalic veins; IVC forms from the common iliac veins at about L5.","Left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the SMA; the left gonadal vein drains into it, while the right gonadal vein drains straight into the IVC.","Hepatic portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins; the inferior mesenteric vein usually joins the splenic vein.","Portal blood passes through liver sinusoids, then leaves by the hepatic veins into the IVC."]},{"h":"Where cardiac output goes at rest","points":["Liver and gut together receive about a quarter of resting cardiac output, kidneys about a fifth.","Skeletal muscle gets about 15% at rest and the brain about 14%.","Heart muscle gets about 4 to 5% but uses oxygen at a very high rate."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Abdominal aorta landmarks","cols":["Branch or event","Vertebral level","Supplies"],"rows":[["Aorta passes the aortic hiatus","T12","Start of the abdominal aorta"],["Celiac trunk","T12","Foregut: stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, gallbladder, part of the pancreas, spleen"],["Superior mesenteric artery","L1","Midgut: distal duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon"],["Renal arteries","About L1","Kidneys"],["Gonadal arteries","About L2","Testes or ovaries"],["Inferior mesenteric artery","L3","Hindgut: distal one-third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal"],["Bifurcation into common iliacs","L4","Pelvis and lower limbs"]]},{"title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Sign in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Lower esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins draining to the azygos system","Esophageal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABC'S","text":"Aortic arch gives the Brachiocephalic trunk, left common Carotid, and left Subclavian, in that order from right to left."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bifourcation","text":"The abdominal aorta bifurcates at L4: bi-FOUR-cation."},{"kind":"trick","label":"One artery per gut segment","text":"Foregut goes with the celiac trunk, midgut with the SMA, hindgut with the IMA. Find which segment the organ came from and you have its artery."}],"highYield":["Celiac T12, SMA L1, renal about L1, gonadal about L2, IMA L3, bifurcation L4.","Left renal vein can be squeezed between the SMA and aorta (nutcracker phenomenon).","Left varicocele is more common because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein.","Splenic flexure is a watershed area between SMA and IMA territories.","Portal hypertension opens anastomoses at the esophagus, umbilicus, and rectum.","The vertebral arteries come off the subclavians and join to form the basilar artery."],"pitfalls":["The spleen develops from mesoderm, yet it is supplied by the celiac trunk (splenic artery).","The pancreatic head has a double supply: superior pancreaticoduodenal (celiac) and inferior pancreaticoduodenal (SMA) arteries.","The popliteal artery starts at the adductor hiatus, while the femoral artery starts at the inguinal ligament; mixing these up shifts the level of a lesion.","The right gonadal vein drains into the IVC; only the left one drains into the renal vein."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the abdominal aorta","p":"Draw the abdominal aorta from the diaphragm to its bifurcation. Mark the vertebral level of each branch and write what each branch supplies.","l":["Aortic hiatus at T12","Celiac trunk at T12 with left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic branches","SMA at L1 supplying the midgut","Renal arteries at about L1 and gonadal arteries at about L2","IMA at L3 supplying the hindgut","Bifurcation into common iliac arteries at L4","Left renal vein crossing anterior to the aorta below the SMA"]},{"t":"Draw the circle of Willis","p":"Draw the circle of Willis as seen from below the brain. Label every artery that forms the circle, the arteries that feed it, and the middle cerebral arteries.","l":["Internal carotid arteries entering on each side","Anterior cerebral arteries joined by the anterior communicating artery","Middle cerebral arteries coming off each internal carotid","Posterior communicating arteries linking internal carotid to posterior cerebral","Posterior cerebral arteries from the top of the basilar artery","Basilar artery formed by the two vertebral arteries","Vertebral arteries arising from the subclavian arteries"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen chapter, section on the abdominal aorta and the arterial supply of the abdominal viscera"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head chapter, section on the arterial supply of the brain"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Arteries of the Thorax & Abdomen | Torso Anatomy Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L4vf12QCy70"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Circulatory System | Arteries & Veins of the Upper & Lower Limbs | Wire Man Model","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EmoOicZqXhI"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Arteries, arterioles, venules, and veins | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iqRTd1NY-pU"}],"related":["CV-3","CV-11","CV-18-H3","CV-18-I6"]},"CV-10":{"id":"CV-10","summary":"This unit covers how lymph is formed, how it moves, where it drains, and how lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, and mucosal lymphoid tissue are built. The exam asks about drainage routes, the zones of each lymphoid organ, and what goes wrong when lymph flow is blocked.","notes":[{"h":"Lymph formation","points":["Lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended tubes with overlapping endothelial cells that act as one-way flap valves.","Anchoring filaments hold them open when the tissue swells.","They return filtered fluid and leaked plasma protein to the blood, about 2 to 3 L per day.","Lacteals in intestinal villi take up dietary fat as chylomicrons, which makes lymph milky (chyle)."]},{"h":"How lymph moves","points":["Collecting lymphatics have valves and smooth muscle that contract in segments (lymphangions).","Skeletal muscle contraction, breathing, nearby arterial pulsation, and outside compression all push lymph forward.","A rise in interstitial fluid pressure increases lymph flow, up to a maximum."]},{"h":"Drainage routes","points":["Thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli (about L1 to L2), passes through the aortic hiatus, and drains into the left venous angle (left internal jugular and left subclavian junction).","Thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper body.","Right lymphatic duct (or its trunks separately) drains the right head, neck, arm, and right side of the thorax into the right venous angle."]},{"h":"Lymphoid organs","points":["Primary lymphoid organs: bone marrow (B cells mature) and thymus (T cells mature).","Secondary lymphoid organs: lymph nodes, spleen, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (tonsils, Peyer patches in the ileum, appendix).","Lymph node path: afferent vessels, subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, medullary sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum.","Spleen filters blood: red pulp removes old red cells; white pulp mounts immune responses to blood-borne antigens.","Thymus comes from the third pharyngeal pouch; positive selection happens in the cortex, negative selection in the medulla."]},{"h":"When lymph flow fails","points":["Lymphedema is protein-rich edema that becomes firm and nonpitting as fibrosis sets in.","Causes include lymph node removal or radiation (such as after breast cancer surgery), tumor, and filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti).","Thoracic duct injury leaks chyle into the pleural space (chylothorax)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Zones of lymphoid organs","cols":["Organ","Zone","Main cells","Job"],"rows":[["Lymph node","Cortex follicles","B cells; germinal centers in secondary follicles","B-cell activation and proliferation"],["Lymph node","Paracortex","T cells and dendritic cells; high endothelial venules","T-cell activation; entry point for circulating lymphocytes"],["Lymph node","Medulla","Plasma cells and macrophages in medullary cords","Antibody secretion and filtering of lymph"],["Spleen","White pulp, periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","T cells","T-cell responses to blood antigens"],["Spleen","White pulp, follicles","B cells","B-cell responses to blood antigens"],["Spleen","Red pulp (cords and sinusoids)","Macrophages","Removes old or damaged red cells"],["Thymus","Cortex","Immature T cells and cortical epithelial cells","Positive selection"],["Thymus","Medulla","Maturing T cells; Hassall corpuscles","Negative selection"]]},{"title":"First lymph node group by region","cols":["Region drained","First node group"],"rows":[["Upper limb and lateral breast","Axillary"],["Medial breast","Parasternal (internal thoracic)"],["Scrotum, vulva, anal canal below the pectinate line, most of the superficial lower limb","Superficial inguinal"],["Lateral foot and posterolateral calf","Popliteal"],["Testes and ovaries","Para-aortic (lumbar)"],["Stomach","Celiac"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Right upper corner","text":"Only the right upper corner of the body (right head, neck, arm, and chest) goes to the right lymphatic duct. Every other region drains to the thoracic duct."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SHiNE SKiS","text":"Encapsulated organisms that threaten people without a spleen: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis, Escherichia coli, Salmonella, Klebsiella pneumoniae, group B Streptococcus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Cortex positive, medulla negative","text":"In the thymus, the cortex keeps T cells that can bind self MHC (positive selection), and the medulla deletes T cells that bind self antigen too strongly (negative selection)."}],"highYield":["Testes drain to para-aortic nodes, while the scrotum drains to superficial inguinal nodes.","Paracortex is underdeveloped in DiGeorge syndrome because T cells are lacking.","Asplenia raises the risk of sepsis from encapsulated bacteria; Howell-Jolly bodies appear on the smear.","Thoracic duct empties into the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins.","Lymphedema fluid is protein rich, which drives fibrosis and nonpitting swelling.","Peyer patches sit in the ileum and contain M cells that sample gut antigens."],"pitfalls":["Anal canal below the pectinate line drains to superficial inguinal nodes; above it drains to internal iliac nodes.","Lymph node B cells sit in the cortex follicles, while T cells sit in the paracortex; in the spleen T cells sit in the periarteriolar sheath.","Lymphedema and cardiac edema both swell the legs, but lymphedema is protein rich and becomes nonpitting, while early edema from high venous pressure pits easily.","Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs; the spleen is secondary."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw a lymph node","p":"Draw a lymph node in cross section. Label where lymph enters and leaves, every sinus it passes through, and the B-cell, T-cell, and plasma-cell zones.","l":["Afferent lymphatic vessels entering the convex surface","Capsule and subcapsular sinus","Cortex with primary and secondary follicles and germinal centers (B cells)","Paracortex with T cells and high endothelial venules","Medullary cords with plasma cells and macrophages","Medullary sinuses leading to the hilum","Efferent lymphatic vessel, artery, and vein at the hilum"]},{"t":"Trace lymph back to the blood","p":"Trace a drop of interstitial fluid from the left foot back into the blood. Then do the same for a drop from the right hand. Name each vessel and node group.","l":["Lymphatic capillary with overlapping endothelial flaps","Collecting vessels with valves","Left foot: superficial inguinal nodes, then deep inguinal, external iliac, common iliac, and lumbar nodes","Lumbar trunks to the cisterna chyli near L1 to L2","Thoracic duct through the aortic hiatus to the left venous angle","Right hand: axillary nodes, then the subclavian trunk","Right lymphatic duct (or the trunks directly) to the right venous angle"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Introduction to the Immune System, section on the tissues of the immune system (lymph nodes, spleen, mucosal lymphoid tissue)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Why we need a lymphatic system | Lymphatic system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_GinTV94hUk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"How lymphatic vessels move fluid | Lymphatic system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CLneY6SoPi0"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-16","CV-18-H4"]},"CV-11":{"id":"CV-11","summary":"This unit covers the physics of blood flow (pressure, resistance, viscosity, turbulence), the Starling forces that move fluid across capillaries, and how tissues control their own flow. Board questions ask you to predict what happens to flow or pressure when one variable changes.","notes":[{"h":"Pressure, flow, and resistance","points":["Flow equals pressure difference divided by resistance (Q = ΔP / R).","For the whole circulation, mean arterial pressure is about cardiac output times total peripheral resistance.","Poiseuille: resistance = 8ηL / (πr⁴), so radius matters most.","Halving the radius raises resistance 16 times and cuts flow to one-sixteenth at the same pressure.","Resistances in series add; in parallel, 1/R total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + and so on, so the total is lower than any single branch.","Organs sit in parallel, which lets each one change its own flow without changing the others much."]},{"h":"Velocity, viscosity, turbulence","points":["Velocity = flow divided by total cross-sectional area, so blood moves slowest in capillaries.","Hematocrit is the main determinant of viscosity; polycythemia raises viscosity and resistance, anemia lowers them.","Turbulence becomes more likely with high velocity, low viscosity, large diameter, and rough or narrowed vessels (Reynolds number).","Turbulence makes bruits and murmurs, such as flow murmurs in anemia."]},{"h":"Arterial pressure and compliance","points":["Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic; it widens with stiff arteries or a larger stroke volume.","MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one-third of pulse pressure at resting heart rates.","Veins are about 24 times more compliant than their matching arteries and hold about 64% of blood volume.","Law of Laplace: wall tension rises with pressure and radius, so a dilated ventricle or aneurysm faces more wall stress."]},{"h":"Capillary exchange and lymph","points":["Starling: net filtration = Kf × [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)].","Edema comes from higher capillary pressure, lower plasma oncotic pressure, leakier capillaries, or blocked lymphatics.","Lymph returns the small net filtrate and leaked protein to the blood.","Venous return is helped by venous valves, the skeletal muscle pump, and the drop in thoracic pressure during inspiration."]},{"h":"Control of blood flow","points":["Metabolic control: adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, lactate, and low O₂ dilate arterioles in active tissue.","Myogenic control: stretch of arteriolar smooth muscle triggers contraction, which keeps flow steady when pressure rises (autoregulation).","Endothelium releases nitric oxide (from shear stress) and prostacyclin to dilate, and endothelin to constrict.","Sympathetic nerves constrict most arterioles through α1 receptors.","Constrictors in blood: norepinephrine, angiotensin II, vasopressin. Dilators: bradykinin, histamine, ANP, nitric oxide.","Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) report pressure to the nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Vessel segments","cols":["Segment","Key feature","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Aorta and large arteries","Elastic, store energy in systole","Keep flow going during diastole"],["Arterioles","Largest share of resistance and the biggest pressure drop","Set total peripheral resistance and divide flow among organs"],["Capillaries","Largest total cross-sectional area, slowest velocity","Time for exchange with tissues"],["Venules and veins","Most compliant, hold about 64% of blood volume","Blood reservoir; venoconstriction raises venous return"]]},{"title":"Main control of flow by organ","cols":["Organ","Dominant control"],"rows":[["Heart","Local metabolites (adenosine, NO, CO₂) and low O₂"],["Brain","Arterial PCO₂ (through pH)"],["Kidney","Myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback"],["Lung","Hypoxia causes vasoconstriction"],["Skeletal muscle","Local metabolites during exercise; sympathetic tone at rest"],["Skin","Sympathetic nerves (temperature control)"]]},{"title":"Starling forces","cols":["Force","Pushes fluid","Changes that cause edema"],"rows":[["Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc)","Out of the capillary","Rises in heart failure, venous obstruction, volume overload"],["Plasma oncotic pressure (πc)","Into the capillary","Falls in nephrotic syndrome, liver failure, malnutrition"],["Interstitial hydrostatic pressure (Pi)","Into the capillary when positive (it is slightly negative in most loose tissues, which favors filtration)","Rarely a primary cause; it rises as edema builds and opposes further filtration"],["Interstitial oncotic pressure (πi)","Out of the capillary","Rises with leaky capillaries (burns, inflammation) or lymphatic blockage"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Blood follows Ohm's law","text":"ΔP = Q × R works like V = I × R. Pressure is voltage, flow is current, and resistance is resistance. MAP = CO × TPR is the same idea for the whole body."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Radius to the fourth","text":"When a question changes vessel radius, think in powers of two: half the radius gives 16 times the resistance; double the radius gives one-sixteenth."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Four ways to edema","text":"Push harder (high Pc), pull weaker (low πc), leak more (high Kf or πi), or drain less (lymph block). Sort any edema cause into one of the four."}],"highYield":["Arterioles are the main resistance vessels and the main site of blood pressure control.","Capillaries have the greatest total cross-sectional area and the slowest velocity.","Radius is raised to the fourth power in Poiseuille's law, so small changes in caliber cause large changes in flow.","Cerebral flow tracks PCO₂: hyperventilation lowers PCO₂ and constricts cerebral vessels.","Hypoxia dilates systemic vessels but constricts pulmonary vessels.","Nephrotic syndrome causes edema by lowering plasma oncotic pressure.","Isolated systolic hypertension in older adults reflects stiff, less compliant arteries."],"pitfalls":["Adding a parallel vascular bed lowers total resistance; adding a segment in series raises it.","Blood velocity is lowest in capillaries even though each capillary is tiny, because their combined area is the largest.","Pulmonary vessels constrict to hypoxia, the opposite of systemic vessels.","Higher hematocrit raises viscosity and resistance; anemia lowers them and favors turbulence."],"dumps":[{"t":"Pressure, area, and velocity","p":"Draw three stacked line graphs across the aorta, arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins, and vena cava: one for pressure, one for total cross-sectional area, one for velocity. Explain each shape.","l":["Pressure is highest in the aorta and pulsatile in large arteries","Steepest pressure drop across the arterioles","Pulsatility is mostly gone by the capillaries","Venous pressure is low and near zero at the right atrium","Cross-sectional area peaks at the capillaries","Velocity is the mirror image of area, lowest in capillaries","Velocity rises again as veins merge into the vena cava"]},{"t":"Starling forces and edema","p":"Build a table on blank paper of the four Starling forces. For each, write which way it pushes fluid and one disease that changes it to cause edema. Add the role of lymph.","l":["Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out","Plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid in","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure opposes filtration when positive","Interstitial oncotic pressure pulls fluid out","Heart failure raises capillary hydrostatic pressure","Nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis lowers plasma oncotic pressure","Burns or inflammation raise permeability","Lymph removes the net filtrate; blockage causes lymphedema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Section on the overview of the circulation and the physics of pressure, flow, and resistance"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Fundamentals of Blood Pressure","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2g7pFPXG2eE"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Resistance in a tube | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8X7yEzgurP8"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Flow and perfusion | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePwN6rHhNyU"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Hemodynamics | Circulatory System Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCyTYsxECLw"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-10","CV-18-A2","CV-18-H4"]},"CV-12":{"id":"CV-12","summary":"This unit covers how the heart and vessels respond during a single bout of exercise and how they adapt with regular training. Questions ask you to predict changes in heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, blood pressure, and resistance, and to tell an athlete's heart from disease.","notes":[{"h":"Starting exercise","points":["Central command from the motor cortex and reflexes from muscle mechanoreceptors and metaboreceptors raise sympathetic output.","The early rise in heart rate comes from vagal withdrawal; sympathetic drive takes over as intensity rises.","Venoconstriction, the skeletal muscle pump, and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart."]},{"h":"Heart and output","points":["Heart rate rises roughly in step with workload.","Stroke volume rises from more filling (Frank-Starling) and stronger contraction; in many untrained people it levels off at moderate intensity.","Cardiac output can rise about four- to fivefold in hard exercise in untrained adults, and more in trained endurance athletes.","Oxygen uptake follows the Fick principle: VO₂ = cardiac output × arteriovenous O₂ difference.","The arteriovenous O₂ difference widens because active muscle extracts more oxygen."]},{"h":"Redistribution of flow","points":["Active muscle arterioles dilate to local metabolites (K⁺, adenosine, H⁺, CO₂, lactate) and nitric oxide.","Muscle blood flow can rise up to about 25 times in strenuous exercise.","Sympathetic constriction cuts flow to the gut, kidneys, and inactive muscle.","Skin flow rises as body temperature climbs, to shed heat.","Brain flow stays fairly constant; coronary flow rises with cardiac work."]},{"h":"Blood pressure in two kinds of exercise","points":["Dynamic (aerobic) exercise: systolic pressure rises, diastolic stays about the same or falls slightly, total peripheral resistance falls.","Static (isometric) exercise: contracting muscle squeezes its own vessels, so systolic and diastolic pressure both rise sharply.","Dynamic exercise is mainly a volume load on the heart; static exercise is mainly a pressure load."]},{"h":"Training adaptations","points":["Endurance training enlarges the LV cavity with proportional wall thickening (eccentric remodeling), so stroke volume rises.","Plasma and blood volume increase, which raises preload.","Resting heart rate falls, mostly from higher vagal tone; maximum heart rate changes little.","Maximal cardiac output and VO₂max rise.","Trained muscle has more capillaries and mitochondria, so it extracts more oxygen.","Regular exercise lowers resting blood pressure, raises HDL, lowers triglycerides, and improves insulin sensitivity and endothelial nitric oxide release."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Acute response: dynamic vs static exercise","cols":["Variable","Dynamic (running, cycling)","Static (heavy hold, handgrip)"],"rows":[["Heart rate","Rises a lot","Rises moderately"],["Stroke volume","Rises","Little change"],["Cardiac output","Rises a lot","Rises modestly"],["Systolic pressure","Rises","Rises sharply"],["Diastolic pressure","Same or slightly lower","Rises sharply"],["Total peripheral resistance","Falls","Same or rises"],["Main load on the LV","Volume","Pressure"]]},{"title":"Athlete's heart vs hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","cols":["Feature","Athlete's heart","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"rows":[["Wall thickening","Symmetric and mild","Often asymmetric, septum most"],["LV cavity","Normal or enlarged","Small"],["Diastolic filling","Normal","Impaired (stiff ventricle)"],["After a period of rest from training","Thickening regresses","No regression"],["Family history of sudden death","Usually absent","May be present (autosomal dominant inheritance)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Fick for exercise","text":"VO₂ = CO × (arterial minus venous O₂ content). Exercise raises both terms: the heart pumps more, and muscle pulls more oxygen out of each liter."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Run vs lift","text":"Running opens muscle vessels, so resistance falls and diastolic pressure holds steady. Lifting squeezes them shut, so both systolic and diastolic pressure climb."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Big tank, slow pump","text":"The trained heart has a bigger LV and more blood volume, so each beat moves more blood and the resting rate can be lower for the same output."}],"highYield":["In dynamic exercise total peripheral resistance falls because active muscle beds dilate.","Diastolic pressure stays about the same or falls slightly in dynamic exercise; it rises in static exercise.","Resting bradycardia in trained athletes is mainly from increased vagal tone.","The arteriovenous O₂ difference widens during exercise and after training.","Exercise shunts blood away from the gut and kidneys toward muscle, heart, and skin.","Athlete's heart regresses after training stops; hypertrophic cardiomyopathy does not."],"pitfalls":["Cardiac output at rest stays about the same after training; the lower heart rate is balanced by a larger stroke volume.","Maximum heart rate does not rise with training; the gains in maximal output come from stroke volume.","A rise in systolic pressure during running is normal; a fall in systolic pressure as workload increases is abnormal.","Brain blood flow stays fairly constant in exercise even though cardiac output rises several-fold."],"dumps":[{"t":"Rest vs hard exercise","p":"Build a table with two columns, rest and hard dynamic exercise. Fill in what happens to heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, systolic pressure, diastolic pressure, total peripheral resistance, arteriovenous O₂ difference, muscle flow, and gut and kidney flow.","l":["Heart rate up","Stroke volume up","Cardiac output up about four- to fivefold (more in trained athletes)","Systolic pressure up","Diastolic pressure about the same or slightly down","Total peripheral resistance down","Arteriovenous O₂ difference wider","Muscle flow up as much as about 25 times","Gut and kidney flow down from sympathetic constriction"]},{"t":"Trace the reflexes of a run","p":"Trace the steps from the moment someone starts running to the rise in cardiac output. Include the nerve signals, the heart, the veins, and the muscle arterioles.","l":["Central command from the motor cortex","Muscle mechanoreceptor and metaboreceptor input","Medullary cardiovascular centers respond","Vagal withdrawal raises heart rate first","Sympathetic drive raises heart rate and contractility","Venoconstriction and the muscle pump raise venous return","More filling raises stroke volume (Frank-Starling)","Local metabolites dilate active muscle arterioles while inactive beds constrict"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiovascular | Cardiac Output","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0O3FfHPE9PU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Autonomic nervous system effects on the heart | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KiouveG278Y"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-11","CV-15","CV-18-E1"]},"CV-13":{"id":"CV-13","summary":"This unit covers how lipids travel in the blood, how the heart makes its ATP, and which vitamins, minerals, and cofactors the heart and vessels depend on. The exam links a biochemical defect or deficiency to a heart or vessel problem, such as thiamine and high-output failure or homocysteine and thrombosis.","notes":[{"h":"Lipoprotein transport","points":["Exogenous path: intestine packs dietary triglyceride into chylomicrons (apo B-48), which reach the blood through lymph.","Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium, activated by apo C-II, frees fatty acids from chylomicrons and VLDL.","Endogenous path: liver makes VLDL (apo B-100), which becomes IDL and then LDL as triglyceride is removed.","LDL delivers cholesterol to cells through the LDL receptor, which binds apo B-100.","HDL (apo A-I) carries cholesterol back to the liver (reverse cholesterol transport); apo A-I activates LCAT, which esterifies cholesterol.","CETP moves cholesteryl esters from HDL to VLDL, IDL, and LDL in exchange for triglyceride.","HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis."]},{"h":"Lipids and the vessel wall","points":["LDL that enters the intima can be oxidized; macrophages take it up through scavenger receptors that are not downregulated, becoming foam cells.","Lipoprotein(a) is an LDL-like particle with apo(a), which resembles plasminogen; its level is mostly genetic and it adds atherosclerotic risk.","Trans fats raise LDL and lower HDL; omega-3 fatty acids (EPA, DHA) lower triglycerides at higher intakes."]},{"h":"Cardiac energy metabolism","points":["The adult heart at rest makes most of its ATP (roughly 60 to 90%) from fatty acid beta-oxidation; glucose, lactate, and ketones supply the rest.","Mitochondria fill about a third of cardiomyocyte volume, so the heart depends on a steady O₂ supply.","Long-chain fatty acids need carnitine and carnitine palmitoyltransferase I to enter mitochondria; malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-I.","Creatine kinase moves high-energy phosphate from mitochondria to myofibrils as creatine phosphate.","In ischemia the heart switches to anaerobic glycolysis, lactate builds up, and contraction fails within a minute or two.","Coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) carries electrons in the respiratory chain and is made from the same mevalonate pathway that statins block."]},{"h":"Vitamins","points":["Thiamine (B1), as thiamine pyrophosphate, is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase, and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase.","Thiamine deficiency with heart failure (wet beriberi) is a high-output state with dilated heart and peripheral vasodilation; risk is high in alcohol use disorder and malnutrition.","Homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by methionine synthase (needs B12 and folate) or converted to cystathionine by cystathionine beta-synthase (needs B6).","Deficiency of B12, folate, or B6 raises homocysteine, which injures endothelium and favors thrombosis.","Vitamin C is needed to hydroxylate proline and lysine in collagen; scurvy causes fragile vessels, bleeding gums, and perifollicular hemorrhage.","Vitamin K is needed to gamma-carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S."]},{"h":"Minerals","points":["Potassium (normal about 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L) sets resting membrane potential; both low and high levels cause arrhythmias.","Calcium (normal about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL) triggers contraction through L-type channels and calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Magnesium is needed for Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity; low magnesium increases renal potassium loss, so hypokalemia is hard to correct, and causes hypocalcemia by impairing PTH release and action.","Iron deficiency anemia causes a high-output, tachycardic state; iron overload (hemochromatosis) causes cardiomyopathy and conduction problems.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan disease, a cardiomyopathy.","Copper is a cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin; deficiency (as in Menkes disease) weakens arteries."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Lipoproteins","cols":["Particle","Made in","Main cargo","Key apolipoproteins","Job"],"rows":[["Chylomicron","Intestine","Dietary triglyceride","B-48, C-II, E","Delivers dietary fat to muscle and adipose tissue; remnant goes to liver"],["VLDL","Liver","Hepatic triglyceride","B-100, C-II, E","Delivers liver-made triglyceride to tissues"],["IDL","From VLDL in plasma","Triglyceride and cholesterol","B-100, E","Taken up by liver or converted to LDL"],["LDL","From IDL in plasma","Cholesteryl ester","B-100","Delivers cholesterol to tissues through the LDL receptor"],["HDL","Liver and intestine","Cholesterol and phospholipid","A-I (also carries C-II and E)","Reverse cholesterol transport; apolipoprotein reservoir"]]},{"title":"Apolipoprotein functions","cols":["Apolipoprotein","Function"],"rows":[["A-I","Activates LCAT"],["B-48","Needed to assemble and secrete chylomicrons"],["B-100","Binds the LDL receptor"],["C-II","Cofactor for lipoprotein lipase"],["E","Mediates uptake of chylomicron remnants and IDL by the liver"]]},{"title":"Electrolytes and the ECG","cols":["Disturbance","ECG findings","Risk"],"rows":[["Hypokalemia","Flattened T waves, U waves","Arrhythmias; raises digoxin toxicity"],["Hyperkalemia","Peaked T waves, wide QRS, loss of P waves, sine wave pattern","Ventricular fibrillation or asystole"],["Hypocalcemia","Prolonged QT","Arrhythmias, tetany"],["Hypercalcemia","Shortened QT","Arrhythmias"],["Hypomagnesemia","Prolonged QT possible","Torsades de pointes; refractory hypokalemia"]]},{"title":"Homocysteine metabolism","cols":["Step","Enzyme","Cofactor needed","Lab clue when deficient"],"rows":[["Homocysteine to methionine","Methionine synthase","Vitamin B12 and folate (as N5-methyl-THF)","B12 deficiency: high homocysteine and high methylmalonic acid. Folate deficiency: high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid"],["Homocysteine to cystathionine","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Vitamin B6","High homocysteine; inherited enzyme defect causes homocystinuria"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Thiamine has four enzymes","text":"List them as pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase. All energy production slows without B1, and the heart fails in a high-output pattern."},{"kind":"trick","label":"MMA splits B12 from folate","text":"Both deficiencies raise homocysteine. Only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Apo letters by job","text":"C-II cuts (activates lipoprotein lipase), E is the entry ticket to the liver for remnants, B-100 docks on the LDL receptor, A-I activates LCAT."}],"highYield":["Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency causing high-output heart failure.","Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase; apo B-100 binds the LDL receptor.","Oxidized LDL taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors forms foam cells.","The resting adult heart burns mostly fatty acids for ATP.","Hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia and raises the risk of torsades de pointes.","Hyperkalemia shows peaked T waves; hypokalemia shows U waves.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan cardiomyopathy.","Homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency) causes arterial and venous thrombosis."],"pitfalls":["LDL receptors on cells are downregulated when cells have enough cholesterol; scavenger receptors on macrophages are not, which is why foam cells overfill.","Lowering homocysteine with B vitamins has not clearly reduced heart attacks in trials, so treat high homocysteine as a marker of risk and a sign of vitamin status.","Thiamine deficiency heart failure is high output with low resistance, unlike the low-output failure of most cardiomyopathies.","Hypocalcemia lengthens QT and hypercalcemia shortens it; potassium changes show up mostly in T and U waves."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lipoprotein pathways","p":"Draw the exogenous and endogenous lipoprotein pathways from the gut and liver to the tissues and back. Label each particle, its main apolipoproteins, and each enzyme or receptor.","l":["Chylomicron from the intestine with B-48, entering blood through lymph","Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium, activated by C-II","Chylomicron remnant taken up by the liver through apo E","VLDL from the liver with B-100","VLDL to IDL to LDL as triglyceride is removed","LDL taken up through the LDL receptor binding B-100","HDL with A-I activating LCAT and returning cholesterol to the liver","CETP swapping cholesteryl ester for triglyceride"]},{"t":"Nutrients the heart needs","p":"Build a table with three columns: nutrient, its biochemical job, and the heart or vessel problem when it is missing or in excess. Include thiamine, B12, folate, B6, vitamin C, potassium, magnesium, iron, and selenium.","l":["Thiamine: TPP for pyruvate dehydrogenase and related enzymes; wet beriberi high-output failure","B12 and folate: methionine synthase; high homocysteine","B6: cystathionine beta-synthase; high homocysteine","Vitamin C: collagen hydroxylation; fragile vessels in scurvy","Potassium: resting membrane potential; arrhythmias when low or high","Magnesium: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; torsades and refractory hypokalemia","Iron: deficiency gives high-output state, overload gives cardiomyopathy","Selenium: deficiency gives Keshan cardiomyopathy"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Section on vitamins (water-soluble and fat-soluble micronutrients)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases, section on nutritional diseases"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Metabolism | Lipoprotein Metabolism | Chylomicrons, VLDL, IDL, LDL, & HDL","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wQY0xpwqPfQ"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hyperlipidemia | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gE9cIQ9vz0k"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Physiology of Lipoproteins Cholesterol","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PkKH8lTxvzA"}],"related":["CV-5","CV-14","CV-18-I3","CV-18-I4","CV-18-C"]},"CV-14":{"id":"CV-14","summary":"This unit covers single-gene and chromosomal disorders that affect the heart and vessels, plus the gene-expression and cell-cycle ideas behind them. The exam asks you to match a gene or inheritance pattern to a cardiovascular finding and to explain the mechanism.","notes":[{"h":"Genetic principles seen in heart disease","points":["Most single-gene cardiovascular disorders are autosomal dominant (familial hypercholesterolemia, HCM, Marfan, most long QT).","Variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance are common, so relatives with the same variant can look very different.","Locus heterogeneity: one disease, many genes (familial hypercholesterolemia from LDLR, APOB, or PCSK9; long QT from many channel genes).","Pleiotropy: one gene, many organs (fibrillin-1 in Marfan affects aorta, eye, and skeleton).","Gene dosage matters: familial hypercholesterolemia homozygotes are far more severe than heterozygotes.","Mitochondrial DNA disorders can cause cardiomyopathy and conduction block (for example Kearns-Sayre syndrome); heteroplasmy explains variable severity."]},{"h":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","points":["Usually a mutation in the LDL receptor gene; APOB and gain-of-function PCSK9 variants cause the same picture.","Fewer working receptors means less LDL and IDL clearance, so more IDL becomes LDL.","Less cholesterol inside liver cells also takes the brake off cholesterol synthesis.","Heterozygotes have about two to three times normal plasma cholesterol; homozygotes about five to six times, often with MI before age 20.","Signs: tendon xanthomas (often Achilles), xanthelasma, early corneal arcus, premature coronary disease."]},{"h":"Cardiomyopathy and channel genes","points":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: sarcomere protein genes, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (myosin-binding protein C).","HCM shows myocyte disarray, asymmetric septal thickening, diastolic dysfunction, and a risk of sudden death in young athletes.","Congenital long QT comes from potassium channel loss (KCNQ1, KCNH2) or sodium channel gain (SCN5A), which delays repolarization and invites torsades de pointes.","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is autosomal recessive long QT with sensorineural deafness; Romano-Ward syndrome is autosomal dominant without deafness.","Brugada syndrome: loss of function in SCN5A, ST elevation in V1 to V3 with a pseudo-right bundle branch block pattern, and sudden death risk.","Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy (dystrophin, X-linked) cause dilated cardiomyopathy; Friedreich ataxia (frataxin, GAA repeat) causes HCM."]},{"h":"Connective tissue disorders","points":["Marfan syndrome: FBN1 (fibrillin-1) on chromosome 15; faulty microfibrils weaken elastic tissue and increase TGF-beta signaling.","Marfan cardiovascular findings: aortic root dilation, aortic dissection, aortic regurgitation, mitral valve prolapse.","Loeys-Dietz syndrome (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2): aggressive aortic aneurysms, arterial tortuosity, bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1, type III collagen): arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture."]},{"h":"Gene expression and the cell cycle in the heart","points":["Adult cardiomyocytes have largely left the cell cycle and renew very slowly, so dead myocardium is replaced by scar.","Under pressure or volume load, cardiomyocytes grow larger (hypertrophy) instead of dividing.","Hypertrophy reactivates fetal genes such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain.","SREBP senses cell sterol levels and turns up LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase genes when cholesterol is low.","Smooth muscle cells in the intima proliferate in response to growth factors such as PDGF in atherosclerosis."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Single-gene cardiovascular disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene and protein","Inheritance","Key cardiovascular features"],"rows":[["Familial hypercholesterolemia","LDLR (most), APOB, or PCSK9 gain of function","Autosomal dominant","Very high LDL, xanthomas, premature coronary disease"],["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Sarcomere genes, most often MYH7 or MYBPC3","Autosomal dominant","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy, outflow obstruction, sudden death"],["Marfan syndrome","FBN1, fibrillin-1","Autosomal dominant","Aortic root dilation and dissection, mitral valve prolapse"],["Loeys-Dietz syndrome","TGFBR1 or TGFBR2","Autosomal dominant","Aortic aneurysm and dissection, arterial tortuosity"],["Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","COL3A1, type III collagen","Autosomal dominant","Arterial rupture"],["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","DMD, dystrophin","X-linked recessive","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],["Friedreich ataxia","FXN, frataxin (GAA repeat)","Autosomal recessive","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],["Homocystinuria (classic)","CBS, cystathionine beta-synthase","Autosomal recessive","Arterial and venous thrombosis"]]},{"title":"Congenital long QT types","cols":["Type","Gene","Channel effect","Classic trigger"],"rows":[["LQT1","KCNQ1","Loss of slow delayed rectifier K⁺ current (IKs)","Exercise, especially swimming"],["LQT2","KCNH2 (hERG)","Loss of rapid delayed rectifier K⁺ current (IKr)","Sudden loud noise or emotional stress"],["LQT3","SCN5A","Gain of function: persistent late Na⁺ current","Rest or sleep"]]},{"title":"Chromosomal syndromes and their heart lesions","cols":["Syndrome","Genetic cause","Typical cardiovascular lesion"],"rows":[["Down syndrome","Trisomy 21","Atrioventricular septal defect (endocardial cushion defect)"],["Turner syndrome","45,X","Bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta"],["22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge)","Microdeletion at 22q11.2","Conotruncal defects: truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot"],["Williams syndrome","7q11.23 microdeletion including the elastin gene","Supravalvular aortic stenosis"],["Noonan syndrome","Often PTPN11","Pulmonary valve stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CATCH-22","text":"22q11.2 deletion: Cleft palate, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cardiac defects (conotruncal), Hypocalcemia from parathyroid aplasia, on chromosome 22."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Lens goes up in Marfan, down in homocystinuria","text":"Both can look tall and thin with a dislocated lens. Marfan lenses usually shift up and out; homocystinuria lenses shift down and in, and homocystinuria adds clots."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Receptor down, LDL up","text":"In familial hypercholesterolemia, picture the liver with half its LDL receptors. LDL piles up in plasma, and the cell, sensing too little cholesterol inside, keeps making more."}],"highYield":["Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant; homozygotes can have MI before age 20.","MYH7 and MYBPC3 are the most common HCM genes; inheritance is autosomal dominant.","Marfan syndrome is caused by FBN1 mutations; aortic dissection is the main cause of death.","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome: long QT plus sensorineural deafness, autosomal recessive.","Down syndrome is most linked to atrioventricular septal defects.","Turner syndrome is linked to bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation.","Williams syndrome causes supravalvular aortic stenosis through elastin gene loss.","Hypertrophied myocytes switch back on fetal genes such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain."],"pitfalls":["Marfan (FBN1) and Loeys-Dietz (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2) both cause aortic aneurysm; Loeys-Dietz adds bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes, and arterial tortuosity.","Long QT from KCNQ1 and KCNH2 is a loss of potassium current, while SCN5A long QT is a gain of sodium current; Brugada is a loss of SCN5A function.","Cardiomyocytes respond to load by hypertrophy, while vascular smooth muscle cells can still proliferate.","Gain-of-function PCSK9 raises LDL; loss-of-function PCSK9 lowers it, because PCSK9 sends LDL receptors to be degraded."],"dumps":[{"t":"Single-gene heart disorders","p":"Build a table on blank paper for familial hypercholesterolemia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, Marfan syndrome, congenital long QT, and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Give the gene, protein, inheritance, and main cardiovascular finding for each.","l":["Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDLR (or APOB, PCSK9), autosomal dominant, premature coronary disease","HCM: MYH7 or MYBPC3, autosomal dominant, asymmetric septal hypertrophy and sudden death","Marfan: FBN1, fibrillin-1, autosomal dominant, aortic root dilation and dissection","Long QT: KCNQ1, KCNH2, or SCN5A, mostly autosomal dominant, torsades de pointes","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos: COL3A1, type III collagen, autosomal dominant, arterial rupture","Notes variable expressivity or incomplete penetrance in at least one","Notes that FH homozygotes are much more severe than heterozygotes"]},{"t":"LDL receptor cycle","p":"Draw a liver cell taking up LDL through its receptor. Show what happens to the receptor and to cholesterol inside the cell. Then redraw it for a person with familial hypercholesterolemia.","l":["LDL apo B-100 binds the LDL receptor in clathrin-coated pits","Receptor and LDL are endocytosed and separate in the endosome","Receptor recycles to the cell surface","LDL is broken down in the lysosome, releasing free cholesterol","Free cholesterol lowers SREBP activity, reducing HMG-CoA reductase and LDL receptor synthesis","PCSK9 directs receptors to degradation","In FH, fewer working receptors means less LDL and IDL clearance and more cholesterol synthesis","Result: heterozygotes about two to three times normal cholesterol, homozygotes about five to six times"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiomyopathies"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy - signs and symptoms, causes, pathophysiology, treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lqn71jvMhQM"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtqrKxJmBn4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiomyopathy | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Miz9kvrrqk"}],"related":["CV-13","CV-18-E1","CV-18-G","CV-18-I2","CV-18-I4"]},"CV-15":{"id":"CV-15","summary":"The right heart pumps its whole output through the lungs, and the left heart receives it back, so the two organs work as one circuit. Exam items test how the pulmonary circulation differs from the systemic one, how breathing changes venous return, and how disease in one organ overloads the other.","notes":[{"h":"Two circuits in series","points":["Right and left ventricular outputs are equal over time (about 5 L/min at rest).","Pulmonary artery pressure is about 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15 mmHg); systemic is about 120/80 mmHg.","Pulmonary vascular resistance is much lower than systemic, roughly one tenth.","Pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mmHg, which keeps fluid out of the alveoli.","Bronchial arteries (systemic) feed the airways; part of their venous blood drains into pulmonary veins, a small normal shunt."]},{"h":"How pulmonary vessels are controlled","points":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: low alveolar O2 constricts nearby arterioles and sends blood to better ventilated alveoli.","Systemic arterioles do the opposite and dilate in hypoxia.","Chronic global hypoxia (COPD, high altitude) constricts vessels throughout the lung and causes pulmonary hypertension.","When cardiac output rises, closed capillaries open (recruitment) and open ones widen (distension), so resistance falls.","Resistance is lowest near FRC; at high lung volume alveolar vessels are stretched and compressed, at low volume extra-alveolar vessels narrow."]},{"h":"Gravity and the zones of the lung","points":["Upright, the base gets more blood flow and more ventilation than the apex; flow rises more steeply.","V/Q is about 3 at the apex and about 0.6 at the base; the whole-lung average is about 0.8.","Zone 1 (no flow) is normally absent; it appears with low arterial pressure (hemorrhage) or high alveolar pressure (positive pressure ventilation)."]},{"h":"Breathing changes the heart","points":["Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, raises venous return to the right heart, and raises right ventricular stroke volume.","At the same time pulmonary veins hold more blood, so left ventricular filling and systolic pressure dip slightly (under 10 mmHg).","A drop over 10 mmHg is pulsus paradoxus: cardiac tamponade, severe asthma, or COPD.","Inspiration delays pulmonic valve closure, which gives normal (physiologic) splitting of S2.","Positive pressure ventilation raises intrathoracic pressure, lowers venous return, and can lower cardiac output."]},{"h":"When one organ fails, the other is loaded","points":["Left heart failure or mitral stenosis raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary pressure and causes pulmonary edema.","Alveolar edema appears once pulmonary capillary pressure exceeds plasma colloid osmotic pressure (about 28 mmHg).","Chronic lung disease or hypoxia raises pulmonary artery pressure and causes right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale).","A large pulmonary embolus suddenly raises right ventricular afterload (acute cor pulmonale).","A right-to-left shunt sends venous blood past the lungs, so hypoxemia barely improves with 100% O2."]},{"h":"The lung as a metabolic organ","points":["ACE on pulmonary capillary endothelium converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","The same enzyme inactivates bradykinin.","The pulmonary capillary bed filters small clots and debris from venous blood before it reaches the systemic arteries."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circulation","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary circulation","Systemic circulation"],"rows":[["Arterial pressure","About 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15)","About 120/80 mmHg (mean about 93)"],["Resistance","Low","High (roughly 10 times pulmonary)"],["Artery wall","Thin, little smooth muscle, very compliant","Thick muscular media"],["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","About 7 mmHg","About 17 mmHg (average functional value)"],["Response to local hypoxia","Vasoconstriction (matches flow to ventilation)","Vasodilation (matches flow to tissue need)"],["Flow","Entire right ventricular output","Entire left ventricular output, shared among organs"]]},{"title":"Zones of the upright lung","cols":["Zone","Pressure order","Blood flow","Where"],"rows":[["Zone 1","Alveolar > arterial > venous","None (alveolar dead space)","Apex, only if arterial pressure falls or alveolar pressure rises"],["Zone 2","Arterial > alveolar > venous","Intermittent, set by arterial minus alveolar pressure","Upper and middle lung"],["Zone 3","Arterial > venous > alveolar","Continuous, set by arterial minus venous pressure","Lower lung (base)"]]},{"title":"Dead space vs shunt","cols":["Feature","Dead space","Shunt"],"rows":[["V/Q","High (ventilation with no perfusion)","Zero (perfusion with no ventilation)"],["Examples","Pulmonary embolism, zone 1","Atelectasis, lobar pneumonia, pulmonary edema, right-to-left cardiac shunt"],["Effect of 100% O2","Hypoxemia usually improves","Hypoxemia improves very little"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Hypoxia flips in the lung","text":"Everywhere else, low O2 dilates arterioles to bring in more blood. In the lung, low O2 constricts them so blood goes where the air is."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Alveolar pressure walks down the line","text":"Write arterial, venous, alveolar. Zone 1 puts alveolar first, zone 2 puts it second, zone 3 puts it last."},{"kind":"trick","label":"The 100% O2 test","text":"If extra oxygen fixes the hypoxemia, think V/Q mismatch or diffusion. If it barely helps, think shunt."}],"highYield":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is the main local control of pulmonary blood flow.","Chronic hypoxic lung disease leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","V/Q is highest at the apex and lowest at the base.","Inspiration raises right heart filling and splits S2.","Pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg points to cardiac tamponade or severe obstructive lung disease.","Pulmonary ACE makes angiotensin II and breaks down bradykinin.","A right-to-left shunt causes hypoxemia that 100% O2 barely corrects."],"pitfalls":["Cor pulmonale means right heart disease caused by lung disease or pulmonary vessels; right heart failure from left heart failure does not count.","Pulmonary embolism is a dead-space (high V/Q) problem in the blocked area, yet hypoxemia comes from low V/Q in the overperfused areas.","Both blood flow and ventilation are greater at the base; V/Q is lower there because flow increases more than ventilation."],"dumps":[{"t":"Lung zones","p":"Draw an upright lung. Mark zones 1, 2, and 3, write the pressure order for each, and label where V/Q is highest and lowest.","l":["Zone 1: alveolar > arterial > venous, no flow","Zone 2: arterial > alveolar > venous, flow set by arterial minus alveolar pressure","Zone 3: arterial > venous > alveolar, continuous flow","Zone 1 normally absent; appears with hemorrhage or positive pressure ventilation","V/Q about 3 at apex","V/Q about 0.6 at base","Flow and ventilation both rise toward the base, flow more steeply"]},{"t":"From a sick lung to a failing right heart","p":"Trace the steps from chronic COPD to right heart failure. Then list the signs you would find on exam.","l":["Alveolar hypoxia","Widespread hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Vessel remodeling (medial hypertrophy) raises pulmonary vascular resistance","Pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular afterload","Right ventricular hypertrophy, then dilation and failure (cor pulmonale)","Raised jugular venous pressure","Hepatomegaly and peripheral pitting edema","Loud P2 and right ventricular heave"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane (section on ventilation-perfusion ratio)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Respiratory | External Respiration: Ventilation Perfusion Coupling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yUtngVm93-c"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Respiratory System Physiology - Ventilation and Perfusion (V:Q Ratio) Physiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-mL_NQ3pKnA"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction | Circulatory system physiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJ1gu_WRx5o"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-18-A1","CV-18-B2","CV-18-I7"]},"CV-16":{"id":"CV-16","summary":"Many heart and vessel diseases are driven by the immune system: atherosclerosis is chronic inflammation, rheumatic fever is molecular mimicry, and most vasculitis is antibody, immune complex, or T-cell injury. The exam asks you to match each disease to its hypersensitivity type and immune cell.","notes":[{"h":"Atherosclerosis is chronic inflammation","points":["Injured endothelium expresses VCAM-1 and recruits monocytes and T cells into the intima.","LDL trapped in the intima is oxidized; macrophages take it up through scavenger receptors and become foam cells.","Scavenger receptors are not shut off by cell cholesterol, so foam cells keep filling.","Cholesterol crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, which releases IL-1 beta.","Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages; PDGF draws smooth muscle cells in to build the fibrous cap.","Macrophage metalloproteinases thin the cap and make the plaque prone to rupture.","C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) is a liver-made marker of this inflammation and predicts cardiac risk."]},{"h":"Rheumatic fever: molecular mimicry","points":["Follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 3 weeks.","Antibodies and T cells against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with heart antigens such as cardiac myosin.","Review books classify it as type II hypersensitivity; CD4 T cells also drive the valve damage.","Acute lesion: Aschoff bodies with Anitschkow (caterpillar) cells; pancarditis.","ASO and anti-DNase B titers show the recent strep infection."]},{"h":"Vasculitis by immune mechanism","points":["Immune complex (type III): polyarteritis nodosa (often hepatitis B), IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis (hepatitis C), lupus vasculitis.","ANCA-associated (few immune deposits): granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3, c-ANCA), microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (MPO, p-ANCA).","ANCA bind neutrophil enzymes on primed neutrophils and trigger degranulation that injures the vessel wall.","Anti-GBM disease is type II: antibodies to type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes.","T-cell granulomatous arteritis (type IV): giant cell arteritis and Takayasu arteritis."]},{"h":"Heart transplant rejection","points":["Hyperacute: preformed antibodies (ABO or HLA) bind graft endothelium within minutes, causing thrombosis.","Acute cellular: CD8 and CD4 T cells attack the myocardium, weeks to months after transplant.","Acute antibody-mediated: antibodies to donor HLA injure graft vessels.","Chronic: cardiac allograft vasculopathy, a diffuse concentric intimal thickening of coronary arteries."]},{"h":"Other immune heart disease","points":["Viral myocarditis: direct viral injury, then a T-cell response that can lead to dilated cardiomyopathy.","Hypersensitivity myocarditis: drug reaction with eosinophils in the myocardium.","Dressler syndrome: autoimmune pericarditis weeks after MI, against myocardial antigens released by necrosis.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis in lupus: sterile vegetations on both sides of the valve, usually mitral.","Maternal anti-Ro (SSA) antibodies cross the placenta and cause congenital heart block.","Rare myocarditis after mRNA COVID-19 vaccines, mostly in young males within days of a dose."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypersensitivity types in cardiovascular disease","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Cardiovascular examples"],"rows":[["I (immediate)","IgE on mast cells; histamine causes vasodilation and capillary leak","Anaphylactic shock"],["II (antibody to fixed antigen)","IgG or IgM binds cell or matrix antigens; complement and phagocytes injure tissue","Acute rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection, anti-GBM disease"],["III (immune complex)","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls, fix complement, draw neutrophils","Polyarteritis nodosa, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, serum sickness, lupus vasculitis"],["IV (T-cell mediated)","CD4 Th1 cells activate macrophages; CD8 cells kill target cells","Acute cellular rejection, giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis"]]},{"title":"Heart transplant rejection","cols":["Type","Timing","Main mechanism","Finding in the graft"],"rows":[["Hyperacute","Minutes to hours","Preformed anti-ABO or anti-HLA antibodies, complement","Graft thrombosis and ischemic necrosis"],["Acute cellular","Weeks to months","Donor-reactive CD8 and CD4 T cells","Lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte damage"],["Acute antibody-mediated","Weeks to months","New antibodies to donor HLA","Capillary injury with complement (C4d) deposits"],["Chronic","Months to years","T cells, antibodies, and cytokines acting on vessel walls","Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (concentric intimal thickening)"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ACID","text":"Hypersensitivity types I to IV: Anaphylactic and Atopic (I), Cytotoxic antibody-mediated (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed cell-mediated (IV)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Throat vs skin","text":"Rheumatic fever follows strep throat only. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis can follow throat or skin infection."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Rejection clock","text":"Minutes means preformed antibody, weeks means T cells, years means vessel narrowing."}],"highYield":["Rheumatic fever: M protein antibodies cross-react with cardiac myosin (molecular mimicry).","Aschoff bodies are the hallmark of acute rheumatic carditis.","Foam cells form through scavenger receptor uptake of oxidized LDL.","Polyarteritis nodosa is linked to hepatitis B and spares the lungs.","c-ANCA (PR3) points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) to microscopic polyangiitis or EGPA.","Chronic heart transplant rejection appears as accelerated coronary artery narrowing.","Maternal anti-Ro antibodies cause neonatal complete heart block."],"pitfalls":["Rheumatic fever vegetations are small and sterile along the closure line; infective endocarditis vegetations are large, bulky, and contain organisms.","Dressler syndrome comes weeks after MI and is autoimmune; fibrinous pericarditis in the first few days comes from the necrosis itself.","ANCA vasculitis is called pauci-immune because few immune deposits are seen, which separates it from immune complex vasculitis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypersensitivity table","p":"On blank paper, build a four-row table for hypersensitivity types I to IV. For each, write the immune effector and two cardiovascular examples.","l":["Type I: IgE and mast cells; anaphylactic shock","Type II: IgG or IgM against fixed antigens","Type II examples: rheumatic fever, hyperacute rejection, anti-GBM","Type III: immune complexes deposit in vessel walls","Type III examples: polyarteritis nodosa, IgA vasculitis, serum sickness","Type IV: T cells and macrophages","Type IV examples: acute cellular rejection, giant cell arteritis"]},{"t":"Plaque as inflammation","p":"Draw a coronary artery wall and show how a plaque forms. Label each immune cell and molecule in the order it acts.","l":["Endothelial injury and VCAM-1 expression","Monocytes enter the intima and become macrophages","LDL is oxidized in the intima","Scavenger receptor uptake makes foam cells","IL-1 and the NLRP3 inflammasome","Th1 cells and IFN-gamma","PDGF draws smooth muscle cells to form the fibrous cap","Metalloproteinases thin the cap and promote rupture"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity: Disorders Caused by Immune Responses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on transplant rejection"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (sections on atherosclerosis and vasculitis)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type II (Cytotoxic reaction) - causes, pathophysiology, complement system","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Em0Wi07dj4E"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Hypersensitivity Type III (Immune complex hypersensitivity) - causes,  pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NKnAXcM5Ly0"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Vasculitis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xHRwhK835QE"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Rheumatic Fever & Heart Disease | Pathology | Dr Najeeb🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ivIE8ARRIgM"}],"related":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-I3","CV-18-I12","CV-18-E2","CV-17"]},"CV-17":{"id":"CV-17","summary":"This unit covers the organisms behind the listed cardiovascular infections: the bacteria of infective endocarditis, Trypanosoma cruzi, Borrelia burgdorferi, Rickettsia rickettsii, the hemorrhagic fever viruses, and the myocarditis viruses. Questions pair each organism with its shape, vector, life cycle, and the virulence factor that explains the disease.","notes":[{"h":"Infective endocarditis bacteria","points":["Staphylococcus aureus: gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase and coagulase positive; acute endocarditis on normal valves and in IV drug use.","S. aureus virulence: protein A binds the Fc end of IgG; clumping factor and fibronectin-binding proteins help it stick to valves.","Viridans streptococci: alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant; subacute endocarditis on damaged valves after dental work.","Viridans strep make dextran, which binds fibrin-platelet clumps on damaged valves.","Enterococcus faecalis: grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile; endocarditis after GI or GU procedures.","Staphylococcus epidermidis: coagulase negative, makes biofilm on prosthetic valves and devices.","Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) endocarditis is linked to colon cancer."]},{"h":"Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas disease)","points":["Flagellated protozoan; spread by triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs in Latin America.","The bug defecates while feeding; trypomastigotes in the feces enter the bite wound or the conjunctiva (Romana sign).","Also spread by blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother to fetus, and contaminated food.","Trypomastigotes circulate in blood and do not divide; amastigotes multiply inside heart muscle, smooth muscle, and macrophages.","Chronic disease years later: dilated cardiomyopathy with apical aneurysm and conduction block, megaesophagus, and megacolon."]},{"h":"Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease)","points":["Spirochete; stains poorly with Gram stain, seen with Giemsa or Wright stain.","Vector: Ixodes ticks (black-legged deer tick); white-footed mouse is the main reservoir; deer feed the adult ticks.","Transmission usually needs about 36 to 48 hours of tick attachment.","Switches outer surface proteins: OspA in the tick gut, OspC when it moves to the host.","Antigenic variation of the VlsE surface protein helps it avoid antibodies.","Unusual genome: a linear chromosome plus many linear and circular plasmids.","Lyme carditis (early disseminated stage) causes AV block of varying degree."]},{"h":"Rickettsia rickettsii (Rocky Mountain spotted fever)","points":["Small gram-negative obligate intracellular bacterium; cannot make enough of its own CoA and NAD+.","Vector: Dermacentor ticks (American dog tick, Rocky Mountain wood tick); most US cases are in the southeast and south-central states.","Infects vascular endothelial cells and spreads cell to cell using host actin.","Endothelial infection causes vasculitis, leaky vessels, edema, low blood pressure, and thrombocytopenia.","Rash starts on wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk, and involves palms and soles."]},{"h":"Viral hemorrhagic fever viruses","points":["Yellow fever and dengue: flaviviruses (enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA), spread by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes.","Yellow fever damages hepatocytes (Councilman bodies) and causes jaundice and bleeding; a live attenuated vaccine prevents it.","Dengue has four serotypes; a second infection with a different serotype raises the risk of severe dengue through antibody-dependent enhancement.","Ebola and Marburg: filoviruses (enveloped, filamentous, negative-sense single-stranded RNA); fruit bats are the likely reservoir; people spread it through body fluids.","Filoviruses infect macrophages, dendritic cells, and endothelium; Ebola VP35 blocks type I interferon.","Common end path: endothelial leak, cytokine storm, and consumptive coagulopathy (DIC)."]},{"h":"Myocarditis viruses","points":["Coxsackie B virus (enterovirus, picornavirus family) is the classic cause; spread mainly fecal-oral.","Coxsackie B enters cardiomyocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR); its protease 2A cleaves dystrophin.","Adenovirus uses the same CAR receptor and is common in children.","Parvovirus B19 and HHV-6 are now frequently found in heart biopsies; parvovirus infects endothelial cells."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infective endocarditis organisms","cols":["Organism","Morphology and lab clues","Typical setting","Key virulence factor or clue"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; catalase and coagulase positive","Acute endocarditis, normal valves, IV drug use (tricuspid)","Protein A, clumping factor, fibronectin-binding proteins"],["Viridans streptococci","Gram-positive cocci in chains; alpha-hemolytic; optochin resistant","Subacute endocarditis on damaged valves after dental work","Dextran binds fibrin-platelet clumps"],["Enterococcus faecalis","Gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains; grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile","After GI or GU procedures; older adults","Intrinsic resistance to many antibiotics"],["Staphylococcus epidermidis","Gram-positive cocci in clusters; coagulase negative","Prosthetic valves and devices","Biofilm"],["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Gram-positive cocci; group D streptococcus; bile esculin positive","Older adults with colorectal neoplasia","Look for a colon tumor"],["HACEK group","Fastidious gram-negative rods","Culture-negative endocarditis","Slow growth delays diagnosis"]]},{"title":"Vector-borne and other systemic cardiovascular pathogens","cols":["Organism","Type and morphology","Vector or route","Life cycle or spread in the body","Cardiovascular effect"],"rows":[["Trypanosoma cruzi","Flagellated protozoan","Triatomine bug feces; transfusion; congenital","Trypomastigote in blood; amastigote divides inside cells","Dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block"],["Borrelia burgdorferi","Spirochete","Ixodes tick","Spreads from bite site through skin and blood","AV block (Lyme carditis)"],["Rickettsia rickettsii","Gram-negative obligate intracellular rod","Dermacentor tick","Infects endothelium; spreads cell to cell by actin","Vasculitis, capillary leak, hypotension"],["Yellow fever virus","Flavivirus, enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA","Aedes aegypti mosquito","Replicates in liver and lymphoid tissue","Hemorrhage, shock"],["Dengue virus","Flavivirus, enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA (4 serotypes)","Aedes mosquitoes","Infects monocytes and dendritic cells","Plasma leakage, shock, bleeding"],["Ebola and Marburg viruses","Filovirus, enveloped, filamentous, negative-sense ssRNA","Bats to humans; body fluids person to person","Infects macrophages, dendritic cells, endothelium","Capillary leak, DIC, shock"]]},{"title":"Common myocarditis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Transmission","Notes"],"rows":[["Coxsackie B","Picornavirus (enterovirus); naked, positive-sense ssRNA","Fecal-oral, respiratory","Uses CAR; protease 2A cleaves dystrophin; also causes pericarditis"],["Adenovirus","Adenovirus; naked, double-stranded DNA","Respiratory droplets, fecal-oral","Uses CAR; common cause in children"],["Parvovirus B19","Parvovirus; naked, single-stranded DNA","Respiratory, blood, mother to fetus","Infects endothelial cells and red cell precursors"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"HACEK","text":"Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter (formerly Actinobacillus), Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella: fastidious gram-negative causes of culture-negative endocarditis."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Match the procedure to the bug","text":"Dental work brings viridans strep. GI or GU procedures bring Enterococcus. A new prosthetic valve brings S. epidermidis. IV drug use brings S. aureus to the tricuspid valve."},{"kind":"trick","label":"RMSF rash travels inward","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever starts at the wrists and ankles and moves to the trunk. Typhus rash does the reverse, starting on the trunk."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of acute endocarditis and of endocarditis in IV drug users.","Viridans strep dextran lets it stick to fibrin-platelet clumps on damaged valves.","S. gallolyticus endocarditis calls for a colonoscopy to look for colon cancer.","Chagas disease: triatomine bug, Latin America, dilated cardiomyopathy plus megaesophagus and megacolon.","Lyme carditis presents as AV block.","R. rickettsii infects endothelium and causes a vasculitis with a rash on palms and soles.","Dengue: secondary infection with a different serotype causes severe disease through antibody-dependent enhancement.","Coxsackie B is the classic cause of viral myocarditis and pericarditis."],"pitfalls":["Viridans strep and S. pneumoniae are both alpha-hemolytic; S. pneumoniae is optochin sensitive and bile soluble, viridans strep is neither.","Ixodes ticks carry Borrelia; Dermacentor ticks carry Rickettsia rickettsii.","Despite the name, most RMSF cases in the US occur in the southeast and south-central states.","Flaviviruses (yellow fever, dengue) are positive-sense RNA; filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg) are negative-sense RNA."],"dumps":[{"t":"Chagas life cycle","p":"Draw the life cycle of Trypanosoma cruzi between the triatomine bug and a human. Label each form and where it lives.","l":["Triatomine bug takes a blood meal","Bug feces contain metacyclic trypomastigotes","Trypomastigotes enter through the bite wound or conjunctiva","Inside host cells they become amastigotes and divide","Cells burst and release trypomastigotes into the blood","A bug takes up trypomastigotes in a blood meal","In the bug gut they become epimastigotes and multiply","Target tissues: heart muscle, GI smooth muscle and nerve plexus"]},{"t":"Endocarditis organism table","p":"Build a table on blank paper for S. aureus, viridans strep, Enterococcus, and S. epidermidis. Include the lab clues, the typical patient, and one virulence factor for each.","l":["S. aureus: clusters, coagulase positive","S. aureus: acute, normal valves, IV drug use","S. aureus: protein A or coagulase","Viridans strep: alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant","Viridans strep: damaged valves, dental work, dextran","Enterococcus: grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile; GI or GU procedures","S. epidermidis: coagulase negative; prosthetic valve; biofilm"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Rickettsia and Related Genera"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology (section on Trypanosoma cruzi)"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaO8110iSzI"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Staphylococcus: Aureus, Epidermidis, Saprophyticus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6BkqWKOG8E0"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Cardiac Pathology | Cardiology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lyiLcpxa2FU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is myocarditis and pericarditis? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xL98lBuAn1A"}],"related":["CV-18-K","CV-18-D3","CV-18-E2","CV-16"]},"CV-18-A":{"id":"CV-18-A","summary":"Systemic hypertension loads the left ventricle and damages arterioles in the brain, kidney, retina, and heart. Pulmonary hypertension loads the right ventricle and ends in right heart failure. Know the definitions, the main causes, the vessel changes, and the organ damage for each.","notes":[{"h":"Systemic hypertension: definition and causes","points":["ACC/AHA 2017: stage 1 is 130 to 139 or 80 to 89 mmHg; stage 2 is 140/90 mmHg or higher.","Primary (essential) hypertension is about 90 to 95% of cases; it is polygenic with environmental triggers.","Risk factors: older age, family history, obesity, high sodium intake, excess alcohol, inactivity, diabetes.","Secondary causes: renal artery stenosis, kidney disease, primary aldosteronism, pheochromocytoma, Cushing syndrome, coarctation, sleep apnea.","Renal artery stenosis: atherosclerosis in older adults, fibromuscular dysplasia in younger women."]},{"h":"Systemic hypertension: vessel and heart changes","points":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: pink glassy arteriole walls from plasma protein leak; seen in chronic hypertension and diabetes.","Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis: onion-skin wall layers, often with fibrinoid necrosis, in severe (malignant) hypertension.","Pressure overload causes concentric left ventricular hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in parallel).","The thick, stiff ventricle fills poorly (diastolic dysfunction), enlarging the left atrium and raising the risk of atrial fibrillation.","Hypertensive emergency: severe pressure (often 180/120 mmHg or higher) with acute organ damage."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension: definition and groups","points":["Mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest by right heart catheterization (older cutoff 25 mmHg).","Left heart disease (group 2) is the most common cause.","Pulmonary arterial hypertension (group 1) includes idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drug-related, connective tissue disease, HIV, and portal hypertension.","BMPR2 loss-of-function mutations allow smooth muscle proliferation in small pulmonary arteries.","Endothelial imbalance: low NO and prostacyclin, high endothelin-1."]},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension: pathology and findings","points":["Medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis of small arteries; plexiform lesions in severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Atherosclerosis can appear in the large pulmonary arteries.","Symptoms: exertional dyspnea, fatigue, chest pain, syncope.","Signs: loud P2, right ventricular heave, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, raised jugular venous pressure, edema.","ECG: right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Systemic: stroke (including intracerebral hemorrhage from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms), coronary disease and MI, heart failure.","Systemic: aortic dissection, abdominal aortic aneurysm, chronic kidney disease (nephrosclerosis), retinopathy.","Pulmonary: right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale), arrhythmias, sudden death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hypertensive heart diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Definition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Pulmonary hypertension","Mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest","Left heart disease, chronic lung disease and hypoxia, chronic thromboembolism, BMPR2 mutation, drugs, connective tissue disease","Vasoconstriction and remodeling of small pulmonary arteries raise resistance and right ventricular afterload","Exertional dyspnea, syncope, loud P2, right ventricular heave, raised JVP, edema","Cor pulmonale, right heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death"],["Systemic hypertension","130/80 mmHg or higher (stage 1); 140/90 mmHg or higher (stage 2)","Primary in about 90 to 95%; secondary from kidney, adrenal, or vascular disease","Raised cardiac output or peripheral resistance with sodium retention and RAAS activity","Usually silent; S4, concentric LVH, retinal arteriolar narrowing and AV nicking","Stroke, MI, heart failure, aortic dissection, kidney disease, retinopathy"]]},{"title":"Pulmonary hypertension groups (WHO)","cols":["Group","Cause","Examples"],"rows":[["1","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drugs and toxins, systemic sclerosis, HIV, portal hypertension, congenital shunts"],["2","Left heart disease (most common)","Left ventricular failure, mitral or aortic valve disease"],["3","Lung disease or hypoxia","COPD, interstitial lung disease, sleep apnea, high altitude"],["4","Pulmonary artery obstruction","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension"],["5","Unclear or multiple mechanisms","Sarcoidosis, myeloproliferative disorders"]]},{"title":"Arteriolosclerosis in hypertension","cols":["Type","Look","Setting"],"rows":[["Hyaline","Homogeneous pink thickened wall, narrow lumen","Long-standing mild hypertension, diabetes, aging"],["Hyperplastic","Concentric onion-skin layers, often fibrinoid necrosis","Severe (malignant) hypertension"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Concentric means pressure","text":"Pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) thickens the wall inward. Volume overload (regurgitation) stretches the chamber outward, which is eccentric hypertrophy."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Order of PH groups","text":"List the groups in order: 1 disease of the pulmonary arteries themselves, 2 left heart disease, 3 lung disease or hypoxia, 4 clots blocking the arteries, 5 everything else. Group 2 is the most common."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Onion skin means emergency","text":"Hyaline arterioles mean long, mild pressure. Onion-skin arterioles with fibrinoid necrosis mean malignant hypertension."}],"highYield":["Essential hypertension makes up about 90 to 95% of cases.","Primary aldosteronism is the most common endocrine cause of secondary hypertension: high blood pressure, high aldosterone, low renin, and sometimes low potassium.","Hypertension is the main risk factor for aortic dissection and intracerebral hemorrhage.","Concentric LVH is the heart's response to chronic pressure overload.","BMPR2 mutations cause heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Plexiform lesions mark severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","The most common cause of pulmonary hypertension is left heart disease."],"pitfalls":["Cor pulmonale is right heart disease from lung or pulmonary vascular disease; pulmonary hypertension from left heart failure is group 2.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis appears in both hypertension and diabetes; onion-skin change points to malignant hypertension.","Fibromuscular dysplasia (young women, string of beads) and atherosclerosis (older adults, ostial narrowing) both cause renal artery stenosis."],"dumps":[{"t":"Hypertension end-organ map","p":"Draw a body outline. Mark every organ that long-term systemic hypertension damages, and write the lesion found in each.","l":["Brain: ischemic stroke and intracerebral hemorrhage (Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms)","Eye: retinopathy with AV nicking, hemorrhages, cotton wool spots; papilledema if malignant","Heart: concentric LVH, diastolic dysfunction, coronary disease","Aorta: dissection and aneurysm","Kidney: hyaline arteriolosclerosis and nephrosclerosis","Malignant hypertension: hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis","Peripheral arteries: atherosclerosis and PAD"]},{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension pathway","p":"Trace how pulmonary arterial hypertension leads to right heart failure, starting with a BMPR2 mutation.","l":["BMPR2 loss of function","Endothelial imbalance: less NO and prostacyclin, more endothelin-1","Smooth muscle proliferation and medial hypertrophy","Intimal fibrosis and plexiform lesions narrow the lumen","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises","Right ventricular hypertrophy (pressure overload)","Right ventricular dilation and failure","Raised JVP, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on hypertensive heart disease, systemic and pulmonary)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (section on hypertensive vascular disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the Kidneys in Long-Term Control of Arterial Pressure and in Hypertension: The Integrated System for Arterial Pressure Regulation"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Hypertension | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sNCtvsY3TS8"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pulmonary Artery Hypertension | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S8ehpYrnX2w"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is hypertension? | Circulatory system diseases | Health & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FBoYigdnLKQ"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Pulmonary hypertension symptoms and causes | Respiratory system diseases | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZVl1M3D2B80"}],"related":["CV-15","CV-11","CV-18-B","CV-18-I2"]},"CV-18-B":{"id":"CV-18-B","summary":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood for the body's needs, or can only do so at high filling pressures. Left-sided failure backs blood up into the lungs; right-sided failure backs it up into the body's veins. Know the causes, the compensations that turn harmful, and the classic findings on each side.","notes":[{"h":"Left-sided heart failure","points":["Main causes: ischemic heart disease, hypertension, aortic and mitral valve disease, cardiomyopathy.","Blood backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries and causes pulmonary congestion and edema.","Symptoms: dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea.","Signs: basal crackles, S3, displaced apex beat, pleural effusions.","Lungs show hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) from leaked red cells.","Low forward output reduces kidney perfusion and activates RAAS (prerenal azotemia); low brain perfusion causes confusion."]},{"h":"Right-sided heart failure","points":["Most common cause is left-sided heart failure.","Isolated causes: cor pulmonale (lung disease, pulmonary embolism, pulmonary hypertension), right ventricular infarct, tricuspid or pulmonic valve disease.","Blood backs up into the systemic veins: raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, dependent pitting edema.","Liver: congestive hepatomegaly with nutmeg pattern (centrilobular congestion and necrosis); long-standing cases develop cardiac cirrhosis.","Also ascites, splenomegaly, and pleural and pericardial effusions."]},{"h":"Ejection fraction types","points":["HFrEF: ejection fraction 40% or lower; the ventricle contracts poorly (systolic failure).","HFpEF: ejection fraction 50% or higher; the stiff ventricle fills poorly (diastolic failure).","Ejection fraction 41 to 49% is called mildly reduced."]},{"h":"Compensation that turns harmful","points":["Frank-Starling: more filling stretches fibers and raises stroke volume until the curve flattens.","Sympathetic activation raises heart rate and contractility but increases O2 demand and arrhythmia risk.","RAAS and ADH retain salt and water, which raises preload and causes edema.","Angiotensin II and aldosterone promote fibrosis and remodeling.","Natriuretic peptides (ANP from atria, BNP from ventricles) are released with stretch and partly oppose RAAS; BNP is a diagnostic marker."]},{"h":"Complications","points":["Acute pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock.","Arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia) and sudden death.","Mural thrombi in dilated chambers that can embolize.","Cardiorenal syndrome and cardiac cachexia."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Left vs right heart failure","cols":["Condition","Common causes","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Left-sided","Ischemic heart disease, hypertension, aortic or mitral valve disease, cardiomyopathy","Low forward output with back-pressure into pulmonary veins and capillaries","Dyspnea, orthopnea, PND, crackles, S3, pleural effusion, heart failure cells in lung","Pulmonary edema, cardiogenic shock, arrhythmia, mural thrombus, right heart failure"],["Right-sided","Left heart failure (most common), cor pulmonale, right ventricular infarct, tricuspid or pulmonic valve disease","Back-pressure into systemic veins","Raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, pitting edema, nutmeg liver, ascites; lungs often clear in pure right failure","Cardiac cirrhosis, ascites, renal congestion, low output"]]},{"title":"HFrEF vs HFpEF","cols":["Feature","HFrEF (systolic)","HFpEF (diastolic)"],"rows":[["Ejection fraction","40% or lower","50% or higher"],["Core problem","Weak contraction","Stiff ventricle, poor relaxation and filling"],["Usual causes","MI, dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertension, older age, obesity, diabetes, hypertrophic or restrictive cardiomyopathy"],["Ventricle shape","Dilated (eccentric hypertrophy)","Thick walled (concentric hypertrophy)"],["Extra heart sound","S3","S4"]]},{"title":"Compensatory mechanisms","cols":["Mechanism","Short-term help","Long-term harm"],"rows":[["Frank-Starling","More stroke volume from more filling","Fails once the curve flattens; congestion"],["Sympathetic activation","Faster rate, stronger contraction, vasoconstriction","Higher O2 demand, arrhythmia, myocyte death"],["RAAS and ADH","Restore volume and blood pressure","Edema, higher afterload, fibrosis"],["Hypertrophy and remodeling","Spreads wall stress over more muscle","Stiffness, ischemia, dilation"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Left goes to the lungs","text":"Left-sided failure backs up into the lungs. Right-sided failure backs up into the rest of the body (neck veins, liver, legs)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"S3 floods, S4 stiffens","text":"S3 comes from blood rushing into an overfilled, dilated ventricle. S4 comes from the atrium kicking against a stiff, thick ventricle."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why orthopnea","text":"Lying flat returns fluid from the legs to the chest, and a failing left ventricle cannot handle the extra volume, so the lungs congest."}],"highYield":["Left heart failure is the most common cause of right heart failure.","Heart failure cells are hemosiderin-laden macrophages in the alveoli.","Nutmeg liver shows centrilobular congestion from right heart failure.","BNP is released by stretched ventricular myocytes and is used to diagnose heart failure.","Ischemic heart disease is the most common cause of left-sided heart failure.","HFpEF is linked with hypertension and concentric hypertrophy."],"pitfalls":["Heart failure cells are found in the lungs, not in the heart.","Edema in right heart failure is pitting and dependent; lymphedema and myxedema are nonpitting.","A normal ejection fraction does not rule out heart failure; HFpEF has a normal or high ejection fraction."],"dumps":[{"t":"Two sides of failure","p":"Draw the heart and both circulations. Show where blood backs up in left-sided and in right-sided failure, and label the organ finding at each spot.","l":["Left ventricle fails and left atrial pressure rises","Pulmonary venous and capillary congestion","Pulmonary edema and heart failure cells","Pulmonary hypertension then loads the right ventricle","Right ventricle fails and systemic venous pressure rises","Raised JVP and hepatojugular reflux","Nutmeg liver and ascites","Dependent pitting edema"]},{"t":"Compensation loop","p":"Trace what happens after cardiac output falls in heart failure. Show each compensatory system and how it worsens the failure over time.","l":["Low cardiac output lowers blood pressure and kidney perfusion","Baroreceptors activate sympathetic nerves","Renin release activates angiotensin II and aldosterone","ADH release adds water retention","Salt and water retention raise preload and cause edema","Vasoconstriction raises afterload","Angiotensin II and aldosterone cause fibrosis and remodeling","BNP rises with stretch and partly opposes RAAS"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on heart failure)"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Congestive Heart Failure | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gsu8NT1yYes"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Heart failure with reduced and preserved ejection fraction, pathophysiology and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ItKGeEbzAis"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Heart Failure | CHF | Congestive Heart Failure | Pathophysiology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi39GGJA16E"}],"related":["CV-6","CV-15","CV-18-A","CV-18-C","CV-18-E1"]},"CV-18-C":{"id":"CV-18-C","summary":"Ischemic heart disease happens when coronary blood flow cannot meet myocardial O2 demand, nearly always because of coronary atherosclerosis. It ranges from angina to infarction to slow ischemic heart failure. Expect questions on plaque events, the histologic timeline of an infarct, and which complication appears on which day.","notes":[{"h":"Causes and risk factors","points":["Coronary atherosclerosis causes over 90% of cases.","Risk factors: age, male sex, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, high LDL, low HDL, family history, obesity, inactivity.","Other causes: coronary vasospasm, emboli, vasculitis, and high demand states (tachycardia, LVH, severe aortic stenosis).","The subendocardium is most vulnerable because it is last on the coronary supply line and squeezed hardest in systole."]},{"h":"Angina pectoris","points":["Stable angina: fixed narrowing (about 70% or more) causes chest pain with exertion that eases with rest or nitroglycerin.","Stable angina shows ST depression during pain (subendocardial ischemia).","Unstable angina: plaque disruption with a partial clot causes pain at rest or worsening pain, without troponin rise.","Prinzmetal (variant) angina: coronary vasospasm at rest with transient ST elevation; triggers include smoking and cocaine."]},{"h":"Myocardial infarction","points":["Usual sequence: plaque rupture, platelet adhesion, and a thrombus that blocks the artery.","Irreversible myocyte injury begins after about 20 to 40 minutes of severe ischemia.","Transmural infarct (often STEMI) spans the full wall; subendocardial infarct (often NSTEMI) is limited to the inner third to half.","Artery involved: LAD about 40 to 50%, RCA about 30 to 40%, left circumflex about 15 to 20%.","ECG leads: LAD in V1 to V4, left circumflex in I, aVL, V5, V6, RCA in II, III, aVF.","Troponin rises within about 3 to 4 hours and stays elevated about 7 to 10 days; CK-MB returns to normal in about 2 to 3 days, so it helps detect reinfarction.","Reperfusion injury: contraction band necrosis and free radical damage when flow returns."]},{"h":"Chronic ischemic heart disease","points":["Progressive heart failure from ischemic myocardial damage, also called ischemic cardiomyopathy.","Usually follows one or more healed infarcts; scars replace muscle and the remaining muscle hypertrophies.","The left ventricle dilates; diffuse subendocardial myocyte vacuolization and fibrosis are seen.","Complications: progressive heart failure, arrhythmias, sudden cardiac death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Ischemic heart disease compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Angina pectoris","Coronary atherosclerosis; vasospasm in variant angina","Transient ischemia without myocyte death","Pressure-like chest pain lasting minutes; ST depression (stable) or transient ST elevation (variant)","Progression to unstable angina or MI"],["Chronic ischemic heart disease","Prior MIs and long-standing coronary disease","Loss of muscle with scarring, hypertrophy of the rest, and chamber dilation","Slowly worsening heart failure, enlarged dilated left ventricle","Heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden cardiac death"],["Myocardial infarction (MI)","Plaque rupture with thrombus; same risk factors as atherosclerosis","Prolonged ischemia causes coagulative necrosis","Crushing chest pain over 20 minutes, diaphoresis, ST elevation or depression, rising troponin","Arrhythmia, shock, rupture, aneurysm, mural thrombus, pericarditis"]]},{"title":"Types of angina","cols":["Type","Mechanism","When pain occurs","ECG during pain","Troponin"],"rows":[["Stable","Fixed stenosis about 70% or more","Exertion; eases with rest","ST depression","Normal"],["Unstable","Plaque disruption with nonocclusive thrombus","At rest or with less effort; worsening","ST depression or T wave inversion","Normal"],["Prinzmetal (variant)","Coronary vasospasm","At rest, often early morning","Transient ST elevation","Normal"]]},{"title":"Timeline of an infarct","cols":["Time","Gross","Microscopic","Main risk"],"rows":[["0 to 4 hours","No change","Wavy fibers at the border may appear","Ventricular arrhythmia"],["4 to 24 hours","Dark mottling","Early coagulative necrosis, edema, contraction bands if reperfused","Arrhythmia, heart failure, shock"],["1 to 3 days","Mottled with yellow-tan center","Neutrophils enter, necrosis advances","Fibrinous pericarditis"],["3 to 7 days","Yellow-tan, soft, hyperemic border","Macrophages clear dead cells","Free wall, septal, or papillary muscle rupture"],["1 to 2 weeks","Red-gray depressed border","Granulation tissue","Rupture risk still present"],["2 weeks to 2 months","Gray-white scar","Dense collagen scar","Ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Cells arrive in order","text":"After an MI the cells arrive in the same order as in any acute inflammation: neutrophils first (days 1 to 3), macrophages next (days 3 to 7), then granulation tissue and scar."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Soft wall, rupture risk","text":"Rupture is most likely when macrophages have eaten the dead muscle and no scar has formed yet, roughly days 3 to 14."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Why the posteromedial papillary muscle","text":"The posteromedial papillary muscle usually has a single supply (posterior descending artery), so an inferior MI can rupture it and cause acute mitral regurgitation."}],"highYield":["Ventricular fibrillation is the main cause of death in the first hours after MI.","LAD occlusion is the most common cause of MI and affects the anterior wall and septum.","Contraction band necrosis is a sign of reperfusion.","Free wall rupture causes sudden cardiac tamponade; septal rupture causes a new left-to-right shunt.","Papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema.","Dressler syndrome is autoimmune pericarditis weeks after MI.","Troponin is the most specific marker of myocyte necrosis."],"pitfalls":["Unstable angina and NSTEMI both have rest pain; only NSTEMI has a troponin rise.","Stable angina shows ST depression; Prinzmetal angina shows transient ST elevation.","A true ventricular aneurysm is a scarred bulging wall; a pseudoaneurysm is a rupture sealed off by pericardium and has a narrow neck.","Early fibrinous pericarditis (days 1 to 3) comes from the necrosis itself; Dressler syndrome is a later immune reaction."],"dumps":[{"t":"Infarct timeline","p":"Draw a timeline from hour 0 to 2 months after an MI. Write the microscopic change and the main complication at each stage.","l":["0 to 4 hours: little visible change; arrhythmia risk highest","4 to 24 hours: coagulative necrosis begins; contraction bands if reperfused","1 to 3 days: neutrophils; fibrinous pericarditis","3 to 7 days: macrophages; rupture risk rises","1 to 2 weeks: granulation tissue","2 weeks to 2 months: collagen scar","Late: ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"]},{"t":"Coronary territories","p":"Draw the heart with the LAD, left circumflex, and RCA. Label the walls each supplies and the ECG leads that show each infarct.","l":["LAD: anterior wall and anterior two thirds of septum","LAD infarct: V1 to V4","Left circumflex: lateral wall","Left circumflex infarct: I, aVL, V5, V6","RCA: inferior wall, right ventricle, usually SA and AV nodes","RCA infarct: II, III, aVF","Inferior MI can cause bradycardia or AV block and posteromedial papillary muscle rupture"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on ischemic heart disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Coronary Artery Disease | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=67tUtS3y_GA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Case Study #18: Myocardial Infarction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JXJEeCZZdQ0"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Heart attack (myocardial infarction) pathophysiology | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T_b9U5gn_Zk"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Coronary Artery Disease | Ischemic Heart Disease | Angina🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oQ235E1gvrU"}],"related":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-I3","CV-18-B","CV-18-F3"]},"CV-18-D":{"id":"CV-18-D","summary":"Valve disease either narrows a valve (stenosis, a pressure load) or lets blood leak back (regurgitation, a volume load). The exam links each lesion to its cause, its murmur, and its effect on the chambers, and adds the special valve diseases: endocarditis, MVP, rheumatic heart disease, and carcinoid heart disease.","notes":[{"h":"Aortic valve","points":["Aortic stenosis: age-related calcification is most common; bicuspid valve causes it at a younger age; rheumatic disease less often.","AS: pressure overload, concentric LVH; syncope, angina, and dyspnea on exertion; weak delayed pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus).","AS can cause hemolysis with schistocytes and GI bleeding from angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome).","Aortic regurgitation: aortic root dilation (hypertension, age, Marfan, syphilis), bicuspid valve, endocarditis, rheumatic disease.","AR: volume overload, eccentric LVH; wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses; acute AR from dissection or endocarditis causes sudden failure."]},{"h":"Mitral valve","points":["Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic.","MS raises left atrial pressure: atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombi and emboli, pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure.","A large left atrium can compress the esophagus (dysphagia) or the left recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness).","Mitral regurgitation: MVP, ischemic papillary muscle damage or rupture, left ventricular dilation, rheumatic disease, endocarditis.","MR: volume overload of left atrium and ventricle; S3; atrial fibrillation."]},{"h":"Endocarditis","points":["Infective: acute (S. aureus, normal valves, destructive) or subacute (viridans strep, damaged valves, slower).","Mitral valve is involved most often overall; the tricuspid valve in IV drug use.","Nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis: sterile clots on valves in cancer and hypercoagulable states.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis: sterile vegetations on both valve surfaces in lupus.","Signs: fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions (painless, palms and soles), Osler nodes (painful, fingers), Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages."]},{"h":"Mitral valve prolapse","points":["Myxomatous degeneration: excess proteoglycan in the spongiosa makes leaflets floppy and they balloon into the atrium in systole.","Common (about 2 to 3% of adults); linked to Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and ADPKD.","Mid-systolic click then a late systolic murmur; standing or Valsalva moves the click earlier.","Usually benign; can lead to mitral regurgitation, endocarditis, arrhythmia, and rarely sudden death."]},{"h":"Rheumatic and carcinoid heart disease","points":["Rheumatic heart disease: repeated rheumatic fever scars valves; commissures fuse and chordae thicken and shorten.","The mitral valve is affected in nearly all cases; mitral plus aortic is next most common.","Classic chronic result is mitral stenosis with a fish-mouth valve.","Carcinoid heart disease: serotonin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases lets serotonin reach the right heart.","Plaque-like fibrous thickening of the right heart valves and endocardium causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis.","The left heart is usually spared because the lungs break down serotonin (monoamine oxidase); urine 5-HIAA is high."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Valvular heart diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Aortic stenosis/insufficiency","AS: calcific aging, bicuspid valve, rheumatic. AI: aortic root dilation, bicuspid valve, endocarditis, rheumatic","AS: pressure overload, concentric LVH. AI: volume overload, eccentric LVH","AS: syncope, angina, dyspnea, slow weak pulse. AI: wide pulse pressure, bounding pulses","Left heart failure, sudden death (AS), hemolysis (AS)"],["Mitral stenosis/insufficiency","MS: rheumatic. MR: MVP, ischemic papillary muscle damage, LV dilation, endocarditis, rheumatic","MS: high left atrial pressure. MR: volume overload of left atrium and ventricle","MS: opening snap, diastolic rumble, dyspnea, hemoptysis. MR: holosystolic apical murmur","Atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus and emboli, pulmonary hypertension, heart failure"],["Endocarditis","Damaged or prosthetic valves, IV drug use, dental and GU procedures, cancer (marantic), lupus (Libman-Sacks)","Vegetations of platelets and fibrin, with organisms in infective endocarditis","Fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages","Valve destruction, heart failure, septic emboli, abscess, mycotic aneurysm, glomerulonephritis"],["Mitral valve prolapse (MVP)","Myxomatous degeneration; Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, ADPKD","Floppy leaflets balloon into the left atrium in systole","Mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur","Mitral regurgitation, endocarditis, arrhythmia, rare sudden death"],["Rheumatic heart disease","Repeated group A strep pharyngitis and rheumatic fever","Molecular mimicry, then valve fibrosis and commissural fusion","Mitral stenosis (fish-mouth valve); history of rheumatic fever","Atrial fibrillation, heart failure, infective endocarditis"],["Carcinoid heart disease","Serotonin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor with liver metastases","Serotonin drives plaque-like fibrosis of right heart valves","Flushing, diarrhea, wheezing, right heart failure; high urine 5-HIAA","Tricuspid regurgitation, pulmonic stenosis, right heart failure"]]},{"title":"Murmurs to know","cols":["Lesion","Timing","Where heard best","Features"],"rows":[["Aortic stenosis","Systolic, crescendo-decrescendo","Right upper sternal border, radiates to carotids","Ejection click possible; soft A2"],["Aortic regurgitation","Early diastolic, decrescendo","Left sternal border","Blowing; wide pulse pressure"],["Mitral stenosis","Mid to late diastolic rumble after an opening snap","Apex","Shorter S2 to snap interval means more severe"],["Mitral regurgitation","Holosystolic","Apex, radiates to axilla","Blowing; S3 possible"],["Mitral valve prolapse","Mid-systolic click, then late systolic murmur","Apex","Standing or Valsalva moves the click earlier"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"SAD","text":"Aortic stenosis symptoms: Syncope, Angina, Dyspnea on exertion."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"J♥NES","text":"Major Jones criteria for rheumatic fever: Joints (migratory polyarthritis), heart (carditis), Nodules (subcutaneous), Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"TIPS","text":"Carcinoid heart disease hits the right side: Tricuspid Insufficiency, Pulmonic Stenosis."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bacteria FROM JANE","text":"Infective endocarditis signs: Bacteremia, Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."}],"highYield":["Most common cause of aortic stenosis is age-related calcification; bicuspid valve causes it earlier.","Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic.","MVP comes from myxomatous degeneration and is linked to Marfan syndrome.","S. aureus causes acute endocarditis; tricuspid involvement suggests IV drug use.","Carcinoid heart disease needs liver metastases and spares the left heart.","Left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis leads to atrial fibrillation and stroke.","Standing or Valsalva makes the MVP click earlier and the HCM murmur louder."],"pitfalls":["Janeway lesions are painless and from septic emboli; Osler nodes are painful and from immune complexes.","Rheumatic fever vegetations are small and sterile; infective endocarditis vegetations are bulky and full of organisms.","Stenosis is a pressure load (concentric hypertrophy); regurgitation is a volume load (eccentric hypertrophy).","The opening snap belongs to mitral stenosis; the mid-systolic click belongs to MVP."],"dumps":[{"t":"Murmur map","p":"Draw the chest with the four listening areas. For aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, mitral stenosis, mitral regurgitation, and MVP, write the timing, location, and one extra clue.","l":["AS: systolic crescendo-decrescendo at right upper sternal border, radiates to carotids","AR: early diastolic decrescendo at left sternal border","AR: wide pulse pressure","MS: opening snap then diastolic rumble at apex","MR: holosystolic at apex, radiates to axilla","MVP: mid-systolic click then late systolic murmur","Standing or Valsalva moves the MVP click earlier"]},{"t":"From strep throat to a stiff valve","p":"Trace the path from group A strep pharyngitis to chronic mitral stenosis and its complications.","l":["Group A strep pharyngitis","M protein antibodies cross-react with heart antigens","Acute rheumatic fever about 2 to 3 weeks later with pancarditis and Aschoff bodies","Repeated attacks scar the valve","Commissural fusion and short, thick chordae make a fish-mouth mitral valve","Left atrial enlargement and atrial fibrillation","Mural thrombus and stroke","Pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (section on valvular heart disease)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Valvular Heart Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KiI9WMXBV_o"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3t1n5szXriQ"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Mitral valve regurgitation and mitral valve prolapse | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bjnw_jwDt1Q"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Endocarditis | Cardiac Pathology🫀","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Df-FzR7S4SM"}],"related":["CV-4","CV-16","CV-17","CV-18-G1","CV-18-K1"]},"CV-18-E":{"id":"CV-18-E","summary":"Primary myocardial diseases are problems of the heart muscle itself: the three cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive) and myocarditis. Exam questions hinge on telling them apart by chamber shape, systolic versus diastolic failure, and cause.","notes":[{"h":"Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)","points":["Most common cardiomyopathy; all four chambers dilate and the walls thin out.","Systolic dysfunction: low ejection fraction, eccentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in series).","Causes: familial (often autosomal dominant, TTN titin gene most common), alcohol, prior myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum, Chagas, thiamine deficiency.","Findings: signs of heart failure, S3, mitral and tricuspid regurgitation from stretched valve rings, balloon-shaped heart on chest X-ray."]},{"h":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","points":["Autosomal dominant sarcomere gene mutations, most often beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) or myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3).","Thick, stiff left ventricle, often asymmetric septal hypertrophy; small cavity; myocyte disarray on histology.","Diastolic dysfunction; ejection fraction normal or high.","Left ventricular outflow obstruction from systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve against the septum.","Classic exam patient: young athlete with exertional syncope or sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia."]},{"h":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM)","points":["Stiff ventricle that resists filling; chamber size and systolic function near normal; both atria enlarge.","Causes: amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation fibrosis, endomyocardial fibrosis, Loeffler endocarditis.","Amyloid clue: low-voltage ECG despite a thick ventricular wall.","Right-sided failure signs dominate: raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, edema, hepatomegaly."]},{"h":"Myocarditis","points":["Inflammation of the myocardium with myocyte injury.","Most common cause in the US: viruses, classically coxsackie B; also adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2.","Other causes: Chagas disease, Lyme disease, diphtheria toxin, drug hypersensitivity, SLE, giant cell myocarditis.","Viral form shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis on biopsy.","Presents after a viral illness with chest pain, dyspnea, arrhythmia, raised troponin; can progress to DCM."]},{"h":"How each one fails","points":["DCM: weak squeeze (systolic failure).","HCM and RCM: poor filling (diastolic failure) with preserved ejection fraction.","All three cause arrhythmias and can cause sudden death."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Primary myocardial diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Familial (TTN), alcohol, prior viral myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum, Chagas, wet beriberi, cocaine","Myocyte loss and eccentric hypertrophy; dilated chambers; systolic dysfunction with low EF","Heart failure signs, S3, regurgitant murmurs, balloon-shaped heart on X-ray","Heart failure, arrhythmia, mural thrombus with emboli, sudden death"],["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Autosomal dominant sarcomere mutations (MYH7, MYBPC3); also seen in Friedreich ataxia","Myocyte disarray, thick septum, small stiff LV; diastolic dysfunction; dynamic LV outflow obstruction","S4, systolic murmur that gets louder with Valsalva or standing and softer with squatting; exertional syncope","Sudden cardiac death, atrial fibrillation, heart failure"],["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation, endomyocardial fibrosis, Loeffler endocarditis","Infiltration or fibrosis stiffens the wall; impaired filling with near-normal systolic function","Biatrial enlargement, raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, low-voltage ECG in amyloid","Heart failure (right-sided signs prominent), atrial fibrillation, conduction block"],["Myocarditis","Viruses (coxsackie B classic), Chagas, Lyme, diphtheria, drugs, autoimmune disease","Inflammatory infiltrate with myocyte necrosis; direct viral injury plus T-cell mediated damage","Recent viral illness, chest pain, dyspnea, arrhythmia, raised troponin","Acute heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death, later dilated cardiomyopathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"ABCCCD (causes of dilated cardiomyopathy)","text":"Alcohol, wet Beriberi, Coxsackie B myocarditis, chronic Cocaine use, Chagas disease, Doxorubicin."},{"kind":"trick","label":"HCM murmur follows LV volume","text":"Anything that shrinks the LV (Valsalva, standing up) pulls the septum and mitral leaflet closer, so the murmur gets louder. Anything that fills the LV (squatting, handgrip, leg raise) makes it softer."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Squeeze versus fill","text":"Ask whether the heart cannot squeeze (DCM, low EF) or cannot fill (HCM and RCM, normal EF). Then use wall thickness and cavity size to split HCM from RCM."}],"highYield":["DCM is the most common cardiomyopathy, about 90 percent of cases.","HCM is a leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes.","HCM inheritance is autosomal dominant; screen first-degree relatives.","Amyloid heart: thick walls with low-voltage QRS complexes.","Coxsackie B is the classic viral cause of myocarditis and of later DCM.","Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity is dose-related and causes DCM."],"pitfalls":["HCM versus athlete heart: athlete heart has a normal-sized or larger cavity, normal diastolic function, and regresses with rest from training.","Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis: both impair filling, but constriction shows a thickened or calcified pericardium and a pericardial knock.","Hemochromatosis can cause either a dilated or a restrictive pattern; do not lock it into one box."],"dumps":[{"t":"Three cardiomyopathies side by side","p":"Draw three hearts in cross-section showing dilated, hypertrophic, and restrictive cardiomyopathy. Label wall thickness, cavity size, ejection fraction, and two causes for each.","l":["DCM: thin walls, large cavity, low ejection fraction","HCM: thick (often septal) wall, small cavity, normal or high ejection fraction","RCM: near-normal wall and cavity, enlarged atria, normal or near-normal ejection fraction","DCM causes from ABCCCD or familial TTN","HCM cause: autosomal dominant sarcomere mutation (MYH7 or MYBPC3)","RCM causes: amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation","DCM is systolic failure; HCM and RCM are diastolic failure"]},{"t":"HCM outflow obstruction","p":"Trace why the HCM murmur gets louder when the patient stands up from squatting. Start with venous return and end with the murmur.","l":["Standing lowers venous return (preload)","Lower preload means a smaller LV cavity","Smaller cavity brings the thick septum and anterior mitral leaflet closer","Systolic anterior motion of the mitral leaflet narrows the outflow tract","More obstruction gives a louder systolic ejection murmur","Squatting raises preload and afterload and softens the murmur","Mitral regurgitation often accompanies the obstruction"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiomyopathies and myocarditis"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cardiomyopathy | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Miz9kvrrqk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Dilated cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ou3a7Htbrvw"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Pathophysiology and diagnosis | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtqrKxJmBn4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Myocarditis - causes, pathophysiology, investigation and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INSa4QQcwFo"}],"related":["CV-18-B","CV-18-K6","CV-18-K2","CV-14"]},"CV-18-F":{"id":"CV-18-F","summary":"Pericardial disease covers fluid in the pericardial sac (effusion and tamponade), inflammation of the pericardium (pericarditis, primary or secondary), and tumor spread to the pericardium. Exams test how each one changes filling of the heart and what the ECG and exam look like.","notes":[{"h":"Pericardium basics","points":["Fibrous outer layer plus a serous sac (parietal and visceral layers).","Normal pericardial fluid is a small volume, about 15 to 50 mL.","The fibrous layer does not stretch quickly, so the rate of fluid build-up matters more than the total amount."]},{"h":"Pericarditis","points":["Primary (acute idiopathic) pericarditis is mostly viral, classically coxsackie B.","Secondary causes: MI (early fibrinous, or Dressler syndrome weeks later), uremia, SLE or RA, radiation, TB, bacterial infection, cancer, cardiac surgery.","Sharp chest pain worse lying flat and with inspiration, better sitting up and leaning forward.","Friction rub; ECG shows diffuse ST elevation with PR depression.","Patterns: fibrinous or serous (most common), purulent (bacterial), hemorrhagic (cancer, TB), caseous (TB)."]},{"h":"Effusion and tamponade","points":["Serous effusion: heart failure, low albumin. Bloody: trauma, cancer, TB, ruptured MI or aortic dissection.","Large effusion: muffled heart sounds, low-voltage QRS, electrical alternans, water-bottle heart on X-ray.","Tamponade: fluid pressure squeezes the heart so it cannot fill; cardiac output falls.","Beck triad: hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds.","Pulsus paradoxus: systolic pressure falls more than 10 mmHg with inspiration.","Diastolic pressures equalize across all four chambers."]},{"h":"Metastatic disease","points":["Metastases are far more common than primary tumors of the heart and pericardium.","Common sources: lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia.","Usually present as a bloody effusion, sometimes with tamponade; malignant cells can be found in the fluid."]},{"h":"Constrictive pericarditis","points":["Chronic scarring, sometimes calcified, encases the heart and limits filling.","Worldwide TB is a major cause; in developed countries it often follows viral pericarditis, cardiac surgery, or radiation.","Findings: Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock in early diastole, pericardial calcification on imaging."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Pericardial diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Metastatic disease","Lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia","Direct extension or lymphatic and blood spread to the pericardium; tumor causes exudate and bleeding","Bloody effusion, malignant cells in fluid, new effusion in a cancer patient","Tamponade, constriction"],["Pericardial effusion","Heart failure, low albumin, kidney failure, infection, cancer, trauma, hypothyroidism","Fluid builds in the sac; fast build-up raises pressure and limits filling","Muffled heart sounds, low-voltage QRS, electrical alternans, water-bottle heart","Cardiac tamponade (Beck triad, pulsus paradoxus, equal diastolic pressures)"],["Pericarditis, primary","Viral (coxsackie B classic) or idiopathic","Fibrinous or serous inflammation of the pericardial layers","Positional pleuritic chest pain, friction rub, diffuse ST elevation with PR depression","Effusion, tamponade, recurrence, later constriction"],["Pericarditis, secondary","MI, Dressler syndrome, uremia, SLE or RA, radiation, TB, bacteria, cancer, cardiac surgery","Inflammation driven by the underlying disease (necrosis, toxins, immune complexes, infection, tumor)","Same pain, rub and ECG pattern plus signs of the underlying disease","Purulent or hemorrhagic effusion, tamponade, constrictive pericarditis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Beck triad as three Ds","text":"Decreased blood pressure, Distended neck veins, Distant heart sounds. Add a fourth clue, pulsus paradoxus."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Pericarditis ECG versus STEMI","text":"Pericarditis: ST elevation in almost every lead with PR depression and no reciprocal changes. STEMI: ST elevation in one coronary territory with reciprocal depression."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Tamponade versus constriction","text":"Tamponade is a fluid problem (pulsus paradoxus, Kussmaul sign usually absent). Constriction is a scar problem (Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, calcification)."}],"highYield":["Pain of pericarditis improves sitting up and leaning forward.","Diffuse ST elevation plus PR depression points to pericarditis.","Electrical alternans means a large effusion with the heart swinging in the fluid.","Rate of fluid build-up matters: a small fast bleed can cause tamponade while a slow large effusion may not.","Dressler syndrome is an autoimmune pericarditis weeks after an MI.","Uremic pericarditis is fibrinous and appears with kidney failure.","TB is a classic cause of caseous pericarditis and constrictive pericarditis."],"pitfalls":["Early post-MI fibrinous pericarditis (first few days, over the infarct) versus Dressler syndrome (weeks later, immune mediated).","Kussmaul sign (JVP rises on inspiration) suggests constriction or restrictive cardiomyopathy more than tamponade.","A primary cardiac tumor is rare; a pericardial mass in an adult with known cancer is most likely metastatic."],"dumps":[{"t":"Tamponade physiology","p":"Draw the heart inside a fluid-filled pericardial sac. Trace what happens to filling, stroke volume and blood pressure as pressure rises, and label the bedside signs.","l":["Rising pericardial pressure limits diastolic filling","Right heart is compressed first (thin walls)","Diastolic pressures equalize across the chambers","Stroke volume and cardiac output fall, so blood pressure drops","Neck veins distend as venous return backs up","Inspiration fills the RV, septum bows left, LV output falls: pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg","Heart sounds are muffled"]},{"t":"Causes of pericarditis table","p":"Build a table with columns for type of pericarditis (fibrinous, serous, purulent, hemorrhagic, caseous) and the cause that best fits each.","l":["Fibrinous: MI, uremia, rheumatic fever, radiation, SLE","Serous: viral, autoimmune disease, uremia","Purulent: bacterial infection","Hemorrhagic: malignancy and TB","Caseous: TB","Dressler syndrome is fibrinous and immune mediated weeks after MI","Classic virus: coxsackie B"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on pericardial disease"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease, section on pericardial disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Pericardial Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQDd9OgwSV4"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - pericardial effusion, causes, pathophysiology, investigations and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9FgpLaCi_KE"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Causes of pericarditis | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ij_ERo1Ashc"}],"related":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-E","CV-3","CV-18-K6"]},"CV-18-G":{"id":"CV-18-G","summary":"This unit covers the congenital heart conditions on the blueprint: bicuspid aortic valve, patent ductus arteriosus, atrial and ventricular septal defects, and tetralogy of Fallot. Expect questions that link the embryology to the shunt direction, the murmur, and the genetic syndrome.","notes":[{"h":"Shunt basics","points":["Left-to-right shunts (VSD, ASD, PDA) send extra blood to the lungs; no cyanosis at first.","Right-to-left shunts (TOF) send blue blood to the body; cyanosis early in life.","Untreated left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary pressure, the shunt reverses, and late cyanosis follows (Eisenmenger syndrome).","Eisenmenger signs: late cyanosis, clubbing, polycythemia, right ventricular hypertrophy."]},{"h":"Septal defects","points":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect (not counting bicuspid aortic valve); the membranous septum is the usual site.","VSD: harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border; small ones are loud and often close on their own.","Ostium secundum ASD is the most common ASD (septum primum over-resorbed or septum secundum too small).","Ostium primum ASD is an endocardial cushion defect, linked to Down syndrome.","ASD: fixed, widely split S2 and a systolic flow murmur at the left upper sternal border.","ASD allows paradoxical emboli (venous clot crossing to the systemic side)."]},{"h":"Patent ductus arteriosus","points":["Ductus arteriosus (from the sixth aortic arch) normally closes in the first days after birth as oxygen rises and prostaglandin E2 falls.","Risk factors: prematurity, congenital rubella.","Continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle; bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure.","With Eisenmenger: differential cyanosis, blue clubbed toes with pink fingers."]},{"h":"Tetralogy of Fallot","points":["Most common cyanotic congenital heart defect.","Cause: anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular (outflow) septum, a neural crest related defect.","Associated with 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome).","Degree of right ventricular outflow obstruction sets how blue the child is.","Tet spells improve with squatting (raises systemic resistance, less right-to-left flow); boot-shaped heart on X-ray."]},{"h":"Bicuspid aortic valve","points":["Most common congenital heart anomaly overall, about 1 to 2 percent of people; more common in males.","Associated with Turner syndrome and coarctation of the aorta.","Valve calcifies early, so aortic stenosis appears decades earlier than in a normal valve.","Also linked to aortic regurgitation, endocarditis, and dilation or dissection of the ascending aorta."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Congenital heart conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Bicuspid aortic valve","Male sex, Turner syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, familial cases","Two cusps instead of three; higher leaflet stress leads to early calcification; aortic wall weakness","Ejection click, systolic ejection murmur, aortic stenosis at a younger age","Aortic stenosis or regurgitation, endocarditis, ascending aortic aneurysm and dissection"],["Patent ductus arteriosus","Prematurity, congenital rubella","Ductus stays open; left-to-right flow from aorta to pulmonary artery","Continuous machine-like murmur, bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure","Heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, Eisenmenger with differential cyanosis, endarteritis"],["Ventricular septal defect","Most common defect after bicuspid aortic valve; Down syndrome, fetal alcohol syndrome; part of TOF","Left-to-right shunt between ventricles; usually membranous septum","Holosystolic murmur at left lower sternal border","Heart failure, Eisenmenger syndrome, endocarditis"],["Atrial septal defect","Secundum most common; primum with Down syndrome; Holt-Oram syndrome; fetal alcohol syndrome","Left-to-right shunt between atria; right heart volume overload","Fixed widely split S2, systolic flow murmur at left upper sternal border","Paradoxical embolism, atrial fibrillation, right heart failure, late Eisenmenger"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome), fetal alcohol exposure","Displaced infundibular septum causes pulmonary stenosis, RVH, overriding aorta, and VSD; right-to-left shunt","Early cyanosis, tet spells eased by squatting, boot-shaped heart, systolic ejection murmur","Hypoxic spells, polycythemia, paradoxical emboli and brain abscess, endocarditis"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"PROVe (tetralogy of Fallot)","text":"Pulmonary stenosis (the most important feature), RVH, Overriding aorta, VSD."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"eaRLy versus LateR cyanosis","text":"Right-to-Left shunts cause eaRLy cyanosis (blue babies). Left-to-Right shunts cause LateR cyanosis (blue kids) once Eisenmenger reversal sets in."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Frequency order","text":"Left-to-right shunts by frequency: VSD, then ASD, then PDA. Picture the shunt moving from the ventricles, up to the atria, then out to the great arteries."}],"highYield":["VSD is the most common congenital heart defect after bicuspid aortic valve; TOF is the most common cyanotic one.","Fixed split S2 is the ASD sound.","Continuous machine-like murmur means PDA.","Squatting helps TOF by raising systemic vascular resistance.","Bicuspid aortic valve plus Turner syndrome plus coarctation is a classic cluster.","Down syndrome links to endocardial cushion defects (AV septal defect, ostium primum ASD).","22q11.2 deletion links to TOF and truncus arteriosus."],"pitfalls":["Fixed split S2 (ASD) versus paradoxical split S2 (aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block).","PDA with Eisenmenger causes cyanosis of the lower body (blue toes, pink fingers), because the ductus enters the aorta beyond the left subclavian artery.","A small VSD makes a louder murmur than a large one, so loudness does not equal size.","Patent foramen ovale is a flap that failed to fuse; an ASD is missing septal tissue."],"dumps":[{"t":"Draw the shunts","p":"Draw a four-chamber heart with the aorta and pulmonary artery. Add an ASD, a VSD and a PDA, draw arrows for blood flow through each, and label the murmur for each one.","l":["ASD arrow from left atrium to right atrium","VSD arrow from left ventricle to right ventricle","PDA arrow from aorta to pulmonary artery","ASD murmur: systolic flow murmur at left upper sternal border with fixed split S2","VSD murmur: holosystolic at left lower sternal border","PDA murmur: continuous machine-like below left clavicle","All three are left-to-right and can lead to Eisenmenger syndrome"]},{"t":"Tetralogy of Fallot from embryo to child","p":"Draw the heart in tetralogy of Fallot and label all four defects. Then explain why a child with TOF squats during a spell.","l":["Anterosuperior shift of the infundibular septum is the single root cause","Pulmonary (RV outflow) stenosis","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Overriding aorta sitting over the VSD","Ventricular septal defect","Squatting kinks leg arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance","Higher resistance pushes more RV blood to the lungs and less across the VSD","Associated with 22q11.2 deletion"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Acyanotic Congenital Heart Defects","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=orjnzBGSNx4"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Cyanotic Congenital Heart Defects","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m-PdQwT8mPU"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Tetralogy of fallot | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Be3tuYMgA9I"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"Patent ductus arteriosus | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U2fGKvbir24"}],"related":["CV-1","CV-14","CV-18-D1","CV-18-I2"]},"CV-18-H":{"id":"CV-18-H","summary":"Hemodynamic conditions are the general pathology of blood flow: thrombosis, embolism, infarction, hemorrhage, edema and shock. They link to each other in chains (clot, then embolus, then infarct) and show up in almost every organ system on the exam.","notes":[{"h":"Thrombosis","points":["Virchow triad: endothelial injury, abnormal flow (stasis or turbulence), hypercoagulability.","Arterial thrombi: platelet-rich, form on ruptured atherosclerotic plaque.","Venous thrombi: red, form in stasis, usually deep leg veins.","Lines of Zahn (layers of platelets and fibrin alternating with red cells) mark a clot formed in flowing blood before death.","Inherited hypercoagulability: factor V Leiden (most common), prothrombin G20210A, antithrombin, protein C or S deficiency.","Fates: propagation, embolization, dissolution, organization and recanalization."]},{"h":"Embolism","points":["Most emboli are thromboemboli.","Venous emboli (mostly from deep leg veins) lodge in the lungs.","Systemic arterial emboli mostly come from intracardiac mural thrombi and lodge in legs, brain, gut, kidney and spleen.","Fat embolism after long-bone fracture: low oxygen, confusion, petechial rash, about 1 to 3 days after injury.","Amniotic fluid embolism: sudden respiratory failure, shock and DIC during or after labor.","Gas embolism includes decompression sickness (nitrogen bubbles on fast ascent)."]},{"h":"Infarction","points":["Area of ischemic necrosis from blocked arterial supply or venous drainage.","White (pale) infarcts: solid organs with a single arterial supply (heart, kidney, spleen).","Red (hemorrhagic) infarcts: venous occlusion, loose tissue, dual blood supply (lung, small bowel), or reperfusion.","Coagulative necrosis in most organs; liquefactive necrosis in the brain.","Infarcts are often wedge-shaped with the blocked vessel at the apex."]},{"h":"Hemorrhage and edema","points":["Hemorrhage sizes: petechiae (1 to 2 mm), purpura (3 mm or more), ecchymoses (larger bruises), hematoma (collected mass of blood).","Acute loss of more than about 20 percent of blood volume can cause hypovolemic shock; chronic loss causes iron deficiency anemia.","Edema forms when Starling forces push fluid out faster than lymphatics remove it.","Transudate (low protein): high hydrostatic pressure or low oncotic pressure. Exudate (high protein): inflammation."]},{"h":"Shock","points":["Shock is widespread poor tissue perfusion from low cardiac output or low effective blood volume.","Types: hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, distributive (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic).","Stages: nonprogressive (compensated), progressive (lactic acidosis), irreversible.","End-organ damage: acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, DIC, ischemic bowel, multiorgan failure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemodynamic conditions compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Thrombosis","Immobility, surgery, cancer, pregnancy, estrogen, factor V Leiden, antiphospholipid syndrome, atherosclerosis","Virchow triad activates platelets and coagulation inside an intact vessel","Venous: swollen painful limb. Arterial: sudden ischemia of the supplied tissue","Embolism, infarction, chronic venous insufficiency"],["Embolism","Deep vein thrombosis, atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, long-bone fracture, labor, rapid ascent from a dive","Detached mass (clot, fat, gas, amniotic fluid, tumor, bacteria) travels and blocks a downstream vessel","Depends on site: pulmonary embolism, stroke, cold pulseless limb, bowel ischemia","Infarction, sudden death, DIC (amniotic fluid)"],["Hemorrhage","Trauma, vessel rupture, aneurysm, coagulation or platelet disorders","Blood escapes the vessel into tissue, a body cavity, or outside the body","Petechiae, purpura, ecchymoses, hematoma","Hypovolemic shock (acute), iron deficiency anemia (chronic), mass effect in the skull"],["Infarction","Arterial thrombosis or embolism, venous occlusion, vasospasm, torsion","Ischemic necrosis of the tissue supplied","White wedge in heart, kidney, spleen; red infarct in lung, bowel, testis","Organ dysfunction, rupture, scar formation"],["Edema","Heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition, lymphatic blockage, inflammation","Raised hydrostatic pressure, lowered oncotic pressure, lymphatic obstruction, sodium retention, or leaky vessels","Pitting leg edema, pulmonary crackles, ascites, periorbital swelling","Pulmonary edema with hypoxemia, cerebral edema with herniation, skin breakdown"],["Shock","Bleeding or fluid loss, MI, tamponade, massive PE, sepsis, anaphylaxis, spinal cord injury","Systemic hypoperfusion leads to anaerobic metabolism and cell injury","Low blood pressure, fast pulse, low urine output, confusion, raised lactate","Acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, DIC, multiorgan failure, death"]]},{"title":"Types of shock by hemodynamics","cols":["Type","Example causes","Cardiac output","Systemic vascular resistance","Wedge pressure (PCWP)","Skin"],"rows":[["Hypovolemic","Hemorrhage, burns, vomiting, diarrhea","Low","High","Low","Cold, clammy"],["Cardiogenic","MI, arrhythmia, acute valve failure","Low","High","High","Cold, clammy"],["Obstructive","Tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive PE","Low","High","Varies (high in tamponade, low in massive PE)","Cold, clammy"],["Distributive: septic","Gram-negative or gram-positive sepsis","High early, may fall later","Low","Low","Warm early"],["Distributive: anaphylactic","IgE-mediated mast cell release (type I hypersensitivity)","High or normal","Low","Low","Warm, flushed, hives"],["Distributive: neurogenic","Spinal cord or brainstem injury","Low","Low","Low","Warm, dry, with slow heart rate"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"An embolus moves like a FAT BAT","text":"Types of emboli: Fat, Air, Thrombus, Bacteria, Amniotic fluid, Tumor."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Red or white infarct","text":"Red infarcts happen where blood can still get in or pool: veins blocked, loose tissue, two blood supplies, or reperfusion. Otherwise expect a white infarct."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Reading a shock profile","text":"Start with SVR. High SVR means the body is compensating for a pump or volume problem, so check PCWP (high in cardiogenic, low in hypovolemic). Low SVR means distributive shock."}],"highYield":["Factor V Leiden resists inactivation by activated protein C and is the most common inherited thrombophilia.","Lines of Zahn tell an antemortem clot from a postmortem clot.","Most pulmonary emboli start in deep veins of the thigh and pelvis.","Brain infarcts show liquefactive necrosis; other solid organs show coagulative necrosis.","Lung infarcts are red because of the dual bronchial and pulmonary supply.","Septic shock: low SVR, low PCWP, and high cardiac output early.","Neurogenic shock is the type with a slow heart rate."],"pitfalls":["Postmortem clots are gelatinous with a chicken-fat upper layer and no lines of Zahn; do not mistake them for antemortem thrombi.","Transudate versus exudate: inflammation leaks protein (exudate); heart failure and low albumin do not (transudate).","Obstructive shock looks like cardiogenic shock on SVR and output; the cause is outside the heart muscle.","Paradoxical embolism (venous clot causing a stroke) needs a right-to-left path such as a patent foramen ovale."],"dumps":[{"t":"Clot to infarct chain","p":"Trace a deep leg vein clot from formation to its effect on the lung. Label each step with the Virchow triad element or pathology term involved.","l":["Stasis from immobility starts the clot (Virchow triad)","Hypercoagulability or vessel injury adds risk","Venous thrombus forms in deep calf or thigh veins and propagates","Fragment breaks off and becomes a thromboembolus","Travels through the right heart to the pulmonary arteries","Blocks flow and creates dead space (V/Q mismatch)","Most do not infarct because of bronchial artery supply; an infarct is red and wedge-shaped"]},{"t":"Shock table","p":"Build a table on blank paper with rows for hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, septic and neurogenic shock, and columns for one cause, cardiac output, SVR, and PCWP.","l":["Hypovolemic: low CO, high SVR, low PCWP","Cardiogenic: low CO, high SVR, high PCWP","Obstructive: low CO, high SVR, PCWP depends on cause","Septic: high CO early, low SVR, low PCWP","Neurogenic: low CO, low SVR, low PCWP, slow heart rate","One correct cause for each type","Lactic acidosis marks progressive shock"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Circulatory Shock and Physiology of Its Treatment"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Shock | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=07N42Ragqas"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Approach to Shock - types, hypovolemic, cardiogenic, distributive, anaphylaxis, pathology, treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NqkdqJtxIfs"},{"channel":"Dr. Najeeb Lectures","title":"Shock | Types And Mechanisms of Shock | Dr Najeeb🧠","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hvoYD2Qu_ms"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is shock? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AvtS_IrlbYk"},{"channel":"Khan Academy","title":"Thromboemboli and thromboembolisms | Miscellaneous | Heatlh & Medicine | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h0207xMD6b8"}],"related":["CV-11","CV-18-I7","CV-18-I10","CV-18-C3"]},"CV-18-I":{"id":"CV-18-I","summary":"Vascular conditions cover disease of the arteries (atherosclerosis, aneurysm, dissection, PAD, familial hypercholesterolemia), the veins (DVT, PE, varicose veins), and vessel inflammation or spasm (vasculitis, giant cell arteritis, Buerger disease, Raynaud). The exam favors risk factors, vessel size, and the classic patient for each.","notes":[{"h":"Arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis","points":["Arteriosclerosis means hardening of arteries; three patterns: atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, and Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis.","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: hypertension and diabetes. Hyperplastic (onion-skin): severe hypertension.","Monckeberg: calcification of the media in muscular arteries; does not narrow the lumen.","Atherosclerosis steps: endothelial injury, LDL entry and oxidation, monocytes become foam-cell macrophages, fatty streak, smooth muscle migration (PDGF), fibrous cap over a necrotic core.","Most affected: abdominal aorta, then coronary, popliteal and carotid arteries.","Modifiable risks: smoking, hypertension, high LDL, diabetes. Fixed: age, male sex, family history."]},{"h":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","points":["Autosomal dominant; most often LDL receptor (LDLR) mutation; also APOB or gain-of-function PCSK9.","Heterozygotes (about 1 in 250) have LDL often above 190 mg/dL and early coronary disease.","Homozygotes can have LDL above 500 mg/dL and MI in childhood or the teen years.","Tendon xanthomas (Achilles), xanthelasma, and early corneal arcus."]},{"h":"Aneurysm and dissection","points":["Abdominal aortic aneurysm: atherosclerosis; older male smokers; usually below the renal arteries; rupture risk rises with diameter.","Thoracic aortic aneurysm: cystic medial degeneration (hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos); tertiary syphilis damages the vasa vasorum.","Berry aneurysms in the circle of Willis link to ADPKD and Ehlers-Danlos.","Aortic dissection: intimal tear lets blood split the media; hypertension is the main risk; also Marfan, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner, cocaine.","Dissection pain is sudden, tearing, radiates to the back; unequal arm pressures; widened mediastinum.","Stanford A involves the ascending aorta; Stanford B does not."]},{"h":"Peripheral arterial disease","points":["Atherosclerosis of leg arteries; smoking and diabetes are the strongest risks.","Claudication (calf pain with walking that stops with rest), then rest pain, ulcers and gangrene.","Weak pulses, shiny hairless skin, cool feet; ankle-brachial index 0.90 or lower (normal about 1.0 to 1.4).","Leriche syndrome (aortoiliac block): buttock claudication, absent femoral pulses, erectile dysfunction."]},{"h":"Veins: DVT, PE, varicose veins","points":["DVT: Virchow triad; one swollen, warm, painful leg, though many are silent.","PE: sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia with a raised A-a gradient; saddle embolus can cause sudden death.","Varicose veins: incompetent valves in superficial veins (great saphenous); risks are age, female sex, pregnancy, obesity, long standing.","Chronic venous disease: edema, stasis dermatitis, ulcers near the medial malleolus; superficial varicose veins rarely embolize."]},{"h":"Vasospasm and vasculitis","points":["Raynaud: cold or stress triggers white, then blue, then red fingers; primary is benign in young women, secondary goes with scleroderma, SLE, Buerger disease.","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger): heavy smokers, often young men; segmental inflammation and thrombosis of small and medium arteries spreading to veins and nerves; stopping tobacco is the key step.","Giant cell arteritis: over age 50, women more than men; headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, high ESR; can cause sudden blindness.","Half or so of GCA patients have polymyalgia rheumatica (shoulder and hip girdle stiffness).","Vasculitis is grouped by vessel size: large, medium, small (see chart)."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Arterial conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis","Smoking, hypertension, high LDL, diabetes, age, male sex, family history","Endothelial injury, LDL oxidation, foam cells, smooth muscle proliferation, fibrous plaque","Often silent until narrowing or rupture; angina, claudication, TIA","MI, stroke, PAD, aneurysm, mesenteric and renal ischemia"],["Familial hypercholesterolemia","Autosomal dominant LDLR mutation (also APOB, PCSK9)","Poor LDL clearance by the liver raises plasma LDL from birth","Very high LDL, tendon xanthomas, xanthelasma, early corneal arcus","Premature coronary disease and MI"],["Aneurysm","Abdominal: atherosclerosis, smoking, male, older age. Thoracic: hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, syphilis","Weakened wall (media damage or matrix defect) bulges outward","Pulsatile abdominal mass; often found by chance on imaging","Rupture, thrombosis, embolism, pressure on nearby structures"],["Aortic dissection","Hypertension, Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner syndrome, cocaine, pregnancy","Intimal tear; blood splits the media and forms a false lumen","Sudden tearing chest pain to the back, unequal arm pressures, widened mediastinum","Tamponade, aortic regurgitation, MI, stroke, organ ischemia, rupture"],["Peripheral arterial disease","Smoking, diabetes, hypertension, high LDL, age","Atherosclerotic narrowing of leg arteries limits flow during exercise","Claudication, weak pulses, hairless shiny skin, ABI 0.90 or lower","Rest pain, nonhealing ulcers, gangrene, amputation"]]},{"title":"Venous and embolic conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Thrombosis, deep vein (DVT)","Immobility, surgery, trauma, pregnancy, estrogen, cancer, inherited thrombophilia","Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability","Unilateral leg swelling, pain, warmth; often silent","Pulmonary embolism, post-thrombotic syndrome"],["Pulmonary embolism (PE)","Source is usually a deep vein of the thigh or pelvis; same risks as DVT","Clot blocks pulmonary arteries, creating dead space and raising RV afterload","Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia, respiratory alkalosis","Acute right heart failure, sudden death, pulmonary infarct, chronic pulmonary hypertension"],["Varicose veins","Age, female sex, pregnancy, obesity, long standing, family history","Incompetent valves raise pressure in superficial veins, which dilate and twist","Visible tortuous leg veins, aching, edema","Stasis dermatitis, venous ulcers, superficial thrombophlebitis"]]},{"title":"Vasospastic and vasculitic conditions","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Giant cell arteritis (temporal arteritis)","Age over 50, female, northern European ancestry; polymyalgia rheumatica","T-cell driven granulomatous inflammation of large and medium arteries (carotid branches); patchy skip lesions","Headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, visual symptoms, high ESR and CRP","Permanent blindness (ophthalmic artery), aortic aneurysm"],["Raynaud phenomenon (primary and secondary)","Primary: young women, no cause. Secondary: scleroderma, SLE, Buerger disease, some drugs","Exaggerated vasospasm of digital arteries and arterioles","White, then blue, then red fingers or toes on cold or stress","Primary: none serious. Secondary: digital ulcers and gangrene"],["Thromboangiitis obliterans","Heavy tobacco use; usually young men","Segmental inflammation and thrombosis of small and medium arteries, spreading to veins and nerves","Instep claudication, superficial migratory phlebitis, Raynaud, severe ischemic pain","Ulcers, gangrene, digit amputation"],["Vasculitis","Immune complexes (hepatitis B in PAN, hepatitis C in cryoglobulinemia), ANCA, T-cell granulomas, infection","Inflammation and necrosis of the vessel wall narrow or weaken the vessel","Fever, weight loss, palpable purpura (small vessel), organ ischemia by vessel size","Organ infarction, aneurysm, hemorrhage, glomerulonephritis"]]},{"title":"Vasculitis by vessel size","cols":["Vessel size","Disease","Classic patient and clues"],"rows":[["Large","Giant cell arteritis","Over 50, headache, jaw claudication, high ESR, granulomas"],["Large","Takayasu arteritis","Asian woman under 40, weak arm pulses (pulseless disease), granulomatous aortic arch disease"],["Medium","Polyarteritis nodosa","Hepatitis B link, spares the lungs, renal and mesenteric microaneurysms, transmural fibrinoid necrosis"],["Medium","Kawasaki disease","Child under 5, fever 5 days or more, rash, red eyes, strawberry tongue; coronary aneurysms"],["Medium","Thromboangiitis obliterans","Young heavy smoker, distal limb ischemia"],["Small","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA); sinus, lung and kidney necrotizing granulomas"],["Small","Microscopic polyangiitis","MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA); lung and kidney, no granulomas"],["Small","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Asthma, eosinophilia, MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA), neuropathy"],["Small","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura)","Child after an upper respiratory infection; palpable purpura on buttocks and legs, joint pain, abdominal pain, IgA nephropathy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"CRASH and Burn (Kawasaki disease)","text":"Conjunctival injection, Rash, Adenopathy (cervical), Strawberry tongue, Hand and foot redness and swelling, and Burn (fever lasting 5 days or more)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Raynaud colors in order","text":"White (spasm, no flow), blue (deoxygenated blood sitting still), red (blood rushing back)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Arterial versus venous leg ulcer","text":"Arterial: toes or pressure points, punched out, painful, pale cold foot, weak pulses. Venous: above the medial malleolus, shallow, edema and brown skin, pulses present."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Smoker with a cold limb","text":"Older smoker with claudication and calf pain points to PAD. Young heavy smoker with digit ischemia and migrating phlebitis points to Buerger disease."}],"highYield":["Hypertension is the most important risk factor for aortic dissection.","Abdominal aortic aneurysm is mainly atherosclerotic; thoracic aneurysm is mainly hypertension or connective tissue disease.","Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDL receptor defect, tendon xanthomas, early MI.","Giant cell arteritis needs prompt treatment to prevent blindness; biopsy can miss skip lesions.","Most PEs come from proximal deep leg veins; sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding.","c-ANCA (PR3) goes with granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) with microscopic polyangiitis and EGPA.","PAN spares the lungs and links to hepatitis B.","Buerger disease is treated by stopping tobacco completely."],"pitfalls":["Monckeberg sclerosis calcifies the media without narrowing the lumen, so it gives falsely high ankle pressures; it differs from atherosclerosis.","Primary Raynaud is symmetric and benign; asymmetric attacks, ulcers, or an older patient suggest secondary Raynaud.","Aortic dissection versus MI: dissection gives tearing back pain and unequal arm pressures, and thrombolytics are dangerous.","Takayasu and giant cell arteritis share granulomas; age (under 40 versus over 50) is the main split."],"dumps":[{"t":"Atherosclerotic plaque","p":"Draw an artery wall in cross-section and build an atherosclerotic plaque step by step. Label each cell type and the order of events.","l":["Endothelial injury from smoking, hypertension, high LDL or high glucose","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized","Monocytes stick, enter, and become macrophages","Macrophages eat oxidized LDL and become foam cells (fatty streak)","Smooth muscle cells migrate from the media (PDGF) and lay down matrix","Fibrous cap over a necrotic lipid core","Cap rupture exposes contents and triggers thrombosis"]},{"t":"Vasculitis map","p":"Draw three vessels of decreasing size (aorta, medium artery, capillary). Write two vasculitides next to each, with one lab or clinical clue for each disease.","l":["Large: giant cell arteritis (over 50, jaw claudication, high ESR)","Large: Takayasu arteritis (young woman, weak arm pulses)","Medium: polyarteritis nodosa (hepatitis B, spares lung, microaneurysms)","Medium: Kawasaki disease (child, coronary aneurysm)","Small: granulomatosis with polyangiitis (PR3-ANCA, sinus, lung, kidney)","Small: IgA vasculitis (child, palpable purpura, IgA nephropathy)","Small vessel disease gives palpable purpura"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Aortic Diseases | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4QvHj5wXdWA"},{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Venous Thromboembolism (DVT, PE) | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0SVP95BOUNI"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Vasculitis Overview - types and classification","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwW_Wwkhxnk"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Giant cell Arteritis and Takayasu arteritis (Large Vessel Vasculitis) - signs, pathophysiology","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ck8W4GcYYe8"}],"related":["CV-18-H","CV-18-C","CV-13","CV-18-G1"]},"CV-18-J":{"id":"CV-18-J","summary":"Vascular neoplasms on the blueprint are hemangiomas (benign, very common, especially in infants) and Kaposi sarcoma (an HHV-8 driven tumor seen with immune suppression). Exam questions focus on the virus, the patient group, and telling these lesions from look-alikes.","notes":[{"h":"Hemangioma basics","points":["Benign tumors made of blood vessels; the most common tumors of infancy and childhood.","Most occur in the skin of the head and neck; cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign tumor of the liver.","Capillary type: small, thin-walled capillary-sized vessels in skin and mucosa.","Cavernous type: large dilated blood-filled channels; less well circumscribed; does not regress."]},{"h":"Infantile hemangioma","points":["Appears in the first weeks of life, grows fast for months, then slowly fades.","Most regress by about age 7.","Bright red raised lesion, often called a strawberry hemangioma.","GLUT1 positive on staining, which separates it from vascular malformations."]},{"h":"Other benign vascular lesions","points":["Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma): fast-growing red polyp on skin, gums or mouth that bleeds easily, often after minor trauma.","Pregnancy tumor (granuloma gravidarum) is a pyogenic granuloma of the gingiva during pregnancy.","Cherry hemangiomas are small red papules that increase with age in adults."]},{"h":"Kaposi sarcoma","points":["Caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV), a DNA virus.","Spindle cells form slit-like vascular spaces with red cells leaking between them.","Lesions progress from purple patches to plaques to nodules.","HHV-8 latent proteins push cell growth: a viral cyclin D homolog, and LANA, which interferes with p53 and Rb.","Immune suppression lets the infected cells grow; tumors may shrink when immunity improves.","HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease."]},{"h":"Four forms of Kaposi sarcoma","points":["Classic: older men of Mediterranean, Eastern European or Ashkenazi Jewish background; slow; lower legs.","Endemic African: children and young adults; children can have aggressive lymph node disease.","Transplant-associated: from immunosuppressive drugs; often regresses when the drugs are reduced.","AIDS-associated: an AIDS-defining illness; spreads widely to skin, mouth, gut, lungs and lymph nodes."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Hemangiomas and Kaposi sarcoma compared","cols":["Condition","Cause or risk factors","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Complications"],"rows":[["Hemangiomas (capillary and cavernous)","Unknown cause; very common in infants and children","Benign proliferation of endothelial cells forming capillary or large cavernous channels","Red to blue skin lesion in head and neck; cavernous hemangioma as a liver mass on imaging","Usually none; large or deep ones can bleed, compress structures, or cause cosmetic or vision problems"],["Infantile (strawberry) hemangioma","Infants, more often girls and preterm babies","Rapid endothelial growth phase then involution; GLUT1 positive","Bright red raised lesion appearing in the first weeks of life","Ulceration, bleeding, airway or eye obstruction; most regress by about age 7"],["Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma)","Minor trauma, pregnancy","Rapidly growing lobules of capillaries with inflamed, often ulcerated surface","Red polypoid lesion on skin, gums or lips that bleeds easily","Repeated bleeding; may recur after removal"],["Kaposi sarcoma","HHV-8 infection plus immune suppression (AIDS, transplant drugs) or older age in the classic form","HHV-8 latent genes drive proliferation of spindle cells forming slit-like vessels","Purple patches, plaques and nodules on skin; lesions in mouth, gut, lungs","GI bleeding, lung involvement with respiratory failure, lymphedema"]]},{"title":"Forms of Kaposi sarcoma","cols":["Form","Who gets it","Typical sites","Behavior"],"rows":[["Classic","Older men of Mediterranean, Eastern European or Ashkenazi Jewish background","Lower legs","Slow, rarely spreads to organs"],["Endemic African","Children and young adults in sub-Saharan Africa","Skin; lymph nodes in children","Can be aggressive in children"],["Transplant-associated","Organ transplant patients on immunosuppressive drugs","Skin, lymph nodes, organs","Often regresses when immunosuppression is reduced"],["AIDS-associated","People with untreated HIV and low CD4 counts","Skin, mouth, gut, lungs, lymph nodes","Widespread and aggressive; improves with antiretroviral therapy"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"trick","label":"Purple lesion in a patient with HIV","text":"Ask whether it responds to antibiotics. Bacillary angiomatosis (Bartonella) has neutrophils and clears with antibiotics. Kaposi sarcoma has lymphocytes and spindle cells and is driven by HHV-8."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Which hemangioma goes away","text":"Infantile hemangioma grows then shrinks on its own. Cavernous hemangioma and cherry hemangioma stay."},{"kind":"trick","label":"HHV-8 three diseases","text":"Link HHV-8 to three things: Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease."}],"highYield":["Kaposi sarcoma is caused by HHV-8 (KSHV).","Kaposi sarcoma is an AIDS-defining illness.","Histology: spindle cells, slit-like vascular spaces, extravasated red cells.","Infantile hemangiomas grow fast and then regress in most children.","Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor.","Pyogenic granuloma bleeds easily and can appear in pregnancy."],"pitfalls":["Kaposi sarcoma versus bacillary angiomatosis: both are red-purple lesions in AIDS; bacillary angiomatosis has neutrophils and is caused by Bartonella.","Pyogenic granuloma is a vascular lesion and is neither infectious nor granulomatous, despite its name.","Angiosarcoma is a separate malignant endothelial tumor (linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and chronic lymphedema); do not confuse it with Kaposi sarcoma."],"dumps":[{"t":"HHV-8 to Kaposi sarcoma","p":"Trace how HHV-8 infection becomes a Kaposi sarcoma lesion in a person with untreated HIV. Draw the lesion stages and label the cell types.","l":["HHV-8 is a DNA herpesvirus that infects endothelial-lineage cells","Low CD4 count weakens T-cell control of the virus","Latent proteins drive growth: viral cyclin D homolog and LANA blocking p53 and Rb","Spindle cells form slit-like vessels with leaking red cells","Lesions progress: patch, plaque, nodule","Spread to mouth, gut, lungs and lymph nodes in the AIDS form","Restoring immunity with antiretroviral therapy can shrink lesions"]},{"t":"Vascular lesion comparison","p":"Build a table comparing infantile hemangioma, cavernous hemangioma, pyogenic granuloma, and Kaposi sarcoma. Use columns for patient, appearance, behavior, and cause.","l":["Infantile: infant, bright red raised lesion, grows then regresses","Cavernous: large dilated channels, skin or liver, does not regress","Pyogenic granuloma: red polyp that bleeds, after trauma or in pregnancy","Kaposi sarcoma: purple patches to nodules, HHV-8","Only Kaposi sarcoma is malignant","Kaposi sarcoma forms: classic, endemic, transplant, AIDS"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on vascular tumors"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses, section on human herpesvirus 8"}],"videos":[],"related":["CV-18-I","CV-16","CV-17"]},"CV-18-K":{"id":"CV-18-K","summary":"Infectious vascular diseases are infections that damage the heart or blood vessels: bacterial endocarditis, Chagas disease, Lyme disease, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, viral hemorrhagic fevers, and viral myocarditis. Exams test the organism, how it spreads, and the cardiovascular damage it causes.","notes":[{"h":"Bacterial endocarditis","points":["Acute: Staphylococcus aureus, high virulence, attacks normal valves; IV drug use favors the tricuspid valve.","Subacute: viridans streptococci, low virulence, attacks damaged valves, often after dental work; dextrans help them stick to fibrin-platelet clumps.","Prosthetic valves: Staphylococcus epidermidis. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) suggests colon cancer.","Culture-negative causes: HACEK organisms, Coxiella, Bartonella.","Signs: fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages.","Complications: valve destruction, heart failure, septic emboli, glomerulonephritis, mycotic aneurysm."]},{"h":"Chagas disease","points":["Trypanosoma cruzi, a flagellated protozoan; Mexico, Central and South America.","Triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bug bites, defecates, and parasites in the feces enter the bite or mucosa.","Also spread by transfusion, transplant, mother to baby, and contaminated food.","Acute: often mild; Romana sign (one swollen eyelid) when entry is through the conjunctiva.","Chronic (in about 20 to 30 percent, years later): dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction block and apical aneurysm, megaesophagus, megacolon."]},{"h":"Lyme disease","points":["Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete; Ixodes tick; Northeast and upper Midwest US.","Early localized: erythema migrans (expanding bull's-eye rash) plus flu-like illness.","Early disseminated: facial nerve palsy (can be bilateral), meningitis, carditis with AV block.","Late: arthritis of large joints such as the knee, and neurologic disease."]},{"h":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever","points":["Rickettsia rickettsii, an obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium; Dermacentor tick.","Most US cases are in the southeastern and south-central states despite the name.","Infects endothelial cells, causing vasculitis, leaky vessels, edema and microhemorrhage.","Fever, headache, and a rash that starts at wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk, and involves palms and soles.","Untreated: shock, pulmonary edema, encephalitis, DIC, death."]},{"h":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers","points":["Enveloped RNA viruses that damage endothelium and clotting, causing leaky vessels, bleeding and shock.","Yellow fever: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito; liver necrosis with Councilman bodies, jaundice, black vomit.","Dengue: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, four serotypes; fever, severe muscle and joint pain, low platelets.","Severe dengue is more likely with a second infection by a different serotype (antibody-dependent enhancement).","Filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg): filamentous negative-sense RNA viruses; spread by body fluids; fruit bats are the likely reservoir; high fatality."]},{"h":"Viral myocarditis","points":["Coxsackie B is the classic cause; also adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, influenza, SARS-CoV-2.","Coxsackie B and adenovirus enter myocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR).","Injury comes from direct viral damage plus cytotoxic T-cell attack.","Young adult after a cold or GI illness with chest pain, arrhythmia, raised troponin, heart failure."]}],"charts":[{"title":"Infectious vascular diseases compared","cols":["Condition","Organism and spread","Key mechanism","Classic findings","Cardiovascular complications"],"rows":[["Bacterial endocarditis","S. aureus (acute, IV drug use), viridans strep (subacute, dental), S. epidermidis (prosthetic valve), enterococci; spread through the bloodstream","Bacteria colonize valve vegetations; acute forms destroy valve tissue","Fever, new murmur, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages","Valve regurgitation, heart failure, septic emboli, mycotic aneurysm, ring abscess"],["Chagas disease","Trypanosoma cruzi (protozoan); triatomine bug feces, transfusion, transplant, congenital, food","Intracellular amastigotes in heart muscle and gut nerves; parasite persistence plus immune damage","Romana sign or chagoma early; decades later heart failure, megaesophagus, megacolon","Dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block, arrhythmia, emboli"],["Lyme disease","Borrelia burgdorferi (spirochete); Ixodes tick","Spirochete spreads from the bite to skin, nerves, heart and joints","Erythema migrans, facial palsy, migratory or knee arthritis","Lyme carditis with AV block, myocarditis"],["Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Rickettsia rickettsii (obligate intracellular gram-negative); Dermacentor tick","Infects vascular endothelium causing vasculitis and leaky vessels","Fever, headache, rash from wrists and ankles inward including palms and soles","Hypotension and shock, edema, DIC, myocarditis"],["Viral hemorrhagic fever (yellow fever, dengue, filoviruses)","Yellow fever and dengue: flaviviruses, Aedes mosquito. Ebola and Marburg: filoviruses, body fluids","Endothelial and immune cell infection causes capillary leak and clotting failure","High fever, bleeding, low platelets; jaundice in yellow fever; bone-breaking pain in dengue","Hypovolemic shock, DIC, multiorgan failure"],["Viral myocarditis","Coxsackie B (classic), adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2; respiratory or fecal-oral spread","Direct viral injury plus cytotoxic T-cell attack on myocytes","Recent viral illness, chest pain, arrhythmia, raised troponin","Acute heart failure, arrhythmia, sudden death, dilated cardiomyopathy"]]},{"title":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers compared","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Spread","Key features"],"rows":[["Yellow fever","Flavivirus; enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Aedes aegypti mosquito; monkeys in the jungle cycle","Fever, jaundice from midzonal liver necrosis, Councilman bodies, black vomit; live vaccine available"],["Dengue","Flavivirus; enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA; four serotypes","Aedes mosquitoes","Fever, retro-orbital pain, severe muscle and joint pain, rash, low platelets; severe dengue with plasma leak more likely on reinfection"],["Ebola and Marburg","Filoviruses; enveloped negative-sense single-stranded RNA, filament-shaped","Contact with blood or body fluids; fruit bats likely reservoir","Fever, vomiting and diarrhea, bleeding, DIC, high case fatality"]]}],"tricks":[{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"Bacteria FROM JANE (endocarditis signs)","text":"Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"FAKE a Key Lyme pie (Lyme disease)","text":"Facial nerve palsy, Arthritis, Kardiac block, Erythema migrans."},{"kind":"mnemonic","label":"You drive CARS using your palms and soles","text":"Rashes on palms and soles: Coxsackie A (hand-foot-mouth disease), Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Syphilis (secondary)."},{"kind":"trick","label":"Osler versus Janeway","text":"Osler nodes are Ouch: painful, on finger pads, immune complex. Janeway lesions are painless, on palms and soles, from septic microemboli."}],"highYield":["S. aureus is the most common cause of endocarditis in IV drug users and attacks the tricuspid valve.","S. gallolyticus (bovis) endocarditis means look for colon cancer.","Chagas disease is a classic infectious cause of dilated cardiomyopathy in Latin America.","Lyme carditis usually shows AV block.","RMSF rash moves from wrists and ankles toward the trunk and involves palms and soles.","Second dengue infection with a new serotype raises the risk of severe dengue.","Yellow fever liver shows Councilman bodies (apoptotic hepatocytes).","Coxsackie B is the classic viral cause of myocarditis."],"pitfalls":["RMSF rash moves from the limbs inward (centripetal); typhus rash starts on the trunk and spreads outward and spares palms and soles.","Lyme erythema migrans appears at the bite site days after a tick bite; a small red bump right after the bite is a local reaction.","Libman-Sacks endocarditis in SLE is sterile, so it does not count as bacterial endocarditis.","Romana sign is unilateral eyelid swelling; bilateral eyelid swelling points to other causes such as nephrotic syndrome."],"dumps":[{"t":"Endocarditis pathway","p":"Trace subacute bacterial endocarditis from a dental procedure to a kidney complication. Draw the valve vegetation and label each step.","l":["Damaged valve (for example after rheumatic disease) has a sterile platelet-fibrin clump","Dental work causes transient bacteremia with viridans streptococci","Dextrans help the bacteria stick to the fibrin-platelet clump","Vegetation grows on the valve, usually the mitral valve","Pieces break off as septic emboli to brain, kidney, spleen","Immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis","Osler nodes and Roth spots reflect immune complex and embolic damage"]},{"t":"Tick and bug map","p":"Build a table of the vector-borne diseases in this unit. Columns: disease, organism, vector, main US or world region, and cardiovascular effect.","l":["Lyme: Borrelia burgdorferi, Ixodes tick, Northeast and upper Midwest, AV block","RMSF: Rickettsia rickettsii, Dermacentor tick, southeastern and south-central US, vasculitis and shock","Chagas: Trypanosoma cruzi, triatomine bug, Latin America, dilated cardiomyopathy","Yellow fever: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, Africa and South America, hemorrhage and shock","Dengue: flavivirus, Aedes mosquito, tropics worldwide, plasma leak and shock","Ebola has no insect vector; spread by body fluids"]}],"refs":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on infective endocarditis and myocarditis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Arthropod-Borne and Rodent-Borne Viral Diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"section on Trypanosoma cruzi in the medical parasitology chapter"}],"videos":[{"channel":"Ninja Nerd","title":"Infective Endocarditis | Clinical Medicine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iaO8110iSzI"},{"channel":"Armando Hasudungan","title":"Myocarditis - causes, pathophysiology, investigation and treatment","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INSa4QQcwFo"},{"channel":"Khan Academy Medicine","title":"What is myocarditis and pericarditis? | Circulatory System and Disease | NCLEX-RN | Khan Academy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xL98lBuAn1A"}],"related":["CV-17","CV-16","CV-18-D3","CV-18-E2"]}};
window.NPLEX_CASES = [{"id":"CV-C01","system":"CV","dx":"Heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","codes":["CV-18-A1"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 14-month history of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and one episode of near-syncope while climbing stairs. Her father died of an undiagnosed cardiopulmonary illness at the age of 45 years. Oxygen saturation is 94% on room air. Physical examination shows a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a lift at the left lower sternal border, and mild ankle edema. Echocardiography shows a dilated, hypertrophied right ventricle and a normal left ventricle. Right heart catheterization shows a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 46 mm Hg (normal about 14; pulmonary hypertension is above 20), a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 9 mm Hg (normal 6 to 12), and increased pulmonary vascular resistance. CT angiography and a ventilation-perfusion scan show no emboli or lung disease. Diagnosis: heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C01-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function mutation in BMPR2, the gene most often mutated in heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension. Which of the following best describes the normal function of the protein encoded by this gene?","options":["A receptor tyrosine kinase for PDGF that promotes smooth muscle proliferation","An extracellular microfibril protein that holds TGF-beta inactive in the vessel wall","A G protein-coupled receptor for endothelin-1 that drives vasoconstriction","A serine/threonine kinase receptor that restrains vascular smooth muscle growth"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize BMPR2 as bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2, a member of the TGF-beta receptor family.","Recall that TGF-beta family receptors are serine/threonine kinases that signal through SMAD proteins.","Link loss of this growth-restraining signal to cell proliferation in small pulmonary arteries."],"why":"BMPR2 encodes bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2, a serine/threonine kinase receptor in the TGF-beta superfamily. Its SMAD signaling normally inhibits proliferation of pulmonary vascular smooth muscle and supports endothelial survival. With one working copy lost, endothelial and smooth muscle cells proliferate, thickening the media and intima of small pulmonary arteries and raising resistance. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance, so many carriers never develop disease.","whyNot":["PDGF does drive smooth muscle growth in pulmonary hypertension, but it acts through a receptor tyrosine kinase. BMPR2 signaling normally opposes this growth.","This describes fibrillin-1. FBN1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome, with aortic root dilation and dissection.","Endothelin-1 constricts and remodels pulmonary vessels and is high in this disease, but its receptors are G protein-coupled receptors and are not encoded by BMPR2.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["BMPR2 and heritable PAH","TGF-beta receptor signaling"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-A1"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q2","stem":"During a commercial airline flight, the reduced cabin pressure lowers her alveolar oxygen tension, and her shortness of breath worsens. Which of the following events in pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle initiates the vascular response to alveolar hypoxia?","options":["Adenosine binding A2A receptors, raising cyclic AMP in the cell","More nitric oxide release, raising cGMP and lowering cytosolic calcium","Inhibition of oxygen-sensitive K+ channels, depolarizing the cell","Opening of ATP-sensitive K+ channels, hyperpolarizing the cell"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles, the opposite of the dilation seen in most systemic arterioles.","Constriction needs a rise in cytosolic calcium, which follows membrane depolarization.","Pick the option that depolarizes the cell: closing potassium channels."],"why":"Pulmonary arterioles constrict when alveolar oxygen falls, which normally diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. Hypoxia inhibits oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in the smooth muscle, the membrane depolarizes, voltage-gated L-type calcium channels open, and calcium entry triggers contraction. At altitude the whole lung is hypoxic, so the constriction is widespread and adds to a pulmonary vascular resistance that is already high in her disease.","whyNot":["Adenosine is a key metabolic vasodilator in the coronary circulation during hypoxia. Rising cyclic AMP relaxes vascular smooth muscle.","Nitric oxide and cGMP relax smooth muscle. Hypoxia does not raise this pathway, and reduced nitric oxide signaling is part of the problem in pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Correct.","This is how hypoxia dilates many systemic arterioles, such as those in skeletal muscle and the heart. Hyperpolarization closes calcium channels and relaxes the cell."],"study":{"topics":["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Pulmonary and systemic responses to hypoxia"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q3","stem":"The lift on her anterior chest wall is palpated best at the left lower sternal border. Which of the following anatomic features best explains why enlargement of the affected chamber is palpable at this site?","options":["The right ventricle forms most of the sternocostal surface","The left atrium forms most of the base on the posterior surface","The right atrium forms the right border of the heart shadow","The left ventricle forms the apex and most of the left border"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the chamber under strain: pulmonary hypertension loads the right ventricle.","Recall which chamber faces the anterior chest wall.","Match the right ventricle to the left parasternal location of the heave."],"why":"Pulmonary hypertension raises right ventricular afterload, so the right ventricle hypertrophies and dilates. The right ventricle lies just behind the sternum and left parasternal region and forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface of the heart. A forceful, enlarged right ventricle therefore lifts the chest wall at the left lower sternal border, called a parasternal heave.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left atrium forms the base and lies against the esophagus. When it enlarges, as in mitral stenosis, it can push the esophagus back, but it cannot be felt on the front of the chest.","The right atrium forms the right heart border on a frontal chest film. It does not lie under the left lower sternum.","Left ventricular enlargement moves the apex beat down and to the left, near or beyond the left midclavicular line. Her left ventricle is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Surfaces and borders of the heart","Parasternal heave"],"comps":["CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C01-Q4","stem":"Two years later, she dies of progressive right-sided heart failure. Which of the following findings in the lungs at autopsy is most characteristic of her disease?","options":["Hemosiderin-laden macrophages filling the alveolar spaces","Plexiform lesions in small muscular pulmonary arteries","Hyaline membranes lining the alveolar ducts","Noncaseating granulomas along bronchovascular bundles"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A1","dok":3,"approach":["Classify her disease as precapillary: high pulmonary artery pressure with a normal wedge pressure.","Recall the vascular changes of severe pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Rule out lesions that point to left heart failure, acute alveolar injury, or granulomatous lung disease."],"why":"Severe pulmonary arterial hypertension remodels small muscular pulmonary arteries and arterioles with medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis, and in advanced idiopathic and heritable disease it produces plexiform lesions. A plexiform lesion is a tuft of small, slit-like capillary channels inside a dilated arterial segment, formed by proliferating endothelial cells. It reflects the uncontrolled endothelial and smooth muscle growth that follows loss of BMPR2 signaling.","whyNot":["These heart failure cells form when high left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure leaks red cells into the alveoli, as in chronic left heart failure or mitral stenosis. Her normal wedge pressure rules out that cause.","Correct.","Hyaline membranes mark diffuse alveolar damage, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome. They reflect acute injury to the alveolar wall.","Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis, which can cause pulmonary hypertension through lung fibrosis. Her imaging showed no lung disease, and granulomas are not a feature of heritable disease."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary vascular remodeling","Plexiform lesions"],"comps":["CV-18-A1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C02","system":"CV","dx":"Systemic hypertension with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy","codes":["CV-18-A2"],"case":"A 58-year-old man who works as a delivery truck driver comes to the physician for a required work physical examination. He was diagnosed with hypertension 12 years ago but stopped treatment many years ago. He reports mild shortness of breath when climbing two flights of stairs. Blood pressure is 174/104 mm Hg on repeated measurements. Physical examination shows a sustained, nondisplaced apical impulse and a fourth heart sound at the apex. An ECG shows increased QRS voltage with a left ventricular strain pattern. Echocardiography shows concentric hypertrophy with an interventricular septal thickness of 15 mm (normal 6 to 10), normal chamber size, an ejection fraction of 62% (normal 52 to 72%), and impaired relaxation. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.1 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3). Diagnosis: hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C02-Q1","stem":"One year later, he develops atrial fibrillation. With medication, his ventricular rate is maintained at about 75/min, but his exercise tolerance decreases further and the fourth heart sound is no longer heard. Loss of which of the following phases of the cardiac cycle best explains this decline?","options":["Atrial systole at the end of ventricular filling","Rapid ventricular filling just after mitral opening","Isovolumetric relaxation before the mitral valve opens","Reduced ejection late in ventricular systole"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Note what changed: atrial fibrillation with a controlled rate, so a short diastole is not the explanation.","Recall that the fourth heart sound comes from atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle.","Conclude that a stiff ventricle relies on atrial systole for filling, which atrial fibrillation removes."],"why":"A stiff, concentrically hypertrophied left ventricle fills poorly in early diastole, so it depends more on atrial contraction at the end of diastole. In a normal heart the atrial kick adds about 20 percent of filling, and in a stiff ventricle its share is larger. Atrial fibrillation removes organized atrial contraction, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume fall. The fourth heart sound is made by atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle, which is why it disappears.","whyNot":["Correct.","Rapid filling still happens in atrial fibrillation because it is driven by the pressure gradient when the mitral valve opens. It was already reduced by his impaired relaxation and does not explain the new decline.","Isovolumetric relaxation still occurs with every beat, and no blood enters the ventricle during it. Slow relaxation in this phase is part of his baseline disease.","Ejection depends on ventricular contraction, which continues in atrial fibrillation. This phase is not lost."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of the cardiac cycle","Atrial kick and diastolic dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-A2"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q2","stem":"Echocardiography shows slowed early diastolic relaxation. Myocardial relaxation requires ATP and is delayed when myocardial ATP is depleted. Which of the following steps in the cross-bridge cycle directly detaches the myosin head from actin?","options":["Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate on the myosin head","Release of phosphate, which triggers the power stroke","Release of ADP at the end of the power stroke","Binding of an ATP molecule to the myosin head"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-5","dok":2,"approach":["Picture the head after the power stroke: attached to actin with no nucleotide (the rigor state).","Ask what must happen for it to let go: a new ATP must bind.","Keep the roles apart: binding detaches, hydrolysis recocks, phosphate release powers the stroke."],"why":"After the power stroke, a myosin head bound to actin with no nucleotide is in the rigor state. Binding of a new ATP molecule lowers myosin's affinity for actin, and the head detaches. Hydrolysis of that ATP then recocks the head for the next cycle. When ATP is scarce, as in energy-starved hypertrophied muscle, heads stay attached longer and relaxation slows. Rigor mortis is the extreme case, with no ATP left to release the heads.","whyNot":["Hydrolysis happens after detachment and recocks the head into its high-energy position. The head is already free of actin at this step.","Phosphate release strengthens binding to actin and starts the power stroke. It moves the filaments and does not separate them.","ADP release ends the power stroke and leaves the head tightly bound to actin in the rigor state. A new ATP must bind before the head can let go.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Energy use in diastolic relaxation"],"comps":["CV-5"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q3","stem":"He later reports pressure-like chest discomfort that occurs with heavy exertion and resolves within minutes of rest. Coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis of the epicardial arteries. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of his exertional ischemia?","options":["Spasm of a large epicardial coronary artery","Rupture of a small plaque with a nonocclusive thrombus","Myocyte mass outgrowing its capillary supply","Vasculitis narrowing small coronary arteries"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: exertional pain, thick ventricle, clean epicardial arteries.","Compare oxygen supply and demand in a hypertrophied wall.","Choose the mechanism tied to hypertrophy itself."],"why":"In concentric hypertrophy, myocytes enlarge but capillary density does not keep pace, so the distance oxygen must diffuse increases. Oxygen demand rises with the larger muscle mass and high systolic wall stress. A thick, stiff wall with high diastolic pressure also compresses subendocardial vessels, which fill mainly in diastole. During exertion, demand outruns supply and the subendocardium becomes ischemic even with normal epicardial arteries.","whyNot":["Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina typically occurs at rest, often in the early morning, and is not reliably brought on by exertion.","Plaque rupture with thrombus causes unstable angina or infarction, and angiography would show a culprit lesion. His arteries are clear.","Correct.","Vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa or Kawasaki disease can damage coronary arteries, but he has no systemic features, and his symptoms track with the work of a thickened ventricle."],"study":{"topics":["Complications of left ventricular hypertrophy","Subendocardial ischemia"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-8"]}},{"id":"CV-C02-Q4","stem":"Eight years later, still untreated, he has the sudden onset of right-sided weakness and slurred speech. CT of the head shows a hemorrhage in the left putamen and internal capsule. Rupture of which of the following vessels most likely caused this hemorrhage?","options":["Bridging veins crossing the subdural space","A saccular aneurysm at the anterior communicating artery","The middle meningeal artery beneath the pterion","Lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the bleed: putamen and internal capsule, deep in the brain.","Recall which arteries supply that region: lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery.","Link chronic hypertension to damage in small penetrating arteries."],"why":"Long-standing hypertension damages small penetrating arteries and can form tiny microaneurysms in their walls. The lenticulostriate arteries arise from the first part of the middle cerebral artery and enter the brain to supply the basal ganglia and internal capsule. Their rupture causes intraparenchymal hemorrhage, and the putamen is the most common site of hypertensive hemorrhage. Damage to the internal capsule produces weakness on the opposite side of the body.","whyNot":["Torn bridging veins cause a subdural hematoma, usually after head trauma in older adults or people with brain atrophy. Blood collects over the brain surface.","A ruptured berry aneurysm causes subarachnoid hemorrhage with a sudden, severe headache. Blood fills the subarachnoid space around the circle of Willis.","A torn middle meningeal artery follows a skull fracture at the pterion and causes an epidural hematoma, often with a lucid interval.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Blood supply of the basal ganglia and internal capsule","Hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-A2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C03","system":"CV","dx":"Renovascular hypertension from atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","codes":["CV-18-A2"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman is referred to the physician because of a blood pressure of 182/98 mm Hg despite treatment with three antihypertensive medications. Her blood pressure was normal until the age of 62 years. She has peripheral artery disease and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years. Physical examination shows an epigastric bruit. Funduscopic examination shows arteriolar narrowing without papilledema. Serum potassium concentration is 3.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0), serum creatinine concentration is 1.3 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.1), and plasma renin activity is increased. Ultrasonography shows a right kidney 8.6 cm long and a left kidney 11.2 cm long (normal 10 to 12). CT angiography shows an 80% stenosis at the origin of the right renal artery caused by an atherosclerotic plaque. Diagnosis: renovascular hypertension due to right renal artery stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C03-Q1","stem":"Assume that flow through the stenosis is laminar and follows Poiseuille's law. If the luminal radius at the stenosis is half its normal value, with length, viscosity, and all other factors unchanged, resistance in that segment increases by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["4-fold","8-fold","16-fold","32-fold"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Write Poiseuille's law: resistance = 8 x viscosity x length / (pi x radius to the fourth power).","Halve the radius: radius to the fourth power falls to 1/16.","Resistance therefore rises 16-fold."],"why":"Poiseuille's law states that resistance is proportional to vessel length and blood viscosity and inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius. Halving the radius raises resistance by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16. This is why a moderate narrowing can sharply cut flow and pressure beyond it, and why the kidney beyond a tight stenosis senses low perfusion pressure.","whyNot":["A 4-fold rise would follow if resistance depended on cross-sectional area (radius squared). Poiseuille's law uses the fourth power.","An 8-fold rise would follow from the third power of the radius, which does not appear in Poiseuille's law.","Correct.","A 32-fold rise would need a fifth-power relationship. Resistance varies with the fourth power of the radius."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Resistance and vessel radius"],"comps":["CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q2","stem":"Decreased perfusion pressure distal to the stenosis is sensed within the right kidney and stimulates renin release. Which of the following cells secretes the renin?","options":["Macula densa cells in the wall of the distal tubule","Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole","Interstitial fibroblasts near the peritubular capillaries","Podocytes on the outer surface of glomerular capillaries"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":2,"approach":["Connect the clue (low pressure in the renal arterial tree) to the renin-angiotensin system.","Recall the juxtaglomerular apparatus and which of its parts stores renin.","Place the juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole near the glomerular vascular pole. They store renin in granules and release it when stretch in the arteriole falls, when the macula densa senses low sodium chloride delivery, and with sympathetic beta-1 stimulation. In renal artery stenosis, low afferent arteriolar pressure in the affected kidney increases renin, which raises angiotensin II and aldosterone. Excess aldosterone explains her low potassium.","whyNot":["Macula densa cells sense sodium chloride in the tubular fluid and signal the juxtaglomerular cells, but they do not secrete renin themselves.","Correct.","Peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the cortex make erythropoietin in response to low oxygen delivery.","Podocytes form the filtration slits of the glomerular barrier. They do not make renin."],"study":{"topics":["Juxtaglomerular apparatus","Renin release"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-A2"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q3","stem":"The plaque at the origin of the renal artery contains many lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells). Which of the following features of macrophage lipid handling best explains why these cells continue to take up cholesterol until they are engorged?","options":["LDL receptors increase as cell cholesterol rises, through SREBP activation","ACAT is inhibited, so free cholesterol cannot be stored as ester","Scavenger receptors for oxidized LDL escape feedback by cell cholesterol","ABCA1 transporters move cholesterol from HDL into the macrophage"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how native LDL uptake is limited by feedback on the LDL receptor.","Note that LDL trapped in the intima is oxidized.","Identify the uptake route that lacks feedback control: scavenger receptors."],"why":"Native LDL enters cells through the LDL receptor, and rising cell cholesterol shuts off SREBP and lowers LDL receptor numbers, which limits uptake. LDL trapped in the intima becomes oxidized. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36, and these receptors are not reduced as cholesterol builds up. ACAT stores the excess as cholesteryl ester droplets, which give the cells their foamy look.","whyNot":["SREBP is activated when cell cholesterol is low. High cell cholesterol suppresses LDL receptor synthesis, which limits native LDL uptake.","ACAT is active in foam cells. It esterifies cholesterol, and the stored cholesteryl ester droplets give the cells their foamy appearance.","Correct.","ABCA1 does the reverse: it exports cholesterol from the macrophage to apolipoprotein A-I to form HDL. Loss of ABCA1 (Tangier disease) causes cholesterol buildup in macrophages."],"study":{"topics":["Foam cell formation","LDL receptor regulation"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C03-Q4","stem":"Her hypertension has been present for about 4 years. Histologic examination of the left (unaffected) kidney would most likely show which of the following findings?","options":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis from chronic high pressure","Ischemic atrophy with shrunken tubules and crowded glomeruli","Concentric ring-like calcification of the arteriolar media","Hyperplastic onion-skin thickening with fibrinoid necrosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","dok":3,"approach":["Note that the stenosis shields the right kidney from high pressure while the left kidney receives it in full.","Recall the arteriolar change of chronic, non-malignant hypertension.","Rule out malignant hypertension: no papilledema and pressures below that range."],"why":"The stenosis protects the right kidney's small vessels from high pressure, while the left kidney receives the full systemic pressure. Over years, plasma proteins leak into the walls of its arterioles and smooth muscle cells lay down extra matrix, producing hyaline arteriolosclerosis. The arterioles narrow, and the kidney develops benign nephrosclerosis. The right kidney instead shrinks from ischemia, which matches its small size on ultrasound.","whyNot":["Correct.","Ischemic atrophy is the expected change in the right kidney, downstream of the stenosis, and it explains that kidney's small size.","Ring-like medial calcification describes Mönckeberg medial sclerosis, which affects medium-sized muscular arteries in older adults and does not narrow the lumen.","Onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis marks malignant hypertension, usually with pressures above about 200/120 mm Hg and papilledema. She has neither."],"study":{"topics":["Hyaline and hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","Renal artery stenosis pathology"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C04","system":"CV","dx":"Acute left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema due to viral myocarditis","codes":["CV-18-B1"],"case":"A 29-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of a 3-day history of progressive shortness of breath. He cannot lie flat and is coughing up pink, frothy sputum. Two weeks ago, he had fever, sore throat, and diarrhea. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 86% on room air. Physical examination shows crackles over both lung fields and a third heart sound. A chest x-ray shows bilateral perihilar alveolar opacities and Kerley B lines. Serum troponin I concentration is increased. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25% (normal 52 to 72%). Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is 28 mm Hg (normal 6 to 12). Diagnosis: acute left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema due to viral myocarditis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C04-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in the forces across the pulmonary capillary wall is the primary cause of the fluid in his alveoli?","options":["Fall in plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Rise in pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure","Rise in capillary permeability to plasma proteins","Blockage of lymph drainage from the lung interstitium"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Read the wedge pressure: 28 mm Hg means high left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure.","Apply the Starling forces: high capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out.","Separate cardiogenic edema (high wedge pressure) from permeability edema (normal wedge pressure)."],"why":"Pulmonary capillary pressure is normally about 7 mm Hg, well below the plasma colloid osmotic pressure of about 28 mm Hg, so the lungs stay dry. A failing left ventricle raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, shown by his wedge pressure of 28 mm Hg. When capillary pressure reaches the colloid osmotic pressure and lymph flow can no longer keep up, fluid fills the interstitium (Kerley B lines) and then the alveoli.","whyNot":["Low oncotic pressure from low albumin, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, favors edema, but nothing suggests low albumin here, and his wedge pressure explains the edema.","Correct.","Increased permeability causes noncardiogenic edema, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome or sepsis. In that setting the wedge pressure is not elevated.","Lymphatic blockage, as from tumor spread, causes interstitial fluid buildup. His lymphatics are working and are simply overwhelmed."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces in the lung","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q2","stem":"PCR of an endomyocardial biopsy specimen detects coxsackievirus B3 RNA. Which of the following best describes this virus and its most likely route of transmission to this patient?","options":["Enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA, spread by respiratory droplets","Nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA, spread fecal-orally","Nonenveloped, double-stranded DNA, spread by droplets and fomites","Enveloped, double-stranded DNA, spread by close contact with saliva"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Place coxsackievirus in its family: an enterovirus within the picornaviruses.","Recall picornavirus structure: small, nonenveloped, positive-sense RNA.","Match the route to his diarrheal prodrome: fecal-oral."],"why":"Coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family. It is small and nonenveloped, with a positive-sense, single-stranded RNA genome that can be translated directly as mRNA. Without an envelope it survives stomach acid and spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, which fits his diarrheal illness two weeks earlier. It enters cardiac myocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR).","whyNot":["This describes influenza, which spreads by droplets and can occasionally cause myocarditis. It is not an enterovirus.","Correct.","This describes adenovirus, another cause of viral myocarditis, especially in children. It is a DNA virus.","This describes herpesviruses such as Epstein-Barr virus and cytomegalovirus, which spread through saliva and other close contact."],"study":{"topics":["Picornaviruses and enteroviruses","Viral causes of myocarditis"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K6"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q3","stem":"His wedge pressure was measured with a balloon-tipped catheter advanced into a small branch of the pulmonary artery and inflated until flow stopped. Which of the following best explains why this measurement approximates his left ventricular filling pressure?","options":["A still column of blood links the tip to the left atrium","The tip lies in a pulmonary vein beside the left atrium","The balloon transmits right ventricular pressure forward","The catheter tip senses pressure in the bronchial arteries"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Trace the path from a pulmonary artery branch through capillaries and pulmonary veins to the left atrium.","Apply the rule that with no flow there is no pressure drop along a vessel.","Link left atrial pressure to left ventricular pressure in diastole through the open mitral valve."],"why":"Inflating the balloon blocks flow in that branch. With no flow there is no pressure drop along the vessels downstream, so the blood in the capillaries and pulmonary veins forms a static column connected to the left atrium. The tip therefore reads left atrial pressure. In diastole the mitral valve is open, so left atrial pressure approximates left ventricular end-diastolic pressure, unless a problem such as mitral stenosis separates them.","whyNot":["Correct.","The catheter stays in the pulmonary artery tree and cannot pass through the capillaries. It reflects venous pressure only through the static column of blood.","The inflated balloon blocks the pressure coming from the right ventricle. Before inflation the catheter reads pulmonary artery pressure.","Bronchial arteries come from the systemic circulation (the aorta or intercostal arteries) and supply the airways. The catheter lies in a pulmonary artery branch."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","Pulmonary circulation pathway"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C04-Q4","stem":"The biopsy specimen also shows lymphocytes infiltrating the myocardium adjacent to necrotic myocytes. The infected myocytes display viral peptides on their surface. Which of the following immune mechanisms most directly kills these infected myocytes?","options":["IgE on mast cells triggering histamine release","Immune complexes depositing in small vessel walls","CD4 T cells recognizing viral peptide on MHC class I","CD8 T cells binding viral peptide on MHC class I"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Note that viral proteins made inside the myocyte are presented on MHC class I.","Recall which T cells recognize MHC class I: CD8 cytotoxic T cells.","Link the lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis to cell-mediated killing."],"why":"Cardiac myocytes, like nearly all nucleated cells, present peptides from viral proteins made in their cytoplasm on MHC class I. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize these complexes and kill the cell with perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand. This clears the virus but also destroys myocytes, and a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis is the hallmark of viral myocarditis. Loss of myocytes weakens contraction and leads to acute left heart failure.","whyNot":["IgE on mast cells drives type I hypersensitivity, such as anaphylaxis and allergic asthma. It plays no direct part in killing virus-infected myocytes.","Immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity) causes vasculitis, as in serum sickness or lupus. It inflames vessel walls and does not target infected myocytes.","CD4 T cells recognize peptide on MHC class II, found mainly on antigen-presenting cells. They help the response but do not directly kill infected myocytes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I and cytotoxic T cells","Viral myocarditis immunopathology"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C05","system":"CV","dx":"Right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale) due to cystic fibrosis lung disease","codes":["CV-18-B2"],"case":"A 24-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis (homozygous for F508del) comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of ankle swelling, abdominal fullness, and fatigue. She has advanced lung disease with chronic airway infection by a mucoid gram-negative rod, and her FEV1 is 28% of predicted. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention to 7 cm above the sternal angle (normal 3 cm or less), a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a tender, enlarged liver, and pitting edema to the mid-shins. On room air, arterial PO2 is 54 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100). Hematocrit is 52% (normal 36 to 46), and serum ALT activity is 88 U/L (normal 7 to 35). Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle, a flattened septum, and normal left ventricular function. Diagnosis: right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale) due to cystic fibrosis lung disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C05-Q1","stem":"Her F508del mutation deletes one phenylalanine residue from the CFTR protein. Which of the following best explains the loss of CFTR function in her airway epithelial cells?","options":["A premature stop codon that produces a short, truncated protein","A protein that reaches the cell membrane but rarely opens its channel","A misfolded protein held in the endoplasmic reticulum and degraded","A promoter mutation that sharply lowers transcription of the gene"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Note the mutation type: an in-frame deletion of one amino acid.","Recall what happens to misfolded membrane proteins: endoplasmic reticulum quality control and proteasomal degradation.","Connect the missing chloride channel to thick mucus, chronic lung disease, and pulmonary hypertension."],"why":"F508del is an in-frame deletion of three nucleotides that removes phenylalanine at position 508. The protein misfolds, is caught by quality control in the endoplasmic reticulum, and is degraded by the proteasome, so little CFTR reaches the apical membrane (a class II processing defect). Without this chloride channel, airway surface liquid becomes dehydrated and mucus thickens, leading to chronic infection, bronchiectasis, hypoxemia, and eventually pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","whyNot":["Nonsense mutations such as G542X create a premature stop codon (class I). F508del removes one amino acid and keeps the reading frame.","Gating mutations such as G551D (class III) produce a protein that reaches the membrane but opens poorly.","Correct.","Promoter or splicing mutations reduce the amount of normal protein made. F508del leaves transcription intact and changes how the protein folds."],"study":{"topics":["CFTR mutation classes","Protein folding and ER quality control"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q2","stem":"Her mucoid gram-negative rod is oxidase positive, produces a blue-green pigment, and has persisted for years despite a dense neutrophilic infiltrate in her airways. Which of the following bacterial products best explains this persistence?","options":["Lipid A, which binds Toll-like receptor 4","Exotoxin A, which ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2","Flagellin, which drives motility through airway mucus","Alginate, which forms a protective biofilm matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism: an oxidase-positive, pigment-making, mucoid gram-negative rod in cystic fibrosis is Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Ask which product shields bacteria from phagocytes over the long term.","Choose the exopolysaccharide that defines the mucoid form: alginate."],"why":"The organism is Pseudomonas aeruginosa. In the cystic fibrosis lung it switches to a mucoid form that overproduces alginate, an exopolysaccharide. Alginate builds a biofilm that blocks phagocytosis by neutrophils and macrophages, scavenges oxidants, and limits antibody and antibiotic penetration. Neutrophils keep arriving but cannot clear the bacteria, and their proteases and oxidants damage the airways, driving bronchiectasis, hypoxemia, and pulmonary hypertension.","whyNot":["Lipid A is the toxic part of endotoxin. It activates TLR4 and drives inflammation and septic shock, which helps the host detect the bacteria.","Exotoxin A blocks host protein synthesis and kills tissue cells, much like diphtheria toxin. It damages tissue but does not shield the bacteria from clearance.","Flagellin is detected by TLR5, and Pseudomonas from chronic cystic fibrosis infection often loses its flagella, which helps it avoid detection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa virulence factors","Biofilms and immune evasion"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q3","stem":"Her jugular venous pressure is assessed using the right internal jugular vein with the head of the bed elevated to 45 degrees. Which of the following best explains why this vein is preferred for estimating right atrial pressure?","options":["It lies superficial to sternocleidomastoid, where it is easy to see","It drains straight into the right atrium through its own opening","It lines up almost straight with the SVC and right atrium","It reaches the heart by crossing the midline in front of the aorta"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the venous path from the right internal jugular vein to the right atrium.","Note that a short, straight column transmits right atrial pressure best.","Rule out options that describe the external jugular or the left-sided veins."],"why":"The right internal jugular vein joins the right subclavian vein to form the right brachiocephalic vein, which runs almost straight down into the superior vena cava (SVC) and right atrium. This short, direct path lets the height of the pulsating column reflect right atrial pressure. In right heart failure, rising right atrial pressure shows as distended neck veins. The external jugular vein is easier to see but takes a more angled course through the deep fascia, so it is less reliable.","whyNot":["This describes the external jugular vein. The internal jugular lies deep to sternocleidomastoid, so its pulsation is seen as movement in the neck rather than as a visible vein.","No neck vein opens directly into the right atrium. Blood reaches it through the brachiocephalic veins and the SVC.","Correct.","This describes the left brachiocephalic vein, which carries left internal jugular blood across the midline. That longer route makes the left side less reliable."],"study":{"topics":["Veins of the neck and thorax","Jugular venous pressure"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-B2"]}},{"id":"CV-C05-Q4","stem":"A liver biopsy specimen would most likely show which of the following patterns?","options":["Congestion and hepatocyte loss around central veins","Fatty change with large lipid droplets in hepatocytes","Portal tract lymphocytes with interface hepatitis","Bile duct destruction by granulomas in portal tracts"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B2","dok":2,"approach":["Trace back-pressure from the right atrium through the inferior vena cava and hepatic veins to the central veins.","Recall that the central (zone 3) hepatocytes are also the least oxygenated.","Choose congestion and injury around central veins."],"why":"Right heart failure raises right atrial pressure, which passes back through the inferior vena cava and hepatic veins into the central veins of the liver lobules. Blood pools around the central veins (zone 3), the zone farthest from the oxygen-rich portal inflow. Hepatocytes there atrophy and may die, while better-oxygenated periportal cells survive. The dark, congested centers against paler surrounding tissue give the cut liver a nutmeg look.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fatty change (steatosis) comes from alcohol, obesity, insulin resistance, or other metabolic causes. It does not reflect venous congestion and would not explain a tender, swollen liver with distended neck veins.","Interface hepatitis with portal lymphocytes is the pattern of chronic viral or autoimmune hepatitis.","Granulomatous bile duct destruction is the classic lesion of primary biliary cholangitis."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic passive congestion of the liver","Hepatic lobule zones"],"comps":["CV-18-B2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C06","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction from Chagas cardiomyopathy, with neurohormonal activation","codes":["CV-18-B1"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of progressive fatigue, shortness of breath after walking one block, and leg swelling. He moved to the United States from rural Bolivia 25 years ago. Pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows bibasilar crackles, a third heart sound, and pitting edema of the legs. An ECG shows right bundle branch block and left anterior fascicular block. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle, an ejection fraction of 27% (normal 52 to 72%), and a small apical aneurysm. Serum BNP is 1,240 pg/mL (normal less than 100), sodium 130 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (normal 0.7 to 1.3). IgG antibodies to Trypanosoma cruzi are positive on two assays. Diagnosis: Chagas cardiomyopathy with chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C06-Q1","stem":"He most likely acquired the infection during childhood. Which of the following is the usual route of transmission of this organism to humans in endemic areas?","options":["Bite of an infected tsetse fly","Feces of an infected triatomine bug","Bite of an infected Anopheles mosquito","Bite of an infected female sandfly"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the serology: Trypanosoma cruzi, the cause of Chagas disease.","Recall its vector: the triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bug.","Note that the parasite passes in the bug's feces during or after the blood meal."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is carried by triatomine (reduviid, or kissing) bugs that feed at night, often on the face. The bug defecates while feeding, and trypomastigotes in its feces enter through the bite wound or the conjunctiva when the person rubs the area. Inside host cells, including cardiac myocytes, the parasite multiplies as amastigotes. Decades later, about 20 to 30 percent of infected people develop chronic heart disease. Blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother-to-child spread, and contaminated food are less common routes.","whyNot":["Tsetse flies transmit Trypanosoma brucei, the cause of African sleeping sickness. That parasite passes in the fly's saliva during the bite.","Correct.","Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, the cause of malaria.","Sandflies transmit Leishmania, which causes cutaneous or visceral leishmaniasis."],"study":{"topics":["Trypanosoma cruzi life cycle","Vector-borne protozoa"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K2"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q2","stem":"Myocytes from failing hearts such as his show smaller calcium transients with each beat and slower removal of calcium from the cytosol. Which of the following changes most directly reduces the calcium available for each contraction?","options":["Lower SERCA2a activity refilling the sarcoplasmic reticulum","More phospholamban phosphorylation by protein kinase A","Less sodium-calcium exchange, trapping calcium in the cytosol","Tighter closure of ryanodine receptors during diastole"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-5","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that each beat releases calcium stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Ask which pump refills that store: SERCA2a.","Link less refilling to smaller release, weaker contraction, and slower relaxation."],"why":"With each beat, calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum through ryanodine receptors binds troponin C. Relaxation and the next beat depend on SERCA2a pumping that calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. In heart failure, SERCA2a expression and activity fall, and its inhibitor phospholamban is less phosphorylated, so it inhibits SERCA2a more. Less calcium is stored, less is released with the next beat, and contraction weakens. Slower reuptake also slows relaxation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Protein kinase A phosphorylation of phospholamban relieves its inhibition of SERCA2a and raises stored calcium, as during sympathetic stimulation. In failing hearts phospholamban phosphorylation is reduced.","The sodium-calcium exchanger is increased in failing myocytes and removes more calcium from the cell. If exchange were reduced, more calcium would stay in the cell.","In heart failure, ryanodine receptors tend to leak calcium during diastole, which further drains the stores. Tighter closure would preserve them."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling","Calcium handling in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the change in his left ventricular pressure-volume loop compared with that of a healthy adult?","options":["Steeper end-diastolic curve, normal end-systolic volume","No isovolumetric relaxation, with a much wider loop","Flatter end-systolic line, loop at larger volumes","Narrower loop shifted to a smaller end-diastolic volume"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the primary defect: reduced contractility (ejection fraction 27%).","Recall that contractility is shown by the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume line.","Predict the result: larger end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes, with a small stroke volume."],"why":"Contractility is shown by the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship. In systolic heart failure that slope falls, so at any afterload the ventricle empties less and end-systolic volume rises. The ventricle dilates and end-diastolic volume rises as the body retains fluid to raise preload. The loop moves to the right and becomes narrow, because stroke volume is small compared with the large end-diastolic volume, giving his ejection fraction of 27%.","whyNot":["A steep end-diastolic curve with normal systolic function describes a stiff ventricle, as in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction from hypertensive hypertrophy.","Loss of a true isovolumetric relaxation phase occurs in aortic regurgitation, because blood leaks back into the ventricle as soon as ejection ends. That loop widens from a large stroke volume.","Correct.","A narrow loop at small volumes reflects low preload, as in hemorrhage or dehydration. His ventricle is dilated."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure-volume loops","Contractility and the ESPVR"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C06-Q4","stem":"His serum sodium concentration is 130 mEq/L although his total body sodium is increased. Which of the following hormones most directly causes the low serum sodium concentration?","options":["Cortisol acting on renal mineralocorticoid receptors","Norepinephrine acting on renal tubular alpha-1 receptors","Aldosterone acting on collecting duct principal cells","Vasopressin acting on collecting duct V2 receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B1","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize hyponatremia with volume overload as a sign of excess free water.","Ask which hormone adds free water: vasopressin (ADH).","Explain its release despite low osmolality: baroreceptors sense low effective arterial volume."],"why":"In heart failure, low cardiac output reduces effective arterial blood volume. Arterial baroreceptors sense this and trigger nonosmotic release of vasopressin (ADH), which overrides the normal suppression of ADH by low plasma osmolality. Vasopressin binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells and inserts aquaporin-2 channels, so free water is reabsorbed. Water retained in excess of sodium dilutes plasma sodium. Low serum sodium in heart failure signals strong neurohormonal activation and a worse prognosis.","whyNot":["At high levels cortisol can act on mineralocorticoid receptors and retain sodium, which tends to keep sodium up. Low sodium is a feature of cortisol deficiency, through increased vasopressin.","Norepinephrine raises heart rate, contractility, renin release, and tubular sodium reabsorption, which adds to fluid retention. It does not directly add free water.","Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption, and water follows, which expands volume and causes edema. On its own it does not dilute plasma sodium, and it also increases potassium secretion.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Neurohormonal activation in heart failure","Hyponatremia in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-18-B1","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C55","system":"CV","dx":"Acute pulmonary embolism","codes":["CV-18-I7"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sudden shortness of breath and sharp right-sided chest pain that worsens with inspiration. He returned from a 14-hour flight 3 days ago, and his right calf has been sore and swollen since the trip. Pulse is 116/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mmHg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 89%. Arterial blood gas analysis on room air at sea level shows pH 7.49 (7.35 to 7.45), PaCO2 30 mmHg (35 to 45), and PaO2 62 mmHg (80 to 100). D-dimer is 2,400 ng/mL FEU (below 500). CT pulmonary angiography shows filling defects in both lower lobe pulmonary arteries. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle. The diagnosis is acute pulmonary embolism.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C55-Q1","stem":"Using the alveolar gas equation (PAO2 = 150 mmHg minus PaCO2/0.8), which of the following best describes this patient's alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient and the primary mechanism of his hypoxemia?","options":["About 10 mmHg, caused by alveolar hypoventilation","About 50 mmHg, caused by ventilation-perfusion mismatch","About 10 mmHg, caused by low inspired oxygen pressure","About 50 mmHg, caused by thickening of the alveolar membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Calculate PAO2: 150 minus (30 divided by 0.8) is about 112 mmHg.","Subtract PaO2: 112 minus 62 gives an A-a gradient of about 50 mmHg, well above the normal 5 to 15 mmHg.","A wide gradient with a low PaCO2 rules out hypoventilation and low inspired oxygen; in PE the cause is ventilation-perfusion mismatch."],"why":"The clots block flow to ventilated lung, so blood is diverted to the remaining vessels, which are now overperfused relative to their ventilation. Blood leaving those low V/Q units is poorly oxygenated, and the A-a gradient widens to about 50 mmHg. Hypoxemia and pain drive hyperventilation, which explains the low PaCO2 and respiratory alkalosis.","whyNot":["Hypoventilation raises PaCO2 and keeps the A-a gradient normal. His PaCO2 is low. This fits opioid overdose or neuromuscular weakness.","Correct.","Low inspired oxygen (high altitude) lowers PaO2 with a normal gradient. He was tested at sea level, and his gradient is wide.","Membrane thickening (pulmonary fibrosis) does widen the gradient, but nothing in this case thickens the alveolar wall. The problem here is blocked blood flow."],"study":{"topics":["Alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient","Causes of hypoxemia","V/Q mismatch in pulmonary embolism"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-I7"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q2","stem":"CT pulmonary angiography shows no infarction of the lung parenchyma distal to the occluded arteries. Which of the following vessels most likely maintains the viability of this lung tissue?","options":["Bronchial veins draining lung tissue into the azygos system","Internal thoracic arteries entering through the visceral pleura","Bronchial arteries arising from the systemic circulation","Pericardiophrenic arteries supplying the lower lobes"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that the lung has a dual blood supply.","Identify the second, systemic source: the bronchial arteries, mostly from the descending thoracic aorta.","Link this to the fact that most emboli in healthy people do not cause infarction."],"why":"The bronchial arteries arise from the systemic circulation, mainly the descending thoracic aorta, and supply the bronchi and supporting lung tissue. When a pulmonary artery branch is blocked, bronchial flow keeps the tissue alive. Infarction becomes likely when this backup is weak, as in left heart failure or existing lung disease.","whyNot":["Veins drain blood away from tissue and cannot deliver oxygen. Bronchial veins do drain into the azygos system, but that is irrelevant to survival.","The internal thoracic arteries supply the anterior chest wall and breast. They do not enter the lung through the visceral pleura.","Correct.","The pericardiophrenic arteries run with the phrenic nerves to the pericardium and diaphragm. They do not supply lung parenchyma."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the lung","Bronchial arteries","Pulmonary infarction"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q3","stem":"One hour later, his blood pressure decreases to 86/54 mmHg. Repeat echocardiography shows flattening of the interventricular septum with bowing toward the left ventricle during diastole. Which of the following mechanisms most likely explains the decrease in his cardiac output?","options":["Septal bowing into the left ventricle that limits its filling","Coronary artery embolism reducing left ventricular contraction","Pericardial fluid compressing all four chambers of the heart","Systemic vasoconstriction raising left ventricular afterload"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Clots suddenly raise pulmonary vascular resistance, so right ventricular afterload rises.","The thin-walled right ventricle dilates, and because both ventricles share the septum and pericardium, the septum shifts left.","A smaller left ventricle fills less, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall (ventricular interdependence)."],"why":"Acute right ventricular pressure overload dilates the right ventricle. Inside the fixed pericardial space, the septum bows into the left ventricle and reduces its preload. Left ventricular stroke volume falls, causing hypotension. Low aortic pressure and high right ventricular wall stress also reduce right coronary perfusion, which explains the troponin rise often seen in PE with right ventricular strain.","whyNot":["Correct.","Venous clots travel to the lungs and cannot reach the coronary arteries unless they cross a right-to-left shunt. Nothing here suggests that.","Tamponade would show a pericardial effusion on echocardiography. His echo shows a dilated right ventricle and septal shift instead.","Higher afterload would raise blood pressure. His problem is low left ventricular preload, and his pressure is falling."],"study":{"topics":["Acute right heart strain","Ventricular interdependence","Obstructive shock"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-B2"]}},{"id":"CV-C55-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sequences of enzymatic steps produces the fragment measured by this patient's D-dimer assay?","options":["Tissue plasminogen activator directly digests cross-linked fibrin","Factor XIIIa cross-links fibrin, then plasmin cleaves the clot","Plasmin cleaves circulating fibrinogen that was never cross-linked","Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen, then antithrombin breaks down fibrin"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["D-dimer is two D domains held together by a covalent cross-link.","That cross-link is made only by factor XIIIa in a formed clot.","Plasmin must then break the cross-linked fibrin to release the fragment."],"why":"Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin and activates factor XIII. Factor XIIIa, a transglutaminase, covalently links neighboring D domains of fibrin. When plasmin degrades this stabilized clot, the linked D domains are released as D-dimer. A high value shows that a clot formed and is being broken down, so it is sensitive but not specific.","whyNot":["Tissue plasminogen activator converts plasminogen to plasmin. Plasmin then does the work of digesting the fibrin.","Correct.","Plasmin acting on fibrinogen alone makes fibrinogen degradation products without D-dimer, because no cross-link exists. This pattern occurs in primary fibrinogenolysis.","Antithrombin inhibits thrombin and factor Xa. It does not degrade fibrin."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrin stabilization by factor XIII","Fibrinolysis","D-dimer"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C56","system":"CV","dx":"Deep vein thrombosis after surgery and immobility","codes":["CV-18-I10"],"case":"A 71-year-old woman is evaluated because of aching and swelling of the left leg 5 days after surgical repair of a left hip fracture. Because of pain, she has been mostly confined to bed since the operation. Temperature is 37.0 °C (98.6 °F). Physical examination shows a warm, tender left calf with pitting edema; its circumference is 4 cm greater than that of the right calf. Compression duplex ultrasonography shows noncompressible left popliteal and femoral veins containing echogenic thrombus. Platelet count is 260,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000). The diagnosis is proximal deep vein thrombosis after surgery and immobility. Treatment with unfractionated heparin is started.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C56-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the composition of this patient's venous thrombus?","options":["Platelets and fibrin attached to a damaged heart valve leaflet","Mostly platelets with little fibrin, formed where shear stress is high","Separated layers of red cells and plasma, formed after flow stops","Many trapped red cells in a fibrin mesh, formed where flow is slow"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: a vein with stasis.","Low flow lets coagulation factors build up, so fibrin forms and traps red cells.","Choose the red (stasis) thrombus."],"why":"Venous thrombi form under low flow, where activated clotting factors are not washed away. Fibrin forms a mesh that traps many red cells, so the thrombus is red and gelatinous and often builds a long tail in the direction of flow. This fibrin-rich makeup is why venous thrombosis is treated with anticoagulants.","whyNot":["This describes a valve vegetation, as in endocarditis. Her clot is in a leg vein.","This is an arterial (pale) thrombus, which forms on a ruptured plaque under high shear and is platelet rich. That is why arterial disease is treated with antiplatelet drugs.","This describes a postmortem clot, which settles into layers after circulation stops and is not attached to the vessel wall.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Venous versus arterial thrombi","Virchow triad"],"comps":["CV-18-H6","CV-18-I10"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q2","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms that normally promotes venous return from the deep veins of the leg was most reduced by her bed rest?","options":["Arteriolar constriction that limits inflow of blood into the leg","Plasma oncotic pressure that pulls tissue fluid into the leg veins","Calf muscle contraction that squeezes blood through one-way valves","Sympathetic venoconstriction that keeps the deep veins narrowed"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the factor in Virchow triad that immobility causes: stasis.","Ask what normally moves venous blood upward from the legs against gravity.","The skeletal muscle pump, with venous valves, drives venous return from the legs."],"why":"The deep leg veins run within muscle compartments. Each calf contraction squeezes them, and the one-way valves let blood move only toward the heart. Without movement this pump stops, blood pools in the valve pockets and soleal sinuses, and stasis allows clotting factors to accumulate.","whyNot":["Arteriolar tone controls inflow to capillaries. Bed rest does not remove it, and less inflow would not explain pooling in the veins.","Plasma oncotic pressure is unchanged by immobility. It governs fluid shifts across capillaries, not forward flow in large veins.","Correct.","Sympathetic venous tone is still present during bed rest. Venoconstriction actually pushes blood toward the heart, so it is not the force she lost."],"study":{"topics":["Skeletal muscle pump","Venous return","Venous valves"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-12"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q3","stem":"Iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis occurs more often in the left leg than in the right leg. Which of the following anatomic relationships best explains this pattern?","options":["The inguinal ligament pressing on the left femoral vein during hip flexion","The right common iliac artery crossing anterior to the left common iliac vein","The left renal vein crossing between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery","The left common iliac artery crossing anterior to the right common iliac vein"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Picture the aortic bifurcation at about L4 and the IVC formed to the right of midline at about L5.","The left common iliac vein must cross to the right to reach the IVC.","On its way it passes behind the right common iliac artery, which presses it against the lower lumbar spine."],"why":"The IVC lies to the right of the aorta, so the left common iliac vein takes a longer course across the midline. The right common iliac artery crosses in front of it and can compress it against the fifth lumbar vertebra. Chronic pulsatile compression narrows the vein and causes stasis. When this compression causes symptoms, it is called May-Thurner syndrome.","whyNot":["The inguinal ligament is symmetric on both sides and does not explain a left-sided pattern.","Correct.","Compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and SMA (nutcracker) causes left flank pain, hematuria, or a left varicocele, not leg DVT.","The right common iliac vein runs a short, nearly straight course to the IVC and is not crossed by the left common iliac artery."],"study":{"topics":["Iliac vessel relationships","May-Thurner anatomy","Formation of the IVC"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I10"]}},{"id":"CV-C56-Q4","stem":"On day 7 of heparin therapy, her platelet count is 98,000/µL, and duplex ultrasonography shows a new thrombus in the right femoral vein. Which of the following mechanisms best explains both findings?","options":["Cytotoxic T cells destroying megakaryocytes in the bone marrow","IgE on mast cells binding heparin and releasing histamine","IgM cold agglutinins fixing complement on platelet surfaces","IgG against platelet factor 4 and heparin activating platelets"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Platelets fell by more than half about 5 to 10 days after heparin started, and a new clot formed.","Low platelets plus new thrombosis points to heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT).","Name the mechanism: IgG antibodies to platelet factor 4 bound to heparin."],"why":"Heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4), creating a new epitope. IgG antibodies form against the PF4-heparin complex, and the immune complexes bind platelet Fc receptors. This activates platelets, which releases more PF4 and procoagulant microparticles, so platelets are consumed and cleared while thrombin generation rises. The result is a low count with new clots, so heparin must be stopped and replaced with a non-heparin anticoagulant.","whyNot":["T cells attacking megakaryocytes would reduce platelet production but would not cause new thrombosis.","An IgE-mediated reaction causes hives, wheezing, or anaphylaxis within minutes. It does not lower platelets or cause clots.","Cold agglutinins are IgM antibodies against red cells, as in Mycoplasma infection. They cause hemolysis, not falling platelets with clots.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Antibody-mediated platelet activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C57","system":"CV","dx":"Deep vein thrombosis in a patient with factor V Leiden","codes":["CV-18-I10"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-day history of pain and swelling of the right leg. She has had no recent surgery, trauma, or prolonged travel. She began taking a combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive 4 months ago. Her mother had a deep vein thrombosis at age 40 years. Duplex ultrasonography shows thrombus in the right femoral vein. Prothrombin time is 12 seconds (11 to 13.5), and activated partial thromboplastin time is 30 seconds (25 to 35). An activated protein C resistance assay is abnormal, and genetic testing shows heterozygosity for factor V Leiden (F5 R506Q). The diagnosis is deep vein thrombosis in a patient with factor V Leiden.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C57-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanism by which this patient's variant promotes thrombosis?","options":["Activated factor V resists cleavage by activated protein C","Antithrombin cannot bind heparan sulfate on endothelial cells","Thrombomodulin fails to bind thrombin on the endothelial surface","A 3' untranslated region change raises plasma prothrombin levels"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["R506Q is a single amino acid change: arginine 506 becomes glutamine.","Arginine 506 is a cleavage site where activated protein C inactivates factor Va.","Factor Va lasts longer, so more thrombin is made."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a missense point mutation (G to A) that replaces arginine 506 with glutamine. Activated protein C, with protein S as cofactor, normally shuts off factor Va by cleaving it, and arginine 506 is the first and fastest cleavage site. The mutant factor Va resists that cleavage, so prothrombinase stays active longer and thrombin generation rises. Routine PT and aPTT are normal because the clotting factors are present in normal amounts and work normally.","whyNot":["Correct.","Antithrombin deficiency or dysfunction is a separate inherited thrombophilia. It does not explain APC resistance.","Thrombomodulin defects are rare and would reduce protein C activation. Her abnormal test is resistance of factor Va to protein C that is already activated.","This describes the prothrombin G20210A variant, the second most common inherited thrombophilia. Her test shows a factor V variant."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Protein C pathway","Inherited thrombophilias"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q2","stem":"Her husband is tested and is also heterozygous for factor V Leiden. Which of the following is the probability that their first child will be homozygous for the variant?","options":["12.5%","25%","50%","75%"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Each heterozygous parent passes the variant allele with a probability of 1/2.","A homozygous child must receive the variant from both parents.","Multiply: 1/2 times 1/2 equals 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern for thrombosis risk, with incomplete penetrance. Two heterozygous parents give a 25% chance of a homozygous child, a 50% chance of a heterozygous child, and a 25% chance of an unaffected child. Homozygotes have a much higher lifetime risk of clotting than heterozygotes, which is why this calculation matters for counseling.","whyNot":["12.5% would apply if one more independent 1/2 event were required. With two heterozygous parents there are only two transmissions.","Correct.","50% is the chance of a heterozygous child, or of any affected child if only one parent carried the variant.","75% is the chance that a child carries at least one copy (heterozygous or homozygous), not the chance of being homozygous."],"study":{"topics":["Punnett square for two heterozygotes","Autosomal dominant inheritance with incomplete penetrance"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q3","stem":"Two days later, she develops sudden weakness of the left arm. MRI of the brain shows a small acute infarct in the territory of the right middle cerebral artery. Echocardiography with agitated saline shows bubbles passing from the right atrium to the left atrium during a Valsalva maneuver through a flap-like opening in the atrial septum; no septal tissue is missing. Failure of which of the following normal processes most likely allowed a leg thrombus to reach her brain?","options":["Sealing of the opening between septum primum and septum secundum","Closure of the ductus arteriosus soon after the first breaths","Obliteration of the ductus venosus to form the ligamentum venosum","Division of the truncus arteriosus by the aorticopulmonary septum"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":3,"approach":["A venous clot causing a stroke means it crossed from the right side of the heart to the left (paradoxical embolism).","A flap-like atrial opening that opens with straining is a patent foramen ovale.","The foramen ovale closes when the septum primum seals against the septum secundum after birth."],"why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale lets blood pass from the right to the left atrium through the gap between the septum secundum and the septum primum. After birth, left atrial pressure rises above right, pressing the septum primum against the septum secundum, and the two usually fuse. In about a quarter of adults they never fuse, leaving a patent foramen ovale. Straining raises right atrial pressure, opens the flap, and lets a venous clot reach the systemic arteries.","whyNot":["Correct.","A patent ductus arteriosus connects the aorta and pulmonary artery, not the atria, and usually shunts left to right after birth.","The ductus venosus bypasses the fetal liver. Its persistence would not create an atrial-level shunt.","Failed truncal division causes persistent truncus arteriosus, a cyanotic defect present from infancy, not an adult atrial flap."],"study":{"topics":["Patent foramen ovale","Paradoxical embolism","Atrial septation"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C57-Q4","stem":"Her thrombosis risk increased sharply after she began taking the combined oral contraceptive. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this added risk?","options":["Estrogen stimulates erythropoietin release and thickens the blood","Estrogen causes thrombocytosis by raising thrombopoietin output","Progestin destroys the valves in the deep veins of the lower leg","Estrogen raises hepatic clotting factors and lowers protein S"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","dok":3,"approach":["Estrogen acts on the liver, where most clotting proteins are made.","It increases procoagulant factors and decreases natural anticoagulants such as protein S and antithrombin.","This acquired hypercoagulable state adds to the inherited APC resistance."],"why":"Oral estrogen passes through the liver and raises synthesis of fibrinogen and factors II, VII, VIII, and X while lowering protein S and antithrombin. Lower free protein S also produces an acquired resistance to activated protein C. Combined with factor V Leiden, these two hits on the protein C pathway multiply her risk well beyond either one alone.","whyNot":["Estrogen does not raise erythropoietin. Androgens and hypoxia do, and the higher hematocrit raises viscosity.","Estrogen does not cause thrombocytosis. High platelet counts arise from inflammation, iron deficiency, or myeloproliferative disease.","No hormone destroys venous valves. Valve damage follows a prior DVT and leads to post-thrombotic syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Estrogen and coagulation","Acquired hypercoagulable states"],"comps":["CV-18-I10","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C58","system":"CV","dx":"Varicose veins with chronic venous insufficiency","codes":["CV-18-I11"],"case":"A 56-year-old man comes to the physician because of heaviness and aching of the right leg that worsen through the day and improve with elevation. He has worked as a line cook for 30 years, standing about 10 hours a day. Physical examination shows dilated, tortuous superficial veins along the medial thigh and calf of the right leg, ankle edema, and brown, thickened skin above the medial malleolus. The ankle-brachial index is 1.05 (0.9 to 1.3). Duplex ultrasonography shows reflux in the main superficial vein of the medial leg and at its junction with the deep system, and incompetent valves in the deep veins. The diagnosis is varicose veins with chronic venous insufficiency.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C58-Q1","stem":"His varicosities follow the medial thigh and calf. Which of the following best describes the incompetent superficial vein and its normal site of termination in the deep system?","options":["Small saphenous vein into the femoral vein in the groin","Great saphenous vein into the popliteal vein behind the knee","Great saphenous vein into the femoral vein in the groin","Small saphenous vein into the popliteal vein behind the knee"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["Medial leg and thigh varicosities follow the great saphenous vein.","Trace its course: anterior to the medial malleolus, up the medial leg, behind the medial femoral condyle, and up the medial thigh.","It passes through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata to join the femoral vein."],"why":"The great saphenous vein begins at the medial end of the dorsal venous arch, passes anterior to the medial malleolus, and ascends the medial leg and thigh. It ends by passing through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata, just below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, to join the femoral vein. Valve failure at this saphenofemoral junction is the most common source of reflux in varicose veins.","whyNot":["The small saphenous vein ends in the popliteal fossa, not the groin.","The great saphenous vein passes behind the knee on its medial side but continues up to the groin. It does not end in the popliteal vein.","Correct.","This is the correct course of the small saphenous vein, which runs behind the lateral malleolus and up the back of the calf. Reflux there gives posterior calf varicosities."],"study":{"topics":["Great and small saphenous veins","Saphenous opening"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I11"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q2","stem":"In a healthy adult, foot venous pressure is about 90 mmHg during quiet standing and falls sharply within a few steps of walking. Which of the following best describes this patient's foot venous pressure during walking?","options":["It falls as far as in a healthy leg because his calf muscles still contract","It falls only a little because blood refluxes through incompetent valves","It rises above arterial pressure because contracting muscles block outflow","It falls below normal because his dilated veins act as a large reservoir"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","dok":3,"approach":["Standing still, foot venous pressure equals the weight of the blood column up to the heart, about 90 mmHg.","During walking, each calf contraction empties the veins, and competent valves break the column into short segments, so pressure falls.","With incompetent valves, blood refluxes back between contractions and the column stays intact."],"why":"During exercise, the muscle pump plus competent valves lowers foot venous pressure, which reduces capillary filtration and edema. When valves fail, the veins refill from above almost as fast as they are emptied, so pressure falls only slightly and stays high during walking (ambulatory venous hypertension). This sustained pressure drives the edema, skin changes, and ulcers of chronic venous insufficiency.","whyNot":["Muscle contraction alone does not lower pressure. Without working valves, blood flows back as soon as the muscle relaxes.","Correct.","Venous pressure cannot rise above arterial pressure in a leg with intact arterial inflow, and muscle contraction pushes blood toward the heart.","Dilated veins hold more blood, which keeps pressure high. They do not lower it below normal."],"study":{"topics":["Ambulatory venous pressure","Muscle pump during exercise"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q3","stem":"Two years later, a shallow, irregular, weeping ulcer develops on the same leg. Which of the following best describes the most likely location and mechanism of this ulcer?","options":["Tips of the toes, from reduced arterial inflow and ischemia","Above the medial ankle, from sustained venous hypertension","Under the metatarsal heads, from repeated pressure on numb skin","Back of the heel, from prolonged pressure while lying in bed"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I11","dok":2,"approach":["Link the disease to its pressure problem: high venous pressure in the lower leg.","That pressure is highest and the skin most affected in the gaiter area above the medial malleolus.","Choose the location and mechanism that match venous disease."],"why":"Sustained venous hypertension distends skin capillaries and lets fluid, fibrinogen, and red cells leak into the tissue. Trapped leukocytes, fibrosis (lipodermatosclerosis), and poor nutrient exchange make the skin fragile, and it breaks down into a shallow, irregular ulcer. These ulcers most often occur above the medial malleolus, where the brown, thickened skin already appears.","whyNot":["Painful, punched-out ulcers on the toe tips come from peripheral arterial disease. His ankle-brachial index is normal.","Correct.","Ulcers under the metatarsal heads are neuropathic, as in diabetic neuropathy. Nothing here suggests sensory loss.","Heel pressure injuries occur in bedbound patients. He is ambulatory, and his skin changes point to venous disease."],"study":{"topics":["Venous stasis ulcers","Arterial versus venous versus neuropathic ulcers"],"comps":["CV-18-I11","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C58-Q4","stem":"Which of the following best describes the brown pigment in the skin above his medial malleolus and its source?","options":["Iron from leaked red cells, stored by macrophages as hemosiderin","Lipofuscin from lipid peroxidation in aging dermal fibroblasts","Bilirubin carried in the blood and deposited in the dermis","Homogentisic acid polymer bound to collagen fibers of the dermis"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["High capillary pressure lets red cells leak into the dermis.","Macrophages engulf the red cells and break down hemoglobin.","The released iron is stored as ferritin, which aggregates into brown hemosiderin."],"why":"Macrophages break down the hemoglobin of leaked red cells. Heme oxygenase opens the heme ring, releasing iron and producing biliverdin, which becomes bilirubin and is carried away. The iron stays in the macrophage, bound to ferritin, and excess ferritin aggregates into golden-brown hemosiderin granules that stain blue with Prussian blue. This local iron deposit is what gives stasis skin its brown color.","whyNot":["Correct.","Lipofuscin is a yellow-brown wear-and-tear pigment inside aging cells such as neurons and cardiac myocytes. It does not explain a localized patch above the ankle.","Bilirubin is cleared from tissue and colors skin diffusely yellow in jaundice. It does not form a fixed brown patch over the ankle.","Homogentisic acid deposits occur in alkaptonuria (ochronosis) and darken cartilage, ears, and sclerae."],"study":{"topics":["Heme degradation","Ferritin and hemosiderin","Stasis dermatitis"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C59","system":"CV","dx":"Kaposi sarcoma in advanced HIV infection","codes":["CV-18-J2"],"case":"A 38-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of painless purple plaques and nodules on both lower legs. He has HIV infection and stopped taking antiretroviral therapy 3 years ago. Physical examination also shows a flat violaceous lesion on the hard palate and mild edema of both legs. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 42/µL (500 to 1,500), and HIV RNA is 210,000 copies/mL. Biopsy of a leg nodule is positive on immunostaining for a latency protein of a virus known to cause this tumor. The diagnosis is Kaposi sarcoma in advanced HIV infection (AIDS).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C59-Q1","stem":"Which of the following best describes the virus that causes this patient's tumor?","options":["Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the betaherpesvirus subfamily","Enveloped single-stranded RNA retrovirus that uses reverse transcriptase","Nonenveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the papillomavirus family","Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus in the gammaherpesvirus subfamily"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Kaposi sarcoma is caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV).","All herpesviruses are enveloped double-stranded DNA viruses that establish latency.","HHV-8 belongs with Epstein-Barr virus in the gammaherpesvirus subfamily, which infects lymphocytes and drives cell growth."],"why":"HHV-8 is an enveloped, linear double-stranded DNA gammaherpesvirus. It spreads mainly through saliva and also through sexual contact and transplanted organs. It stays latent in B cells and endothelial cells, where latency proteins such as LANA keep the viral genome and push cell growth. HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease.","whyNot":["Cytomegalovirus (HHV-5) is a betaherpesvirus. In AIDS it causes retinitis and colitis, not Kaposi sarcoma.","This describes HIV itself. HIV causes the immune deficiency that allows Kaposi sarcoma but is not the tumor virus.","Papillomaviruses are nonenveloped dsDNA viruses that cause warts and cervical, anal, and oropharyngeal cancers, not Kaposi sarcoma.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Herpesvirus subfamilies","HHV-8 transmission and latency"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-J2"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q2","stem":"After antiretroviral therapy is restarted, his CD4+ T-lymphocyte count increases over several months and his lesions regress. Restoration of which of the following immune responses best explains the regression?","options":["Complement lysis of infected cells by the attack complex","IgE-triggered mast cell degranulation in the dermis","Virus-specific CD8 T-cell killing aided by CD4 T cells","Neutrophil phagocytosis of the infected spindle cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Latent virus sits inside cells, out of reach of antibodies and complement.","Cells infected with virus show viral peptides on MHC class I, which CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize.","CD8 responses need CD4 helper T cells, so restoring CD4 cells restores control."],"why":"Intracellular viral infection is controlled by cell-mediated immunity. CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize HHV-8 peptides on MHC class I and kill infected cells with perforin and granzymes. CD4 helper T cells make IL-2 and license dendritic cells, which sustains effective CD8 responses. When HIV depletes CD4 cells, HHV-8 escapes this control, and when ART restores them, lesions often regress.","whyNot":["The membrane attack complex is most important against Neisseria and other extracellular bacteria. Latent virus inside cells escapes it.","IgE and mast cells defend against parasites and cause allergy. They play no role in controlling herpesvirus-infected cells.","Correct.","Neutrophils mainly kill extracellular bacteria and fungi. They do not target virus-infected tumor cells."],"study":{"topics":["Cell-mediated immunity to viruses","CD4 help for CD8 T cells","Immune reconstitution"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q3","stem":"Which of the following histologic findings is most likely in the biopsy specimen of the leg nodule?","options":["Spindle cells forming slitlike spaces with extravasated red cells","Thin-walled capillaries lined by a single layer of flat endothelium","Lobular capillaries with neutrophils and silver-staining bacilli","Atypical melanocytes spreading upward through the epidermis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J2","dok":2,"approach":["Kaposi sarcoma is a tumor of endothelial-type spindle cells.","The spindle cells form jagged, slitlike vascular channels.","Red cells leak out of these channels, leaving hemosiderin, which gives the lesion its purple color."],"why":"Nodular Kaposi sarcoma shows sheets of plump spindle cells forming slitlike vascular spaces packed with red cells, along with extravasated red cells and hemosiderin-laden macrophages. The spindle cells carry HHV-8, which is shown by immunostaining for LANA. The leaked blood and slow flow give the lesions their purple to brown color.","whyNot":["Correct.","Well-formed capillaries lined by normal flat endothelium describe a benign capillary hemangioma, which lacks spindle cells and slitlike spaces.","This describes bacillary angiomatosis, caused by Bartonella henselae or quintana in AIDS. It can look like Kaposi sarcoma on the skin but responds to antibiotics.","Upward (pagetoid) spread of atypical melanocytes describes melanoma, a pigmented tumor of melanocytes with no vascular channels."],"study":{"topics":["Kaposi sarcoma histology","Bacillary angiomatosis","Vascular tumors"],"comps":["CV-18-J2","CV-18-J1"]}},{"id":"CV-C59-Q4","stem":"The causative virus encodes a homolog of cellular cyclin D. Which of the following best describes how this viral cyclin promotes proliferation of infected cells?","options":["It stabilizes the Rb and E2F complex to hold cells in G1","It inhibits CDK1 and blocks the cell from entering mitosis","It activates p53, which induces p21 and arrests the cycle","It pairs with CDK6 to phosphorylate Rb and free E2F"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-J2","dok":3,"approach":["Cyclin D normally binds CDK4 or CDK6 in G1.","The cyclin-CDK complex phosphorylates Rb, which releases E2F.","Free E2F turns on S-phase genes, so the cell passes the G1 to S checkpoint."],"why":"In G1, cyclin D partners with CDK4 or CDK6 to phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein (Rb). Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F transcription factors, which turn on genes needed for DNA synthesis. The HHV-8 viral cyclin binds CDK6 and resists normal CDK inhibitors, so it keeps Rb phosphorylated and drives continuous cell cycle entry. Other HHV-8 latency proteins, such as LANA, also block p53 and Rb.","whyNot":["Hypophosphorylated Rb bound to E2F holds cells in G1. A cyclin that promoted this would stop growth, the opposite of an oncogenic effect.","Blocking CDK1 would arrest cells at G2. Cyclin D partners act earlier, at the G1 to S transition.","p53 activation and p21 production stop the cell cycle. Viruses that cause cancer block p53 instead.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Cyclins, CDKs, and Rb","Viral oncogenesis"],"comps":["CV-18-J2","CV-14"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C60","system":"CV","dx":"Secondary lymphedema after axillary lymph node dissection","codes":["CV-10","CV-18-H4"],"case":"A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of gradually increasing swelling and heaviness of the right arm and hand; her rings no longer fit. Ten months ago, she underwent right modified radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection, followed by axillary radiation, for invasive ductal carcinoma. The right forearm circumference is 5 cm greater than the left. The swelling was pitting at first but is now firm and nonpitting, with thickened skin. Venous duplex ultrasonography shows no thrombosis. Serum albumin is 4.1 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0). Lymphoscintigraphy shows delayed lymph transport, dermal backflow, and no axillary node uptake. The diagnosis is secondary lymphedema of the right arm.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C60-Q1","stem":"Before her surgery, lymph from her right hand entered the venous circulation at which of the following sites?","options":["Cisterna chyli, anterior to the first and second lumbar vertebrae","Azygos vein, through lymphatics that follow the intercostal spaces","Junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins","Junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-10","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the path: hand lymphatics, then axillary nodes, then apical axillary nodes.","The right subclavian lymph trunk carries this lymph to the right lymphatic duct or directly to the vein.","It enters the blood at the right venous angle, where the right subclavian and internal jugular veins meet."],"why":"Lymph from the upper limb drains through the axillary nodes to the apical group, then into the subclavian lymph trunk. On the right, this trunk joins the right lymphatic duct or opens on its own at the right venous angle. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. All other lymph reaches the left venous angle through the thoracic duct.","whyNot":["The cisterna chyli collects lymph from the abdomen and lower limbs and is the start of the thoracic duct. It is not a point of entry into the blood.","Intercostal lymphatics drain the chest wall to parasternal and intercostal nodes. The azygos vein does not receive upper limb lymph.","Correct.","The left venous angle receives the thoracic duct, which drains everything below the diaphragm plus the left side of the head, neck, thorax, and left upper limb. It would be correct for her left arm."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic drainage of the upper limb","Right lymphatic duct and thoracic duct"],"comps":["CV-10"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q2","stem":"Her serum albumin concentration is normal, and her veins are patent. Which of the following changes in Starling forces most likely sustains the fluid accumulation in her arm?","options":["Higher capillary permeability from widened endothelial cell gaps","Higher interstitial oncotic pressure from retained tissue protein","Lower plasma oncotic pressure from loss of albumin in the urine","Higher capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous outflow blockage"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Capillaries normally filter a small net amount of fluid and protein that only lymphatics can return.","With lymphatics removed, the protein stays in the interstitium.","Interstitial oncotic pressure rises, which increases net filtration and traps more water."],"why":"Even with normal Starling forces, capillaries filter a little more fluid than they reabsorb, along with small amounts of plasma protein. Lymphatics normally return this fluid and protein to the blood, about 2 to 3 L per day for the whole body. Without lymph drainage, protein accumulates in the tissue, raising interstitial oncotic pressure and drawing still more fluid out of the capillaries. This is why lymphedema fluid is high in protein, unlike most other edemas.","whyNot":["Increased permeability occurs in inflammation, burns, and sepsis. Her edema came on gradually after node removal, with no sign of inflammation.","Correct.","Low plasma oncotic pressure occurs in nephrotic syndrome and liver failure. Her albumin is normal.","Venous obstruction raises capillary hydrostatic pressure, as in DVT. Her duplex scan shows open veins."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Lymph flow","Mechanisms of edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains the change in her swelling from soft and pitting to firm and nonpitting?","options":["Glycosaminoglycans deposited in the dermis from thyroid disease","Clot organizing in the deep veins and stiffening the arm tissue","Hemosiderin deposited from repeated bleeding into soft tissue","Protein-rich fluid driving fibrosis and fat buildup in tissue"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H4","dok":2,"approach":["Early lymphedema is free fluid that can be pushed aside, so it pits.","Long-standing protein-rich fluid causes chronic inflammation that stimulates fibroblasts and fat cells.","Fibrosis and fat do not move under pressure, so the swelling becomes firm and nonpitting."],"why":"Stagnant, protein-rich interstitial fluid triggers chronic low-grade inflammation. Fibroblasts lay down collagen, adipose tissue expands, and the skin thickens. Once fibrosis and fat replace free fluid, pressing on the arm no longer leaves a pit, and elevation alone no longer reduces the swelling. Skin may take on a thick, dimpled texture (peau d'orange), and long-standing cases carry a small risk of angiosarcoma.","whyNot":["Glycosaminoglycan deposition causes nonpitting myxedema in hypothyroidism or pretibial myxedema in Graves disease. Nothing suggests thyroid disease here.","Her duplex scan shows no venous thrombosis.","Hemosiderin causes brown discoloration, as in venous stasis. It does not make tissue firm, and she has no history of bleeding.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic lymphedema","Pitting versus nonpitting edema"],"comps":["CV-18-H4","CV-10"]}},{"id":"CV-C60-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, she has a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and a warm, spreading area of erythema without pus on the swollen forearm. Blood cultures grow beta-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci in chains that are sensitive to bacitracin. Which of the following surface proteins of this organism resists phagocytosis and is the main target of type-specific antibodies?","options":["M protein, which recruits factor H and limits C3b deposition","Internalin, which binds E-cadherin to enter epithelial cells","Coagulase, which clots plasma and wraps the organism in fibrin","Protein A, which binds the Fc portion of IgG and blocks opsonization"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":3,"approach":["Beta-hemolytic cocci in chains that are bacitracin sensitive are Streptococcus pyogenes (group A).","Lymphedema impairs local immune surveillance and is a strong risk factor for recurrent streptococcal cellulitis.","The major group A antiphagocytic surface protein, and the basis for typing, is M protein."],"why":"Streptococcus pyogenes is a common cause of nonpurulent cellulitis, and a lymphedematous limb is especially prone to repeated attacks. M protein extends from the cell wall and binds host factor H, which speeds breakdown of C3b, so less complement opsonin coats the bacterium and phagocytes cannot grab it. Antibodies against a given M type protect against that type, and M protein also shows molecular mimicry with cardiac proteins, the basis of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Correct.","Internalin lets Listeria monocytogenes enter gut epithelial cells. Listeria is a gram-positive rod.","Coagulase identifies Staphylococcus aureus. It is an enzyme that converts fibrinogen to fibrin, not a streptococcal surface protein.","Protein A is the antiphagocytic surface protein of Staphylococcus aureus, which grows in clusters and is catalase positive."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus pyogenes virulence factors","Cellulitis risk in lymphedema"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C07","system":"CV","dx":"Stable angina pectoris from a fixed atherosclerotic coronary narrowing","codes":["CV-18-C1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman who works as a letter carrier comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of pressure-like substernal chest discomfort. The discomfort occurs when she walks uphill carrying her mail bag and resolves within 5 minutes of rest. It has never occurred at rest. She has type 2 diabetes mellitus and smokes cigarettes. Pulse is 76/min and blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. An ECG at rest shows no abnormalities. Serum LDL cholesterol is 168 mg/dL (reference below 100 mg/dL), and HDL cholesterol is 38 mg/dL (reference 50 mg/dL or higher for women). During treadmill exercise testing, at a heart rate of 142/min, she develops her usual discomfort and 2-mm horizontal ST-segment depression in leads V4 to V6 that resolves during recovery. Diagnosis: stable angina pectoris from a fixed atherosclerotic coronary narrowing.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C07-Q1","stem":"During exercise, rising shear stress on healthy coronary endothelium triggers release of nitric oxide, which dilates the artery. In atherosclerotic segments like hers, nitric oxide output is reduced and the artery dilates poorly. Which of the following reactions produces nitric oxide in endothelial cells?","options":["Heme oxygenase breaking down heme into biliverdin and nitric oxide","Arginase splitting L-arginine into ornithine and nitric oxide","Nitric oxide synthase oxidizing L-arginine to L-citrulline","Guanylyl cyclase converting GTP into nitric oxide and cGMP"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["The clue is a gas made by endothelium that relaxes smooth muscle.","Recall the enzyme that makes it and its amino acid substrate.","Separate the enzyme that makes nitric oxide from the enzyme nitric oxide activates."],"why":"Endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) oxidizes L-arginine, using oxygen and NADPH with tetrahydrobiopterin as a cofactor, to form nitric oxide and L-citrulline. Shear stress and calcium-calmodulin activate eNOS. Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, and raises cGMP, which lowers cytosolic calcium and relaxes the vessel. Atherosclerosis and its risk factors reduce nitric oxide availability (endothelial dysfunction), so the narrowed artery cannot dilate well when demand rises.","whyNot":["Heme oxygenase breaks heme into biliverdin, iron, and carbon monoxide. Carbon monoxide is a different gas signal.","Arginase converts L-arginine to ornithine and urea in the urea cycle. It competes with nitric oxide synthase for arginine but makes no nitric oxide.","Correct.","Guanylyl cyclase is the target of nitric oxide in smooth muscle. It converts GTP to cGMP and does not make nitric oxide."],"study":{"topics":["Endothelial nitric oxide synthesis","Endothelial dysfunction in atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q2","stem":"Her high LDL enters the intima of her coronary arteries, where some of it is oxidized. Macrophages take up the oxidized LDL and become lipid-filled foam cells. Which of the following best explains why macrophages continue to take up cholesterol after their cholesterol content is high?","options":["Rising cell cholesterol activates SREBP and raises LDL receptor numbers","Scavenger receptors for oxidized LDL are not suppressed by cholesterol","Oxidized LDL crosses the cell membrane only by passive diffusion","Macrophages lack the enzyme that esterifies cholesterol for storage"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Recall how most cells stop LDL uptake when cholesterol is high.","Identify which receptor macrophages use for oxidized LDL.","Ask whether that receptor is under the same sterol feedback."],"why":"Most cells take up native LDL through the LDL receptor. When cell cholesterol rises, SREBP stays in the endoplasmic reticulum, so transcription of the LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase falls and uptake stops. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36, which lack this feedback. Uptake therefore continues, the cholesterol is esterified by ACAT and stored in droplets, and the cell becomes a foam cell.","whyNot":["High cell cholesterol does the opposite: it keeps SREBP inactive and lowers LDL receptor numbers. This feedback is why the LDL receptor pathway cannot make foam cells.","Correct.","Oxidized LDL is a large particle taken up by receptor-mediated endocytosis. It does not diffuse across the membrane.","Macrophages do esterify cholesterol with ACAT. The stored cholesteryl ester droplets are what give foam cells their foamy look."],"study":{"topics":["Scavenger receptors and foam cell formation","SREBP regulation of the LDL receptor"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q3","stem":"Eight months later she has chest discomfort at rest that lasts about 20 minutes and recurs several times over 2 days. During pain her ECG shows ST depression in V3 to V6. Serial troponin levels stay within the reference range. Which of the following changes in her coronary plaque most likely caused this new pattern?","options":["Complete thrombotic occlusion causing full-thickness necrosis","A fissured plaque cap with a thrombus partly blocking the lumen","Dystrophic calcification of the core with an intact plaque surface","Slow plaque growth that thickens its collagen-rich fibrous cap"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C1","dok":3,"approach":["Rest pain that is new and recurring with normal troponin defines unstable angina.","Unstable angina comes from an acute change in the plaque, not gradual growth.","Normal troponin rules out myocardial necrosis."],"why":"Pain at rest that is new or increasing, with ST depression and no troponin rise, is unstable angina. In most cases a plaque fissures or ruptures, platelets aggregate, and a thrombus forms that narrows the lumen without completely blocking it. Flow can fall at rest, and fragments may embolize downstream. Because troponin stays normal, the ischemia has not caused myocyte necrosis.","whyNot":["Complete occlusion with full-thickness necrosis is an ST-elevation MI. Troponin would rise and the ECG would show ST elevation.","Correct.","Calcification makes a plaque stiffer but does not by itself cause a sudden change in symptoms. An intact surface does not trigger thrombosis.","Slow growth with a thick fibrous cap describes a stable plaque. It causes symptoms only when demand rises, which is her original pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Unstable angina versus stable angina","Acute plaque change"],"comps":["CV-18-C1","CV-18-C3","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C07-Q4","stem":"Her exercise ECG showed ST depression, which reflects ischemia limited to the inner layer of the left ventricular wall. Her heart rate rose to 142/min during the test. Which of the following best explains why the subendocardium is to become ischemic when demand rises past what a narrowed artery can supply?","options":["Its myocytes depend on anaerobic glycolysis even when oxygen is plentiful","It is supplied by end arteries that arise from the coronary sinus","It is fed by blood from the ventricular cavity that is low in oxygen","It is squeezed in systole and perfused mainly during diastole"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-8","dok":3,"approach":["Note where the ischemia is: the inner layer of the left ventricle.","Recall when left ventricular myocardium receives most of its flow.","Link a faster heart rate to a shorter diastole."],"why":"Coronary arteries run on the epicardial surface and send branches inward, so the subendocardium is farthest from the supply. During systole, pressure inside the left ventricular wall is highest in the subendocardium and compresses its vessels, so most of its flow occurs during diastole. Tachycardia shortens diastole more than systole, which cuts subendocardial perfusion just as oxygen demand rises. With a fixed stenosis, this layer becomes ischemic first, producing ST depression on the surface ECG.","whyNot":["Cardiac myocytes rely on aerobic metabolism, mostly fatty acid oxidation, when oxygen is available. Anaerobic glycolysis takes over only during ischemia.","The coronary sinus is a vein that drains the heart into the right atrium. It does not supply any myocardium.","Small veins (Thebesian veins) drain into the chambers, but the ventricular cavity does not supply the myocardium in a normal adult heart. The subendocardium is fed by coronary branches.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Subendocardial perfusion","Effect of heart rate on diastolic time"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-11","CV-18-C1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C08","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy) with heart failure","codes":["CV-18-C2"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of increasing shortness of breath when climbing one flight of stairs, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. He has had no recent chest pain. He had myocardial infarctions at ages 60 and 66 years. Pulse is 88/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows crackles at both lung bases, an S3 gallop, and edema of both ankles. Serum BNP is 820 pg/mL (reference below 100 pg/mL). Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30% (reference 55% to 70%), a thin, akinetic scar in the inferior wall, and a hypokinetic anterior wall. Coronary angiography shows severe stenosis of all three major coronary arteries. Diagnosis: chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy) with heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C08-Q1","stem":"Further imaging shows that his hypokinetic anterior wall has normal thickness and takes up a metabolic tracer, so its myocytes are alive. After bypass surgery restores blood flow to it, the anterior wall contracts normally over the following weeks. Which of the following best describes the anterior wall before surgery?","options":["Hibernating myocardium","Ventricular aneurysm","Stunned myocardium","Transmural collagen scar"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C2","dok":2,"approach":["The muscle is alive but weak while flow is chronically low.","Function returns only after flow is restored.","Match that pattern to the term, and separate it from stunning, which follows a brief ischemic episode."],"why":"Hibernating myocardium is living muscle that lowers its contraction to match a chronically reduced blood supply. It stays weak as long as flow stays low and recovers after revascularization. It is common in chronic ischemic heart disease and is one reason bypass or stenting can improve ejection fraction. Detecting viable tissue with metabolic or perfusion imaging helps predict which segments will recover.","whyNot":["Correct.","A ventricular aneurysm is a thin scar that bulges outward during systole. It has no living muscle to recover.","Stunned myocardium follows a brief episode of severe ischemia after flow has already returned. It recovers on its own over hours to days without revascularization.","A scar is dead tissue replaced by collagen. It is thin, does not take up a metabolic tracer, and does not regain contraction, like his inferior wall."],"study":{"topics":["Hibernating versus stunned myocardium","Chronic ischemic heart disease"],"comps":["CV-18-C2","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q2","stem":"His healthy myocardium gets most of its ATP from oxidizing fatty acids. In his poorly perfused but living segments, a shift toward oxidizing glucose instead would help those cells keep working. Which of the following best explains why this shift helps when oxygen supply is limited?","options":["Glucose oxidation yields more ATP per gram of fuel than fatty acids","Glucose oxidation makes all of its ATP without using mitochondria","Glucose oxidation yields more ATP for each molecule of oxygen used","Glucose oxidation avoids the citric acid cycle and the NADH it forms"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["The limiting factor in ischemia is oxygen, not fuel.","Compare fuels by ATP made per oxygen consumed.","Fatty acids are more reduced and use more oxygen for each ATP they produce."],"why":"Fatty acids are more reduced than glucose, so their oxidation passes a larger share of electrons through FADH2 and uses more oxygen per carbon. As a result, complete oxidation of glucose produces more ATP for each molecule of oxygen consumed. When oxygen delivery is the limit, as in chronically ischemic myocardium, shifting fuel use toward glucose gets more work from the same oxygen. This is the rationale behind metabolic approaches that partly inhibit fatty acid oxidation in angina.","whyNot":["Fatty acids yield more ATP per gram than glucose, which is why fat is the body's main energy store. The advantage of glucose is per oxygen, not per gram.","Only the glycolysis portion of glucose metabolism runs outside the mitochondria and yields 2 ATP. Complete oxidation needs the citric acid cycle and electron transport chain in mitochondria.","Correct.","Pyruvate from glucose enters the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA and generates NADH, just as fatty acids do. Both fuels depend on the cycle."],"study":{"topics":["Myocardial fuel selection","ATP yield per oxygen for glucose and fatty acids"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-C2"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q3","stem":"His left ventricle has dilated over several years. At the same systolic pressure, which of the following best describes the effect of the larger chamber on the oxygen demand of the remaining viable myocardium?","options":["Demand stays the same, because myocardial oxygen use depends only on heart rate","Demand rises, because a larger radius raises the tension in the wall","Demand falls, because stretched fibers contract with greater mechanical efficiency","Demand falls, because each fiber shortens less to eject the same stroke volume"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Wall tension is a major driver of myocardial oxygen use.","Apply the law of Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness.","A dilated, thinned ventricle has a larger radius and a thinner wall."],"why":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A dilated ventricle has a larger radius and often a thinner wall, so each fiber must generate more tension to produce the same pressure. Wall tension, heart rate, and contractility are the main determinants of myocardial oxygen demand. Dilation therefore raises demand in a heart whose supply is already limited, which feeds the decline in chronic ischemic heart disease.","whyNot":["Heart rate is one determinant of oxygen demand, but wall tension and contractility matter as well.","Correct.","Moderate stretch improves force through the Frank-Starling mechanism, but in a chronically dilated heart this benefit is exhausted and wall stress dominates.","A larger chamber does need less fiber shortening per stroke volume, but each fiber must produce more tension. The rise in tension raises oxygen use."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace and wall stress","Determinants of myocardial oxygen demand"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1","CV-18-C2"]}},{"id":"CV-C08-Q4","stem":"A plaque removed from one of his coronary arteries contains many activated CD4+ T cells that secrete interferon-gamma. Which of the following best explains how this cytokine makes a plaque more likely to rupture?","options":["It blocks collagen synthesis by smooth muscle cells in the cap","It shifts macrophages toward a repair state that lays down matrix","It drives B cells to switch to IgE production against plaque lipids","It stimulates fibroblasts to cross-link type I collagen in the cap"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Interferon-gamma is the main Th1 cytokine.","Plaque strength depends on collagen in the fibrous cap, made by smooth muscle cells.","Ask whether the cytokine adds or removes cap collagen."],"why":"Th1 cells in plaques secrete interferon-gamma. It inhibits collagen synthesis by intimal smooth muscle cells and activates macrophages, which release matrix metalloproteinases that break down collagen. Less collagen is made and more is destroyed, so the fibrous cap thins and becomes vulnerable to rupture. Plaque rupture with thrombosis is the usual trigger for acute coronary syndromes in patients like him.","whyNot":["Correct.","Interferon-gamma promotes classically activated (M1) macrophages that are pro-inflammatory. Repair-type (M2) macrophages are driven by IL-4 and IL-13.","Class switching to IgE is driven by IL-4 from Th2 cells, and interferon-gamma inhibits it. This is not how interferon-gamma weakens the cap.","Interferon-gamma reduces collagen production in the cap. Collagen cross-linking would strengthen the cap, the opposite effect."],"study":{"topics":["Inflammation and plaque stability","Th1 cytokines and macrophage activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C09","system":"CV","dx":"Acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction from proximal LAD occlusion","codes":["CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 46-year-old man who is a high school teacher is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the onset of crushing chest pain and diaphoresis that began while he was shoveling snow. He does not smoke. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 50 years. Pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4. Serum troponin I is 4.8 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). LDL cholesterol is 118 mg/dL (reference below 100 mg/dL), and lipoprotein(a) is 210 nmol/L (reference below 75 nmol/L). Coronary angiography shows a thrombus occluding the proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery, which is opened with a stent 90 minutes after the onset of pain. Diagnosis: acute anterior wall ST-elevation myocardial infarction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C09-Q1","stem":"On day 2, his ECG shows a new right bundle branch block. Which of the following vessels most likely supplied the injured conduction tissue?","options":["AV nodal branch of the right coronary artery","Obtuse marginal branches of the circumflex artery","Sinoatrial nodal branch of the right coronary artery","Septal perforating branches of the LAD artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","dok":3,"approach":["The right bundle branch runs down the interventricular septum.","The anterior two-thirds of the septum is supplied by septal branches of the LAD.","His occluded artery is the LAD, which fits."],"why":"The LAD runs in the anterior interventricular groove and gives off septal perforating branches that supply the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. The right bundle branch and the anterior fascicle of the left bundle branch run through this territory and depend mainly on these branches. A new bundle branch block in anterior MI signals a large septal infarct and a risk of progression to complete heart block below the AV node.","whyNot":["The AV nodal artery supplies the AV node and the start of the bundle of His, not the length of the right bundle branch. It is usually involved in inferior MI from right coronary occlusion.","Obtuse marginal branches supply the lateral wall of the left ventricle. They do not reach the septum.","The sinoatrial nodal artery supplies the SA node in the right atrium. Its loss causes sinus node dysfunction, not bundle branch block.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Septal blood supply and the bundle branches","Conduction blocks in anterior MI"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q2","stem":"Flow was restored about 90 minutes after occlusion. Which of the following microscopic findings is most characteristic of irreversibly injured myocytes that are reperfused?","options":["Wavy fibers at the infarct border stretched by nearby beating cells","Myocyte nuclei dividing to replace the cells that were lost","Dense eosinophilic cross bands of hypercontracted sarcomeres","Granulation tissue with new capillaries and loose collagen"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","dok":3,"approach":["The key clue is reperfusion of tissue that is already dying.","Restored flow brings a surge of calcium into cells with damaged membranes.","Calcium overload causes the myofibrils to hypercontract."],"why":"When blood flow returns to myocytes that are already irreversibly injured, calcium floods in through damaged membranes. The sarcomeres contract intensely and lock, forming dense, eosinophilic transverse bands called contraction bands. This contraction band necrosis is typical of reperfused infarcts, which may also be hemorrhagic because injured vessels leak when flow returns. Reperfusion still salvages myocytes that were only reversibly injured, so it limits infarct size overall.","whyNot":["Wavy fibers appear at the border of an early infarct as noncontracting dead fibers are stretched. They can appear within hours with or without reperfusion, so they do not mark reperfusion.","Adult cardiac myocytes are permanent cells and do not divide to replace lost tissue in any meaningful amount. Infarcts heal by scar.","Correct.","Granulation tissue appears at the infarct margins at about 7 to 14 days. It is far too late for the first hours after reperfusion."],"study":{"topics":["Contraction band necrosis and reperfusion injury","Infarct histology"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-2"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q3","stem":"His lipoprotein(a) is high. Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle in which apolipoprotein B-100 is linked to apolipoprotein(a). In addition to carrying cholesterol into the artery wall, which of the following best explains how apolipoprotein(a) promotes coronary thrombosis?","options":["It activates factor X directly and speeds thrombin formation","It inhibits antithrombin, leaving thrombin unopposed","It binds von Willebrand factor and increases platelet adhesion","It resembles plasminogen and interferes with fibrinolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the structure of apolipoprotein(a).","Its repeated kringle domains are similar to those in plasminogen.","A plasminogen look-alike competes with plasminogen and slows clot breakdown."],"why":"Apolipoprotein(a) contains many kringle domains that closely resemble those of plasminogen. It competes with plasminogen for binding sites on fibrin and cells, so less plasmin is formed and clots are broken down more slowly. Lp(a) level is mostly set by inheritance at the LPA gene and changes little with diet. A high level is an independent risk factor for premature coronary disease, as in this patient with only modestly raised LDL.","whyNot":["Factor X is activated by the tissue factor and intrinsic tenase complexes. Apolipoprotein(a) is not a known activator.","Lp(a) does not inhibit antithrombin. Low antithrombin activity comes from inherited deficiency, liver disease, or protein loss.","Von Willebrand factor links platelets to exposed collagen through glycoprotein Ib. That is not the mechanism of Lp(a).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lipoprotein(a) structure and function","Fibrinolysis and plasminogen"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-14","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C09-Q4","stem":"On day 4 he develops new chest pain with fresh ST elevation in V1 to V4. His troponin I is still elevated from the first infarct. Which of the following serum markers is most useful for detecting a new infarct at this point?","options":["Lactate dehydrogenase","Aspartate aminotransferase","Creatine kinase MB","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","dok":3,"approach":["Troponin stays high for about 7 to 10 days, so it cannot easily show a second event on day 4.","Look for a cardiac marker that returns to normal within a few days.","A fresh rise in that marker points to new necrosis."],"why":"Troponin I and T begin to rise about 2 to 4 hours after infarction and stay elevated for about 7 to 10 days. CK-MB rises over a similar early period, peaks at about 24 to 48 hours, and returns to normal within about 72 hours. By day 4 his CK-MB should be back near baseline, so a new rise strongly suggests reinfarction. This timing difference is the classic reason CK-MB is used to detect early reinfarction. Current practice can also detect reinfarction by a further rise in troponin of about 20% or more, but among these markers CK-MB is the one that has already returned to baseline.","whyNot":["Lactate dehydrogenase rises later than CK-MB and stays high for a week or more. It is also nonspecific, so it cannot separate a new infarct from the first.","AST is found in liver and muscle as well as heart and is no longer used to diagnose MI. It is too nonspecific to confirm reinfarction.","Correct.","BNP rises with ventricular wall stress and heart failure. It does not measure myocyte necrosis."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac biomarker timing","Diagnosis of reinfarction"],"comps":["CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C10","system":"CV","dx":"Inferior wall MI with right ventricular involvement from right coronary artery occlusion, with bradycardia and second-degree AV block","codes":["CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the onset of heavy pressure in her lower chest and upper abdomen with nausea and diaphoresis. Pulse is 44/min and blood pressure is 84/56 mm Hg. Jugular venous pressure is increased, and the lungs are clear to auscultation. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF and in the right-sided lead V4R, with ST-segment depression in leads I and aVL. A rhythm strip shows progressive lengthening of the PR interval until a P wave is not followed by a QRS complex. Serum troponin I is 2.6 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). Coronary angiography shows occlusion of a dominant right coronary artery (RCA) proximal to its right ventricular branches. Diagnosis: inferior wall MI with right ventricular involvement from RCA occlusion, with bradycardia and second-degree AV block.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C10-Q1","stem":"Which of the following anatomic features best explains why her coronary occlusion has disturbed AV conduction?","options":["The AV nodal artery usually arises from the RCA near the crux","The AV node is supplied by the first diagonal branch of the LAD","The AV nodal artery comes from the circumflex in right-dominant hearts","The AV node lies in the free wall of the right ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","dok":2,"approach":["She has an RCA occlusion and a conduction block at the AV node.","Recall where the AV nodal artery arises.","Link the artery to the node it supplies."],"why":"The AV node lies in the lower part of the interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the opening of the coronary sinus. Its artery usually arises from the RCA near the crux of the heart, the point where the posterior interventricular groove meets the atrioventricular groove. In most people the RCA is dominant, giving rise to the posterior descending artery and the AV nodal artery. RCA occlusion can therefore cause AV block along with inferior wall infarction.","whyNot":["Correct.","Diagonal branches of the LAD supply the anterolateral left ventricle. They do not supply the AV node.","In right-dominant hearts, the RCA gives the AV nodal artery. The circumflex supplies it mainly in left-dominant hearts.","The AV node lies in the lower interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the coronary sinus opening. It is not in the ventricular wall."],"study":{"topics":["AV nodal blood supply","Coronary dominance"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q2","stem":"Her rhythm strip shows Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block. This block often improves with atropine after inferior myocardial infarction. Which of the following best describes the site of the conduction delay and the reason for this response?","options":["In the SA node, where vagal tone delays each atrial impulse","In the AV node, where vagal tone and ischemia slow conduction","In the bundle branches, which are supplied by the LAD","In the His-Purkinje system, which has dense vagal innervation"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","dok":3,"approach":["A PR interval that lengthens before a dropped beat places the block in the AV node.","The AV node is strongly influenced by the vagus nerve.","Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors and removes vagal slowing."],"why":"In Mobitz type I block, conduction through the AV node slows a little more with each beat until one impulse fails to pass, and the cycle restarts. The AV node receives dense vagal innervation, and inferior MI often increases vagal tone through reflexes from the inferior wall, along with ischemia of the node itself. Acetylcholine acting on M2 receptors slows nodal conduction, so atropine often improves the block. Block at this level is usually transient after inferior MI.","whyNot":["SA node slowing causes sinus bradycardia or sinus pauses in which the P wave itself is missing. Here P waves appear on time but fail to conduct.","Correct.","Bundle branch block widens the QRS but does not cause a progressively lengthening PR interval. The bundle branches are supplied mainly by the LAD and are usually affected in anterior MI.","The His-Purkinje system has little vagal innervation. Block there causes Mobitz type II or complete block with a fixed PR interval and responds poorly to atropine."],"study":{"topics":["Mobitz type I versus type II block","Autonomic control of the AV node"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q3","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains her low blood pressure with raised jugular venous pressure and clear lungs?","options":["Cytokine-driven vasodilation with a low systemic vascular resistance","Loss of over 40% of left ventricular mass with pulmonary congestion","Rupture of a papillary muscle with acute mitral regurgitation","Right ventricular failure that underfills the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","dok":3,"approach":["Combine the findings: hypotension, high venous pressure, and clear lungs.","High venous pressure with clear lungs points to a problem on the right side.","ST elevation in V4R confirms right ventricular infarction."],"why":"Proximal RCA occlusion can infarct the right ventricle as well as the inferior left ventricle. The weak right ventricle cannot pump venous return forward, so blood backs up in the systemic veins (raised jugular venous pressure) while the left ventricle receives too little preload. Left ventricular output and blood pressure fall, yet the lungs stay clear because the left side is not congested. These patients depend on preload, so lowering it with venodilators can worsen the hypotension.","whyNot":["Vasodilatory shock, as in sepsis, has a low or normal venous pressure. Her raised jugular venous pressure points to pump failure on the right.","Massive left ventricular loss causes cardiogenic shock with pulmonary congestion and crackles. Her lungs are clear.","Acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture causes sudden pulmonary edema, so the lungs would not be clear. It also usually occurs a few days after MI, not in the first hours.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Right ventricular infarction","Preload dependence"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-B2","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C10-Q4","stem":"Within the first day after infarction, neutrophils begin to enter her necrotic myocardium. Which of the following most likely initiates this inflammatory response?","options":["Antibodies to cardiac myosin forming immune complexes in the wall","Necrotic myocyte contents activating pattern recognition receptors","IgE on mast cells binding cardiac proteins released from the infarct","Cytotoxic T cells recognizing myocyte antigens shown on MHC class I"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Necrosis releases cell contents that are normally hidden inside cells.","These molecules are danger signals recognized by innate immune receptors.","Innate immunity acts within hours, faster than any antibody or T-cell response."],"why":"Necrotic myocytes release damage-associated molecular patterns such as HMGB1, ATP, heat shock proteins, and mitochondrial DNA. These bind pattern recognition receptors, including Toll-like receptors and the NLRP3 inflammasome, on resident macrophages and endothelial cells. The cells release IL-1, TNF, and chemokines and display adhesion molecules, and complement is also activated. Neutrophils are recruited within hours and peak at about 1 to 3 days.","whyNot":["Antibodies against heart antigens take weeks to form. They are linked to later immune complications such as post-MI (Dressler) pericarditis, not to early neutrophil entry.","Correct.","IgE and mast cells drive immediate (type I) hypersensitivity to allergens. They do not start the response to necrotic tissue.","A CD8 T-cell response needs priming and takes days. It does not explain neutrophil entry within hours."],"study":{"topics":["Damage-associated molecular patterns","Acute inflammation after infarction"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-2","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C11","system":"CV","dx":"Post-myocardial infarction (Dressler) syndrome","codes":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 4-day history of sharp chest pain that worsens with inspiration and when lying supine and improves when she sits up and leans forward. Three weeks ago, she had an anterior wall myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. A three-component friction rub is heard at the left sternal border. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 68 mm/h (reference below 30 mm/h), and leukocyte count is 11,800/µL (reference 4,500 to 11,000/µL). Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion and no new wall-motion abnormality. Diagnosis: post-myocardial infarction (Dressler) syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C11-Q1","stem":"Some patients develop pericarditis 1 to 3 days after a transmural infarct, but hers began about 3 weeks later. Which mechanism best explains her pericarditis?","options":["An immune response to myocardial antigens exposed by the infarct","Fibrinous inflammation spreading from the necrotic wall to the overlying pericardium","Coxsackievirus B infection picked up during her hospital stay","Bacterial seeding of the pericardium during the stent procedure"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Use the timing: weeks after MI, not days.","A delay of weeks fits an adaptive immune response.","Necrosis exposes heart proteins the immune system does not normally see."],"why":"Dressler syndrome usually appears a few weeks after MI or heart surgery. Necrosis releases myocardial antigens, the immune system forms antibodies against them, and immune complexes are thought to deposit on the pericardium and pleura. The result is fever, pericarditis, sometimes pleuritis, and high inflammatory markers. The delay reflects the time needed for an adaptive immune response.","whyNot":["Correct.","Early post-MI pericarditis comes from inflammation over a transmural infarct and appears in the first few days. Her onset at 3 weeks does not fit.","Coxsackievirus B is a common cause of viral pericarditis and myocarditis. Nothing here links a new viral infection to her infarct, and the timing fits Dressler syndrome.","Purulent bacterial pericarditis is rare after a stent and causes a much sicker patient with a purulent effusion. Her small effusion and recent MI fit an immune cause."],"study":{"topics":["Dressler syndrome","Early versus late pericarditis after MI"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-F3","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q2","stem":"If the infarcted region of her anterior wall were examined now, 3 weeks after the infarct, which of the following microscopic findings would most likely be seen?","options":["Macrophages engulfing dead myocytes at the infarct edge","Coagulation necrosis with a dense band of neutrophils","Maturing granulation tissue with increasing collagen","Dense collagen scar that contains very few cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","dok":2,"approach":["Place 3 weeks on the healing timeline.","Neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days and macrophages at about 3 to 7 days.","Granulation tissue forms at about 7 to 14 days, and a dense scar forms by about 2 months."],"why":"Infarcts heal in a predictable order. Neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days, macrophages clear dead cells at about 3 to 7 days, and granulation tissue with new capillaries forms at about 7 to 14 days. From about 2 to 8 weeks, collagen steadily increases and cellularity falls. By about 2 months the area is a dense collagen scar, so at 3 weeks she has maturing granulation tissue.","whyNot":["Macrophage removal of dead myocytes peaks at about 3 to 7 days. This is also when the wall is softest and most likely to rupture.","Coagulation necrosis with a neutrophil band is the picture at about 1 to 3 days.","Correct.","A dense, cell-poor collagen scar is expected by about 2 months. At 3 weeks healing is still in progress."],"study":{"topics":["Histologic timeline of myocardial infarction","Repair by connective tissue"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-2"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q3","stem":"She also has pain along the ridge of her left trapezius muscle. Which of the following nerves carries pain from the parietal pericardium that is referred to this area?","options":["Second intercostal nerve","Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Trapezius ridge pain is referred to the C3 to C5 dermatomes.","Find the nerve with C3 to C5 roots that supplies the pericardium.","Separate pericardial pain from pain arising in the heart muscle itself."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium and the parietal layer of serous pericardium receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves (C3 to C5). Pain from inflamed parietal pericardium is referred to skin supplied by the same spinal segments, including the trapezius ridge and shoulder. Trapezius ridge pain is a classic clue to pericarditis. Myocardial ischemic pain travels with sympathetic fibers instead.","whyNot":["Intercostal nerves supply the chest wall and parietal pleura of the ribs, not the parietal pericardium. Their pain stays over the chest wall.","The vagus nerve carries some visceral sensation from the heart and lungs, mainly for reflexes, and does not cause referred pain to the trapezius.","Correct.","The cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers and the visceral pain fibers that run with them from the heart to spinal segments about T1 to T4. That ischemic pain is referred to the chest and inner arm, not the trapezius."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the pericardium","Referred pain from the thorax"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C11-Q4","stem":"A new myocardial infarction must be excluded. Which of the following ECG findings most strongly supports pericarditis rather than a new ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","options":["ST elevation across many territories with PR-segment depression","Horizontal ST depression in V4 to V6 that resolves with rest","ST elevation in V1 to V4 with reciprocal depression in II, III, aVF","Convex ST elevation in II, III, and aVF with new Q waves there"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F3","dok":3,"approach":["Pericarditis inflames the whole epicardial surface, not one artery's territory.","An MI produces ST elevation in one territory, often with reciprocal depression.","Inflamed atrial epicardium shifts the PR segment."],"why":"Pericarditis injures the epicardium over most of the heart, so concave ST elevation appears in leads from several coronary territories, usually without reciprocal ST depression except in aVR. Inflammation of the atrial epicardium creates an atrial injury current that depresses the PR segment. An ST-elevation MI follows the territory of one artery, often shows reciprocal depression, and may develop new Q waves. These differences let clinicians separate the two at the bedside.","whyNot":["Correct.","Horizontal ST depression that resolves with rest fits exercise-induced subendocardial ischemia, as in stable angina.","ST elevation in V1 to V4 with reciprocal inferior depression follows the LAD territory. That pattern points to a new anterior infarct.","Convex ST elevation limited to the inferior leads with new Q waves points to an RCA or circumflex infarct."],"study":{"topics":["ECG in pericarditis versus STEMI","Atrial injury current and PR depression"],"comps":["CV-18-F3","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C12","system":"CV","dx":"Cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure after a large anterior myocardial infarction","codes":["CV-18-H5","CV-18-C3"],"case":"A 74-year-old man in the coronary care unit is evaluated 14 hours after a large anterior wall myocardial infarction. He is confused, and his skin is cool, pale, and clammy. Pulse is 122/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 78/52 mm Hg. Crackles are heard over both lung fields. Urine output has been 10 mL/h for 4 hours. Serum lactate is 6.1 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L), and creatinine has increased from 1.0 to 2.3 mg/dL (reference 0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL). A pulmonary artery catheter shows a cardiac index of 1.6 L/min/m² (reference 2.5 to 4.0 L/min/m²), a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 28 mm Hg (reference 6 to 12 mm Hg), and an increased systemic vascular resistance. Diagnosis: cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C12-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his cool, pale skin and high systemic vascular resistance?","options":["Nitric oxide released from activated endothelium in the skin","More carotid baroreceptor firing, which stimulates the vagus","Direct constriction of skin vessels by high lactate levels","Less baroreceptor firing, which raises sympathetic tone"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Low arterial pressure reduces stretch on the carotid sinus and aortic arch.","Less baroreceptor firing removes inhibition of the sympathetic outflow.","Sympathetic alpha-1 stimulation constricts skin arterioles."],"why":"When blood pressure falls, the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors fire less. The medullary centers respond by raising sympathetic output and lowering vagal output. Norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors constricts arterioles in the skin, kidneys, and gut, which raises systemic vascular resistance and makes the skin cool and pale, while sympathetic stimulation of sweat glands makes it clammy. The response protects brain and heart flow but adds afterload to a failing ventricle.","whyNot":["Nitric oxide dilates vessels. Widespread nitric oxide release causes the warm skin and low resistance of septic shock.","Increased baroreceptor firing happens when pressure rises, and it slows the heart and dilates vessels. His pressure is low.","Lactate and acidosis tend to dilate local vessels. They cannot explain the high systemic resistance.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor reflex","Hemodynamic profiles of shock"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q2","stem":"His lactate is 6.1 mmol/L. In poorly perfused tissues, which of the following best explains why converting pyruvate to lactate allow ATP production to continue?","options":["It reoxidizes FADH2 so the electron transport chain can work","It feeds acetyl-CoA into the citric acid cycle without oxygen","It makes ATP directly by substrate-level phosphorylation","It restores the NAD+ that glycolysis needs to continue"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Without oxygen, the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Lactate dehydrogenase turns NADH back into NAD+ as it reduces pyruvate."],"why":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase needs NAD+ to keep glycolysis running. Normally the NADH it forms is reoxidized by the mitochondria, but that requires oxygen. In hypoperfused tissue, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and regenerates NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making its 2 ATP per glucose. Lactate builds up in the blood, and a high level is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion and a poor prognosis in shock.","whyNot":["FADH2 is reoxidized only by the electron transport chain, which needs oxygen. Lactate formation does not involve FAD.","Pyruvate enters the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA through pyruvate dehydrogenase. Making lactate diverts pyruvate away from that pathway.","The lactate dehydrogenase reaction makes no ATP. The ATP comes from earlier glycolytic steps (phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis and lactate","NAD+ regeneration"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q3","stem":"His creatinine continues to rise. Urine dipstick is negative for blood, and urine microscopy shows many muddy brown granular casts. Which process is most likely occurring in his kidneys?","options":["Prerenal azotemia with intact tubules that reabsorb sodium avidly","Ischemic injury of tubular epithelium from low renal blood flow","Tubules blocked by myoglobin released from injured skeletal muscle","Immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H5","dok":3,"approach":["Shock lowers renal perfusion.","Muddy brown granular casts are made of shed tubular epithelial cells.","A dipstick negative for blood argues against myoglobin."],"why":"In shock, low cardiac output and intense renal vasoconstriction reduce blood flow to the kidneys. Tubular epithelial cells, especially in the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb, have high energy needs and are injured first. Injured cells detach and form muddy brown granular casts, tubules become blocked, and glomerular filtration falls. This acute tubular necrosis is one of the main forms of organ damage in progressive shock.","whyNot":["Prerenal azotemia shows bland urine or hyaline casts. Muddy brown granular casts mean the tubular cells themselves are injured.","Correct.","Myoglobin causes a dipstick that is positive for blood without red cells on microscopy. His dipstick is negative.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis causes blood and red cell casts in the urine. His dipstick is negative for blood."],"study":{"topics":["Acute tubular necrosis in shock","Stages of shock"],"comps":["CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C12-Q4","stem":"On day 2, his C-reactive protein is 160 mg/L (reference below 10 mg/L). Which of the following cytokines, released by macrophages in the infarct, is the main stimulus for hepatocytes to make this protein?","options":["Interleukin-6","Interleukin-2","Interleukin-10","Interleukin-4"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["C-reactive protein is an acute-phase protein made by the liver.","Identify the cytokine that most strongly drives acute-phase protein synthesis.","Rule out anti-inflammatory and lymphocyte growth cytokines."],"why":"C-reactive protein is an acute-phase reactant made by hepatocytes. Tissue injury, such as a large infarct, activates macrophages to release IL-1, TNF, and especially IL-6. IL-6 is the main stimulus for hepatocytes to make C-reactive protein and other acute-phase proteins such as fibrinogen and serum amyloid A. C-reactive protein binds damaged cells and microbes and activates complement.","whyNot":["Correct.","IL-2 is a T-cell growth factor made mainly by activated T cells. It does not drive the acute-phase response.","IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophages. It does not drive acute-phase protein production.","IL-4 drives Th2 responses and IgE class switching. It does not stimulate C-reactive protein production."],"study":{"topics":["Acute-phase response","IL-6 and C-reactive protein"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C13","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic aortic regurgitation from aortic root dilation","codes":["CV-18-D1"],"case":"A 34-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue and a pounding sensation in his chest when he lies on his left side. He is 196 cm tall, and his arm span exceeds his height. Ophthalmologic examination 1 year ago showed bilateral upward subluxation of the lenses. His father died suddenly of an aortic dissection at age 41 years. Blood pressure is 152/48 mm Hg, and peripheral pulses are bounding. A high-pitched, early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard best with the patient sitting up and leaning forward at end-expiration. Echocardiography shows an aortic root diameter of 5.1 cm (reference under about 4.0 cm), moderate to severe aortic regurgitation, and a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 58% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: chronic aortic regurgitation due to aortic root dilation.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C13-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing in this patient is most likely to show a mutation in the gene encoding which of the following proteins?","options":["Type III collagen, a fibrillar collagen of vessel walls","Cystathionine beta-synthase, an enzyme of homocysteine use","Fibrillin-1, a glycoprotein of elastic fiber microfibrils","Elastin, the core protein of arterial elastic lamellae"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Group the clues: tall build, long arm span, upward lens displacement, aortic root dilation, father with dissection.","Recognize Marfan syndrome, autosomal dominant.","Recall the gene: FBN1 on chromosome 15, which encodes fibrillin-1."],"why":"This is Marfan syndrome. FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, which forms the microfibril scaffold that elastic fibers are built on. Defective fibrillin-1 weakens the aortic media (cystic medial degeneration) and the suspensory ligaments of the lens. Fibrillin-1 also normally holds TGF-beta in an inactive form, so loss of it increases TGF-beta signaling, which adds to aortic root enlargement.","whyNot":["Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1) causes arterial and bowel rupture with thin, translucent skin. It does not cause tall stature or lens dislocation.","Homocystinuria (CBS deficiency) can give a tall build and lens dislocation, but the lenses usually shift downward, inheritance is autosomal recessive, and the main vascular problem is thrombosis.","Correct.","Elastin (ELN) loss in Williams syndrome causes supravalvular aortic stenosis, not root dilation with lens displacement."],"study":{"topics":["Marfan syndrome genetics","Elastic fiber structure"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q2","stem":"Which of the following changes in his left ventricular pressure-volume loop, compared with a normal loop, is most likely in this chronic, compensated state?","options":["Normal end-diastolic volume and smaller stroke volume, with a much higher peak systolic pressure","Smaller end-diastolic volume and smaller stroke volume, with a normal peak pressure","Larger end-diastolic volume and stroke volume, with a higher peak systolic pressure","Larger end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes, with a smaller stroke volume"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: wide pulse pressure and early diastolic decrescendo murmur mean aortic regurgitation.","Ask which phase is lost: the leaky aortic valve lets blood enter the ventricle from the aorta as soon as ejection ends.","Add the volume load: regurgitant blood plus normal atrial filling raises end-diastolic volume and total stroke volume."],"why":"In aortic regurgitation, blood flows back into the left ventricle from the aorta during diastole, so the ventricle starts filling the moment ejection stops. Because the ventricle is never a closed chamber at that point, there is no true isovolumetric relaxation. The regurgitant volume adds to normal filling from the atrium, so end-diastolic volume rises, and the Frank-Starling mechanism produces a large total stroke volume. That large stroke volume raises systolic pressure, and the back-leak lowers diastolic pressure, which explains the 152/48 reading.","whyNot":["This is aortic stenosis: the ventricle must generate a much higher systolic pressure to push blood through a narrow valve, while stroke volume does not rise.","This is mitral stenosis: a narrow mitral valve limits filling, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume both fall.","Correct.","This is systolic heart failure, as in dilated cardiomyopathy. His ejection fraction of 58% shows that contraction is still preserved."],"study":{"topics":["Pressure-volume loops in valve disease","Frank-Starling mechanism"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q3","stem":"His murmur is produced by blood flowing across one valve during part of the cardiac cycle. Which of the following best describes when this flow begins?","options":["At S1, when left ventricular pressure rises above left atrial pressure","At A2, when left ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure","After an opening snap, when left atrial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure","After an ejection click, when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","dok":2,"approach":["Place the murmur in the cycle: early diastolic and decrescendo.","Name the valve: the aortic valve should be closed during diastole.","Link it to pressures: once ejection ends, aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure, and a leaky aortic valve lets blood flow back."],"why":"At the end of ejection, left ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure and the aortic valve closes, producing A2. In aortic regurgitation the cusps do not seal, so blood flows back from the high-pressure aorta into the relaxing ventricle starting at that moment. The gradient is largest early in diastole and narrows as the ventricle fills and aortic pressure falls, which is why the murmur is decrescendo.","whyNot":["This describes mitral regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur that starts with S1 and is heard best at the apex.","Correct.","This describes mitral stenosis, a low-pitched diastolic rumble that starts after the opening snap of a stiff mitral valve.","This describes forward flow through a stenotic aortic valve (often a bicuspid valve with an ejection click), which produces a systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur."],"study":{"topics":["Timing of murmurs in the cardiac cycle","Wiggers diagram"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C13-Q4","stem":"His ventricle has handled the extra volume for years with a near-normal ejection fraction. Which of the following structural adaptations of the left ventricle best explains this?","options":["Eccentric hypertrophy, with new sarcomeres added in series","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with myocyte disarray","Interstitial amyloid deposition with a stiff, thick wall","Concentric hypertrophy, with new sarcomeres added in parallel"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D1","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the load: aortic regurgitation causes volume overload.","Match load to remodeling: volume overload leads to eccentric hypertrophy, pressure overload leads to concentric hypertrophy.","Link structure to function: a larger cavity can eject a larger stroke volume at the same ejection fraction."],"why":"Chronic aortic regurgitation is a volume overload. Myocytes respond by adding sarcomeres in series, so the cells lengthen and the chamber enlarges, with a modest increase in wall thickness. The larger chamber holds the extra volume at a tolerable filling pressure and ejects a large stroke volume, so forward output is maintained for years. Over time, rising wall stress and fibrosis lead to systolic dysfunction and heart failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a genetic sarcomere disease, not an adaptation to valve leakage.","Amyloid infiltration causes restrictive cardiomyopathy with a stiff, non-dilated ventricle.","Concentric hypertrophy is the response to pressure overload, as in aortic stenosis or systemic hypertension. Wall thickness rises while the cavity stays normal or small."],"study":{"topics":["Ventricular remodeling: volume versus pressure overload"],"comps":["CV-18-D1","CV-18-B1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C14","system":"CV","dx":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis","codes":["CV-18-D2"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman, gravida 1, para 0, at 26 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of worsening shortness of breath, two-pillow orthopnea, and cough with blood-streaked sputum. She grew up in a rural area with limited access to medical care. At age 10 years, she had an illness with fever and painful, swollen knees and ankles. Pulse is 108/min and regular. Cardiac examination shows a loud S1, an opening snap after S2, and a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex. Echocardiography shows thickened mitral leaflets with commissural fusion, a mitral valve area of 1.1 cm2 (reference 4 to 6 cm2), and an enlarged left atrium. Diagnosis: rheumatic mitral stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C14-Q1","stem":"The valve damage began years ago after a streptococcal pharyngitis. Which immune mechanism best explains how that infection injured her heart?","options":["Type I hypersensitivity, with IgE-armed mast cells degranulating in the valve","Type II hypersensitivity, with antibodies that cross-react with heart proteins","Type III hypersensitivity, with immune complexes trapped in valve capillaries","Persistent bacterial infection of the valve leaflets with local complement activation"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Link the history: fever and migrating large-joint arthritis in childhood suggest acute rheumatic fever.","Recall the cause: group A streptococcal pharyngitis followed by an immune reaction, not direct infection.","Classify it: antibodies against streptococcal antigens (M protein) bind similar host proteins, which is molecular mimicry and type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies made against streptococcal antigens, especially M protein, cross-react with host proteins in the heart such as cardiac myosin and valve components. This is molecular mimicry, classified as type II hypersensitivity, and T cells reacting to the same shared antigens add to the damage. Repeated inflammation heals by fibrosis, producing thickened leaflets with commissural fusion years later.","whyNot":["Type I reactions cause allergy and anaphylaxis within minutes of antigen exposure. They do not cause delayed valve scarring.","Correct.","Immune complex deposition is the mechanism of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which follows the same organism but affects the kidney.","Rheumatic fever is a sterile, immune-mediated disease. Live bacteria growing on a valve describes infective endocarditis."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Hypersensitivity types"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q2","stem":"Before pregnancy, her resting pulse was 72/min and she had no symptoms. It is now 108/min. Which of the following best explains how this change in heart rate produced her symptoms?","options":["A faster rate increases backflow from the ventricle into the left atrium in systole","A faster rate weakens ventricular contraction and lowers forward stroke volume","A shorter systole raises aortic pressure and the afterload on the left ventricle","A shorter diastole raises the left atrial pressure needed to fill the ventricle"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the lesion: a narrow mitral valve limits flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle.","Recall that tachycardia shortens diastole much more than systole.","Apply it: the same filling volume must cross the valve in less time, so the pressure gradient and left atrial pressure rise and back up into the lungs."],"why":"Flow across a stenotic mitral valve happens only in diastole and depends on the pressure gradient and the time available. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. To fill the ventricle in less time, the gradient across the valve must rise, so left atrial pressure climbs. That pressure passes back to the pulmonary veins and capillaries, causing dyspnea, orthopnea, and blood-streaked sputum. This is why pregnancy, fever, exercise, and atrial fibrillation with a fast rate often unmask mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["Systolic backflow is mitral regurgitation. In pure mitral stenosis the valve closes, so there is no systolic leak to increase.","Healthy myocardium contracts more strongly at faster rates (the force-frequency effect), and in mitral stenosis the left ventricle itself is normal. The problem is filling.","Heart rate does not raise afterload in this way, and afterload is not the limiting step in mitral stenosis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Diastolic filling time and heart rate","Hemodynamics of mitral stenosis"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q3","stem":"Months later she develops atrial fibrillation, and transesophageal echocardiography shows a thrombus in the left atrial appendage. This part of the left atrium develops from which embryonic structure?","options":["Septum primum","Common pulmonary vein","Primitive atrium","Right horn of the sinus venosus"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the typical site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation: the left atrial appendage.","Recall the two embryonic sources of the adult left atrium.","Match them: trabeculated appendage from the primitive atrium, smooth-walled part from the pulmonary veins."],"why":"The adult left atrium has two embryonic sources. The smooth-walled main portion forms when the common pulmonary vein and its branches are absorbed into the atrial wall. The original primitive atrium is left behind as the trabeculated, pouch-like appendage. Blood stagnates in this narrow, ridged pouch when the atrium no longer contracts, so it is the usual site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation and mitral stenosis.","whyNot":["The septum primum forms part of the interatrial septum (including the flap of the foramen ovale), not the appendage.","The common pulmonary vein is absorbed into the left atrial wall to form its smooth-walled main part, not the trabeculated appendage.","Correct.","The right sinus horn forms the smooth-walled part of the right atrium (sinus venarum), not any part of the left atrium."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the atria","Atrial fibrillation and thrombus"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C14-Q4","stem":"Her sputum contains macrophages filled with golden-brown granules that stain blue with Prussian blue. Which process best explains these cells?","options":["Bilirubin crystals formed in macrophages from hemolysis within the circulation","Small bleeds from congested alveolar capillaries, with hemoglobin stored as hemosiderin","Inhaled carbon particles from air pollution taken up by alveolar macrophages","Peroxidation of organelle membranes inside the macrophages, leaving lipofuscin granules"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D2","dok":3,"approach":["Read the stain: Prussian blue positive means iron, which in tissue is hemosiderin.","Ask where the iron came from: red cells that leaked into alveoli.","Link to the lesion: mitral stenosis raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary pressure, causing chronic passive congestion and small bleeds."],"why":"In mitral stenosis, high left atrial pressure passes back to the pulmonary veins and capillaries, causing chronic passive congestion. Engorged capillaries leak red cells into the alveoli. Alveolar macrophages engulf these red cells and break hemoglobin down, storing the iron as hemosiderin, which is golden-brown and Prussian blue positive. These hemosiderin-laden macrophages are often called heart failure cells, and the same small bleeds explain her blood-streaked sputum.","whyNot":["Bilirubin does not contain iron, so it is Prussian blue negative, and intravascular hemolysis does not load alveolar macrophages with pigment.","Correct.","Anthracotic pigment is black and does not contain iron, so it does not stain with Prussian blue.","Lipofuscin is a wear-and-tear pigment of aging cells. It is yellow-brown but does not stain with Prussian blue because it holds no iron."],"study":{"topics":["Chronic passive congestion of the lung","Hemosiderin and other pigments"],"comps":["CV-18-D2","CV-18-B1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C15","system":"CV","dx":"Mitral valve prolapse","codes":["CV-18-D4"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the physician because of brief episodes of palpitations and occasional sharp chest pain unrelated to exertion. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Auscultation at the apex shows a midsystolic click followed by a short, late systolic murmur. She does not have tall stature, lens subluxation, or joint hypermobility. Echocardiography shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets, with the posterior leaflet billowing more than 2 mm above the annulus into the left atrium during systole, and mild mitral regurgitation. Ejection fraction is 63% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: mitral valve prolapse.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C15-Q1","stem":"She is examined while squatting and again immediately after she stands up. Which of the following changes in her auscultatory findings is most likely on standing?","options":["Later click and shorter murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls","Later click and shorter murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume rises","Earlier click and longer murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume rises","Earlier click and longer murmur, because left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what standing does: blood pools in the legs, venous return and preload fall.","Link volume to prolapse: a smaller ventricle leaves the redundant leaflet and chordae relatively longer, so the leaflet prolapses earlier in systole.","Predict: earlier click, longer murmur."],"why":"Standing suddenly reduces venous return, so left ventricular end-diastolic volume falls. In a smaller ventricle the redundant leaflet and its chordae are relatively too long for the chamber, so the leaflet reaches its prolapse point earlier in systole. The click moves closer to S1 and the late systolic murmur lengthens. Squatting, which raises venous return and afterload, does the reverse. Valsalva strain acts like standing.","whyNot":["Smaller volume makes the prolapse happen sooner, so the click moves earlier, not later.","This is the effect of squatting: more venous return and higher afterload enlarge the ventricle, so the click moves later. Standing does the opposite.","Standing lowers venous return, so ventricular volume falls rather than rises.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Bedside maneuvers and murmurs","Preload and ventricular volume"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q2","stem":"The midsystolic click is produced when the prolapsing leaflet is suddenly checked during ventricular contraction. Sudden tensing of which structure produces the click?","options":["Chordae tendineae","Pectinate muscles","Septomarginal trabecula","Crista terminalis"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the mitral valve is held shut in systole: papillary muscles contract and pull on chordae tendineae.","Apply it to prolapse: the redundant leaflet balloons back until its chordae snap taut.","Choose the structure that tenses at that moment."],"why":"During ventricular systole the papillary muscles contract and their chordae tendineae hold the mitral leaflets so they meet without everting into the left atrium. In mitral valve prolapse the leaflets and chordae are redundant, so the leaflet billows backward until the slack chordae suddenly tighten. That sudden tensing produces the midsystolic click. The murmur that follows is the mitral regurgitation that starts once the leaflets no longer meet.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls and appendages. They do not connect to the valve leaflets.","The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is in the right ventricle and carries the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. It is not attached to the mitral valve.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium between its smooth and trabeculated parts, unrelated to the mitral valve."],"study":{"topics":["Mitral valve apparatus","Heart sounds"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q3","stem":"A resected leaflet from a patient with the same disease shows a markedly expanded middle (spongiosa) layer. The material that expands this layer and draws water into it is best described as which of the following?","options":["Amyloid fibrils with a beta-pleated sheet structure","Hydroxyapatite crystals of calcium phosphate","Cholesterol esters stored within foam cells","Proteoglycans with glycosaminoglycan side chains"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the histology of mitral valve prolapse: myxomatous degeneration of the spongiosa.","Ask which matrix molecules hold water: glycosaminoglycans are highly negatively charged and attract water.","Pick proteoglycans, the core-protein molecules that carry glycosaminoglycan chains."],"why":"Mitral valve prolapse is caused by myxomatous degeneration. The spongiosa layer fills with proteoglycans, whose glycosaminoglycan side chains (such as chondroitin sulfate and dermatan sulfate) carry many negative charges and bind water. At the same time collagen and elastic fibers in the fibrosa become fragmented. The leaflets become thick, soft, and redundant, so they balloon into the atrium under systolic pressure.","whyNot":["Amyloid is a misfolded protein deposit that stiffens tissue. It is seen in restrictive cardiomyopathy, not floppy leaflets.","Calcium phosphate deposits make valve cusps rigid, as in calcific aortic stenosis.","Lipid-laden foam cells are a feature of atherosclerotic plaques.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Myxomatous degeneration","Proteoglycans and glycosaminoglycans"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D4"]}},{"id":"CV-C15-Q4","stem":"Six years later she has had fever up to 38.4 °C (101.1 °F) and fatigue for three weeks. The fever began about two weeks after a tooth extraction. Blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, gram-positive cocci in chains. Which property of this organism best explains how it settles on her mitral valve?","options":["Dextran that binds fibrin-platelet deposits on the damaged leaflet","M protein that blocks complement deposition and neutrophil phagocytosis","Coagulase that converts fibrinogen to fibrin around the bacteria","IgA protease that cleaves secretory antibody at mucosal surfaces"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the organism: alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant streptococci after dental work are viridans group streptococci.","Recall the setting: subacute endocarditis on a previously abnormal valve.","Link virulence to site: dextran lets the bacteria stick to fibrin-platelet deposits on the damaged leaflet."],"why":"Viridans group streptococci live in the mouth and enter the blood during dental procedures. An abnormal, regurgitant valve develops small sterile fibrin-platelet deposits where flow is turbulent. Strains such as Streptococcus sanguinis make dextran, an extracellular polysaccharide that binds these fibrin-platelet deposits, so the bacteria adhere and grow into vegetations. The course is subacute, with weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue.","whyNot":["Correct.","M protein is the main virulence factor of group A streptococci, which are beta-hemolytic and rarely cause endocarditis.","Coagulase is the marker of Staphylococcus aureus, a gram-positive coccus in clusters that causes acute endocarditis, often on normal valves.","IgA protease helps Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria, and Haemophilus colonize mucosa. Pneumococci are optochin sensitive."],"study":{"topics":["Viridans streptococci","Subacute infective endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C16","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease with mitral stenosis and aortic regurgitation","codes":["CV-18-D5"],"case":"A 52-year-old man is referred to a cardiac surgeon because of worsening exertional dyspnea and ankle swelling. He had acute rheumatic fever at age 9 years and a recurrence at age 14 years after another episode of pharyngitis. Pulse is 84/min and irregularly irregular. Cardiac examination shows a low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex, a soft early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, a loud P2, and a left parasternal heave. Echocardiography shows thickened, restricted mitral leaflets with a valve area of 1.3 cm2 (reference 4 to 6 cm2), mild aortic regurgitation, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 58 mm Hg (reference 15 to 30 mm Hg). Diagnosis: chronic rheumatic heart disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C16-Q1","stem":"He undergoes mitral valve replacement. Which of the following gross findings is most likely in the excised mitral valve?","options":["Thin, ballooning leaflets with long, thin, stretched chordae","Thick leaflets, fused commissures, and short, thick, fused chordae","Bulky, friable vegetations that have eroded through the leaflets","Calcium nodules at the leaflet bases with free, unfused commissures"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that chronic rheumatic disease follows repeated inflammation that heals by fibrosis.","Picture where scarring occurs: leaflet edges and commissures fuse, and chordae shorten and fuse.","Match the gross picture: a narrow, fish-mouth orifice."],"why":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease is the scarred end stage of repeated bouts of valvulitis. Fibrosis thickens the leaflets, fuses them at the commissures, and shortens, thickens, and fuses the chordae tendineae. The result is a rigid, funnel-shaped mitral orifice often called a fish-mouth or buttonhole deformity. The mitral valve is involved most often, followed by the aortic valve, as in this patient.","whyNot":["Ballooning leaflets with elongated chordae describe myxomatous mitral valve prolapse.","Correct.","Destructive vegetations are typical of acute infective endocarditis, often caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","Calcium at the base with free commissures describes degenerative calcification, such as mitral annular calcification or calcific aortic stenosis."],"study":{"topics":["Gross pathology of rheumatic valve disease"],"comps":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q2","stem":"His second attack at 14 followed a new streptococcal pharyngitis and added to the damage from the first. Which feature of adaptive immunity best explains why a later infection can quickly re-trigger the cross-reactive response?","options":["Plasma cells switch from IgG back to IgM production to respond faster on re-exposure","Natural killer cells recognize streptococcal peptides presented on MHC class I","Mast cells coated with IgE in the first attack degranulate on re-exposure","Memory B and T cells formed in the first attack respond rapidly on re-exposure"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Recall that rheumatic fever is an adaptive, antigen-specific response that cross-reacts with heart proteins.","Apply the principle of immunologic memory: the first response leaves memory B and T cells.","On a new infection those cells respond faster and more strongly, so recurrences add cumulative damage."],"why":"The first episode of rheumatic fever creates long-lived memory B and T cells specific for streptococcal antigens that also cross-react with heart proteins. A later group A streptococcal pharyngitis reactivates these cells, so the secondary response is faster and larger. Each recurrence adds new valve inflammation, and the scarring is cumulative. This is why patients with a history of rheumatic fever are given long-term antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent new streptococcal infections.","whyNot":["Class switching goes from IgM to IgG, IgA, or IgE and cannot be reversed. A secondary response is mainly IgG.","Natural killer cells do not recognize specific peptides on MHC class I. They kill cells that have lost MHC class I or show stress signals.","IgE-coated mast cells mediate type I hypersensitivity, which is not the mechanism of rheumatic fever.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary versus secondary immune response","Recurrent rheumatic fever"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q3","stem":"A throat culture from his original pharyngitis would most likely have shown which laboratory profile?","options":["Alpha-hemolytic, optochin-sensitive, bile-soluble gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-resistant, CAMP-positive gram-positive cocci in chains","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive, PYR-positive gram-positive cocci in chains","Catalase-positive, coagulase-positive gram-positive cocci in grape-like clusters"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Name the trigger of rheumatic fever: Streptococcus pyogenes (group A) pharyngitis.","Recall its identifying tests: beta-hemolysis, bacitracin sensitivity, and PYR positivity.","Rule out the other streptococci and staphylococci by their own tests."],"why":"Rheumatic fever follows pharyngitis caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, the group A streptococcus. In the lab it grows as gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase negative, produce complete (beta) hemolysis on blood agar, are inhibited by bacitracin, and are PYR positive. Skin infections with group A strains can cause poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis but are not the classic trigger of rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["This is Streptococcus pneumoniae, which causes pneumonia, otitis media, and meningitis.","This is Streptococcus agalactiae (group B), a cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis, not rheumatic fever.","Correct.","This is Staphylococcus aureus. It does not trigger rheumatic fever."],"study":{"topics":["Streptococcus identification","Group A streptococcus"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C16-Q4","stem":"Which of the following sequences best explains his increased pulmonary artery pressure and right ventricular hypertrophy?","options":["A left-to-right shunt delivers excess blood flow into the pulmonary circulation","Chronic alveolar hypoxia from obstructive lung disease constricts and then remodels pulmonary arterioles","High left atrial pressure backs up into the lungs, then pulmonary arterioles constrict and remodel","Recurrent thromboemboli from leg veins lodge in and gradually obstruct the pulmonary arteries"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Start upstream at the obstruction: mitral stenosis raises left atrial pressure.","Follow the pressure back: pulmonary veins, capillaries, and then pulmonary arteries.","Add the arterial response and its result: reactive constriction and remodeling raise the right ventricle's afterload, causing hypertrophy, a loud P2, and a parasternal heave."],"why":"Mitral stenosis keeps left atrial pressure high. With no valves in the pulmonary veins, that pressure passes straight back to the pulmonary capillaries. The pulmonary arteries must then run at a higher pressure to keep blood moving forward, and over time the arterioles constrict and their walls thicken, raising pulmonary artery pressure further. The right ventricle faces this higher afterload and develops concentric hypertrophy, which produces the parasternal heave, while the loud P2 reflects forceful closure of the pulmonic valve.","whyNot":["A shunt such as an atrial or ventricular septal defect causes pulmonary hypertension from excess flow. He has no shunt.","Hypoxic vasoconstriction from lung disease is a cause of pulmonary hypertension, but nothing in his case suggests chronic lung disease.","Correct.","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension needs a history of clots. His valve disease already explains the finding."],"study":{"topics":["Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease","Right ventricular response to afterload"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-A1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C17","system":"CV","dx":"Carcinoid heart disease","codes":["CV-18-D6"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital because of a 1-year history of episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhea, and wheezing, and more recent leg swelling and abdominal fullness. Imaging shows a small tumor in the terminal ileum and multiple liver metastases. Her 24-hour urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) is 180 mg/24 h (reference about 2 to 9 mg/24 h). Physical examination shows jugular venous distention and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Echocardiography shows thickened, retracted, poorly mobile tricuspid leaflets with severe tricuspid regurgitation, a thickened pulmonic valve, and normal mitral and aortic valves. Diagnosis: carcinoid heart disease from a metastatic ileal neuroendocrine tumor.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C17-Q1","stem":"She also has a scaly rash on sun-exposed skin of her hands and neck and has become forgetful. Reduced synthesis of which cofactor best explains these new findings?","options":["Tetrahydrobiopterin, made from guanosine triphosphate","NAD+ and NADP+, made in part from tryptophan","FAD and FMN, made from riboflavin","Thiamine pyrophosphate, made from thiamine"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the new findings as pellagra: dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, and dementia.","Link to the tumor: carcinoid cells convert a large share of dietary tryptophan into serotonin.","Recall that tryptophan is also a precursor for niacin, which forms NAD+ and NADP+."],"why":"Tryptophan normally supplies part of the body's niacin, which is built into NAD+ and NADP+. A carcinoid tumor diverts a much larger share of tryptophan than normal into serotonin, so less is left for niacin synthesis. The result can be pellagra: dermatitis in sun-exposed areas, diarrhea, and dementia. Her diarrhea may come from both serotonin excess and pellagra.","whyNot":["Tetrahydrobiopterin is a cofactor for tryptophan hydroxylase, but it is made from GTP, and its deficiency causes a form of hyperphenylalaninemia with neurologic signs rather than a photosensitive rash.","Correct.","Riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis, angular stomatitis, and glossitis. Riboflavin is not made from tryptophan.","Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, not a photosensitive dermatitis, and thiamine is not made from tryptophan."],"study":{"topics":["Tryptophan metabolism","Niacin and pellagra"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q2","stem":"An ileal carcinoid often causes no systemic symptoms until it has spread to the liver. Which venous route best explains why her symptoms appeared only after liver metastases formed?","options":["Hepatic veins draining into the inferior vena cava","Superior mesenteric vein draining into the portal vein","Left gastric vein draining into the portal vein","Inferior mesenteric vein draining into the splenic vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Trace venous drainage of the ileum: superior mesenteric vein to portal vein to liver.","Recall that the liver inactivates serotonin (by monoamine oxidase) on this first pass.","Ask how liver metastases differ: they release products into the hepatic veins, which drain straight into the inferior vena cava and right heart."],"why":"Blood from the ileum travels through the superior mesenteric and portal veins to the liver, which breaks down serotonin and other vasoactive products before they reach the systemic circulation. Liver metastases secrete these products downstream of that filter, into the hepatic veins, which empty directly into the inferior vena cava. The products reach the right heart in high concentration, causing flushing, diarrhea, bronchospasm, and right-sided valve damage. Primary tumors outside the portal drainage, such as bronchial or ovarian carcinoids, can cause the syndrome without liver spread.","whyNot":["Correct.","The ileum drains through the superior mesenteric vein into the portal vein, so serotonin from the primary tumor passes through the liver and is inactivated there.","The left gastric vein drains the stomach into the portal vein, so its blood is filtered by the liver as well.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the distal colon and reaches the portal system through the splenic vein, so its blood also passes through the liver."],"study":{"topics":["Portal and systemic venous drainage","First-pass hepatic metabolism"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-D6"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q3","stem":"A biopsy of the thickened tricuspid tissue would most likely show which of the following?","options":["Granulomas with plump macrophages, giant cells, and foci of fibrinoid necrosis","Small, sterile fibrin-platelet vegetations along the lines of leaflet closure","Ballooning leaflets with an expanded spongiosa and fragmented collagen fibers","Surface plaques of smooth muscle cells in a mucopolysaccharide-rich matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: right-sided valve thickening and retraction in a patient with carcinoid syndrome.","Recall that carcinoid plaques are fibrous deposits laid on top of the valve and endocardium.","Match the histology: smooth muscle cells and myofibroblasts in a matrix rich in mucopolysaccharides."],"why":"Carcinoid heart disease produces plaque-like fibrous thickenings on the endocardium of the right heart chambers and on the tricuspid and pulmonic valves. They are made of smooth muscle cells and myofibroblasts in a matrix rich in acid mucopolysaccharides, laid on top of the existing valve. Serotonin and related products are thought to drive this fibrosis. The plaques stiffen and retract the leaflets, which typically causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis.","whyNot":["Granulomas with activated macrophages (Anitschkow cells) and giant cells are Aschoff bodies of acute rheumatic carditis.","Small sterile vegetations describe nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis, seen with hypercoagulable states such as advanced cancer, mainly on left-sided valves.","An expanded spongiosa in floppy leaflets describes myxomatous degeneration in mitral valve prolapse.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Carcinoid heart disease pathology"],"comps":["CV-18-D6","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C17-Q4","stem":"Her mitral and aortic valves are normal even though the tumor products are reaching her right heart in high amounts. Which enzyme and location best explain why the left-sided valves are spared?","options":["Monoamine oxidase in the pulmonary vascular bed","Catechol-O-methyltransferase in the left atrial wall","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase in the lung","Tryptophan hydroxylase in the alveolar epithelium"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Note the pattern: right-sided lesions with left-sided sparing.","Ask what lies between the right and left heart: the pulmonary circulation.","Recall that monoamine oxidase in the lung vascular bed breaks serotonin down to 5-HIAA before blood reaches the left heart."],"why":"Blood from the right heart passes through the lungs before reaching the left heart. The pulmonary vascular bed takes up serotonin and breaks it down with monoamine oxidase to 5-HIAA, so much less reaches the mitral and aortic valves. Left-sided lesions do occur when this filter is bypassed, such as with a patent foramen ovale and right-to-left shunting, a primary bronchial carcinoid, or very high hormone levels.","whyNot":["Correct.","COMT mainly breaks down catecholamines such as epinephrine and dopamine, not serotonin, and the left atrial wall is not a major site of serotonin clearance.","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase makes serotonin from 5-hydroxytryptophan, so it would add serotonin rather than remove it.","Tryptophan hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for serotonin synthesis, so it would also add serotonin."],"study":{"topics":["Serotonin metabolism","Lung as a metabolic filter"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-15"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C18","system":"CV","dx":"Calcific aortic stenosis","codes":["CV-18-D1"],"case":"A 79-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he lost consciousness while climbing two flights of stairs. For 6 months, he has had chest pressure and shortness of breath with exertion. Blood pressure is 118/84 mm Hg. A harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border and radiates to both carotid arteries. S2 is soft and single, and the carotid upstroke is weak and delayed. Echocardiography shows a three-cusp aortic valve with heavily calcified, poorly mobile cusps, an aortic valve area of 0.7 cm2 (reference about 3 to 4 cm2), a mean gradient of 48 mm Hg, concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, and an ejection fraction of 60% (reference 55% to 70%). Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.2 mg/dL). Diagnosis: severe calcific aortic stenosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C18-Q1","stem":"Which process best explains the calcium deposits in his aortic valve?","options":["Metastatic calcification of normal tissue due to raised serum calcium and phosphate","Dystrophic calcification of damaged valve tissue despite normal serum calcium","Fibrous fusion of the commissures from earlier rheumatic valve inflammation","Myxomatous expansion of the spongiosa with proteoglycan accumulation"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D1","dok":2,"approach":["Note the normal serum calcium.","Recall the two types of pathologic calcification: dystrophic (damaged tissue, normal calcium) and metastatic (normal tissue, high calcium).","Link to age-related wear on a three-cusp aortic valve: dystrophic."],"why":"Calcific aortic stenosis of older adults results from years of mechanical stress and low-grade injury to the cusps, with lipid deposition, inflammation, and calcification that resembles bone formation. Because the calcium is laid down in damaged tissue while serum calcium is normal, it is dystrophic calcification. The calcium masses form in the cusps from the aortic side and stiffen them, typically without fusing the commissures.","whyNot":["Metastatic calcification needs high serum calcium or phosphate, as in hyperparathyroidism or kidney failure. His calcium is normal.","Correct.","Rheumatic aortic stenosis shows commissural fusion and almost always involves the mitral valve too. His cusps are calcified with no mention of fusion or mitral disease.","Myxomatous change makes leaflets soft and floppy, causing prolapse and regurgitation rather than stenosis."],"study":{"topics":["Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification","Calcific aortic stenosis"],"comps":["CV-18-D1","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q2","stem":"The cusps of the diseased valve developed from which of the following embryonic structures?","options":["Septum primum tissue at the upper border of the primitive atrium","Mesenchyme of the right and left horns of the sinus venosus","Endocardial cushion swellings of the truncus arteriosus","Atrioventricular endocardial cushions surrounding the atrioventricular canal"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the valve from the murmur: systolic ejection murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids means the aortic valve.","Recall that the aortic and pulmonic (semilunar) valves form in the outflow tract.","Pick the outflow tract cushions, which also receive neural crest cells."],"why":"The aortic and pulmonic valves develop from endocardial cushion tissue in the outflow tract (truncal swellings), with a contribution from cardiac neural crest cells. As the outflow tract divides into the aorta and pulmonary trunk, small swellings on the cushions are hollowed out to form three cusps for each valve. Abnormal formation or fusion of these swellings produces a bicuspid aortic valve, which calcifies and becomes stenotic at a younger age than a three-cusp valve.","whyNot":["The septum primum forms part of the interatrial septum and the flap of the foramen ovale.","The sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium, the coronary sinus, and part of the sinoatrial region.","Correct.","The atrioventricular cushions give rise to tissue of the mitral and tricuspid valves and the atrioventricular septum, not the semilunar valves."],"study":{"topics":["Development of the semilunar valves","Bicuspid aortic valve"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q3","stem":"Two days later, he develops atrial fibrillation. His shortness of breath worsens and his blood pressure falls abruptly. Which of the following best explains why loss of atrial contraction harms him more than it would harm a person with a normal heart?","options":["Atrial fibrillation lengthens diastole and lets too much blood fill his thick ventricle","His stiff, thick ventricle relies on atrial contraction for a larger share of filling","Losing the atrial contraction raises the gradient across his narrow aortic valve directly","Atrial fibrillation removes the main source of coronary blood flow to his thick ventricle"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the remodeling: aortic stenosis causes pressure overload and concentric hypertrophy.","Recall the cost: a thick, stiff ventricle relaxes and fills poorly in early diastole.","Apply it: atrial contraction supplies a larger share of filling, so its loss drops preload and stroke volume sharply."],"why":"In a normal heart, most ventricular filling happens passively in early diastole, and atrial contraction adds a smaller share at the end. A concentrically hypertrophied ventricle from aortic stenosis is stiff and relaxes slowly, so passive filling falls and atrial contraction supplies a much larger part of the end-diastolic volume. When atrial fibrillation removes that contraction, and the fast rate also shortens diastole, preload and stroke volume drop. Pressure backs up into the lungs and cardiac output falls.","whyNot":["Atrial fibrillation usually brings a fast ventricular rate, which shortens diastole and reduces filling, the opposite of this option.","Correct.","The atrium sits upstream of the mitral valve. Losing its contraction lowers ventricular filling and stroke volume, so the aortic gradient tends to fall rather than rise.","Coronary flow is driven by aortic pressure during diastole. Atrial contraction does not supply coronary blood flow."],"study":{"topics":["Atrial contribution to ventricular filling","Diastolic dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C18-Q4","stem":"Over the past year he has had recurrent melena from bleeding gastrointestinal angiodysplasia. He had no unusual bleeding earlier in life, including after a tooth extraction at age 60. Testing now shows loss of the largest von Willebrand factor (vWF) multimers. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this change?","options":["High shear stress across the narrow valve unfolds vWF so ADAMTS13 cleaves it","Autoantibodies against ADAMTS13 block normal cleavage of the largest vWF multimers","A germline VWF mutation prevents normal assembly of multimers in endothelial cells","Congestive liver injury reduces hepatic synthesis and secretion of vWF"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: no bleeding problems until recently, so the defect is acquired.","Link it to the valve: blood jets through a narrow aortic valve at high shear.","Recall that shear unfolds large vWF multimers and exposes the site cleaved by the protease ADAMTS13."],"why":"Von Willebrand factor circulates as multimers, and the largest ones are the most effective at binding platelets to injured vessel walls. High shear stress across a stenotic aortic valve stretches these large multimers open and exposes the site that the plasma protease ADAMTS13 cleaves. The largest multimers are lost, which resembles type 2A von Willebrand disease. Together with bleeding from angiodysplasia this is called Heyde syndrome, and it often improves after the valve is replaced.","whyNot":["Correct.","Antibodies that block ADAMTS13 cause acquired thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, in which very large vWF multimers build up, the opposite of this finding.","An inherited defect usually causes bleeding from early life. His normal bleeding history until recently points to an acquired cause.","vWF is made by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes, not the liver."],"study":{"topics":["Von Willebrand factor structure and function","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-D1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C19","system":"CV","dx":"Subacute infective endocarditis (viridans group streptococci) on a rheumatic mitral valve","codes":["CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1"],"case":"A 58-year-old woman with mitral regurgitation from childhood rheumatic fever comes to the physician because of low-grade fevers, night sweats, fatigue, and a 4-kg weight loss over 5 weeks. Two molars were extracted 7 weeks ago. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F). Physical examination shows splinter hemorrhages, tender nodules on the pads of two fingers, and a palpable spleen tip. A holosystolic murmur that radiates to the left axilla is louder than it was last year. Hemoglobin is 10.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), and MCV is 84 fL (80 to 100). Three blood cultures grow alpha-hemolytic, gram-positive cocci in chains that are optochin resistant and bile insoluble. Echocardiography shows a 9-mm vegetation on the atrial surface of the anterior mitral leaflet. Diagnosis: subacute infective endocarditis caused by viridans group streptococci.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C19-Q1","stem":"The organism adheres to fibrin-platelet deposits on the scarred leaflet by means of an extracellular polysaccharide. Which of the following dietary sugars is the substrate for synthesis of this polysaccharide?","options":["Maltose","Lactose","Galactose","Sucrose"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, bile-insoluble streptococci are viridans streptococci.","Recall their adhesin: dextran (a glucose polymer) that binds fibrin-platelet deposits on damaged valves.","Name the substrate: glucosyltransferase builds dextran from sucrose."],"why":"Viridans streptococci such as S. mutans and S. sanguinis carry glucosyltransferase (dextransucrase). This enzyme splits sucrose, links the glucose units into dextran, and releases fructose. Dextran helps the bacteria stick to tooth surfaces and to the sterile fibrin-platelet deposits that form on valves scarred by rheumatic disease. Bacteremia after dental work lets them seed those deposits.","whyNot":["Maltose is a glucose-glucose disaccharide from starch breakdown. Glucosyltransferase does not use it to build dextran.","Lactose is a glucose-galactose disaccharide from milk. Many bacteria ferment it, but it is not the substrate for dextran synthesis.","Galactose is a monosaccharide from lactose. It is not used to build dextran, which is a glucose polymer.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Viridans streptococci virulence","Dextran and glucosyltransferase"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q2","stem":"The mitral valve lies posterior to the left half of the sternum at the level of the fourth costal cartilage. At which of the following sites is her murmur most likely to be heard best?","options":["Second left intercostal space at the sternal border","Fourth left intercostal space at the lower sternal border","Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Second right intercostal space at the sternal border"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","dok":2,"approach":["Link the murmur to the valve: a holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla in this case is mitral regurgitation.","Separate the valve's position from its listening area: sound travels with blood flow toward the left ventricle.","Pick the mitral area: the cardiac apex, fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line."],"why":"Each valve is heard best downstream of it, in the direction its blood flows. Mitral sounds travel toward the left ventricular apex, so the mitral area is the fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line. Mitral regurgitation is holosystolic, loudest at the apex, and radiates to the left axilla. A vegetation that erodes a leaflet or chordae makes the regurgitation and murmur worse.","whyNot":["This is the pulmonic area. Pulmonic stenosis or a split S2 is heard best here.","This is the tricuspid area. Tricuspid regurgitation, common in endocarditis in people who inject drugs, is heard best here.","Correct.","This is the aortic area. Aortic stenosis would be heard best here, with radiation to the carotids."],"study":{"topics":["Auscultation areas of the heart valves","Mitral regurgitation murmur"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q3","stem":"Urinalysis shows red blood cells, red blood cell casts, and 1+ protein. Serum C3 concentration is 58 mg/dL (90 to 180). Which of the following mechanisms best explains her renal findings?","options":["Antibodies binding type IV collagen in the glomerular basement membrane","Bacteria invading the renal tubules and interstitium from the blood","Immune complexes deposited in glomeruli, activating complement","Septic emboli causing wedge-shaped infarcts in the renal cortex"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Read red cell casts as glomerular bleeding, which means glomerulonephritis.","Read the low C3 as complement being consumed.","Match a long-standing infection with low complement and glomerulonephritis to immune complex deposition (type III hypersensitivity)."],"why":"Weeks of bacteremia produce antibodies that bind circulating bacterial antigens. These immune complexes lodge in the glomeruli and activate complement, which lowers serum C3 and recruits inflammatory cells. The result is a glomerulonephritis with red cell casts, a type III hypersensitivity reaction.","whyNot":["Anti-GBM disease is type II hypersensitivity. It often affects the lungs as well, and serum complement is usually normal.","Bacterial invasion of the tubules and interstitium is pyelonephritis, which produces white cell casts.","Correct.","Renal infarcts from emboli cause flank pain and hematuria, but they do not produce red cell casts or low C3."],"study":{"topics":["Type III hypersensitivity","Glomerulonephritis in endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C19-Q4","stem":"Additional laboratory studies show serum iron 35 µg/dL (60 to 170), total iron-binding capacity 210 µg/dL (250 to 450), and ferritin 380 ng/mL (12 to 150). Which of the following mechanisms best explains her anemia?","options":["Chronic blood loss that has depleted total body iron stores","Hepcidin release driven by IL-6 that causes ferroportin breakdown","Antibody-mediated destruction of circulating red blood cells","Vitamin B12 deficiency slowing DNA synthesis in red cell precursors"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pattern: low iron, low TIBC, and high ferritin with a normal MCV.","Recognize anemia of chronic disease (anemia of inflammation) from a long infection.","Name the mechanism: IL-6 raises hepcidin, which makes cells internalize and degrade ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and enterocytes."],"why":"Ongoing infection raises IL-6, which makes the liver release hepcidin. Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the iron exporter on enterocytes and macrophages, and causes it to be taken in and degraded. Iron stays locked in storage, so ferritin is high while serum iron falls. The liver also makes less transferrin, so TIBC is low. Red cell production is limited by the iron supply.","whyNot":["Iron deficiency from blood loss gives low ferritin and high TIBC, the opposite of this pattern.","Correct.","Immune hemolysis would raise reticulocytes, LDH, and indirect bilirubin. It does not trap iron in stores.","B12 deficiency gives a macrocytic anemia with a high MCV. Her MCV is normal."],"study":{"topics":["Anemia of chronic disease","Hepcidin and ferroportin"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C20","system":"CV","dx":"Acute bacterial endocarditis of the aortic valve (Staphylococcus aureus)","codes":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"],"case":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and an infected foot ulcer is brought to the emergency department because of 4 days of fever, rigors, and shortness of breath. An echocardiogram 1 year ago showed normal valves. Temperature is 39.7 °C (103.5 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 104/52 mm Hg. Physical examination shows painless erythematous macules on both soles and a new early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border. Serum lactate is 4.6 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2). Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase positive and coagulase positive. Echocardiography shows a 14-mm vegetation on the aortic valve with a perforated leaflet and moderate to severe regurgitation. Diagnosis: acute bacterial endocarditis caused by Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C20-Q1","stem":"His new murmur begins immediately after S2 and decreases in intensity through diastole. Which of the following pressure relationships best explains the timing and shape of this murmur?","options":["Ventricle-to-aorta gradient is highest in mid systole","Ventricle-to-atrium gradient stays high throughout all of systole","Atrium-to-ventricle gradient is highest at the end of diastole","Aorta-to-ventricle gradient is highest just after S2, then falls"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","dok":3,"approach":["Connect the early diastolic decrescendo murmur and the perforated aortic leaflet to aortic regurgitation.","Ask when the leak is driven hardest: right after the aortic valve closes, when aortic pressure is far above left ventricular pressure.","Follow the gradient through diastole: aortic pressure falls and ventricular pressure rises as the ventricle fills, so flow and loudness fade."],"why":"A murmur is loudest when the pressure gradient driving the abnormal flow is largest. In aortic regurgitation, backflow begins as soon as the aortic valve closes (S2), when aortic pressure is far above left ventricular pressure. Through diastole the aorta drains backward and the ventricle fills, so the gradient narrows and the murmur fades. This produces a high-pitched, blowing, early diastolic decrescendo murmur.","whyNot":["A gradient that peaks in mid systole fits aortic stenosis, a crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur.","A gradient that stays high through all of systole fits mitral regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur.","A gradient that peaks at the end of diastole fits mitral stenosis, where atrial contraction causes presystolic accentuation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Aortic regurgitation murmur","Pressure gradients in the cardiac cycle"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q2","stem":"Susceptibility testing shows that the isolate grows in the presence of oxacillin and cefoxitin. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this resistance?","options":["A mecA-encoded penicillin-binding protein with low drug affinity","D-Ala-D-Lac replacing D-Ala-D-Ala in cell wall precursors","Methylation of the binding site on 23S ribosomal RNA","Hydrolysis of the beta-lactam ring by a plasmid-encoded penicillinase"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize oxacillin and cefoxitin resistance in S. aureus as methicillin resistance (MRSA).","Recall that oxacillin resists staphylococcal penicillinase, so penicillinase cannot explain it.","Name the mechanism: mecA codes for PBP2a, which keeps building cell wall when beta-lactams are present."],"why":"Methicillin-resistant S. aureus carries the mecA gene on the SCCmec element. mecA encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase that binds beta-lactams poorly, so cell wall cross-linking continues. Laboratories use cefoxitin or oxacillin to detect it. The plain penicillinase most S. aureus strains make cannot break down oxacillin.","whyNot":["Correct.","This is the vancomycin resistance mechanism of enterococci (vanA). It does not affect beta-lactams.","This (erm) methylation causes resistance to macrolides and clindamycin.","Most S. aureus make penicillinase, which explains resistance to penicillin G. Oxacillin and nafcillin are built to resist it."],"study":{"topics":["MRSA and mecA","Beta-lactam resistance mechanisms"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K1"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q3","stem":"His elevated serum lactate reflects poor tissue perfusion. Which of the following best describes the reaction that produces lactate in hypoxic cells and its purpose?","options":["Alanine aminotransferase converting pyruvate to alanine for transport to liver","Pyruvate carboxylase converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","Pyruvate dehydrogenase converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA for the citric acid cycle","Lactate dehydrogenase reducing pyruvate to regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Without enough oxygen, the electron transport chain slows and NADH builds up.","Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and oxidizes NADH back to NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making ATP."],"why":"When oxygen delivery falls, mitochondria cannot reoxidize NADH. Glycolysis would stop at glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase without NAD+. Lactate dehydrogenase solves this by reducing pyruvate to lactate while turning NADH into NAD+. The cell keeps a small ATP supply, and lactate spills into the blood. A rising lactate is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion in sepsis.","whyNot":["Alanine aminotransferase makes alanine, used in the glucose-alanine cycle. It does not make lactate.","Pyruvate carboxylase is a mitochondrial gluconeogenic enzyme. It does not make lactate.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase sends pyruvate into aerobic metabolism. Its flux falls when oxygen is short, which is why pyruvate is diverted to lactate.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis","Lactic acidosis in shock"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C20-Q4","stem":"On hospital day 3, he has no chest pain, and ECG shows no ST-segment changes. The PR interval has lengthened to 0.30 s (0.12 to 0.20), and intermittent second-degree AV block develops later. Which of the following complications most likely explains these findings?","options":["Septic embolus occluding the left anterior descending artery","Rupture of mitral chordae tendineae causing acute regurgitation","Aortic annular abscess invading nearby conduction tissue","Immune complex deposition damaging the sinoatrial node"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","dok":3,"approach":["Notice new and progressing AV conduction delay in a patient with aortic valve endocarditis.","Recall the anatomy: the aortic annulus sits beside the membranous septum, where the AV bundle (bundle of His) passes.","Conclude that infection has spread beyond the leaflet into a perivalvular (ring) abscess, a typical S. aureus complication."],"why":"S. aureus is destructive and often spreads from the leaflets into the valve ring. The aortic annulus lies next to the membranous interventricular septum and the AV node and bundle of His. An abscess there causes new PR prolongation or higher-grade AV block. In aortic valve endocarditis, a new conduction block suggests a perivalvular abscess until proven otherwise.","whyNot":["An embolus to a coronary artery would cause an infarct, usually with chest pain or ST-segment changes. Neither is present.","Chordal rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema. It does not damage conduction tissue.","Correct.","Immune complex disease in endocarditis affects the kidneys and skin. SA node damage would cause sinus pauses or sinus bradycardia. The PR interval reflects AV conduction."],"study":{"topics":["Perivalvular abscess","AV node and bundle of His location"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-7"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C21","system":"CV","dx":"Tricuspid valve endocarditis in a person who injects drugs (Staphylococcus aureus)","codes":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman who injects heroin comes to the emergency department because of 9 days of fever, chills, pleuritic chest pain, and cough with blood-streaked sputum. Temperature is 39.2 °C (102.6 °F). Physical examination shows injection marks over both antecubital fossae. A holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and the jugular venous pulse shows large v waves. Blood cultures grow catalase-positive, coagulase-positive, gram-positive cocci in clusters that are oxacillin susceptible. Echocardiography shows a 15-mm vegetation on the tricuspid valve with severe regurgitation. CT scan of the chest shows multiple peripheral nodules in both lungs, several with central cavitation. Diagnosis: tricuspid valve endocarditis caused by methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C21-Q1","stem":"Which of the following processes best explains the multiple peripheral, cavitating lung nodules on her CT scan?","options":["Reactivated tuberculosis spreading through the bronchial tree","Vegetation fragments carried by the pulmonary arteries to the lungs","Aspirated oral anaerobes forming a single dependent abscess","Vegetation fragments crossing a patent foramen ovale to systemic arteries"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","dok":3,"approach":["Locate the source: a right-sided (tricuspid) vegetation.","Trace where its fragments go: right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries.","Match the result: many peripheral nodules that cavitate as the infected emboli form small abscesses (septic pulmonary emboli)."],"why":"Fragments that break off a tricuspid vegetation travel with venous blood through the right ventricle into the pulmonary arteries. They lodge in small peripheral branches of both lungs, cause infarcts, and form abscesses that cavitate. This explains her pleuritic pain and blood-streaked sputum. Left-sided vegetations embolize instead to the brain, kidneys, spleen, and limbs.","whyNot":["Reactivation tuberculosis usually causes upper lobe cavitation with weeks to months of symptoms. It does not explain a tricuspid vegetation with many bilateral peripheral nodules.","Correct.","Aspiration causes a lung abscess in a dependent segment, usually a single lesion. Her lesions are multiple and peripheral.","Crossing a patent foramen ovale sends emboli into the systemic circulation (paradoxical embolism), so they would bypass the lungs."],"study":{"topics":["Septic pulmonary emboli","Right-sided versus left-sided embolization"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-H1"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q2","stem":"Her murmur at the left lower sternal border increases in intensity during inspiration. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this change?","options":["Lung expansion that displaces the left ventricle toward the chest wall","Increased intrathoracic pressure that augments left ventricular filling","Increased right ventricular contractility during inspiration","Decreased intrathoracic pressure that augments right heart preload"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the murmur: holosystolic at the left lower sternal border with large v waves means tricuspid regurgitation.","Recall that inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure.","Apply it: lower intrathoracic pressure draws more venous blood into the right heart, so the right ventricle ejects and regurgitates more, and the murmur gets louder."],"why":"During inspiration the diaphragm descends and intrathoracic pressure falls. More blood returns from the venae cavae to the right atrium and right ventricle. A larger right ventricular volume means more blood flows back through the leaky tricuspid valve in systole, so the murmur grows louder (Carvallo sign). Right-sided murmurs tend to increase with inspiration, while left-sided murmurs do not.","whyNot":["Inflating lungs move between the heart and the chest wall, which tends to soften heart sounds.","Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, and it briefly reduces left heart filling.","Inspiration does not raise right ventricular contractility. The louder murmur comes from greater right ventricular filling (preload).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Heart sounds and respiration","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-4"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q3","stem":"Which of the following factors best explains why the infection involves the tricuspid valve rather than a left-sided valve?","options":["Injected particles injure the first valve that venous blood crosses","The tricuspid valve closes against the highest pressure in the heart","Rheumatic damage, which favors the tricuspid valve, was already present","Low oxygen tension in venous blood selects for anaerobic organisms"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K1","dok":3,"approach":["Trace injected material: from an arm vein to the superior vena cava, right atrium, and then the tricuspid valve.","Recall that particles and drug additives in injected material are thought to damage the endothelium of the tricuspid valve.","Add skin flora (S. aureus) introduced with each injection, which can then attach to the injured valve."],"why":"In people who inject drugs, every injection delivers particles, drug additives, and skin bacteria into venous blood. The tricuspid valve is the first valve this material crosses, and repeated endothelial injury there is the accepted explanation for the right-sided preference. S. aureus from the skin is the most common organism. It is virulent enough to infect even a previously normal valve.","whyNot":["Correct.","The tricuspid valve closes against right ventricular pressure, which is far lower than left ventricular pressure. Higher pressure stress on left-sided valves would favor the mitral and aortic valves.","Rheumatic heart disease mostly affects the mitral valve, then the aortic valve.","S. aureus is a facultative anaerobe that grows well in oxygenated blood, and anaerobes are rare causes of endocarditis."],"study":{"topics":["Endocarditis in people who inject drugs","Valve involvement patterns in endocarditis"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-18-D3"]}},{"id":"CV-C21-Q4","stem":"She injects into her right median cubital vein. Which of the following sequences of veins most directly carries the injected bacteria to the right atrium?","options":["Basilic, axillary, subclavian, right brachiocephalic, inferior vena cava","Basilic, axillary, subclavian, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Basilic, brachial, internal jugular, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Cephalic, external jugular, azygos, right brachiocephalic, superior vena cava"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["The median cubital vein links the cephalic and basilic veins in the cubital fossa.","The basilic vein joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein, which becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib.","The subclavian joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein, and the two brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava."],"why":"Blood from the median cubital vein drains into the basilic vein, which joins the brachial veins at the lower border of teres major to form the axillary vein. The axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein at the outer border of the first rib. The subclavian and internal jugular veins join to form the brachiocephalic vein. Both brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium.","whyNot":["The inferior vena cava drains the lower body. Upper limb blood returns through the superior vena cava.","Correct.","The internal jugular vein drains the head and neck. Arm blood reaches the brachiocephalic vein through the subclavian vein.","The cephalic vein drains into the axillary vein, and the azygos vein drains the posterior thoracic wall into the superior vena cava. This route is wrong."],"study":{"topics":["Venous drainage of the upper limb","Formation of the superior vena cava"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-3"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C22","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (Trypanosoma cruzi)","codes":["CV-18-K2","CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 54-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of palpitations, exertional dyspnea, and ankle swelling. He also has dysphagia to both solids and liquids. He grew up in a rural adobe home in Bolivia and moved to California 20 years ago. ECG shows right bundle branch block with left anterior fascicular block and frequent premature ventricular contractions. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30% (55 to 70) and a thin-walled apical aneurysm. Two different serologic tests detect IgG antibodies against Trypanosoma cruzi. Diagnosis: chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C22-Q1","stem":"He most likely acquired the infection as a child in his home. Which of the following was the most likely mode of transmission?","options":["Triatomine bug feces rubbed into the bite wound or eye","Bite of a tsetse fly that injects parasites in its saliva","Bite of a female sandfly that injects promastigotes","Bite of a female Anopheles mosquito injecting sporozoites"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism from the diagnosis: Trypanosoma cruzi.","Recall its vector: triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs that live in cracks of adobe walls and thatched roofs in Latin America.","Recall the route: the bug defecates while feeding, and parasites in the feces enter through the bite wound or the conjunctiva."],"why":"T. cruzi is spread by triatomine bugs that hide in the walls of poorly built rural homes and feed at night. The bug passes infective trypomastigotes in its feces while feeding. Scratching rubs the feces into the bite wound, or the parasites enter through the conjunctiva, which can cause one-sided eyelid swelling (Romaña sign). Blood transfusion, organ transplant, mother-to-baby spread, and contaminated food are less common routes.","whyNot":["Correct.","Tsetse flies transmit Trypanosoma brucei, the cause of African sleeping sickness, through their saliva.","Sandflies transmit Leishmania species.","Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, which cause malaria."],"study":{"topics":["Trypanosoma cruzi life cycle","Protozoan vectors"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K2"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q2","stem":"Right bundle branch block is the most common conduction abnormality in chronic Chagas heart disease. In the right ventricle, the right bundle branch normally reaches the base of the anterior papillary muscle by way of which of the following structures?","options":["Crista terminalis of the right atrium","Pectinate muscles of the right auricle","Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","Chordae tendineae of the tricuspid leaflets"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","dok":2,"approach":["Trace the right bundle branch: it runs down the right side of the interventricular septum.","Recall the muscular bridge from the septum to the anterior papillary muscle in the right ventricle.","Name it: the septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) carries the right bundle branch, so the anterior papillary muscle contracts in time."],"why":"The right bundle branch descends on the right side of the interventricular septum. Much of it crosses the right ventricular cavity in the septomarginal trabecula, also called the moderator band, to reach the base of the anterior papillary muscle. This shortcut lets the papillary muscle tense the tricuspid chordae before the ventricle ejects. Chagas inflammation and fibrosis commonly involve the right bundle branch, so right bundle branch block is a classic finding.","whyNot":["The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium that separates its smooth and rough parts. The SA node lies near its upper end.","Pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls and auricles. They carry no bundle branch.","Correct.","Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords from papillary muscles to valve leaflets. They contain no conduction tissue."],"study":{"topics":["Right bundle branch and moderator band","Right ventricle internal anatomy"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q3","stem":"His dilated, thin-walled left ventricle generates about the same systolic pressure as a normal ventricle, yet its wall stress and oxygen demand per beat are higher. Which of the following relationships best explains this?","options":["Flow through a vessel rises with the fourth power of its radius","Force of contraction rises as end-diastolic fiber length increases","Fluid movement depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressure gradients","Wall stress rises with chamber radius and falls with wall thickness"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Note what changed: the chamber radius is larger and the wall is thinner, while pressure is unchanged.","Recall the law of Laplace: wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius, divided by wall thickness.","Apply it: a larger radius and thinner wall raise wall stress, and higher wall stress raises myocardial oxygen demand."],"why":"The law of Laplace says that wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A dilated ventricle has a larger radius and, in this case, a thinner wall, so each fiber must carry more tension to make the same pressure. Wall stress is a major driver of myocardial oxygen use, which is one reason a dilated heart works less efficiently.","whyNot":["This is Poiseuille's law for flow through a tube. It explains how vessel radius controls flow and resistance.","This is the Frank-Starling relationship. It describes how filling changes stroke volume. It does not explain the rise in wall stress.","These are Starling forces across capillaries. They explain capillary fluid exchange and edema.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Myocardial oxygen demand"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C22-Q4","stem":"A barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus that tapers smoothly to a narrow point at a lower esophageal sphincter that fails to relax. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this finding?","options":["Fibrous replacement of smooth muscle from systemic sclerosis","Peptic stricture from long-standing acid reflux","Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","Circumferential growth of squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K2","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the pattern of achalasia: dilated esophagus, smooth taper, and a lower esophageal sphincter that does not relax.","Recall that sphincter relaxation depends on inhibitory neurons of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus.","Link it to the diagnosis: chronic Chagas disease destroys these enteric neurons, causing megaesophagus and megacolon."],"why":"Chronic T. cruzi infection damages neurons of the enteric nervous system, especially the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus. Loss of inhibitory neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP means the lower esophageal sphincter cannot relax, and peristalsis fails. The esophagus slowly dilates above the closed sphincter (megaesophagus). The same process in the colon produces megacolon.","whyNot":["Systemic sclerosis replaces esophageal smooth muscle with fibrosis, which leaves the lower sphincter weak and open and causes reflux.","A peptic stricture narrows the distal esophagus, but the sphincter still relaxes and dysphagia is usually to solids first.","Correct.","Carcinoma usually causes progressive dysphagia to solids first, with weight loss and an irregular narrowing on barium study."],"study":{"topics":["Chagas megaesophagus","Enteric nervous system"],"comps":["CV-18-K2","CV-17"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C23","system":"CV","dx":"Lyme carditis with third-degree AV block (Borrelia burgdorferi)","codes":["CV-18-K3","CV-18-E2"],"case":"A 26-year-old man from Connecticut comes to the physician because of lightheadedness and one episode of syncope. He went camping in wooded areas in June. Five weeks ago, he had an expanding erythematous rash with central clearing on his thigh that resolved without treatment. Pulse is 42/min. ECG shows P waves at 86/min with no relationship to narrow QRS complexes at 42/min. Two-tier serologic testing (enzyme immunoassay followed by Western blot) is positive for IgM and IgG antibodies against Borrelia burgdorferi. Diagnosis: Lyme carditis with third-degree AV block.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C23-Q1","stem":"His escape QRS complexes are narrow, which places the block above the bundle branches. In Lyme carditis, the block most often occurs in the structure that normally delays conduction between the atria and ventricles. In which of the following locations is this structure found?","options":["Interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus","Posterior wall of the left atrium between the pulmonary veins","Muscular interventricular septum near the cardiac apex","Subepicardium where the superior vena cava meets the right atrium"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","dok":3,"approach":["Use the narrow escape QRS: the block is at or above the bundle of His.","Identify the structure that delays the impulse between atria and ventricles: the AV node.","Locate it: the lower interatrial septum, in the triangle of Koch near the coronary sinus opening, just above the septal tricuspid leaflet."],"why":"The AV node sits in the lower part of the interatrial septum, within the triangle of Koch, close to the opening of the coronary sinus and the septal leaflet of the tricuspid valve. It slows conduction so the ventricles fill before they contract. Lyme carditis most often blocks conduction at this level. A junctional escape rhythm then takes over, giving narrow QRS complexes at about 40 to 60/min.","whyNot":["Correct.","The area between the pulmonary veins is where many atrial fibrillation triggers arise. It contains no AV conduction tissue.","The septum near the apex holds bundle branch and Purkinje fibers. Block there would give a wide, slow ventricular escape rhythm.","This is the location of the SA node, the normal pacemaker. His P waves at 86/min show the SA node is working."],"study":{"topics":["AV node and triangle of Koch","Escape rhythms"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q2","stem":"He recalls removing a small tick from his thigh about 2 days after the trip began. An infected Ixodes tick removed within about 24 hours of attachment rarely transmits B. burgdorferi. Which of the following best explains why transmission usually requires 36 to 48 hours or more of feeding?","options":["The tick must molt into its adult stage while it is still attached","Spirochetes must move from the tick midgut to its salivary glands","Spirochetes must first multiply inside the host's red blood cells","The tick must ingest the human's blood before it becomes infected"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Recall where B. burgdorferi lives in an unfed tick: the midgut.","Recall the trigger: the incoming blood meal activates the spirochetes, which multiply, change surface proteins, and migrate to the salivary glands.","Conclude that this migration takes time, so prompt tick removal lowers the risk."],"why":"In an unfed Ixodes tick, B. burgdorferi sits in the midgut. Once the tick starts feeding, the spirochetes multiply, switch surface proteins (OspA down, OspC up), and travel to the salivary glands. Only then are they injected with saliva. This process usually needs 36 to 48 hours or more of attachment, so checking for ticks daily and removing them promptly prevents most infections.","whyNot":["Ticks molt between stages only after they drop off the host. Nymphs, which are small and easy to miss, transmit most human cases.","Correct.","Babesia, which the same tick can carry, infects red blood cells. B. burgdorferi is an extracellular spirochete.","The tick acquired the spirochete earlier, as a larva or nymph feeding on infected mice. It does not need human blood to become infected."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme disease transmission","Ixodes tick life cycle"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q3","stem":"His Western blot shows IgG bands as well as IgM bands. Switching from IgM to IgG against the spirochete's protein antigens requires T-cell help. Which of the following interactions is required for this class switching?","options":["Complement C5a binding its receptor on neutrophils","Bacterial lipopolysaccharide binding TLR4 on macrophages","CD40 ligand on helper T cells binding CD40 on B cells","Peptide on MHC class I binding CD8 on cytotoxic T cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Note the shift from IgM to IgG over the weeks after infection.","Recall that class switching to protein antigens is T-cell dependent.","Name the contact signal: CD40 ligand (CD154) on activated helper T cells binding CD40 on B cells, together with cytokines."],"why":"B cells first make IgM. To switch to IgG, a B cell presents protein peptides on MHC class II to a helper T cell. The T cell then binds CD40 on the B cell through CD40 ligand and releases cytokines. These signals turn on activation-induced cytidine deaminase and class switch recombination. Without CD40 ligand, as in X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome, patients make IgM but little IgG.","whyNot":["C5a attracts and activates neutrophils. It plays no direct role in class switching.","B. burgdorferi lacks classic lipopolysaccharide, and TLR4 on macrophages drives innate inflammation. Its lipoproteins signal mainly through TLR2.","Correct.","MHC class I with CD8 activates cytotoxic T cells, which kill infected cells. It does not drive B cell class switching."],"study":{"topics":["Class switch recombination","T-dependent antibody responses"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K3"]}},{"id":"CV-C23-Q4","stem":"If his infection had not been treated, which of the following manifestations would most likely have developed months after the rash?","options":["Symmetric small-joint arthritis with morning stiffness of the hands","Episodic swelling of one or a few large joints, often the knee","Sudden monoarthritis of the big toe with urate crystals in fluid","Migratory polyarthritis with subcutaneous nodules and chorea"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K3","dok":2,"approach":["Place the patient on the Lyme timeline: early localized (erythema migrans), early disseminated (carditis, facial palsy), late (arthritis).","Recall the late form: attacks of arthritis in one or a few large joints, most often the knee.","Rule out look-alikes from other diseases by their distinct features."],"why":"Untreated Lyme disease can progress over months to late disease. The most common late finding in the United States is Lyme arthritis, with intermittent attacks of swelling in one or a few large joints, especially the knee. The swelling is often large and less painful than it looks. Carditis and facial nerve palsy belong to the earlier disseminated stage, which this patient is in now.","whyNot":["Symmetric small-joint arthritis with morning stiffness describes rheumatoid arthritis.","Correct.","This is acute gout from monosodium urate crystals.","This combination is acute rheumatic fever after group A streptococcal pharyngitis."],"study":{"topics":["Stages of Lyme disease","Lyme arthritis"],"comps":["CV-18-K3","CV-17"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C24","system":"CV","dx":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii)","codes":["CV-18-K4","CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 9-year-old girl in rural North Carolina is brought to the emergency department in July because of 5 days of fever, severe headache, and myalgias. Her parents found a tick on her scalp 8 days ago after she played with the family dog. Temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), pulse is 132/min, and blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg. A rash that began as pink macules on the wrists and ankles has spread to the trunk and become petechial, including the palms and soles. There is edema of the hands and feet. Platelet count is 64,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), and serum sodium is 129 mEq/L (135 to 145). Diagnosis: Rocky Mountain spotted fever caused by Rickettsia rickettsii. Doxycycline is started.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C24-Q1","stem":"Her petechial rash, edema of the hands and feet, and thrombocytopenia all result from infection of one principal host cell type. Which of the following cells does R. rickettsii primarily infect?","options":["Red blood cells in the circulation","Monocytes in blood and tissues","Neutrophils in blood and tissues","Endothelial cells of small vessels"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K4","dok":3,"approach":["List the findings: petechiae, edema, low blood pressure, and low platelets.","Find the common cause: damage to small blood vessels (a vasculitis).","Name the target: R. rickettsii infects vascular endothelial cells (and vascular smooth muscle) of small vessels."],"why":"R. rickettsii enters and multiplies in the endothelial cells of small vessels throughout the body. Infected endothelium is injured, which produces a small-vessel vasculitis. Leaky vessels cause edema and low blood volume, bleeding into the skin causes petechiae, and platelets are used up at sites of endothelial damage. The same vessel damage in the brain, lungs, and kidneys explains the severe forms of the disease.","whyNot":["Babesia infects red blood cells, causing hemolytic anemia.","Ehrlichia chaffeensis infects monocytes, forming morulae in their cytoplasm.","Anaplasma phagocytophilum infects neutrophils (granulocytes).","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Rickettsial vasculitis","Tick-borne intracellular bacteria"],"comps":["CV-18-K4","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q2","stem":"Two sets of routine blood cultures show no growth after 5 days. Which of the following properties of the organism best explains this finding?","options":["It is a bacterium that grows only inside host cells","It is a virus that replicates only in host cell nuclei","It is a spirochete too thin to see on Gram stain","It has a mycolic acid cell wall that needs weeks to grow"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: R. rickettsii.","Recall its key property: an obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium.","Apply it: it cannot grow on the cell-free media used for routine blood cultures, so diagnosis relies on serology, PCR, or skin biopsy staining."],"why":"Rickettsiae are small gram-negative bacteria that stain poorly and grow only inside living host cells. They take nutrients and energy molecules from the host cell, so they do not grow in routine blood culture bottles. Diagnosis uses indirect immunofluorescent antibody testing, PCR, or immunohistochemistry on a skin biopsy. Treatment must start on clinical suspicion because early tests are often negative.","whyNot":["Correct.","R. rickettsii is a gram-negative bacterium that multiplies mainly in the host cell cytoplasm.","Thin spirochetes such as Treponema and Borrelia are hard to see on Gram stain. R. rickettsii is a gram-negative coccobacillus.","Mycobacteria have mycolic acid walls and grow slowly. That would not fit this acute tick-borne illness."],"study":{"topics":["Obligate intracellular bacteria","Diagnosis of Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K4"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q3","stem":"Within endothelial cells, R. rickettsii escapes the phagosome and spreads directly into adjacent cells without re-entering the bloodstream. Which of the following host processes does it use for this movement?","options":["Polymerization of host actin into a tail that pushes it","Clathrin-coated pits taking it up from the cell surface","Dynein motors carrying it along microtubules","SNARE-mediated fusion of vesicles with the plasma membrane"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize cell-to-cell spread in the cytoplasm, like Listeria.","Recall which cytoskeletal protein builds a moving tail: actin.","Apply it: the bacterium recruits host actin to its surface and is pushed by actin polymerization into a neighboring cell."],"why":"Spotted fever group rickettsiae, including R. rickettsii, recruit host actin to one end of the bacterium. Continuous actin polymerization forms a tail that pushes the bacterium through the cytoplasm and into protrusions that enter the next cell. This lets infection spread along the endothelium while the bacteria stay hidden from antibodies. Listeria monocytogenes and Shigella use the same host system.","whyNot":["Correct.","Clathrin-coated pits bring cargo into the cell. They do not move bacteria from one cell to the next.","Dynein moves cargo toward the minus ends of microtubules. Some viruses use it to reach the nucleus, but rickettsiae do not use it to spread between cells.","SNARE proteins fuse vesicles with membranes during secretion. They do not move cytoplasmic bacteria."],"study":{"topics":["Actin-based motility","Cell-to-cell spread of intracellular bacteria"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K4"]}},{"id":"CV-C24-Q4","stem":"Her serum albumin concentration is 3.9 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), and her jugular veins are flat. Which of the following changes in capillary fluid exchange best explains the edema of her hands and feet?","options":["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion","Increased capillary permeability to fluid and protein","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia","Impaired lymphatic drainage from lymph node obstruction"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["List the Starling factors: capillary hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, capillary permeability, and lymph drainage.","Use the clues: flat neck veins rule out venous congestion, and normal albumin rules out low oncotic pressure.","Connect the remaining factor to endothelial infection: damaged endothelium makes capillaries leaky."],"why":"Injured endothelium in rickettsial vasculitis lets fluid and protein leak into the interstitium. In Starling terms, the filtration coefficient rises and the reflection coefficient for protein falls. Fluid leaves the vessels even though hydrostatic and oncotic pressures are not abnormal. This causes edema and, at the same time, low blood volume and hypotension.","whyNot":["Venous congestion would raise jugular venous pressure. Her neck veins are flat.","Correct.","Low albumin would lower oncotic pressure, but her albumin is normal.","Lymphatic blockage causes lymphedema over weeks to months, usually from surgery, cancer, or filarial infection."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces and edema","Capillary permeability"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C25","system":"CV","dx":"Dilated cardiomyopathy from chronic alcohol use (alcoholic cardiomyopathy)","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 52-year-old right-handed man comes to the physician because of 4 months of progressive dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. He has consumed 10 to 12 beers daily for 15 years. His pulse is 104/min and blood pressure is 112/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention, bibasilar crackles, an S3 gallop, and a laterally displaced apical impulse. Coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis. Echocardiography shows dilation of all four chambers with relatively thin walls, diffuse hypokinesis, and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 25% (reference 55% to 70%). Diagnosis: dilated cardiomyopathy caused by chronic alcohol use (alcoholic cardiomyopathy).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C25-Q1","stem":"Ethanol and its metabolite acetaldehyde are directly toxic to myocytes and weaken their contraction. In a healthy ventricular myocyte, which of the following directly triggers release of most of the calcium that activates contraction?","options":["T-tubule voltage sensors mechanically pulling ryanodine receptors open","Calcium binding calmodulin and activating myosin light-chain kinase","IP3 binding to receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum after Gq signaling","Calcium entering through L-type channels and opening ryanodine receptors"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","dok":2,"approach":["The question asks what starts calcium release in cardiac muscle specifically.","Cardiac muscle uses calcium-induced calcium release: a small trigger influx releases a much larger store.","Rule out the skeletal muscle (mechanical coupling) and smooth muscle (IP3, calmodulin) mechanisms."],"why":"During the plateau of the ventricular action potential, L-type (dihydropyridine) calcium channels in the T tubules open. This small calcium influx opens ryanodine receptors (RyR2) on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which release most of the calcium that binds troponin C. This is calcium-induced calcium release. Because the trigger influx and the sarcoplasmic reticulum store set how much calcium is released, they help set the force of each beat.","whyNot":["Direct mechanical coupling of the T-tubule voltage sensor (DHP receptor) to RyR1 is how skeletal muscle releases calcium. Cardiac muscle needs calcium entry to open RyR2.","Calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light-chain kinase drives smooth muscle contraction. Cardiac muscle is regulated by calcium binding to troponin C on the thin filament.","IP3-mediated calcium release is a main pathway in smooth muscle after Gq-coupled receptor activation. It plays only a minor role in ventricular myocytes.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Excitation-contraction coupling in cardiac muscle","Cardiac versus skeletal versus smooth muscle calcium handling"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q2","stem":"In his liver, alcohol dehydrogenase converts ethanol to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase converts acetaldehyde to acetate. Both reactions reduce the same cofactor. Which of the following ratios is most likely increased in his hepatocytes?","options":["NADPH to NADP+","ATP to ADP","NADH to NAD+","FADH2 to FAD"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the two dehydrogenase steps of ethanol metabolism.","Both use NAD+ as the electron acceptor, producing NADH.","Large alcohol loads therefore raise the NADH to NAD+ ratio."],"why":"Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase both transfer electrons to NAD+, so heavy drinking raises the NADH to NAD+ ratio. The high ratio pushes pyruvate toward lactate and oxaloacetate toward malate, which slows gluconeogenesis. It also slows fatty acid oxidation and favors glycerol-3-phosphate formation, which promotes fatty liver.","whyNot":["NADPH is used, not produced, by the microsomal ethanol-oxidizing system (CYP2E1), which oxidizes NADPH to NADP+. NADPH is mainly made by the pentose phosphate pathway.","ATP and ADP describe energy charge. They are not the electron carriers reduced by these dehydrogenase reactions.","Correct.","FAD is the cofactor for enzymes such as succinate dehydrogenase. Neither alcohol dehydrogenase nor aldehyde dehydrogenase uses FAD."],"study":{"topics":["Ethanol metabolism","Effects of a high NADH to NAD+ ratio"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q3","stem":"A new grade 2/6 holosystolic murmur is heard best at the apex and radiates to the left axilla. Which of the following best explains this murmur?","options":["Mitral valve, leaflets pulled apart by a dilated ventricle","Aortic valve, cusps unable to close in a dilated aortic root","Tricuspid valve, leaflets pulled apart by a dilated right ventricle","Pulmonary valve, cusps unable to close in a dilated pulmonary trunk"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","dok":3,"approach":["A holosystolic murmur means blood leaks backward through an atrioventricular valve during systole.","Apex location with radiation to the axilla points to the mitral valve.","In dilated cardiomyopathy, annular dilation and outward displacement of the papillary muscles stop the leaflets from meeting (functional regurgitation)."],"why":"Mitral regurgitation produces a holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex that radiates to the left axilla. In dilated cardiomyopathy the leaflets are structurally normal, but the mitral annulus stretches and the papillary muscles are pulled outward and apically as the ventricle enlarges. The leaflets can no longer meet in systole, so blood leaks back into the left atrium. This is called functional or secondary mitral regurgitation.","whyNot":["Correct.","Aortic regurgitation from a dilated root causes an early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, not a holosystolic apical murmur.","Tricuspid regurgitation is also holosystolic and common in dilated cardiomyopathy, but it is heard best at the left lower sternal border and gets louder with inspiration. It does not radiate to the axilla.","Pulmonary regurgitation causes a diastolic murmur at the left upper sternal border, usually with pulmonary hypertension."],"study":{"topics":["Valve auscultation areas","Functional mitral regurgitation"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-D2"]}},{"id":"CV-C25-Q4","stem":"Eight months later, he develops sudden weakness of the right face and right arm and nonfluent speech with intact comprehension. Echocardiography shows a thrombus at the left ventricular apex. An embolus from this thrombus has most likely occluded which of the following arteries?","options":["Left anterior cerebral artery","Left middle cerebral artery","Right middle cerebral artery","Basilar artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Localize the deficit: right face and arm weakness with nonfluent speech and intact comprehension (expressive aphasia) means a left (dominant) frontal lesion in a right-handed person.","Face and arm motor cortex and Broca area are supplied by the middle cerebral artery.","Trace the embolus: left ventricle, aorta, left common carotid, left internal carotid, then straight into the left middle cerebral artery."],"why":"A poorly contracting, dilated ventricle allows blood to pool (stasis), so mural thrombi form, often at the apex. An embolus leaving the left ventricle enters the aorta and can reach either carotid. The middle cerebral artery is the direct continuation of the internal carotid and is the most common site for cardiac emboli. The left middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral motor cortex for the right face and arm and, in most right-handed people, the Broca speech area.","whyNot":["The left anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial motor cortex, so it causes right leg more than arm weakness and spares the face.","Correct.","A right middle cerebral artery embolus would cause left-sided face and arm weakness and usually neglect, not aphasia.","Basilar artery occlusion affects the brainstem and occipital lobes, causing cranial nerve deficits, bilateral signs, or reduced consciousness."],"study":{"topics":["Cardioembolic stroke","Cerebral arterial territories"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C26","system":"CV","dx":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he collapsed during a sprint drill at college soccer practice and regained consciousness within 1 minute. He has had chest tightness with strenuous exercise for several months. His paternal uncle died suddenly at age 34 years. Physical examination shows a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases in intensity when he stands from a squatting position and decreases with sustained handgrip. ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy with deep, narrow Q waves in the lateral leads. Echocardiography shows asymmetric septal thickening of 24 mm (reference up to about 11 mm), a small left ventricular cavity, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, and a resting outflow gradient of 50 mm Hg. Diagnosis: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C26-Q1","stem":"Genetic testing finds a pathogenic variant in MYH7, the gene for beta-myosin heavy chain. His father carries the same variant. If the patient later has children with a partner who does not carry the variant, which of the following is the probability that each child inherits it?","options":["25%","75%","100%","50%"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the inheritance pattern: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from sarcomere gene variants is autosomal dominant.","A heterozygous parent passes the variant allele to each child with a probability of one half.","Penetrance affects whether a carrier gets disease, not whether the allele is inherited."],"why":"Most familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is autosomal dominant and is caused by variants in sarcomere genes, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (cardiac myosin-binding protein C). The patient is heterozygous, so each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the variant. Penetrance is incomplete and age-dependent, and severity varies even within one family, so relatives who carry the same variant may have mild disease or none. First-degree relatives are offered genetic testing and echocardiographic screening.","whyNot":["25% is the chance that a child of two carrier parents is affected in an autosomal recessive disorder.","75% is the chance of inheriting at least one variant allele when both parents are heterozygous for a dominant trait.","100% would apply only if the patient were homozygous for the variant, which is rare.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal dominant inheritance and penetrance","Sarcomere genes in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"comps":["CV-14"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q2","stem":"A teammate's screening echocardiogram shows a left ventricular wall thickness of 13 mm. Which of the following additional findings would most strongly favor physiologic athlete's heart over hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","options":["Thickening confined to the septum, sparing the free wall","Impaired early diastolic relaxation on Doppler imaging","Regression of wall thickness after 3 months of detraining","A small left ventricular cavity, under 45 mm at end-diastole"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","dok":3,"approach":["A wall thickness of 13 to 15 mm is a gray zone where athlete's heart and mild hypertrophic cardiomyopathy overlap.","Athlete's heart is a reversible adaptation to training with normal relaxation and an enlarged cavity.","Pick the finding that only a physiologic adaptation would show."],"why":"Training causes a physiologic, reversible increase in cardiac mass, usually with an enlarged left ventricular cavity and normal diastolic function. When training stops for about 3 months, the wall thickness falls in athlete's heart but not in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, whose hypertrophy comes from a sarcomere defect. Regression with detraining therefore points strongly toward a physiologic adaptation.","whyNot":["Asymmetric septal thickening is the classic pattern of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Athletic hypertrophy is usually symmetric.","Impaired relaxation favors hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Athlete's heart typically has normal or enhanced diastolic filling.","Correct.","A small cavity under about 45 mm favors hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Trained athletes usually have a normal or enlarged cavity, often over 55 mm."],"study":{"topics":["Athlete's heart versus hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Cardiac adaptation to training"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q3","stem":"His syncope occurred during an intense sprint. Which of the following pairs of changes during exercise most increases his outflow tract obstruction?","options":["Decreased heart rate and increased end-diastolic volume","Increased afterload and increased end-diastolic volume","Decreased contractility and increased afterload","Increased contractility and decreased afterload"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","dok":3,"approach":["In this disease the obstruction is dynamic: it worsens whenever the left ventricular cavity gets smaller or ejects faster.","During exercise, sympathetic drive raises contractility and heart rate, and dilation of muscle arterioles lowers systemic vascular resistance.","Stronger, faster ejection into a lower resistance pulls the anterior mitral leaflet toward the septum and narrows the outflow tract."],"why":"Exercise raises sympathetic tone, which increases contractility and heart rate, and metabolic vasodilation in working muscle lowers systemic vascular resistance. Stronger contraction empties the small ventricle further, and lower afterload speeds ejection. Both bring the thickened septum and the anterior mitral leaflet (systolic anterior motion) closer together, raising the outflow gradient. Cardiac output then cannot rise to meet demand, and syncope or a ventricular arrhythmia can follow.","whyNot":["A slower heart rate and a larger end-diastolic volume widen the outflow tract and lessen the obstruction, as with squatting or leg raising.","Higher afterload and a larger ventricular volume both reduce the obstruction. Squatting produces both and softens the murmur.","Decreased contractility and increased afterload both reduce the obstruction. This is why handgrip, which raises afterload, softens his murmur.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Dynamic outflow obstruction","Cardiovascular response to exercise"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C26-Q4","stem":"If his collapse had been fatal, which of the following findings on microscopic examination of the interventricular septum would most likely be seen at autopsy?","options":["Amorphous pink extracellular deposits with apple-green birefringence","Coagulation necrosis with neutrophils and loss of nuclei","Hypertrophied myocytes arranged in a chaotic, swirling pattern","Lymphocytic infiltrate surrounding necrotic myocytes"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","dok":2,"approach":["Link the diagnosis to its histologic hallmark.","Sarcomere protein defects produce hypertrophied myocytes that lose their parallel arrangement (myofiber disarray).","Rule out the patterns of myocarditis, amyloidosis, and acute infarction."],"why":"The characteristic histology of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is myocyte disarray: large, hypertrophied myocytes that branch and run in a disorganized, swirling pattern, most marked in the septum, with interstitial and replacement fibrosis. Disarray and fibrosis create an unstable electrical substrate. Ventricular arrhythmias from this substrate are the main cause of sudden death in young athletes with this disease.","whyNot":["Amorphous pink deposits with apple-green birefringence on Congo red staining define amyloidosis, which causes restrictive cardiomyopathy.","Coagulation necrosis with neutrophils is seen about 1 to 3 days after a myocardial infarction.","Correct.","A lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis is the picture of viral or other lymphocytic myocarditis."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of the cardiomyopathies","Sudden cardiac death in young athletes"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C27","system":"CV","dx":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy from hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of 6 months of progressive dyspnea on exertion, leg swelling, and early satiety. He underwent surgery for bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome 5 years ago. Blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention that increases with inspiration and hepatomegaly. ECG shows low QRS voltage in the limb leads. Echocardiography shows a left ventricular wall thickness of 17 mm (reference up to about 11 mm), a normal-sized ventricle, an ejection fraction of 55%, markedly enlarged atria, and no pericardial effusion. Serum and urine studies show no monoclonal protein. A technetium pyrophosphate scan shows intense cardiac uptake, and genetic testing finds a pathogenic transthyretin (TTR) variant. Diagnosis: restrictive cardiomyopathy from hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C27-Q1","stem":"Transthyretin is made mainly in the liver and normally circulates as a tetramer that carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein. Which of the following mechanisms best explains how his TTR variant causes disease?","options":["Unstable tetramers release monomers that misfold into beta-sheet fibrils","Clonal plasma cells secrete free light chains that form fibrils","Low alpha-galactosidase A activity lets glycosphingolipids build up in lysosomes","Excess intestinal iron uptake deposits iron within myocytes"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the protein: transthyretin is a transport tetramer made by the liver.","Pathogenic variants make the tetramer less stable, so it falls apart into monomers.","The monomers misfold into insoluble beta-pleated sheet fibrils that deposit in the heart and nerves."],"why":"Hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis is autosomal dominant. A single amino acid substitution destabilizes the transthyretin tetramer, which dissociates into monomers that misfold and assemble into beta-pleated sheet amyloid fibrils. Fibrils deposit in the myocardial interstitium, the carpal tunnel, and peripheral nerves. Wild-type transthyretin can do the same with age, mostly in older men, without any variant.","whyNot":["Correct.","Free light chains from a plasma cell clone cause AL amyloidosis. His negative serum and urine studies for monoclonal protein and the TTR variant point away from it.","Alpha-galactosidase A deficiency is Fabry disease, an X-linked lysosomal storage disorder that can thicken the ventricle. It does not form amyloid fibrils.","Increased iron absorption is hereditary hemochromatosis (usually HFE variants), which can cause a dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy through iron overload, not amyloid."],"study":{"topics":["Protein misfolding and amyloid","Hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q2","stem":"Two months later, he reports dysphagia to solid foods. A barium swallow shows smooth extrinsic compression of the anterior wall of the esophagus at the level of the heart. Enlargement of which of the following chambers most likely explains this finding?","options":["Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy enlarges the atria because they must push blood into stiff ventricles.","Recall which surface of the heart each chamber forms.","The left atrium forms most of the base (posterior surface) and sits directly in front of the esophagus."],"why":"The left atrium forms most of the posterior surface (base) of the heart and lies just anterior to the esophagus, separated from it by the pericardium. When the left atrium enlarges, it can indent the esophagus and cause trouble swallowing solids. The same relationship is why transesophageal echocardiography gives such clear views of the left atrium.","whyNot":["The right atrium forms the right border of the heart and receives the venae cavae. It does not lie against the esophagus.","Correct.","The right ventricle forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface, the side farthest from the esophagus.","The left ventricle forms the apex, the left border, and much of the inferior (diaphragmatic) surface."],"study":{"topics":["Surfaces and borders of the heart","Relations of the left atrium"],"comps":["CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q3","stem":"His echocardiogram shows thick ventricular walls, yet his ECG shows low QRS voltage. Which of the following best explains this combination of findings?","options":["Concentric myocyte hypertrophy from long-standing pressure overload","Fluid in the pericardial sac that damps the cardiac electrical signal","Thinning and scarring of the wall after a large transmural infarction","Extracellular deposits thickening the wall without adding myocytes"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","dok":3,"approach":["QRS voltage reflects the mass of electrically active myocytes.","Wall thickness on echo reflects everything in the wall, including extracellular material.","Thick walls with low voltage mean the thickening is from something electrically silent."],"why":"Amyloid fibrils accumulate between myocytes, so the wall thickens while the amount of working, electrically active muscle stays the same or falls. The ECG therefore shows low voltage despite a thick wall on echo, a classic clue to infiltrative cardiomyopathy. The deposits also make the ventricle stiff, which causes the restrictive filling, enlarged atria, and rising neck veins on inspiration.","whyNot":["True myocyte hypertrophy from pressure overload adds electrically active muscle and raises QRS voltage.","A pericardial effusion can lower QRS voltage, but his echocardiogram shows no effusion, and an effusion would not thicken the walls.","A large infarction thins and scars the wall. His walls are thick, and nothing in the case suggests a prior infarction.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Infiltrative cardiomyopathy","Restrictive physiology"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C27-Q4","stem":"His earlier carpal tunnel syndrome resulted from amyloid deposits in the carpal tunnel compressing a nerve. Which of the following deficits would this compression most likely cause?","options":["Weak wrist extension with wrist drop","Weak thumb adduction with a positive Froment sign","Weak thumb opposition with index fingertip sensory loss","Weak finger abduction with sensory loss in the little finger"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-E1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the nerve that passes through the carpal tunnel: the median nerve.","Recall what the median nerve supplies distal to the wrist: the thenar muscles and palmar skin of the thumb, index, middle, and half of the ring finger.","Match the deficit to that distribution."],"why":"The median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel under the flexor retinaculum. Amyloid in the retinaculum and tendon sheaths narrows the tunnel and compresses it. Distal to the wrist, the median nerve supplies the thenar muscles, including opponens pollicis, and the palmar skin of the lateral three and a half digits. Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome often appears years before the heart disease in transthyretin amyloidosis and is a useful early clue.","whyNot":["Wrist drop comes from radial nerve injury, usually in the arm, and the radial nerve does not pass through the carpal tunnel.","Weak thumb adduction (adductor pollicis) with a positive Froment sign is an ulnar nerve deficit.","Correct.","Weak finger abduction (dorsal interossei) and a numb little finger point to the ulnar nerve, which passes through the Guyon canal, not the carpal tunnel."],"study":{"topics":["Median nerve at the wrist","Extracardiac clues to amyloidosis"],"comps":["CV-18-E1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C28","system":"CV","dx":"Acute viral myocarditis from coxsackievirus B","codes":["CV-18-K6"],"case":"A 23-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 2 days of sharp chest pain, fatigue, and dyspnea. Ten days earlier she had fever, sore throat, and diarrhea that resolved spontaneously. Temperature is 38.1 °C (100.6 °F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Serum troponin I concentration is 3.2 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). ECG shows sinus tachycardia with diffuse nonspecific ST-segment and T-wave changes. Echocardiography shows global hypokinesis with an ejection fraction of 35% (reference 55% to 70%). Coronary angiography is normal. Endomyocardial biopsy shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis, and PCR of the tissue detects coxsackievirus B3. Diagnosis: acute viral myocarditis caused by coxsackievirus B.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C28-Q1","stem":"Coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family. After it enters a cell, its genome is translated directly by host ribosomes into one large polyprotein that viral proteases then cleave. Which of the following genome types allows this?","options":["Linear double-stranded DNA","Segmented double-stranded RNA","Negative-sense single-stranded RNA","Positive-sense single-stranded RNA"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Ask what a genome must be for ribosomes to read it right away.","Only positive-sense RNA can act directly as messenger RNA.","Picornaviruses are small, nonenveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses."],"why":"Picornaviruses, including coxsackieviruses, have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that works as messenger RNA as soon as it enters the cytoplasm. Host ribosomes translate it into one polyprotein, which viral proteases cleave into structural proteins and the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. The virus is nonenveloped, spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, and enters cardiomyocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR).","whyNot":["Double-stranded DNA must be transcribed into messenger RNA before translation, usually in the nucleus, as in adenoviruses and herpesviruses.","Segmented double-stranded RNA is the genome of reoviruses such as rotavirus, which must transcribe messenger RNA with a packaged polymerase.","Negative-sense RNA cannot be translated directly. These viruses (for example influenza and rabies) must carry their own RNA polymerase to make a positive strand first.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Picornavirus structure and replication","Viral genome types"],"comps":["CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q2","stem":"Infected cardiomyocytes display viral peptides on MHC class I molecules. Which of the following cells recognizes these complexes and directly kills the myocytes, contributing to the injury seen on her biopsy?","options":["CD8+ cytotoxic T cell","CD4+ helper T cell","Natural killer (NK) cell","Antibody-secreting plasma cell"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Peptide on MHC class I is read by a T-cell receptor plus the CD8 coreceptor.","CD8+ T cells kill their targets with perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand.","Match the effector cell to both the antigen presentation and the killing."],"why":"Every nucleated cell, including cardiomyocytes, presents peptides from intracellular proteins on MHC class I. CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognize viral peptide on MHC class I with their T-cell receptor and kill the infected cell through perforin and granzymes or Fas ligand. This clears the virus but also destroys myocytes, so much of the damage in viral myocarditis comes from the immune response itself.","whyNot":["Correct.","CD4+ helper T cells recognize peptide on MHC class II, which is found on antigen-presenting cells, and they mainly direct other cells rather than kill directly.","NK cells kill cells that have lost MHC class I (missing self) or are coated with antibody. They do not recognize specific peptide-MHC complexes.","Plasma cells secrete antibody against free virus and surface antigens. They do not recognize peptide-MHC complexes or kill cells themselves."],"study":{"topics":["MHC class I and CD8+ T cells","Immune injury in viral myocarditis"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q3","stem":"Myocarditis often causes arrhythmias. Inflammation that damages the intercalated discs can slow the spread of action potentials from one myocyte to the next. Which of the following junctions in the disc directly carries this current?","options":["Desmosomes","Gap junctions","Fasciae adherentes","Tight junctions"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":2,"approach":["List the parts of the intercalated disc: fasciae adherentes, desmosomes, and gap junctions.","Separate mechanical junctions from the electrical junction.","Gap junctions (connexin 43 channels) let ions flow between neighboring cells."],"why":"Gap junctions are channels built from connexins (mainly connexin 43 in ventricular muscle) that let ions pass directly between neighboring myocytes. This electrical coupling lets the myocardium act as a functional syncytium. Inflammation and scarring disturb gap junctions, which slows and fragments conduction and helps create reentrant arrhythmias.","whyNot":["Desmosomes anchor intermediate filaments (desmin) and hold cells together under strain. They do not pass current.","Correct.","Fasciae adherentes anchor actin filaments of the sarcomeres to the cell membrane and transmit force, not current.","Tight junctions seal epithelial and endothelial cells. They are not part of the intercalated disc."],"study":{"topics":["Intercalated disc structure","Cardiac muscle histology"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C28-Q4","stem":"One year later, her ejection fraction is 25% and all four chambers are dilated. Which of the following processes best explains this outcome?","options":["Atherosclerotic narrowing of the coronaries causing repeated infarcts","Interstitial amyloid deposits stiffening an otherwise normal ventricle","Inherited sarcomere protein defect causing myocyte disarray","Ongoing immune injury causing myocyte loss and replacement fibrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K6","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the new picture as dilated cardiomyopathy following myocarditis.","Most myocarditis resolves, but in some patients viral persistence or autoimmune injury continues.","Ongoing myocyte loss is replaced by fibrous tissue, and the weakened ventricle dilates."],"why":"Viral myocarditis is a recognized cause of dilated cardiomyopathy. In a minority of patients, persistent viral genome or an autoimmune response against cardiac proteins such as myosin keeps injuring myocytes after the acute infection. Lost myocytes are replaced by fibrosis, the remaining muscle cannot keep up, and the ventricle remodels into a dilated, poorly contracting chamber.","whyNot":["Her angiography was normal and she is young, so ischemic cardiomyopathy from coronary disease is very unlikely.","Amyloid causes a stiff, thick-walled restrictive cardiomyopathy with a normal-sized ventricle, not four-chamber dilation, and is not a result of myocarditis.","Sarcomere gene defects with myocyte disarray cause hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which is inherited and would not follow an infection.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Outcomes of myocarditis","Causes of dilated cardiomyopathy"],"comps":["CV-18-K6","CV-18-E1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C29","system":"CV","dx":"Dengue hemorrhagic fever (dengue with warning signs) in a second dengue infection","codes":["CV-18-K5"],"case":"A 31-year-old woman comes to the emergency department on day 5 of a febrile illness because of abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, and bleeding gums. The illness began 4 days after she returned from a 2-week trip to Southeast Asia, with sudden fever to 39.8 °C (103.6 °F), severe headache, retro-orbital pain, and intense myalgias and arthralgias. Her fever resolved today. She had confirmed dengue while living in the Caribbean 4 years ago. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 32,000/µL (reference 150,000 to 400,000/µL), leukocyte count of 2,900/µL (reference 4,500 to 11,000/µL), and hematocrit of 47% (reference 36% to 46%), up from 38% on day 2. Ultrasonography shows ascites and a right pleural effusion. Dengue NS1 antigen is positive. Diagnosis: dengue hemorrhagic fever (dengue with warning signs) during a second dengue infection.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C29-Q1","stem":"Her travel companion asks how to avoid this infection on a future trip. Which of the following characteristics of the vector should guide prevention?","options":["Anopheles mosquitoes biting mainly from dusk to dawn","Triatomine bugs biting the face during sleep at night","Aedes mosquitoes biting mainly during the daytime","Ixodes ticks needing to stay attached for more than a day"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Name the vector of dengue virus: Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti.","Recall its behavior: it bites during the day and breeds in small containers of standing water near homes.","Prevention therefore means daytime repellent and covering skin, not only bed nets at night."],"why":"Dengue virus is a flavivirus spread by Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti and also Aedes albopictus. Aedes aegypti bites mostly during the day, with peaks in the early morning and late afternoon, and breeds in water-holding containers around homes. Daytime repellent, long sleeves, and removing standing water matter more than bed nets alone.","whyNot":["Night-biting Anopheles mosquitoes transmit malaria, where bed nets are a mainstay of prevention.","Triatomine (kissing) bugs transmit Trypanosoma cruzi, the cause of Chagas disease, through feces rubbed into the bite or mucosa.","Correct.","Ixodes ticks transmit Lyme disease, and transmission usually requires attachment for more than a day."],"study":{"topics":["Arthropod vectors of cardiovascular infections","Dengue virus transmission"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-K5"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q2","stem":"Her first dengue infection was with a different serotype. Which of the following mechanisms best explains why this second infection is more severe?","options":["Non-neutralizing IgG boosts viral entry into Fc receptor-bearing monocytes","Cross-reactive IgE from the first infection triggers mast cell degranulation","Antigenic drift produces a new viral hemagglutinin that her antibodies miss","Virus-induced loss of CD4+ T cells leaves the new infection unchecked"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Note the key history: a prior infection with a different dengue serotype.","Antibodies from the first serotype bind the new serotype but do not neutralize it.","The antibody-coated virus is taken up through Fc gamma receptors on monocytes and macrophages, where it replicates (antibody-dependent enhancement)."],"why":"There are four dengue serotypes. Infection gives lasting immunity to that serotype only. In a later infection with a different serotype, cross-reactive IgG binds the virus without neutralizing it, and the Fc portion directs it into Fc gamma receptor-bearing monocytes and macrophages. This antibody-dependent enhancement raises viral load and, with cross-reactive T cells and cytokine release, drives the capillary leak of dengue hemorrhagic fever.","whyNot":["Correct.","IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation is type I hypersensitivity (allergy and anaphylaxis). It is not the basis of severe dengue.","Antigenic drift of hemagglutinin describes influenza. Her two dengue infections were caused by different, established serotypes, not a drifted strain.","Progressive loss of CD4+ T cells is the hallmark of HIV infection, not dengue."],"study":{"topics":["Antibody-dependent enhancement","Dengue serotypes and immunity"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-K5"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q3","stem":"Between day 2 and day 5, her hematocrit rose while ascites and a pleural effusion appeared. She has no history of heart, liver, or kidney disease, and her neck veins are flat. Which of the following changes in capillary fluid exchange best explains these findings?","options":["Obstructed lymphatic drainage of the pleura and peritoneum","Increased capillary permeability to plasma and protein","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from impaired albumin synthesis","Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["A rising hematocrit with new effusions means plasma is leaving the vessels while red cells stay behind.","Check each Starling force against the case: flat neck veins argue against high venous pressure, and she has no liver or kidney disease.","Dengue injures the endothelium, so permeability rises and the reflection coefficient for protein falls."],"why":"Fluid movement across capillaries depends on hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and on how permeable the wall is to water and protein. In dengue, viral and immune effects on the endothelium make capillaries leak plasma and protein into serous cavities. Plasma volume falls, red cells are concentrated, and the hematocrit rises. This leak usually peaks around the time the fever breaks and can lead to shock.","whyNot":["Lymphatic obstruction causes protein-rich edema over weeks, usually from tumor, surgery, or filariasis. Nothing in the case suggests it.","Correct.","Failing albumin synthesis occurs with chronic liver disease and develops over weeks because albumin has a half-life of about 3 weeks, which does not fit a 3-day change.","Venous congestion raises capillary hydrostatic pressure in heart failure, but her neck veins are flat and she has no heart disease."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Capillary leak in dengue"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C29-Q4","stem":"Which of the following complications is she at greatest risk of developing over the next 24 to 48 hours?","options":["Ischemic stroke from a cardioembolic clot","Rupture of an enlarged spleen","Acute kidney failure from immune complex glomerulonephritis","Hypovolemic shock from continued plasma leakage"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-K5","dok":2,"approach":["Place her in the course of dengue: the fever has just broken and warning signs have appeared, which marks the critical phase.","The critical phase usually lasts about 24 to 48 hours and is driven by capillary leak.","Ongoing leak shrinks plasma volume and can progress to shock."],"why":"Dengue has a febrile phase, a critical phase, and a recovery phase. The critical phase begins around defervescence, usually about days 3 to 7 of illness, and lasts about 24 to 48 hours. Plasma leakage during this window can reduce circulating volume enough to cause hypovolemic shock (dengue shock syndrome), which is the main cause of death. Her rising hematocrit, effusions, and warning signs put her at high risk.","whyNot":["Cardioembolic stroke requires a cardiac source of clot, such as atrial fibrillation, which she does not have.","Splenic rupture is a rare event and is classically linked to infectious mononucleosis.","Immune complex glomerulonephritis is not a typical early complication of dengue. Kidney injury in dengue usually follows shock.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of dengue","Dengue shock syndrome"],"comps":["CV-18-K5","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C30","system":"CV","dx":"Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency with high-output heart failure","codes":["CV-18-E1"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician 4 months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery because of dyspnea, leg swelling, and burning paresthesias in both feet. He has vomited after most meals for 6 weeks. His pulse is 116/min and blood pressure is 112/46 mm Hg. Physical examination shows warm extremities, bounding pulses, jugular venous distention, and pitting edema to the knees. Echocardiography shows dilated ventricles, an ejection fraction of 65%, and a cardiac output of 10 L/min (reference 4 to 8 L/min). Serum lactate concentration is 4.6 mmol/L (reference 0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). Whole-blood thiamine concentration is low. Diagnosis: wet beriberi, thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causing high-output heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C30-Q1","stem":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of thiamine and a cofactor for several enzymes. Decreased activity of which of the following enzymes best explains his increased serum lactate concentration?","options":["Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase","Lactate dehydrogenase"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Identify which listed enzyme needs thiamine pyrophosphate.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle by converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA.","When it slows, pyruvate builds up and is converted to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase."],"why":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex needs thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, and NAD+. Without enough thiamine, pyruvate cannot enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA. Pyruvate accumulates and is reduced to lactate, causing lactic acidosis, and aerobic ATP production falls in high-demand tissues such as heart, nerve, and brain.","whyNot":["Pyruvate carboxylase uses biotin, not thiamine. Its deficiency also raises lactate, but his deficiency is thiamine.","Correct.","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase is a glycolytic enzyme that uses NAD+, not thiamine. Reduced activity would slow glycolysis and lower pyruvate and lactate production.","Lactate dehydrogenase makes lactate from pyruvate. Reduced activity would lower lactate, and it does not need thiamine."],"study":{"topics":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex cofactors","Causes of lactic acidosis"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q2","stem":"To confirm a functional deficiency, a laboratory measures an enzyme's activity in his erythrocytes before and after adding thiamine pyrophosphate. A large increase with the added cofactor confirms deficiency. Which of the following enzymes and pathways does this test measure?","options":["Transketolase, pentose phosphate pathway","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, pentose phosphate pathway","Glutathione reductase, glutathione recycling","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, citric acid cycle"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["List the thiamine-dependent enzymes: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Red blood cells have no mitochondria, so the three mitochondrial dehydrogenases are absent.","That leaves transketolase in the nonoxidative pentose phosphate pathway."],"why":"Transketolase is a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme in the nonoxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway, which red blood cells use. The erythrocyte transketolase activity test measures activity with and without added thiamine pyrophosphate. A large increase with added cofactor shows that the enzyme was short of thiamine, which confirms functional deficiency.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is in the pentose phosphate pathway but uses NADP+, not thiamine pyrophosphate.","Glutathione reductase uses FAD, so a similar activation test with added FAD assesses riboflavin (vitamin B2) status, not thiamine.","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase does need thiamine pyrophosphate, but it is mitochondrial and red blood cells have no mitochondria."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pentose phosphate pathway"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q3","stem":"His deficiency also reduces mitochondrial ATP production in his cardiomyocytes. In the cross-bridge cycle, which of the following steps is directly caused by ATP binding to the myosin head?","options":["Shift of tropomyosin off the myosin-binding sites on actin","Power stroke that slides actin toward the sarcomere center","Release of the myosin head from actin","Release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","dok":2,"approach":["Walk through the cross-bridge cycle step by step.","ATP binding detaches myosin from actin, ATP hydrolysis cocks the head, and phosphate release triggers the power stroke.","Calcium release and the tropomyosin shift come from excitation and calcium binding to troponin, not from ATP binding to myosin."],"why":"In the cross-bridge cycle, myosin stays tightly bound to actin until ATP binds the myosin head, which lowers its affinity for actin and releases it. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate then re-cocks the head, and release of phosphate after rebinding triggers the power stroke. Without ATP, myosin stays locked to actin (rigor). ATP is also needed for SERCA to pump calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum so the muscle can relax, so an energy-starved heart struggles with both contraction and relaxation.","whyNot":["Tropomyosin moves off the myosin-binding sites when calcium binds troponin C.","The power stroke is triggered by release of inorganic phosphate after the cocked head binds actin, not by ATP binding.","Correct.","Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is triggered by calcium entry through L-type channels opening ryanodine receptors."],"study":{"topics":["Cross-bridge cycle","Myocardial energy use"],"comps":["CV-5"]}},{"id":"CV-C30-Q4","stem":"His cardiac output is high, yet he has heart failure with edema. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the high-output state?","options":["A left-to-right shunt through an acquired septal defect","Increased blood viscosity from a rising hematocrit","Sympathetic vasoconstriction raising afterload and filling pressure","Peripheral vasodilation lowering systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-E1","dok":3,"approach":["Read the clues: warm extremities, bounding pulses, a low diastolic pressure, and a wide pulse pressure.","These point to low systemic vascular resistance.","Low resistance raises venous return and cardiac output, while the energy-poor heart and kidney sodium retention lead to congestion."],"why":"In wet beriberi, impaired oxidative metabolism in tissues is thought to cause local metabolic vasodilation, so peripheral arterioles dilate. Systemic vascular resistance falls, venous return and cardiac output rise, and the pulse pressure widens. The kidneys sense low effective arterial filling and retain sodium and water through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. The heart, short of energy, cannot keep pace, so filling pressures rise and edema forms. Thiamine replacement usually reverses the high-output state quickly.","whyNot":["A left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary flow, not systemic output, and nothing in the case suggests a septal defect.","Higher blood viscosity raises resistance and would tend to lower cardiac output, and his hematocrit is not reported as high.","Sympathetic vasoconstriction raises resistance and lowers output. It describes low-output heart failure with cool, pale extremities.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Wet versus dry beriberi"],"comps":["CV-18-E1","CV-13","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C31","system":"CV","dx":"Malignant pericardial effusion from metastatic lung adenocarcinoma","codes":["CV-18-F1","CV-18-F2"],"case":"A 61-year-old woman who has never smoked comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of worsening shortness of breath, fatigue, and a dull sensation of fullness in her chest. Six months ago, biopsy of a right upper lobe lung mass showed adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 102/min, respirations are 22/min, and blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows distant heart sounds. Systolic pressure falls by 6 mm Hg with inspiration (normal fall is under 10 mm Hg). An ECG shows low QRS voltage, and a chest x-ray shows an enlarged, water-bottle-shaped cardiac silhouette. Echocardiography shows a large circumferential pericardial effusion with no collapse of the right atrium or right ventricle. Pericardiocentesis drains 800 mL of bloody fluid. Diagnosis: malignant pericardial effusion from metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C31-Q1","stem":"Pericardiocentesis through the left fifth intercostal space immediately adjacent to the sternum is considered. Which of the following anatomic features allows a needle at this site to enter the pericardial sac without passing through the left lung?","options":["The costodiaphragmatic recess, which holds no lung during quiet breathing","The cardiac notch of the left lung, with a matching notch in the pleura","The oblique fissure, which separates the left upper and lower lobes here","The lingula, which curls behind the heart and out of the needle path"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Picture what lies under the left fifth intercostal space beside the sternum.","Recall that the anterior border of the left lung and its pleura curve away from the midline at the level of the fourth and fifth costal cartilages.","That gap leaves part of the pericardium covered only by the chest wall, so a needle can enter the sac without crossing lung."],"why":"The anterior border of the left lung bends laterally to form the cardiac notch, and the left pleural sac has a smaller notch in the same place. This leaves a small area of pericardium with no lung or pleura in front of it, sometimes called the bare area. A needle placed in the left fifth or sixth intercostal space close to the sternum can enter the pericardial sac here. Staying close to the sternum also matters because the internal thoracic artery runs just lateral to the sternal border.","whyNot":["The costodiaphragmatic recess lies much lower, near the eighth to tenth ribs at the lateral chest wall, and it is still lined by parietal pleura. It is the target for draining a pleural effusion.","Correct.","The oblique fissure is a cleft between two lobes. A needle placed into a fissure still passes through pleura and lung tissue on either side.","The lingula is the tongue-shaped lower part of the left upper lobe. It lies in front of the heart, below the cardiac notch, so it would be in the way of a needle aimed lower."],"study":{"topics":["Surface anatomy of the lungs and pleura","Pericardiocentesis approaches"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q2","stem":"Her 800-mL effusion developed without hypotension. In contrast, about 200 mL of blood accumulating within minutes after a penetrating cardiac wound can cause fatal tamponade. Which of the following properties of the pericardium best explains this difference?","options":["The fibrous pericardium stretches when filled slowly, keeping sac pressure low","The serous pericardium absorbs slowly forming fluid as fast as it is produced","The right ventricle thickens over weeks and resists compression from outside","The pericardium stiffens over several weeks and shields the chambers from fluid"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-3","dok":3,"approach":["Compare the two cases: a large volume tolerated versus a small volume that kills. The difference is the rate of filling.","Recall the pericardial pressure and volume curve: the tough fibrous pericardium is stiff over minutes but stretches over weeks.","Tamponade depends on the pressure inside the sac exceeding the filling pressures of the heart, and pressure is what slow filling keeps low."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium is dense collagen that barely stretches over minutes. When fluid enters quickly, pressure in the sac rises steeply, exceeds right heart filling pressure, and the chambers cannot fill. When fluid builds over weeks, the sac gradually enlarges, so pressure rises little even with a large volume. This is why a slowly growing malignant effusion can reach 1 L or more before tamponade, while acute hemopericardium of about 200 mL can be fatal.","whyNot":["Correct.","If the serous layer removed fluid as fast as it formed, no effusion would build up. Her 800 mL effusion shows that formation outpaced removal.","The right ventricle does not hypertrophy as a defense against outside pressure. Right ventricular hypertrophy follows pressure overload from inside, as in pulmonary hypertension.","A stiffer pericardium would raise sac pressure faster, making tamponade more likely. Stiffening and scarring describe constrictive pericarditis, which limits filling."],"study":{"topics":["Pericardial pressure and volume relationship","Rate of fluid accumulation and tamponade"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q3","stem":"Molecular testing of her tumor shows a small deletion in one allele of the EGFR gene. The deletion keeps the receptor tyrosine kinase active in the absence of bound growth factor. Which of the following best describes this mutation?","options":["Loss of function in a tumor suppressor gene, so both copies must be lost","Loss of function in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing unstable repeats","Gain of function in a proto-oncogene, so one altered copy is enough","Gain of function from a translocation that fuses two separate genes"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the effect: the receptor is active without its ligand, so the change adds function.","Classify the gene: a normal gene whose overactivity drives cell growth is a proto-oncogene.","Recall that activating changes in proto-oncogenes act dominantly at the cell level, so one altered allele is enough."],"why":"EGFR is a receptor tyrosine kinase. Normally, binding of a growth factor causes the receptors to pair up and phosphorylate each other, which turns on growth signals such as the RAS and MAPK pathway. Small in-frame deletions in the kinase region keep the receptor active without ligand. Because the altered protein is overactive, one mutated copy drives growth even with a normal second copy. EGFR mutations are more common in lung adenocarcinomas of people who have never smoked.","whyNot":["Tumor suppressors such as TP53 and RB1 usually need both copies lost (the two-hit model). Her mutation adds activity to a growth receptor, which is the opposite effect.","Mismatch repair defects, as in Lynch syndrome, cause microsatellite instability. They do not switch on a single receptor directly.","Correct.","A fusion gene from a chromosomal rearrangement, such as EML4 and ALK in some lung adenocarcinomas, also adds function. Her change is a small deletion within one gene."],"study":{"topics":["Proto-oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes","Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-F1"]}},{"id":"CV-C31-Q4","stem":"Cytologic examination of the pericardial fluid shows clusters of large atypical cells, some forming small glands. Reactive mesothelial cells can have a similar appearance. Which of the following immunohistochemical staining patterns best supports metastatic lung adenocarcinoma?","options":["TTF-1 and napsin A positive, calretinin negative","CK20 and CDX2 positive, CK7 negative","S-100 and HMB-45 positive, pan-cytokeratin negative","Calretinin positive and TTF-1 negative"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F1","dok":3,"approach":["Name the two cell types to separate: reactive mesothelium versus metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","Recall the markers: TTF-1 and napsin A mark lung adenocarcinoma, while calretinin marks mesothelial cells.","Pick the pattern that is positive for the lung markers and negative for the mesothelial marker."],"why":"Lung and breast cancers are the most common sources of metastasis to the pericardium, and malignant effusions are often bloody. TTF-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1) and napsin A are expressed by most lung adenocarcinomas. Calretinin is a calcium-binding protein expressed by mesothelial cells and mesothelioma. A tumor cell population that is TTF-1 and napsin A positive and calretinin negative supports metastatic lung adenocarcinoma.","whyNot":["Correct.","CK20 and CDX2 positive with CK7 negative is the typical colorectal adenocarcinoma pattern. Lung adenocarcinoma is usually CK7 positive and CK20 negative.","S-100 and HMB-45 positivity with no cytokeratin points to melanoma, which can also spread to the pericardium.","This pattern marks mesothelial cells. It fits reactive mesothelium or mesothelioma."],"study":{"topics":["Metastatic tumors of the pericardium","Immunohistochemistry of carcinoma versus mesothelium"],"comps":["CV-18-F1","CV-18-F2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C32","system":"CV","dx":"Tuberculous pericardial effusion progressing to cardiac tamponade","codes":["CV-18-F2","CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with untreated HIV infection (CD4 count 140 cells/µL, reference 500 to 1500) is brought to the emergency department because of lightheadedness and shortness of breath for 2 days. He has had night sweats, weight loss, and cough for 6 weeks. Temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 86/64 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention to the angle of the jaw and muffled heart sounds. Systolic pressure falls by 18 mm Hg with each inspiration (normal fall is under 10 mm Hg). Echocardiography shows a large pericardial effusion with diastolic collapse of the right atrium and right ventricle. The drained fluid is an exudate with a high adenosine deaminase level, and culture later grows Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Diagnosis: tuberculous pericardial effusion progressing to cardiac tamponade.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C32-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains the large fall in his systolic pressure during each inspiration?","options":["Inspiration triggers vagal slowing that lowers cardiac output with each breath","Inspiration lowers right ventricular filling, so less blood reaches the lungs","Inspiration adds right ventricular filling that bows the septum leftward","Inspiration raises intrathoracic pressure and squeezes the venae cavae shut"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Name the sign: a fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mm Hg on inspiration is pulsus paradoxus.","Recall normal breathing effects: inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure and increases venous return to the right heart.","Apply the fixed space: with the sac full, the right ventricle can only expand by pushing the septum into the left ventricle, which then fills less and ejects less."],"why":"Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, which draws more blood into the right atrium and right ventricle. Normally the free walls of the heart expand to take this volume. In tamponade the fluid-filled sac fixes the total volume of the heart, so the extra right ventricular filling bows the interventricular septum toward the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, so systolic pressure drops more than 10 mm Hg. This ventricular interdependence is the main cause of pulsus paradoxus.","whyNot":["Inspiration speeds the heart rate slightly by reducing vagal tone (respiratory sinus arrhythmia). Vagal slowing comes with expiration.","Inspiration increases right ventricular filling. The problem is that the extra right-sided volume crowds the left ventricle.","Correct.","Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure. Raised intrathoracic pressure that limits venous return happens during a Valsalva maneuver or positive pressure ventilation."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsus paradoxus","Ventricular interdependence"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-F2"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q2","stem":"Before the fluid is drained, a right heart catheter is placed. Normal values are right atrial (RA) pressure 2 to 6 mm Hg and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 6 to 12 mm Hg. Which of the following sets of pressures (mm Hg) is most likely to be found?","options":["RA 2, RV 18/2, wedge 3","RA 5, RV 42/6, wedge 26","RA 17, RV 64/17, wedge 10","RA 19, RV 34/19, wedge 19"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that in tamponade the pressure in the sac squeezes every chamber equally during diastole.","Predict that right atrial pressure, right ventricular diastolic pressure, and wedge pressure all rise to about the same value as the pericardial pressure.","Pick the set with high, equal diastolic pressures on both sides of the heart."],"why":"In tamponade the chambers can only fill until their pressure matches the pressure in the pericardial sac. Because the fluid surrounds all four chambers, the diastolic pressures in the right atrium, right ventricle, and left atrium (measured as the wedge pressure) all rise and become nearly equal, often around 15 to 20 mm Hg. Equal, raised diastolic pressures across the heart are the classic catheter finding. Constrictive pericarditis also equalizes pressures, but the clinical picture of a large effusion and diastolic chamber collapse points to tamponade.","whyNot":["Low pressures everywhere fit hypovolemia. His distended neck veins show that his right-sided pressure is high.","A high wedge pressure with a normal right atrial pressure points to left heart failure or mitral valve disease, with the left side congested and the right side spared.","A high right ventricular systolic pressure with a normal wedge pressure fits pulmonary hypertension or a pulmonary embolus with right heart strain. The left-sided filling pressure is not raised to match.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hemodynamics of cardiac tamponade","Right heart catheter pressures"],"comps":["CV-18-F2","CV-6"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q3","stem":"A pericardial biopsy specimen shows caseating granulomas. Which of the following cytokines, secreted by CD4+ T lymphocytes, activates his macrophages to kill this organism and form granulomas?","options":["Transforming growth factor beta","Interferon gamma","Interleukin 4","Interleukin 12"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the response: granulomas are a cell-mediated (type IV) response to an intracellular organism.","Identify the helper cell: Th1 CD4 T cells drive this response.","Name the Th1 cytokine that activates macrophages: interferon gamma."],"why":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis survives inside macrophages. Macrophages present its antigens and release interleukin 12, which pushes CD4 T cells toward the Th1 type. Th1 cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages to kill ingested bacilli and to transform into epithelioid cells and giant cells that form granulomas. With few CD4 cells, people with HIV form poor granulomas and are prone to spread of tuberculosis outside the lungs, including to the pericardium.","whyNot":["Transforming growth factor beta comes from regulatory T cells and other cells. It dampens immune responses and promotes fibrosis.","Correct.","Interleukin 4 comes from Th2 cells. It drives IgE class switching and allergic responses and does not activate macrophages to kill mycobacteria.","Interleukin 12 is made by macrophages and dendritic cells. It steers T cells toward Th1, but it comes from the macrophage side of the loop."],"study":{"topics":["Th1 response and granuloma formation","Tuberculosis in HIV infection"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C32-Q4","stem":"The organism stains poorly with Gram stain but retains carbol fuchsin after decolorization with acid-alcohol. Which of the following components of its cell envelope is responsible for this staining property?","options":["Polysaccharide capsule","Mycolic acid","Lipoteichoic acid","Lipopolysaccharide"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Name the stain: holding carbol fuchsin after an acid-alcohol wash means the organism is acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen stain).","Recall what makes mycobacteria acid-fast: a waxy, lipid-rich cell wall.","Pick the long-chain fatty acid that forms that waxy layer."],"why":"Mycobacteria have a cell wall rich in mycolic acids, very long-chain fatty acids linked to arabinogalactan and peptidoglycan. This waxy layer keeps out the crystal violet and iodine of the Gram stain, so the organism stains poorly. Once carbol fuchsin is driven in with heat, the same layer holds the dye against acid-alcohol, which is what acid-fast means. Mycolic acids also help the organism resist drying and survive inside macrophages.","whyNot":["A polysaccharide capsule, as in Streptococcus pneumoniae, blocks phagocytosis. Capsules are shown with special stains and do not cause acid-fastness.","Correct.","Lipoteichoic acid is found in Gram-positive cell walls. It anchors the wall to the membrane and triggers inflammation, and it does not make cells acid-fast.","Lipopolysaccharide is the endotoxin of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. Mycobacteria lack it."],"study":{"topics":["Acid-fast stain","Mycobacterial cell wall"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C33","system":"CV","dx":"Acute viral pericarditis (coxsackievirus B)","codes":["CV-18-F3"],"case":"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of sharp central chest pain for 2 days. The pain worsens when he lies supine or inspires deeply, improves when he sits up and leans forward, and radiates to the ridge of the left trapezius muscle. One week ago he had a sore throat, fever, and diarrhea, and his roommate had a similar illness. Temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F). Auscultation with the patient leaning forward shows a scratchy, three-component sound at the left sternal border. An ECG shows diffuse ST-segment elevation with PR depression. Serum troponin is within the reference range. Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion with normal wall motion. Stool PCR detects coxsackievirus B. Diagnosis: acute viral pericarditis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C33-Q1","stem":"Pain from his inflamed parietal pericardium is referred to the skin over the trapezius ridge. Which of the following nerves carries this pain?","options":["Intercostobrachial nerve, from spinal segment T2","Vagus nerve, through its cardiac branches","Phrenic nerve, from spinal segments C3 to C5","Upper thoracic sympathetic afferents, from T1 to T4"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the pain source: the parietal pericardium, which has sensory fibers, unlike the visceral layer.","Recall its nerve supply: the phrenic nerve, which runs between the fibrous pericardium and the mediastinal pleura.","Link segments to skin: C3 to C5 also supply the skin over the trapezius ridge and shoulder, so the brain maps the pain there."],"why":"The fibrous pericardium and the parietal layer of serous pericardium receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves, which arise from C3 to C5. The skin over the trapezius ridge is supplied by the same cervical segments through the supraclavicular nerves. Pain from the parietal pericardium is therefore referred to that area. Trapezius ridge pain is a useful clue that separates pericarditis from myocardial ischemia.","whyNot":["The intercostobrachial nerve supplies skin of the axilla and medial upper arm. It carries no fibers from the pericardium.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the heart and visceral sensations such as stretch, which take part in reflexes. It does not carry the pain of pericarditis.","Correct.","Sympathetic afferents from T1 to T4 carry pain from the heart muscle itself. That explains ischemic pain referred to the chest wall and medial left arm."],"study":{"topics":["Innervation of the pericardium","Referred pain"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q2","stem":"His ECG shows ST-segment elevation in most leads, reflecting an injury current in the outer myocardium. Which of the following features of the heart wall best explains how pericardial inflammation affects the myocardium?","options":["The fibrous pericardium is fused to the myocardium with no space between them","The endocardium continues around the valve rings onto the outer heart surface","The parietal serous layer is attached directly to the outer myocardium","The epicardium is the visceral serous layer and lies on the myocardium"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":3,"approach":["List the layers from outside in: fibrous pericardium, parietal serous layer, pericardial cavity, visceral serous layer (epicardium), myocardium, endocardium.","Notice which inflamed layer touches heart muscle: the visceral serous layer is the epicardium itself.","Conclude that inflammation of the epicardium spreads to the subepicardial myocardium, producing diffuse ST elevation."],"why":"The serous pericardium has two layers. The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, and the visceral layer covers the heart as the epicardium. The epicardium is a layer of mesothelium over loose connective tissue and fat that carries the coronary vessels, and it sits directly on the myocardium. Inflammation of the epicardium irritates the outer myocardium beneath it. Because the whole surface is involved, ST elevation appears in most leads, not in one coronary territory.","whyNot":["The fibrous pericardium is the outer sac. It is separated from the heart by the two serous layers and the pericardial cavity.","The endocardium lines the inside of the chambers and covers the valves. It does not wrap onto the outer surface of the heart.","The parietal serous layer lines the fibrous pericardium. It faces the heart across the pericardial cavity and does not touch the myocardium.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of the heart wall","ECG changes in pericarditis"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-7"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q3","stem":"Purified RNA from this virus, without any viral proteins, can initiate infection when introduced into a cell. Which of the following features of the virus best explains this finding?","options":["Positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome that serves directly as mRNA","Double-stranded RNA genome split into segments within a nonenveloped capsid","RNA genome that is copied into DNA by a reverse transcriptase it carries","Negative-sense single-stranded RNA genome packaged with its own polymerase"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the virus: coxsackievirus B is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family.","Recall its genome: nonenveloped, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA.","Reason it out: host ribosomes can translate a positive-sense genome right away, making the viral polymerase, so naked RNA is infectious."],"why":"Picornaviruses such as coxsackievirus have a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome. It reads like mRNA, so host ribosomes translate it into one large polyprotein that is cut into viral proteins, including the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Because the virus needs nothing from the virion to start, the purified RNA alone is infectious. Coxsackievirus B spreads by the fecal-oral route and is a leading cause of viral pericarditis and myocarditis.","whyNot":["Correct.","Double-stranded RNA viruses such as rotavirus must bring their own polymerase to make mRNA, so their naked genome is not infectious.","Retroviruses such as HIV need the reverse transcriptase packaged in the virion to make DNA, so their purified RNA alone does not start infection.","Negative-sense RNA must first be copied into positive-sense mRNA by the polymerase carried in the virion, so the naked RNA alone cannot start infection. Influenza and measles viruses work this way."],"study":{"topics":["Picornavirus genome and replication","Positive-sense and negative-sense RNA viruses"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-F3"]}},{"id":"CV-C33-Q4","stem":"The scratchy, three-component sound at his left sternal border is a pericardial friction rub. Which of the following types of exudate on the pericardial surfaces is most likely responsible for this sound?","options":["Purulent exudate packed with neutrophils and bacteria","Caseous material from necrotizing granulomas","Fibrinous exudate that roughens the serous surfaces","Serous fluid with few cells that pools between the layers"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-F3","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the usual pattern of viral pericarditis: fibrinous or serofibrinous inflammation.","Link the pattern to the sound: deposits of fibrin make the smooth serous surfaces rough.","Rough layers rubbing together with each heartbeat produce the scratchy rub."],"why":"Acute viral pericarditis usually produces a fibrinous or serofibrinous exudate. Fibrin deposits make the normally smooth, moist serous layers dry and shaggy, sometimes called bread and butter pericarditis. As the heart moves, these rough surfaces rub, producing a scratchy sound that can have three parts linked to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and early ventricular filling. The same fibrinous pattern is seen in pericarditis after a myocardial infarction and in uremia.","whyNot":["Purulent pericarditis follows bacterial spread, for example from pneumonia or a chest wound. It fits a very ill patient with high fever, and his course and virus test point to a viral cause.","Caseous pericarditis comes from tuberculosis or fungal infection and develops over weeks. His acute illness after a viral prodrome does not fit.","Correct.","Thin serous fluid separates the layers and keeps them smooth. A large serous effusion tends to muffle heart sounds and can make a rub fade."],"study":{"topics":["Morphologic types of pericarditis","Pericardial friction rub"],"comps":["CV-18-F3","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C34","system":"CV","dx":"Bicuspid aortic valve with coarctation of the aorta in Turner syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G1"],"case":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of short stature and absent breast development. Her height is 142 cm. Physical examination shows a broad chest with widely spaced nipples and a low posterior hairline. Blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/70 mm Hg in the right leg (normally the leg pressure equals or exceeds the arm pressure). Femoral pulses are weak and delayed. Auscultation shows an ejection click and a soft midsystolic murmur at the right upper sternal border and a separate systolic murmur over the left upper back. Karyotype is 45,X. Echocardiography shows a bicuspid aortic valve with mild stenosis, a mildly dilated ascending aorta, and coarctation of the aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery. Diagnosis: bicuspid aortic valve with coarctation of the aorta in Turner syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C34-Q1","stem":"In a 46,XX female, most genes on one X chromosome are inactivated in each cell. Which of the following best explains why loss of the second X chromosome causes this patient's short stature?","options":["Some X genes, such as SHOX, escape inactivation and need two working copies","Her remaining X carries an expanded trinucleotide repeat that silences growth genes","Her single X is always paternal, and imprinting silences its growth genes","Her cells inactivate the single remaining X, so no X genes are expressed"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":3,"approach":["Note the puzzle: if one X is normally silenced, a female with one X should match a 46,XX female.","Resolve it: some X genes escape inactivation, including those in the pseudoautosomal region shared with Y, so normal cells use two active copies.","Apply it: with one X, these genes are expressed at half dose. Half a dose of SHOX causes short stature."],"why":"X inactivation silences most of one X chromosome in each female cell, but a set of genes escapes it. These include genes in the pseudoautosomal regions, which are shared with the Y chromosome so that males also carry two copies. SHOX lies in pseudoautosomal region 1 and is needed in two copies for normal long bone growth. A 45,X female has only one copy, and this haploinsufficiency explains much of the short stature in Turner syndrome. Loss of other escaping genes is thought to contribute to the other features.","whyNot":["Correct.","Trinucleotide repeat expansion causes disorders such as fragile X syndrome and Huntington disease. Turner syndrome is a loss of a whole chromosome.","In most cases of Turner syndrome the single X comes from the mother, because the paternal sex chromosome is the one lost. Imprinting does not explain the short stature.","A cell with one X keeps it active. X inactivation leaves one active X in every cell, and a cell with no active X could not survive."],"study":{"topics":["X inactivation and genes that escape it","Turner syndrome genetics"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q2","stem":"A chest x-ray shows notching of the inferior borders of several posterior ribs, caused by enlarged collateral arteries that bypass the narrowed segment. Which of the following collateral pathways is responsible for the rib notching?","options":["Superior epigastric arteries to the inferior epigastric arteries","Internal thoracic arteries to the intercostal arteries","Celiac trunk to the superior mesenteric artery through pancreatic arcades","Vertebral arteries joining to form the basilar artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the block: the coarctation lies just distal to the left subclavian artery, so the subclavian arteries are proximal to it and the descending aorta is distal.","Find a bridge: the internal thoracic artery (a subclavian branch) feeds anterior intercostal arteries, which connect to posterior intercostal arteries from the descending aorta.","Link to the x-ray: the enlarged, tortuous posterior intercostal arteries run in the costal grooves and erode the lower rib borders."],"why":"With coarctation distal to the left subclavian artery, blood reaches the lower body by detouring around the narrowing. The internal thoracic arteries arise from the subclavian arteries and give off anterior intercostal arteries. These connect with the posterior intercostal arteries, which carry blood backward into the descending aorta beyond the coarctation. The posterior intercostal arteries enlarge and press on the costal grooves on the lower edge of the ribs, producing notching. The first two ribs are usually spared because their intercostal arteries come from the costocervical trunk.","whyNot":["The internal thoracic artery also continues as the superior epigastric artery, which joins the inferior epigastric artery in the rectus sheath. This collateral carries blood to the lower body but runs in the abdominal wall, so it does not notch ribs.","Correct.","Pancreaticoduodenal arcades connect the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery. They enlarge with narrowing of one of those vessels and lie in the abdomen.","The vertebral arteries arise from the subclavian arteries proximal to the coarctation and supply the brain. Their junction as the basilar artery is normal anatomy and does not bypass the aorta."],"study":{"topics":["Collateral circulation in coarctation of the aorta","Intercostal arteries"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-G1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q3","stem":"If her aortic stenosis worsens over the years, her left ventricle will face a higher systolic pressure, and its wall will thicken while the chamber size stays normal. Which of the following relationships best explains why this thickening benefits the ventricle?","options":["Frank-Starling law: more stretch of the fibers raises the force of contraction","Fick principle: flow equals oxygen uptake divided by arteriovenous O2 difference","Poiseuille law: resistance to flow rises steeply as the radius of a tube falls","Law of Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure and radius, falls with thickness"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the load: aortic stenosis causes pressure overload of the left ventricle.","Recall the law that links pressure, chamber size, and wall thickness: Laplace, wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness.","Apply it: a thicker wall brings stress back toward normal, which is why pressure overload produces concentric hypertrophy."],"why":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. When systolic pressure rises, as with aortic stenosis or coarctation, stress on the wall rises. Adding sarcomeres in parallel thickens the wall and brings stress back toward normal without enlarging the chamber, a pattern called concentric hypertrophy. The cost is a stiffer ventricle with impaired filling and a higher oxygen demand.","whyNot":["The Frank-Starling law links end-diastolic volume to stroke volume. It explains beat-to-beat matching of output to filling and does not explain why a thicker wall lowers stress.","The Fick principle is used to measure cardiac output or organ blood flow from oxygen uptake. It says nothing about wall stress.","Poiseuille law explains why a narrow valve or vessel greatly increases resistance to flow. It explains why the stenosis raises pressure, but it does not explain the response of the ventricular wall.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Concentric and eccentric hypertrophy"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-18-D1"]}},{"id":"CV-C34-Q4","stem":"A dilated ascending aorta is common with a bicuspid aortic valve and increases the later risk of aneurysm and dissection. The main abnormality lies in the layer that normally gives the aorta its elastic recoil. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on histologic examination of this layer?","options":["Lipid-filled foam cells under a fibrous cap in the intima","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum in the adventitia","Granulomas with giant cells surrounding a fragmented elastic lamina","Fragmented elastic lamellae and loss of smooth muscle in the media"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-G1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the structure of an elastic artery: the media of the aorta holds many layers of elastic lamellae with smooth muscle between them.","Identify the lesion of bicuspid valve aortopathy: noninflammatory medial degeneration.","Pick the option that describes loss of elastic tissue and smooth muscle in the media with no inflammation."],"why":"The aorta is an elastic artery. Its thick media contains dozens of concentric elastic lamellae with smooth muscle cells and matrix between them, which let it stretch in systole and recoil in diastole. In aortas linked to a bicuspid valve, as in Marfan syndrome, the media shows medial degeneration: elastic lamellae fragment, smooth muscle cells are lost, and pools of proteoglycan-rich matrix collect. The weakened wall dilates and can tear, causing dissection.","whyNot":["Foam cells and a fibrous cap in the intima describe an atherosclerotic plaque. Atherosclerosis mainly affects the abdominal aorta and is the main cause of abdominal aortic aneurysm.","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum is the lesion of tertiary syphilis. It starves the media of blood and gives a tree-bark appearance to the aortic root.","Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells near the internal elastic lamina describes giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis. Her aortic dilation has no inflammation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of elastic arteries","Medial degeneration of the aorta"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C35","system":"CV","dx":"Patent ductus arteriosus with congenital rubella syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G2"],"case":"A 3-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of poor feeding, sweating during feeds, and poor weight gain. Her mother was never vaccinated and had a fever and a fine rash during the first trimester. The infant has bilateral cataracts and failed her newborn hearing screen. Pulse is 168/min (normal about 100 to 160), and blood pressure is 82/34 mm Hg, a wide pulse pressure for her age. Peripheral pulses are bounding. A continuous, machine-like murmur is heard best below the left clavicle. Echocardiography shows a large patent ductus arteriosus with left-to-right flow and an enlarged left atrium and left ventricle. Diagnosis: patent ductus arteriosus with congenital rubella syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C35-Q1","stem":"Serologic testing for rubella is performed. Which of the following results best indicates that the infant was infected in utero rather than carrying antibody passed from her mother?","options":["Rubella IgG in her serum at the same level as her mother's","Rubella IgA in her mother's breast milk","Rubella-specific IgM in her own serum","Rubella IgG in her serum that falls steadily over her first 6 months"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Recall which antibody class crosses the placenta: only IgG, through neonatal Fc receptors.","Reason that any IgM in the infant must have been made by the infant herself.","Conclude that rubella-specific IgM in her serum shows her own immune response to infection."],"why":"IgG is the only antibody class carried across the placenta, so every newborn whose mother is immune has maternal rubella IgG. IgM is a large pentamer that does not cross. Rubella-specific IgM in an infant's serum therefore means the infant's own B cells responded to the virus, which confirms congenital infection. Rubella IgG that persists beyond the age when maternal IgG should have disappeared, roughly 6 to 12 months, also supports the diagnosis.","whyNot":["IgG equal to the mother's level is what passive placental transfer produces. It cannot separate infection from maternal antibody.","IgA in breast milk is made by the mother and protects the infant's gut. It says nothing about whether the infant was infected.","Correct.","A steady fall in IgG over the first months is the expected decay of maternal antibody (half-life about 3 to 4 weeks). It points away from the infant making her own antibody."],"study":{"topics":["Placental transfer of IgG","Diagnosing congenital infection"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q2","stem":"During surgical ligation of the ductus, the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which curves under the aortic arch beside the ductus, is at risk. The ductus and the low course of this nerve on the left are both explained by the same pharyngeal arch artery. Which of the following arch arteries is it?","options":["Left sixth arch artery","Left fourth arch artery","Left third arch artery","Left second arch artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the derivatives: the sixth arch arteries form the proximal pulmonary arteries, and the distal part of the left sixth forms the ductus arteriosus.","Recall that the recurrent laryngeal nerves are sixth arch nerves that hook under the sixth arch arteries as the heart descends.","On the left the distal sixth artery persists as the ductus, so the nerve stays hooked under it beside the aortic arch."],"why":"The recurrent laryngeal nerves supply the muscles of the sixth pharyngeal arch and loop beneath the sixth arch arteries. On the right, the distal sixth arch artery and the fifth disappear, so the right nerve moves up and hooks around the right subclavian artery, a fourth arch derivative. On the left, the distal sixth arch artery persists as the ductus arteriosus, which becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. The left nerve therefore stays hooked under the aortic arch next to the ductus, where it can be injured in ductus surgery.","whyNot":["Correct.","The left fourth arch artery forms part of the aortic arch. The left nerve passes under the arch, but its low course is set by the sixth arch artery, the ductus.","The third arch arteries form the common carotid arteries and the start of the internal carotid arteries. They are unrelated to the ductus.","The second arch artery mostly regresses and leaves small remnants such as the stapedial artery. It does not form the ductus."],"study":{"topics":["Pharyngeal arch artery derivatives","Course of the recurrent laryngeal nerves"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-3"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q3","stem":"Before birth, patency of the ductus is maintained by prostaglandin E2, produced by both the placenta and the ductus wall. Which of the following precursors and enzymes produce prostaglandin E2?","options":["Sphingosine, by sphingosine kinase","Arachidonic acid, by cyclooxygenase","Arachidonic acid, by 5-lipoxygenase","Cholesterol, by side-chain cleavage enzyme"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the molecule: prostaglandins are eicosanoids, 20-carbon signals made from a membrane fatty acid.","Name the precursor: arachidonic acid, released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.","Split the pathways: cyclooxygenase makes prostaglandins and thromboxanes, while 5-lipoxygenase makes leukotrienes."],"why":"Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon omega-6 fatty acid, from membrane phospholipids. Cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) turns it into prostaglandin H2, which other enzymes convert to prostaglandin E2, prostacyclin, and thromboxane A2. PGE2 relaxes the smooth muscle of the ductus. After birth the placental source is gone and the lungs break down circulating PGE2, so the level falls and the ductus constricts. This is why COX inhibitors can close a patent ductus and why PGE1 is given to keep it open in duct-dependent heart defects.","whyNot":["Sphingosine kinase makes sphingosine 1-phosphate, a lipid signal that guides lymphocyte movement. It is unrelated to prostaglandin synthesis.","Correct.","5-lipoxygenase turns arachidonic acid into leukotrienes, which cause bronchoconstriction and attract neutrophils. It does not make prostaglandins.","Side-chain cleavage of cholesterol is the first step in steroid hormone synthesis. Prostaglandins are made from a fatty acid."],"study":{"topics":["Eicosanoid synthesis","Prostaglandins and the ductus arteriosus"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-G2"]}},{"id":"CV-C35-Q4","stem":"Suppose her ductus is never closed. Years later, pulmonary vascular resistance rises until flow through the ductus reverses (Eisenmenger syndrome). Her toes become cyanotic and clubbed while her fingers remain pink. Which of the following anatomic facts best explains this pattern?","options":["The ductus joins the aorta at its root, before the coronary arteries arise","The ductus joins the arch between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid","The ductus joins the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery","The ductus joins the right subclavian artery close to where that vessel begins"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the shunt: with reversed flow, deoxygenated blood moves from the pulmonary artery into the aorta through the ductus.","Locate where it enters: the ductus joins the aorta just beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery.","Trace the distribution: branches that leave the aorta before that point (to the head and arms) carry oxygenated blood, while the descending aorta carries mixed blood to the lower body."],"why":"The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk near its bifurcation to the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery. When pulmonary pressure exceeds aortic pressure, deoxygenated blood enters the aorta at this point. The brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries have already branched off and still carry well-oxygenated blood to the head and arms. The descending aorta carries the mixed blood to the legs, so cyanosis and clubbing appear in the toes but not the fingers (differential cyanosis).","whyNot":["Entry at the aortic root would send deoxygenated blood to every branch, including the coronary arteries, causing cyanosis of both hands and feet.","Entry between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid would also darken the left head and arm. The ductus enters further along, past all three arch branches.","Correct.","The ductus does not connect to the right subclavian artery. If it did, the right arm would turn blue."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the aortic arch","Eisenmenger syndrome and differential cyanosis"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-G2"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C36","system":"CV","dx":"Ostium secundum atrial septal defect in Holt-Oram syndrome","codes":["CV-18-G3"],"case":"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-year history of shortness of breath when climbing stairs and recent episodes of palpitations. Her father and brother have similar thumb abnormalities, and her father had a congenital heart defect repaired as a child. Physical examination shows bilateral hypoplastic thumbs. The second heart sound is widely split and does not vary with respiration. A soft midsystolic murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border. An ECG shows incomplete right bundle branch block. Echocardiography shows a 14-mm defect in the center of the atrial septum at the fossa ovalis with left-to-right flow, an enlarged right atrium and right ventricle, and a pulmonary-to-systemic flow ratio (Qp:Qs) of 2.1 (normal 1.0). Genetic testing shows a TBX5 mutation. Diagnosis: ostium secundum atrial septal defect in Holt-Oram syndrome.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C36-Q1","stem":"She asks about the risk to her future children. Based on her family history, which of the following is the inheritance pattern of her condition and the risk to each child?","options":["Mitochondrial, nearly 100% for each child","Autosomal dominant, 50% for each child","Autosomal recessive, 25% for each child","X-linked dominant, 50% for each child"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":3,"approach":["Read the pedigree: affected people appear in two generations, and her father passed it to a son (her brother).","Rule out patterns: father-to-son transmission excludes X-linked inheritance, and a father cannot pass on mitochondrial DNA.","Conclude autosomal dominant: an affected parent passes the mutant allele to each child with a 50% chance."],"why":"Holt-Oram syndrome is autosomal dominant and is caused by mutations in TBX5, a transcription factor needed for development of the upper limb and the heart. Her family shows affected people in each generation and transmission from father to son, which fits autosomal dominant inheritance. Each child of an affected parent has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutant allele. Severity can vary within a family, so an affected child may have different heart or limb findings from hers.","whyNot":["Mitochondria are passed down through the egg, so an affected father cannot pass on a mitochondrial disorder.","Correct.","Autosomal recessive traits usually appear in siblings of unaffected carrier parents, not in parent and child across generations. Her child would be at risk only if her partner were also a carrier.","A father passes his X chromosome only to daughters, so father-to-son transmission rules out any X-linked pattern."],"study":{"topics":["Pedigree analysis","Holt-Oram syndrome"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q2","stem":"A secundum defect forms when the flap that should cover the foramen ovale is too short, often from excessive resorption. Which of the following embryonic structures normally forms this flap, the floor of the fossa ovalis?","options":["Septum secundum","Sinus venosus","Endocardial cushions","Septum primum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the two atrial septa: the thin septum primum grows first, and the thick septum secundum grows to its right.","Recall the opening: the foramen ovale is a gap in the septum secundum that the septum primum covers from the left like a flap valve.","Link to the adult heart: the septum primum becomes the floor of the fossa ovalis, and the septum secundum forms its rim (limbus)."],"why":"The septum primum grows down toward the endocardial cushions. Before it fuses, holes appear in its upper part and merge into the ostium secundum. The thicker septum secundum then grows on its right side but leaves an opening, the foramen ovale. The septum primum acts as a flap valve over this opening, letting blood pass from right to left in the fetus. After birth, rising left atrial pressure presses the flap against the septum secundum, and it becomes the floor of the fossa ovalis. If the septum primum is resorbed too much, the flap is too short and a secundum defect remains.","whyNot":["The septum secundum forms the thick upper rim (limbus) of the fossa ovalis. A small septum secundum can also cause a secundum defect, but it is not the flap.","The sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium. A defect near the opening of the superior vena cava is a sinus venosus defect, which lies outside the fossa ovalis.","The endocardial cushions close the ostium primum and form part of the atrioventricular valves. Failure here causes an ostium primum defect or an atrioventricular septal defect, often in Down syndrome.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Atrial septation","Types of atrial septal defect"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q3","stem":"Which of the following best explains why her second heart sound is widely split and does not vary with respiration?","options":["High pulmonary artery pressure makes the pulmonic valve close early with every beat","Right bundle branch block delays right ventricular contraction only on inspiration","Shunt flow keeps right ventricular volume about equal in both breathing phases","Delayed left ventricular activation makes the aortic valve close after the pulmonic valve"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-4","dok":3,"approach":["Recall normal splitting: inspiration increases venous return to the right heart, delaying pulmonic valve closure, so the split widens.","Add the defect: the large left-to-right shunt keeps the right ventricle loaded, so pulmonic closure is late in every beat.","Explain the fixed part: when inspiration brings more systemic venous blood to the right atrium, less blood crosses from the left atrium, so right ventricular volume barely changes."],"why":"In an atrial septal defect the two atria act as one shared chamber. Shunted blood adds to right ventricular volume, prolonging right ventricular ejection and delaying pulmonic valve closure, so S2 is widely split. During inspiration, more systemic venous blood returns to the right atrium, but this raises right atrial pressure slightly and reduces the left-to-right flow across the defect. During expiration the shunt increases again. Right ventricular filling stays about the same through the breathing cycle, so the split is fixed.","whyNot":["High pulmonary artery pressure makes P2 loud. It does not explain a split that stays wide and fixed, and her shunt flow is still left to right.","Right bundle branch block widens the split, but the split still increases on inspiration because the breathing effect on venous return remains. Block affects every beat, not only inspiration.","Correct.","Delayed left ventricular activation, as in left bundle branch block, delays aortic closure and causes paradoxical splitting, which narrows on inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Splitting of S2","Physiology of atrial septal defect"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"CV-C36-Q4","stem":"Before her defect is closed, she develops a deep vein thrombosis of the calf. Immediately after straining to lift a heavy box, she has sudden weakness of the right face and arm from an embolic stroke. Which of the following events best explains how a venous thrombus reached her brain?","options":["Right atrial pressure briefly rose above left atrial pressure","Pulmonary capillaries widened enough for the clot to pass through","The clot crossed from the pulmonary artery to the aorta by a ductus","Left atrial pressure briefly rose above right atrial pressure"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-G3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the event: a venous clot causing a stroke is a paradoxical embolism, which needs a path from the right heart to the left heart.","Recall the usual direction: left atrial pressure is normally higher, so her shunt runs left to right.","Apply the trigger: straining, then releasing it, sends a surge of venous blood to the right atrium, so right atrial pressure briefly exceeds left atrial pressure and the clot crosses the defect."],"why":"Normally left atrial pressure is a few mm Hg higher than right atrial pressure, so flow across an atrial septal defect is left to right. Straining against a closed glottis (a Valsalva maneuver) holds back venous return, and when the strain is released, pooled venous blood rushes into the right atrium. For a few beats right atrial pressure exceeds left atrial pressure, and blood, along with any clot it carries, crosses into the left atrium and the systemic arteries. Coughing can have the same effect. This is a paradoxical embolism.","whyNot":["Correct.","Pulmonary capillaries are about 5 to 10 µm wide and trap venous clots, which is how pulmonary emboli form. They do not widen enough to let a clot through.","Her echocardiogram shows an atrial septal defect only. A ductus is a different shunt, and she has no evidence of one.","Left atrial pressure higher than right atrial pressure is the usual state and drives left-to-right flow. It would carry blood away from the systemic side, not bring a venous clot to it."],"study":{"topics":["Paradoxical embolism","Atrial pressures and shunt direction"],"comps":["CV-18-G3","CV-18-H1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C37","system":"CV","dx":"Tetralogy of Fallot","codes":["CV-18-G4"],"case":"A 4-month-old boy is brought to a pediatric clinic because his lips turn blue when he feeds or cries. During one episode he became limp and deeply cyanotic, then improved when his mother held his knees to his chest. Physical examination shows a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border and a single second heart sound. Oxygen saturation is 82% (95 to 100%) on room air. Chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with decreased pulmonary vascular markings. Echocardiography shows a large ventricular septal defect, an aorta that overrides the septum, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract, and right ventricular hypertrophy. The diagnosis is tetralogy of Fallot.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C37-Q1","stem":"The four structural findings on this infant's echocardiogram trace back to one error during septation of the outflow tract. Which of the following developmental errors best explains all four findings?","options":["Excess resorption of the septum primum","Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse","Failure of the conotruncal ridges to spiral","Anterosuperior shift of the outflow septum"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":2,"approach":["List the four findings: VSD, overriding aorta, right ventricular outflow narrowing, right ventricular hypertrophy.","Ask which single shift could narrow the right outflow and leave the aorta sitting over a septal gap.","Match that to displacement of the infundibular (outflow) septum toward the right ventricular side."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot the infundibular (outflow) septum, built largely from neural crest derived conotruncal tissue, is displaced anteriorly and superiorly. This narrows the right ventricular outflow tract and leaves a malaligned ventricular septal defect with the aorta riding over it. Right ventricular hypertrophy follows because the ventricle pumps against the outflow obstruction. One displacement therefore explains all four findings.","whyNot":["Excess resorption of the septum primum leaves a secundum atrial septal defect, which causes a left-to-right shunt at the atrial level and no outflow obstruction.","Endocardial cushion fusion failure produces atrioventricular septal defects (ostium primum ASD, inlet VSD, a common AV valve), classically in trisomy 21. It does not narrow the outflow tract.","Failure of the conotruncal ridges to spiral produces transposition of the great arteries, where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk from the left.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Outflow tract septation","Conotruncal defects"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G4"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q2","stem":"A different newborn has tetralogy of Fallot with complete pulmonary atresia. On day 2 of life his cyanosis suddenly worsens because a fetal channel is closing. That channel develops from which embryonic aortic arch?","options":["Third aortic arch","Left sixth aortic arch","Left fourth aortic arch","Right fourth aortic arch"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":3,"approach":["With pulmonary atresia, no blood leaves the right ventricle for the lungs, so lung flow must come from the aorta.","The fetal channel that carries aortic blood to the pulmonary artery is the ductus arteriosus, which closes in the first days of life.","Recall that the ductus arteriosus is the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch."],"why":"When the pulmonary valve is atretic, all pulmonary blood flow in the newborn depends on the ductus arteriosus carrying blood from the aorta into the pulmonary artery. The ductus begins to constrict within hours of birth and usually closes functionally over the first few days as arterial oxygen rises and prostaglandin E2 falls, so lung flow drops and cyanosis worsens. The ductus arteriosus forms from the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch, while the proximal sixth arches form the proximal pulmonary arteries.","whyNot":["The third arches form the common carotid arteries and the first part of the internal carotid arteries. They have no role in neonatal pulmonary flow.","Correct.","The left fourth arch forms part of the adult arch of the aorta. It does not form a channel that closes after birth.","The right fourth arch forms the proximal right subclavian artery, a permanent vessel that does not close after birth."],"study":{"topics":["Aortic arch derivatives","Ductus-dependent pulmonary flow"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G2"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q3","stem":"Further testing of the 4-month-old shows a serum calcium of 6.9 mg/dL (8.8 to 10.8 mg/dL), a low parathyroid hormone level, and no thymic shadow on chest x-ray. Which genetic finding is most likely?","options":["Microdeletion at 22q11.2","Monosomy X on karyotype","Trisomy 21 on karyotype","Microdeletion at 7q11.23"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Group the findings: a conotruncal heart defect, low calcium from low parathyroid hormone, and an absent thymus.","Link parathyroid and thymus loss to failed development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches.","Name the deletion syndrome that combines pouch defects with conotruncal defects."],"why":"A 22q11.2 microdeletion (DiGeorge syndrome) disrupts neural crest migration and development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. The pouches form the parathyroid glands and thymus, so infants have hypocalcemia from hypoparathyroidism and T-cell deficiency from thymic hypoplasia. Neural crest cells also build the conotruncal septum, so tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus are common. The deletion is found by FISH or chromosomal microarray, since it is too small to see on a routine karyotype.","whyNot":["Correct.","Monosomy X (Turner syndrome) is linked to coarctation of the aorta and bicuspid aortic valve, with normal parathyroid and thymic development.","Trisomy 21 is most strongly linked to atrioventricular septal defects. It does not cause hypoparathyroidism or an absent thymus.","A 7q11.23 deletion causes Williams syndrome, with supravalvular aortic stenosis and high, not low, calcium."],"study":{"topics":["22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-1"]}},{"id":"CV-C37-Q4","stem":"During the spell described in the case, his color improved when his knees were held to his chest. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["It reduced venous return and lengthened ventricular filling","It lowered pulmonary resistance and drew blood to the lungs","It lowered outflow resistance in the narrowed right ventricle","It raised systemic resistance and cut the right-to-left shunt"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Shunt direction across the VSD depends on the balance between right ventricular outflow resistance and systemic vascular resistance.","Flexing the legs compresses the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance.","Higher systemic resistance pushes less blood right to left and sends more right ventricular output through the pulmonary valve."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot, blood leaving the right ventricle chooses between the narrowed outflow tract and the VSD into the aorta. When systemic vascular resistance falls or outflow spasm worsens, more deoxygenated blood shunts right to left and a hypercyanotic spell follows. Pressing the knees to the chest (the infant version of squatting) kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance, which reduces the right-to-left shunt and sends more blood to the lungs. It also increases venous return to the right heart.","whyNot":["Squatting and knee-chest positioning increase venous return from the legs. Reduced venous return would lower right ventricular output to the lungs and would not help.","Knee-chest positioning acts on the systemic side. Pulmonary vascular resistance is already low here, and the fixed outflow narrowing, not pulmonary vascular tone, limits lung flow.","Knee-chest positioning does not relax the infundibular obstruction. Drugs such as beta blockers are used to reduce infundibular spasm.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Tet spells and squatting","Shunt direction and vascular resistance"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-G4"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C38","system":"CV","dx":"Systemic embolism to the superior mesenteric artery from a left atrial thrombus","codes":["CV-18-H1"],"case":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department with sudden, severe periumbilical pain that began 3 hours ago. She has atrial fibrillation and stopped taking her anticoagulant 2 months ago. Pulse is 118/min and irregularly irregular, and blood pressure is 142/86 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a soft, mildly tender abdomen, and the pain is out of proportion to these findings. Serum lactate is 5.8 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus blocking the superior mesenteric artery a few centimeters beyond its origin from the aorta. Echocardiography shows remaining thrombus in the left atrial appendage. The diagnosis is acute mesenteric ischemia from a cardiac embolus.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C38-Q1","stem":"Given the site where the embolus has lodged, which of the following segments of bowel is most likely to keep its normal arterial supply?","options":["Sigmoid colon","Terminal ileum","Ascending colon","Cecum"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["An embolus in the proximal superior mesenteric artery (SMA) cuts off the branches that arise beyond it.","List the SMA territory: jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon.","Pick the segment supplied by a different trunk, the inferior mesenteric artery."],"why":"The SMA supplies midgut structures: the small bowel beyond the duodenum, the cecum and appendix, the ascending colon, and about the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives blood from the sigmoid branches of the inferior mesenteric artery, so an SMA embolus leaves it perfused. Knowing the boundary between midgut and hindgut supply predicts which bowel will be ischemic.","whyNot":["Correct.","The terminal ileum is supplied by the ileocolic artery, a branch of the SMA, so it is at high risk here.","The ascending colon is supplied by the right colic and ileocolic branches of the SMA, so it is at risk. It would stay perfused if the inferior mesenteric artery were the vessel blocked.","The cecum is supplied by the ileocolic artery from the SMA and is at risk with this embolus."],"study":{"topics":["Mesenteric arterial territories","Midgut versus hindgut supply"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H1"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q2","stem":"Which of the following factors is mainly responsible for formation of the thrombus that later broke off and became her embolus?","options":["Hyperviscosity from an increased red cell mass","Stasis of blood in the poorly emptying appendage","Endothelial injury from a jet of regurgitant blood","Antibodies against phospholipid-binding proteins"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the source: a left atrial appendage thrombus in a patient with atrial fibrillation.","Apply Virchow triad and ask which arm atrial fibrillation disturbs.","Loss of organized atrial contraction leaves blood pooling in the appendage, which is stasis."],"why":"In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver and no longer contract in an organized way. Blood pools in the left atrial appendage, a blind pouch, and stasis lets clotting factors accumulate and keeps platelets in contact with the wall. A thrombus forms there and pieces can break off into the systemic circulation, where they lodge in the brain, gut, kidneys, spleen, or limbs. This is why anticoagulation is used in atrial fibrillation to prevent stroke and other systemic emboli.","whyNot":["Hyperviscosity from polycythemia can promote thrombosis, but nothing in her case suggests a raised red cell mass. Her atrial fibrillation explains the thrombus.","Correct.","Jet lesions from regurgitant valves injure endothelium, but nothing in the case points to valve disease. Endothelial injury is the main factor in arterial thrombosis on a plaque.","Antiphospholipid syndrome causes arterial and venous thrombi, but nothing in the case suggests it, such as lupus, pregnancy losses, or a prolonged PTT. Atrial fibrillation fully explains her thrombus."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Atrial fibrillation and systemic emboli"],"comps":["CV-18-H1","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q3","stem":"Her increased lactate comes from ischemic intestinal cells that keep making ATP through glycolysis without oxygen. Which of the following reactions allows glycolysis to continue in these cells?","options":["Transamination of pyruvate to alanine by alanine aminotransferase","Conversion of lactate back to glucose in hepatocytes","Reduction of pyruvate to lactate, regenerating NAD+","Oxidation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in mitochondria"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step.","Without oxygen, the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and returns NAD+ so glycolysis can keep running."],"why":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase uses NAD+ and produces NADH. Normally mitochondria reoxidize NADH through the electron transport chain, but that requires oxygen. In ischemic tissue, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate and converts NADH back to NAD+, so glycolysis continues and yields a net 2 ATP per glucose. The lactate spills into the blood, which is why a raised serum lactate is a clue to bowel ischemia.","whyNot":["Alanine aminotransferase transfers an amino group from glutamate to pyruvate to make alanine. It does not regenerate NAD+, so it cannot sustain anaerobic glycolysis.","Gluconeogenesis from lactate (the Cori cycle) happens in the liver and uses ATP. It clears lactate and does not keep glycolysis running in the ischemic gut.","Correct.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase feeds pyruvate into the citric acid cycle. This path depends on oxygen being available to reoxidize NADH and is what fails in ischemia."],"study":{"topics":["Anaerobic glycolysis","Lactate dehydrogenase and NAD+"],"comps":["CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C38-Q4","stem":"On day 2 she develops fever, hypotension, and leukocytosis after gram-negative bacteria cross the necrotic bowel wall into the blood. Which of the following host recognition events most directly initiates this inflammatory response?","options":["Toll-like receptor 9 binding unmethylated CpG DNA","Toll-like receptor 3 binding double-stranded RNA","Toll-like receptor 4 binding lipid A","Toll-like receptor 2 binding lipoteichoic acid"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Gram-negative bacteria carry lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) in their outer membrane.","Lipid A is the toxic part of lipopolysaccharide.","Lipid A is recognized by toll-like receptor 4 (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages, which triggers TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 release."],"why":"Once the bowel wall dies, gut gram-negative bacteria and their lipopolysaccharide reach the circulation. Lipid A binds the TLR4 complex (with CD14 and MD-2) on macrophages and other innate cells. Signaling through NF-kappa B releases TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6, which cause fever, vasodilation, and leukocytosis and can progress to septic shock.","whyNot":["TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA inside endosomes. It contributes to responses against many microbes, but lipid A is the main trigger of gram-negative septic shock.","TLR3 recognizes double-stranded RNA inside endosomes and responds mainly to viral infection.","Correct.","TLR2 recognizes lipoteichoic acid and lipoproteins, typical of gram-positive bacteria. It would be the main receptor in a gram-positive infection."],"study":{"topics":["Toll-like receptors","Endotoxin and septic shock"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C39","system":"CV","dx":"Hemorrhage from a ruptured spleen with hypovolemia","codes":["CV-18-H2"],"case":"A 19-year-old college student is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after he was tackled during an intramural rugby game. Two weeks ago he had sore throat, fever, fatigue, and swollen posterior cervical lymph nodes, and a heterophile antibody test was positive. He reports left upper quadrant pain and pain at the tip of his left shoulder. Pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg. Physical examination shows cool, pale skin and left upper quadrant tenderness. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (13.5 to 17.5 g/dL). Bedside ultrasound shows a large amount of free fluid in the abdomen. The diagnosis is hemorrhage from a ruptured spleen with hypovolemia.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C39-Q1","stem":"His heart rate rose within minutes of the bleed. Which of the following changes in the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptor reflex best explains this finding?","options":["Increased stretch receptor firing with decreased sympathetic outflow","Decreased stretch receptor firing with increased sympathetic outflow","Increased stretch receptor firing with increased vagal outflow","Decreased stretch receptor firing with increased vagal outflow"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Blood loss lowers arterial pressure, so the arterial wall stretches less.","Less stretch means fewer impulses from the carotid sinus (via cranial nerve IX) and aortic arch (via cranial nerve X) to the medulla.","The medulla responds by reducing vagal tone and raising sympathetic outflow, which speeds the heart and constricts vessels."],"why":"Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch fire in proportion to arterial wall stretch. A fall in pressure after hemorrhage reduces their firing, which removes their normal inhibition of the vasomotor center. Sympathetic outflow rises and vagal outflow falls, so heart rate, contractility, arteriolar tone, and venous tone all increase. This is why tachycardia with cool, pale skin is an early sign of significant blood loss.","whyNot":["Increased firing with less sympathetic outflow is also the response to a rise in arterial pressure, the opposite of his situation.","Correct.","Increased stretch receptor firing happens when pressure rises. The extra vagal outflow would slow the heart, which is the response to hypertension.","Firing does fall after hemorrhage, but the reflex lowers vagal outflow. Raised vagal outflow would slow his heart."],"study":{"topics":["Baroreceptor reflex","Compensation for hemorrhage"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H5"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q2","stem":"His hemoglobin concentration is near normal even though he has lost a large volume of blood. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["Red cells lost into the abdomen have already been reabsorbed","Aldosterone has expanded his plasma volume by retaining sodium","Erythropoietin has already increased red cell production","Whole blood was lost, so its concentration has not changed yet"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H2","dok":3,"approach":["Hemoglobin is a concentration (grams per deciliter), not a total amount.","In acute hemorrhage, cells and plasma are lost together in the same proportion.","Hemoglobin falls only after interstitial fluid shifts into vessels or IV fluid is given, which takes hours."],"why":"In acute bleeding, red cells and plasma leave together, so the volume falls but the concentration of hemoglobin stays close to baseline at first. Over the next hours, lower capillary hydrostatic pressure pulls interstitial fluid into the capillaries, and IV fluids add more volume, which dilutes the remaining red cells. The full drop in hemoglobin often appears over about 24 to 72 hours. A near normal early hemoglobin therefore does not rule out major hemorrhage, and pulse and blood pressure are better early guides.","whyNot":["Blood in the peritoneal cavity is absorbed slowly over days to weeks, too late to affect a hemoglobin drawn 40 minutes after injury.","Aldosterone-driven sodium retention takes hours and adds plasma volume. Any added plasma would dilute red cells and lower hemoglobin, not keep it normal.","Erythropoietin rises within hours, but new red cells take several days to reach the blood, seen as a reticulocyte rise at about 3 to 5 days.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin in acute blood loss","Transcapillary refill"],"comps":["CV-18-H2","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q3","stem":"The virus that caused his illness two weeks ago enters B cells through CD21. A blood smear at that time showed many large atypical lymphocytes. Which of the following cell types are these atypical lymphocytes most likely to be?","options":["Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells reacting to infected B cells","Infected B cells maturing into antibody-secreting plasma cells","CD4+ helper T cells infected through the CD4 molecule","Monocytes that carry viral inclusion bodies"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Heterophile-positive infectious mononucleosis is caused by Epstein-Barr virus, which infects B cells through CD21.","The body controls infected B cells with a large cytotoxic T-cell response.","The atypical lymphocytes on the smear are these activated CD8+ T cells."],"why":"Epstein-Barr virus binds CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells and drives them to proliferate. The host responds with a strong expansion of cytotoxic CD8+ T cells that kill infected B cells. These activated T cells are large with abundant cytoplasm that scallops around neighboring red cells, and they are the atypical lymphocytes seen in mononucleosis. The same lymphoid expansion enlarges the spleen and makes it fragile, which is why contact sports are avoided for several weeks after the illness.","whyNot":["Correct.","Infected B cells are present, but they make up a small fraction of circulating lymphocytes. The atypical cells are mostly the T cells responding to them.","Infection of CD4+ T cells through the CD4 molecule describes HIV. Epstein-Barr virus targets B cells through CD21.","Monocytes are not the atypical cells in mononucleosis. Large cells with owl-eye inclusions suggest cytomegalovirus infection of tissue cells."],"study":{"topics":["Epstein-Barr virus","Atypical lymphocytes"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-17"]}},{"id":"CV-C39-Q4","stem":"He undergoes splenectomy. A blood smear several weeks later shows erythrocytes containing small, round, dark purple nuclear remnants. Loss of which of the following splenic functions best explains this finding?","options":["Removal of red cell inclusions by red pulp macrophages","Storage of about a third of platelets in the spleen","Synthesis of erythropoietin by splenic interstitial cells","Production of IgM by marginal zone B cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-10","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the inclusions as Howell-Jolly bodies, which are DNA remnants in red cells.","Recall that red cells normally squeeze through the cords and sinusoids of the red pulp.","Red pulp macrophages remove inclusions during this passage, so the inclusions persist after splenectomy."],"why":"Blood entering the red pulp passes through the splenic cords and must squeeze through narrow slits in the sinusoid walls. Macrophages there remove old or damaged red cells and pluck out inclusions such as nuclear remnants, a process called pitting. Without a spleen, red cells keep their Howell-Jolly bodies, so these bodies on a smear are a marker of absent or poorly functioning splenic tissue.","whyNot":["Correct.","The spleen holds about a third of the platelet mass. Losing this pool causes a rise in platelet count after splenectomy, not red cell inclusions.","Erythropoietin is made mainly by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney, with a small share from the liver. The spleen is not a source.","Marginal zone B cells make IgM against encapsulated bacteria. Losing them explains the infection risk after splenectomy, not nuclear remnants in red cells."],"study":{"topics":["Red pulp and white pulp function","Howell-Jolly bodies"],"comps":["CV-10"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C40","system":"CV","dx":"Splenic infarction in sickle cell trait at high altitude","codes":["CV-18-H3"],"case":"A 38-year-old man with sickle cell trait develops sudden, sharp left upper quadrant pain on the second day of a ski trip at about 3,000 m (about 9,800 ft) elevation. He has never had a similar episode. Temperature is 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows left upper quadrant tenderness. Lactate dehydrogenase is 410 U/L (140 to 280 U/L). Hemoglobin electrophoresis shows about 38% hemoglobin S and 60% hemoglobin A. Contrast CT shows a wedge-shaped area of the spleen that does not enhance, with its base at the capsule. The diagnosis is splenic infarction.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C40-Q1","stem":"This splenic infarct is pale. A lung infarct after a pulmonary embolus is usually dark red and hemorrhagic. Which of the following features of the lung best explains this difference?","options":["Its arteries are end arteries with no collateral flow","Bronchial arteries keep perfusing the dead tissue","Its cells consume more oxygen than splenic cells do","The embolus also blocks the pulmonary venous outflow"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Pale infarcts form in solid organs with end-arterial supply, such as the spleen, kidney, and heart.","Red infarcts form where blood keeps entering the dead tissue.","The lung has a dual supply: pulmonary arteries and bronchial arteries from the systemic circulation."],"why":"The spleen and kidney are supplied by end arteries, so when one branch is blocked the territory loses its blood and dies pale, in a wedge with its base at the capsule. The lung receives blood from both the pulmonary arteries and the bronchial arteries. After a pulmonary embolus, bronchial blood keeps flowing into the dying, loose lung tissue and leaks out, giving a hemorrhagic red infarct. The same dual supply explains why most pulmonary emboli in healthy lungs do not cause infarction at all.","whyNot":["End arteries without collaterals describe the spleen and kidney and lead to pale infarcts. The lung has a dual supply.","Correct.","Oxygen demand affects how fast cells die, but it does not decide whether blood fills the infarct.","A pulmonary embolus lodges in a pulmonary artery and does not block venous outflow, so it cannot explain the blood-filled infarct this way."],"study":{"topics":["Dual blood supply of the lung","End arteries"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q2","stem":"If tissue from the infarcted area were examined 2 days after the onset of pain, which of the following microscopic patterns would most likely be seen?","options":["Liquefactive necrosis with a cyst forming","Caseous necrosis with surrounding granulomas","Fat necrosis with chalky deposits of calcium soaps","Coagulative necrosis with preserved cell outlines"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the cause of cell death: ischemia in a solid organ.","Recall that ischemic death in solid organs other than the brain gives coagulative necrosis.","In coagulative necrosis, proteins denature, so cell outlines and tissue architecture remain while nuclei fade."],"why":"Ischemia in solid organs such as the spleen, kidney, and heart causes coagulative necrosis. Denaturation of structural proteins and enzymes slows breakdown of the dead cells, so ghost outlines of cells and the basic architecture persist for days while nuclei disappear. Neutrophils gather at the edge of the infarct within 1 to 2 days, and the area is slowly replaced by a fibrous scar.","whyNot":["Liquefactive necrosis with cyst formation is the pattern of ischemic infarcts in the brain and of abscesses.","Caseous necrosis with granulomas suggests tuberculosis or some fungal infections, not an ischemic infarct.","Fat necrosis with calcium soaps is typical of acute pancreatitis or trauma to fatty tissue such as the breast.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Patterns of necrosis","Morphology of infarcts"],"comps":["CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q3","stem":"Low oxygen tension at altitude triggered sickling in his spleen. Which of the following molecular events most directly drives the sickling?","options":["Unstable globin that precipitates as Heinz bodies","Excess alpha chains that form insoluble aggregates","Valine contacts linking deoxygenated HbS molecules","Spectrin deficiency that makes the membrane rigid"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the sickle mutation: a point mutation in the beta-globin gene that replaces glutamate with valine at position 6.","Valine is hydrophobic and, in deoxygenated hemoglobin S, fits into a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin molecule.","These contacts build long polymers that deform the red cell."],"why":"Hemoglobin S results from a missense point mutation in the beta-globin gene that places valine instead of glutamate at position 6. When hemoglobin S gives up its oxygen, the hydrophobic valine binds a complementary site on an adjacent molecule, and the molecules stack into long polymers that distort and stiffen the cell. In sickle cell trait, about 40% of hemoglobin is hemoglobin S, so sickling needs severe stress such as high altitude, dehydration, or acidosis. The spleen is vulnerable because blood moves slowly through its red pulp, where oxygen and pH are low.","whyNot":["Heinz bodies are precipitated hemoglobin. They form when oxidant stress damages hemoglobin in G6PD deficiency, or with unstable hemoglobin variants. They do not cause sickling.","Excess alpha chains precipitate in beta-thalassemia because too little beta globin is made. They damage red cell precursors and do not form sickle polymers.","Correct.","Spectrin deficiency causes hereditary spherocytosis, where red cells lose membrane and become spheres."],"study":{"topics":["Hemoglobin S polymerization","Sickle cell trait"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C40-Q4","stem":"Which of the following would most likely produce a red (hemorrhagic) infarct instead of a pale one like this patient's?","options":["Torsion of the testis on its spermatic cord","Embolic occlusion of a renal artery branch","Thrombosis of a coronary artery branch","Atherosclerotic occlusion of a splenic artery branch"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H3","dok":3,"approach":["Pale infarcts follow arterial occlusion in solid organs with end-arterial supply.","Red infarcts form when blood keeps entering dead tissue, as with venous occlusion.","Torsion twists the cord and compresses the thin-walled veins before the thicker arteries, so blood enters but cannot leave."],"why":"In testicular torsion the spermatic cord twists. Veins have thin walls and low pressure, so they collapse first while the arteries keep pushing some blood in. The testis becomes congested, then infarcts with blood trapped inside, producing a red infarct. Ovarian torsion and volvulus of the bowel behave the same way, while arterial occlusion in the kidney, heart, and spleen gives pale infarcts.","whyNot":["Correct.","The kidney has end arteries, so a branch embolus produces a pale wedge-shaped infarct.","The heart muscle is solid tissue supplied by end arteries, so a coronary thrombosis typically produces a pale infarct.","An occluded splenic artery branch gives a pale infarct like this patient's, since the spleen is supplied by end arteries."],"study":{"topics":["Red versus pale infarcts","Venous occlusion and infarction"],"comps":["CV-18-H3","CV-9"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C41","system":"CV","dx":"Generalized edema from nephrotic syndrome (minimal change disease)","codes":["CV-18-H4"],"case":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to the clinic because his eyes have been puffy every morning for a week, and now his legs and scrotum are swollen. He had a mild cold 2 weeks ago. Blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Physical examination shows periorbital edema, scrotal edema, and pitting edema of both legs. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no red cells. The urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is 6.2 mg/mg (under 0.2 mg/mg). Serum albumin is 1.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0 g/dL), total cholesterol is 380 mg/dL (under 170 mg/dL), and serum creatinine is 0.3 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7 mg/dL). The diagnosis is nephrotic syndrome, most likely minimal change disease, with generalized edema.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C41-Q1","stem":"Which of the following changes in Starling forces at his systemic capillaries is the main cause of fluid accumulation in his tissues?","options":["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Increased capillary permeability to protein","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased interstitial hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Find the key lab: serum albumin of 1.6 g/dL from heavy urinary protein loss.","Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pressure that holds fluid inside capillaries.","With less oncotic pull, net filtration rises and fluid collects in loose tissues such as the eyelids and scrotum."],"why":"Net filtration across a capillary depends on capillary hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out and plasma oncotic pressure pulling it back. Albumin supplies most of the plasma oncotic pressure, so heavy proteinuria with a low serum albumin lowers this pull and more fluid stays in the interstitium. Edema shows first where tissue is loose, such as around the eyes in the morning. Renal sodium retention also adds to the edema in nephrotic syndrome, but the low oncotic pressure is the classic Starling explanation.","whyNot":["Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure drives edema in heart failure and venous obstruction. Nothing in his case points to high venous pressure.","Increased permeability to protein drives edema in inflammation, burns, and sepsis. The leak in his disease is at the glomerulus, while his systemic capillaries are normal.","Correct.","A fall in interstitial hydrostatic pressure would favor filtration slightly, but it is not the main problem here. Interstitial pressure actually rises as edema fluid collects."],"study":{"topics":["Starling forces","Hypoalbuminemia and edema"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q2","stem":"A woman develops chronic arm swelling after removal of her axillary lymph nodes. Compared with this boy's edema fluid, her edema fluid is most likely to have which of the following features?","options":["More neutrophils and fibrin strands","A lower protein concentration","Similar protein but more red blood cells","A higher protein concentration"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-10","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that lymphatics return filtered fluid and the protein that leaks out of capillaries back to the blood.","When lymphatics are blocked, the protein stays behind in the interstitium.","His edema comes from low plasma protein with normal capillaries, so his fluid is a low-protein transudate."],"why":"Each day about 2 to 3 L of filtered fluid, along with plasma proteins that slowly leak from capillaries, returns to the blood through lymphatics. Lymphatic obstruction, as after axillary node dissection or in filariasis, traps that protein in the tissue, raising interstitial oncotic pressure and producing protein-rich edema that can lead to fibrosis over time. In nephrotic syndrome, plasma albumin is low and capillary walls are intact, so the edema fluid is a protein-poor transudate.","whyNot":["Neutrophils and fibrin are features of an inflammatory exudate, such as in infection or acute inflammation. Simple lymphatic obstruction is not inflammatory at the start.","A lower protein concentration describes his transudate. Lymphatic obstruction does the opposite because protein can no longer be cleared from the tissue.","Red cells in edema fluid suggest hemorrhage or trauma. Lymphatic obstruction changes protein content, not red cell content.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Lymphatic function","Lymphedema versus transudate"],"comps":["CV-10","CV-18-H4"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q3","stem":"Three weeks later he develops fever and diffuse abdominal tenderness. Paracentesis yields cloudy ascitic fluid with a neutrophil count of 1,500/µL (under 250/µL). Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause?","options":["Candida albicans","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Clostridioides difficile"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the infection as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis in a child with nephrotic ascites.","Recall that nephrotic children lose IgG and complement factors in the urine, which weakens opsonization.","Choose the encapsulated organism classically linked to peritonitis in nephrotic children."],"why":"Children with nephrotic syndrome lose IgG and some complement factors in the urine. Encapsulated bacteria resist phagocytosis unless they are coated by antibody and complement, so weak opsonization raises the risk of invasive infection by them. Streptococcus pneumoniae is the classic cause of spontaneous peritonitis in nephrotic children, and gram-negative rods such as Escherichia coli are the other main cause.","whyNot":["Candida peritonitis is mainly seen with bowel perforation, peritoneal dialysis, or long antibiotic courses, not as a spontaneous infection in nephrotic syndrome.","Correct.","Staphylococcus aureus causes skin, line, and bone infections. It is an unusual cause of spontaneous peritonitis and is not the organism linked to loss of opsonins here.","Clostridioides difficile causes colitis after antibiotic use. It does not cause spontaneous peritonitis."],"study":{"topics":["Opsonization and encapsulated bacteria","Infections in nephrotic syndrome"],"comps":["CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C41-Q4","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his total cholesterol concentration of 380 mg/dL?","options":["Loss-of-function mutation in the LDL receptor gene","Increased hepatic synthesis of apoB lipoproteins","Increased intestinal uptake of dietary cholesterol","Reduced use of cholesterol for bile acid synthesis"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Link the hyperlipidemia to the other nephrotic findings: low albumin and low plasma oncotic pressure.","Recall that the liver responds by increasing protein synthesis, including albumin and lipoproteins.","More apoB-containing lipoproteins (VLDL and LDL) leave the liver, so cholesterol rises."],"why":"In nephrotic syndrome the liver responds to protein loss and low oncotic pressure by making more proteins, including apoB-containing lipoproteins such as VLDL, which becomes LDL. Clearance of lipoproteins is also reduced, partly because lipoprotein lipase activity falls. The combined result is high cholesterol and often high triglycerides, which usually settle once the proteinuria resolves.","whyNot":["An LDL receptor mutation causes familial hypercholesterolemia, which is present from birth and would not appear suddenly with proteinuria and edema.","Correct.","Intestinal absorption of cholesterol is not the driver in nephrotic syndrome. The extra lipoproteins come from the liver.","Reduced conversion of cholesterol to bile acids can raise cholesterol, but it is not the mechanism linked to protein loss in nephrotic syndrome."],"study":{"topics":["Hyperlipidemia in nephrotic syndrome","Lipoprotein metabolism"],"comps":["CV-13"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C42","system":"CV","dx":"Acute arterial thrombosis of the superficial femoral artery","codes":["CV-18-H6"],"case":"A 64-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because his right leg suddenly became painful, pale, and cool this morning. For the past year he has had calf pain after walking two blocks. He has type 2 diabetes and a 45-pack-year smoking history. Physical examination shows that the right femoral pulse is present, but the right popliteal and pedal pulses are absent. His heart rhythm is regular, and echocardiography shows no cardiac thrombus. Platelet count is 260,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000/µL). Angiography shows a thrombus on an ulcerated atherosclerotic plaque that blocks the right superficial femoral artery. The diagnosis is acute arterial thrombosis of the superficial femoral artery.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C42-Q1","stem":"The first step in forming his thrombus was adhesion of platelets to the subendothelium exposed by the ulcerated plaque. Which of the following interactions mediates this adhesion under the high shear stress of arterial flow?","options":["Tissue factor binding factor VIIa","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor binding fibrinogen","Glycoprotein Ib binding von Willebrand factor","P2Y12 receptor binding adenosine diphosphate"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-H6","dok":2,"approach":["Separate the steps of platelet plug formation: adhesion, activation, then aggregation.","Adhesion is the step where a resting platelet first sticks to exposed collagen.","At high arterial shear, von Willebrand factor bound to collagen captures platelets through glycoprotein Ib."],"why":"When a plaque ulcerates, subendothelial collagen is exposed. Von Willebrand factor from plasma and endothelium binds the collagen and unfolds under the high shear of arterial flow. Platelets then stick to it through their glycoprotein Ib receptor, which is the adhesion step. The adherent platelets activate, release ADP and thromboxane A2, and then aggregate, building a platelet-rich arterial thrombus.","whyNot":["Tissue factor binding factor VIIa starts the coagulation cascade that makes thrombin and fibrin. It is not the receptor interaction that makes platelets stick to the vessel wall.","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa binding fibrinogen links platelets to one another. This is aggregation, the step that follows adhesion and activation.","Correct.","ADP binding the P2Y12 receptor amplifies platelet activation and recruits more platelets. It acts after the platelets have already adhered."],"study":{"topics":["Platelet adhesion and von Willebrand factor","Arterial thrombus formation"],"comps":["CV-18-H6","CV-18-I"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q2","stem":"Before today, his leg tolerated a year of gradual narrowing of the superficial femoral artery. Collateral branches of which of the following arteries most likely carried blood around the narrowing to the region of the knee?","options":["Obturator artery","Inferior epigastric artery","Superficial circumflex iliac artery","Deep femoral artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["The superficial femoral artery continues as the popliteal artery behind the knee.","Look for an artery that arises above the narrowing and has branches that reach the knee.","The deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery sends perforating branches and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery down to join the genicular arteries."],"why":"The femoral artery divides into the superficial femoral artery and the deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery. Branches of the deep femoral artery, including its perforating arteries and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery, connect with genicular branches of the popliteal artery around the knee. When the superficial femoral artery narrows slowly, these channels enlarge and carry blood around the blockage. This is why chronic narrowing caused only calf pain on walking, while sudden thrombosis today overwhelmed the collaterals.","whyNot":["The obturator artery supplies the medial thigh muscles and the hip. Its branches do not reach the knee anastomosis.","The inferior epigastric artery runs up the anterior abdominal wall and joins the superior epigastric artery. It is a collateral around aortic or iliac blockage, not around the thigh.","The superficial circumflex iliac artery supplies skin near the anterior superior iliac spine and does not reach the knee.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the thigh","Genicular anastomosis"],"comps":["CV-9"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q3","stem":"He is started on daily aspirin. Which of the following best explains why one dose of aspirin impairs a platelet for the rest of its life span of about 7 to 10 days?","options":["Platelets have no nucleus to make new cyclooxygenase","Aspirin binds glycoprotein IIb/IIIa for about 10 days","Aspirin is stored inside platelet dense granules","Platelets lack the enzymes that clear salicylate"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the target: aspirin irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1).","Without COX-1, platelets cannot turn arachidonic acid into thromboxane A2, a key signal for aggregation.","A platelet has no nucleus, so it cannot replace the blocked enzyme, and the effect lasts until new platelets are made."],"why":"Aspirin acetylates a serine in the active site of COX-1, which blocks the enzyme permanently. Platelets use COX-1 to convert arachidonic acid into thromboxane A2, which promotes vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation. Platelets have no nucleus and cannot make meaningful amounts of new enzyme, so each exposed platelet stays inhibited for its life span. Normal function returns only as the marrow releases new platelets, about 10% of the total each day.","whyNot":["Correct.","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa is blocked by drugs such as abciximab and eptifibatide. Aspirin does not bind this receptor.","Dense granules store ADP, calcium, and serotonin. Aspirin is not stored there.","Salicylate is cleared by the liver and kidneys within hours. The lasting effect comes from the permanently acetylated enzyme, not from drug left in the platelet."],"study":{"topics":["Thromboxane A2 synthesis","Aspirin and COX-1"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C42-Q4","stem":"He undergoes bypass surgery and receives unfractionated heparin. Six days later his platelet count is 95,000/µL, and a new thrombus forms in a vein of his left arm. Which of the following mechanisms best explains these events?","options":["Heparin directly clumps platelets without any antibody","IgE against heparin causes mast cell degranulation","IgG to heparin-platelet factor 4 activates platelets","Heparin depletes antithrombin, leaving thrombin unchecked"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing and pattern: a platelet fall of more than 50% about 5 to 10 days after starting heparin, plus new thrombosis.","This is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), an antibody-mediated reaction.","IgG binds heparin-platelet factor 4 complexes, and the immune complexes activate platelets through Fc gamma receptors."],"why":"In heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, heparin binds platelet factor 4 (PF4) and creates a new antigen. The patient makes IgG against heparin-PF4 complexes, and the antibody-coated complexes bind Fc gamma IIa receptors on platelets and activate them. Activated platelets release procoagulant particles and are cleared, so the count falls while clots form in veins and arteries. The usual onset is about 5 to 10 days after first exposure, the time needed to make IgG.","whyNot":["A mild, early fall in platelets from direct heparin effect can occur within the first 1 to 2 days, but it is harmless and does not cause thrombosis.","IgE-mediated reactions cause hives, wheezing, or anaphylaxis, not a falling platelet count with clots.","Correct.","Heparin works by boosting antithrombin. Any small drop in antithrombin does not explain a large fall in platelets."],"study":{"topics":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Fc receptor-mediated platelet activation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C43","system":"CV","dx":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm (infrarenal)","codes":["CV-18-I1"],"case":"A 72-year-old man comes to the physician for a routine health maintenance examination. He has hypertension and has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. He has no abdominal or back pain. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). Physical examination shows a nontender, pulsatile mass just above the umbilicus. Ultrasonography shows fusiform dilation of the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries, with an outer diameter of 5.8 cm (normal about 2 cm). The renal arteries arise from normal-caliber aorta above the dilation. The diagnosis is infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C43-Q1","stem":"He undergoes open surgical repair. During the operation, an unpaired artery arising from the anterior wall of the aneurysm at about the level of the third lumbar vertebra is ligated. If collateral flow to this artery's territory is poor, which of the following segments of bowel is at greatest risk for ischemia after surgery?","options":["Jejunum","Sigmoid colon","Ascending colon","Second part of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Name the vessel: an unpaired anterior branch of the aorta at L3, below the renal arteries, is the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).","Recall the IMA territory: distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum.","Pick the segment in that territory that is classically injured after aortic repair: the sigmoid colon."],"why":"The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L3, so it arises from the segment that an infrarenal AAA usually involves. It supplies the left colon, including the sigmoid colon and upper rectum. When it is tied off during repair, the left colon depends on collaterals from the superior mesenteric artery (through the marginal artery) and from the internal iliac arteries. If these are poor, ischemic colitis develops, most often in the sigmoid colon.","whyNot":["The jejunum is supplied by jejunal branches of the superior mesenteric artery, which arises at about L1, above the renal arteries and above this aneurysm.","Correct.","The ascending colon is supplied by the ileocolic and right colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery, which are not tied off in an infrarenal repair.","The second part of the duodenum is supplied by the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, from the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery."],"study":{"topics":["Inferior mesenteric artery territory","Branches of the abdominal aorta by vertebral level"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q2","stem":"Which of the following processes best explains the progressive weakening of the aortic wall in this patient's aneurysm?","options":["Protease breakdown of medial elastin and collagen","Granulomatous inflammation with giant cells in the media","Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum","An intimal tear that lets blood split the media"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the setting: an older male smoker with hypertension and an infrarenal aneurysm has the typical atherosclerosis-associated AAA.","Recall what thins the media in AAA: inflammatory cells release matrix metalloproteinases that digest elastin and collagen.","Rule out mechanisms that belong to other aortic diseases (syphilis, dissection, arteritis)."],"why":"Typical AAAs arise in older smokers with atherosclerosis. Macrophages and other inflammatory cells in the aortic wall release matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) that break down elastin and collagen in the media, and their inhibitors are reduced. Thick intimal plaque also slows diffusion of nutrients to the inner media. The thinned, weakened wall slowly dilates under arterial pressure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomas with giant cells describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis, which mainly involve the thoracic aorta and its branches.","This describes tertiary syphilis, which narrows the vasa vasorum and causes aneurysms of the ascending aorta and arch, not the infrarenal aorta.","An intimal tear with blood tracking through the media describes aortic dissection, which is a different disease from a fusiform aneurysm."],"study":{"topics":["Pathogenesis of abdominal aortic aneurysm","Matrix metalloproteinases in vessel wall remodeling"],"comps":["CV-18-I1","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q3","stem":"For a cylindrical vessel, Laplace's law states that wall tension equals transmural pressure times radius. If the pressure inside this patient's aneurysm equals that in a normal 2-cm aorta, which of the following is closest to the ratio of wall tension in the aneurysm to normal wall tension?","options":["About 70 times","About 8 times","About 1.7 times","About 3 times"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Write Laplace's law for a cylinder: tension = pressure x radius.","Compare radii: 5.8 cm divided by 2 cm is 2.9, so the radius is about 3 times normal.","With pressure unchanged, tension rises in direct proportion to radius: about 3 times."],"why":"Laplace's law for a cylinder says wall tension equals pressure times radius. The aneurysm's radius is about 2.9 times normal, so at the same pressure the wall tension is about 3 times normal. Because the aneurysm wall is also thinner, the stress on each unit of wall (tension divided by thickness) rises even more. This growing load on a weakening wall is why rupture risk climbs steeply as an aneurysm enlarges, and why elective repair is advised at about 5.5 cm in men.","whyNot":["About 70 times comes from raising the radius ratio to the fourth power, which belongs to Poiseuille's law for resistance and flow.","About 8 times comes from squaring the radius ratio, which would apply to cross-sectional area, not wall tension.","About 1.7 times would come from taking the square root of the radius ratio, which has no basis in Laplace's law.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Law of Laplace","Aneurysm rupture risk and size"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"CV-C43-Q4","stem":"Before the repair is scheduled, he develops fever and new back pain. One week earlier he had diarrhea after eating undercooked chicken. Temperature is 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Blood cultures grow a motile, oxidase-negative, gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose and produces hydrogen sulfide. CT scan shows rapid enlargement of the aneurysm with surrounding soft-tissue thickening. Which of the following is the most likely causal organism?","options":["Salmonella enterica","Escherichia coli","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Shigella sonnei"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":2,"approach":["List the lab clues: gram-negative rod, oxidase negative, motile, lactose nonfermenter, hydrogen sulfide producer.","Add the history: poultry-borne diarrhea followed by bacteremia in an older adult with a diseased aorta.","Match to the organism known for seeding atherosclerotic or aneurysmal arteries: nontyphoidal Salmonella."],"why":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella is a motile, oxidase-negative, gram-negative rod that does not ferment lactose and makes hydrogen sulfide. It is commonly acquired from poultry and eggs. In adults over about 50, Salmonella bacteremia can seed atherosclerotic or aneurysmal arterial walls and cause an infected (mycotic) aneurysm, which tends to enlarge quickly and rupture.","whyNot":["Correct.","Escherichia coli usually ferments lactose and does not produce hydrogen sulfide.","Klebsiella ferments lactose and is nonmotile, so it does not fit these culture results.","Shigella does not ferment lactose but is nonmotile and does not produce hydrogen sulfide. It has no animal reservoir and rarely enters the bloodstream."],"study":{"topics":["Nontyphoidal Salmonella","Infected (mycotic) aneurysm","Enteric gram-negative rod identification"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-I1"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C44","system":"CV","dx":"Acute Stanford type A aortic dissection in Marfan syndrome","codes":["CV-18-I2"],"case":"A 34-year-old man with Marfan syndrome is brought to the emergency department because of sudden, severe, tearing chest pain that radiates to the area between his shoulder blades. He had bilateral ectopia lentis as a teenager. He is 196 cm tall. Blood pressure is 158/92 mm Hg in the right arm and 124/76 mm Hg in the left arm. Physical examination shows arachnodactyly. A soft early diastolic murmur is heard along the left sternal border. CT angiography shows an intimal flap that begins in the ascending aorta just above the aortic valve and extends beyond the origin of the left subclavian artery. The diagnosis is acute Stanford type A aortic dissection.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C44-Q1","stem":"His Marfan syndrome is caused by a mutation in FBN1. Normal fibrillin-1 microfibrils sequester a growth factor in a latent form in the extracellular matrix. Increased signaling by which of the following factors contributes to degeneration of this patient's aortic media?","options":["Platelet-derived growth factor","Tumor necrosis factor alpha","Transforming growth factor beta","Vascular endothelial growth factor"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Name the gene product: FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a scaffold for elastic fibers.","Recall the second role of fibrillin-1: it binds and sequesters latent TGF-beta in the matrix.","Connect the defect to disease: abnormal fibrillin releases too much active TGF-beta, which damages the aortic wall."],"why":"Marfan syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in FBN1 on chromosome 15. Fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that support elastic fibers and also hold transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta) in a latent complex. Mutant fibrillin fails to sequester TGF-beta, and the excess signaling contributes to loss of smooth muscle and elastic tissue in the aortic media. This medial weakness leads to aortic root dilation and dissection.","whyNot":["Platelet-derived growth factor drives smooth muscle migration in atherosclerosis but is not stored by fibrillin-1.","Tumor necrosis factor alpha is an inflammatory cytokine, and Marfan aortic disease is characteristically noninflammatory.","Correct.","Vascular endothelial growth factor drives new vessel growth and is not held latent by fibrillin microfibrils."],"study":{"topics":["Fibrillin-1 and TGF-beta signaling","Marfan syndrome genetics"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q2","stem":"The false lumen of an aortic dissection forms within the aortic wall, usually between the middle and outer thirds of one layer. In a normal adult aorta, which of the following best describes the main components of that layer?","options":["Endothelium resting on a thin layer of loose connective tissue","Fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle between them","Longitudinal smooth muscle bundles with few elastic fibers","Loose collagen containing vasa vasorum, nerves, and fibroblasts"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the layer: a dissection plane runs within the tunica media.","Recall that the aorta is an elastic artery whose media is its thickest layer.","Describe that media: many concentric fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle cells, collagen, and ground substance between them."],"why":"In a dissection, blood enters through an intimal tear and splits the tunica media, most often between its middle and outer thirds. The aorta is an elastic artery. Its media is the thickest layer and contains dozens of concentric, fenestrated elastic laminae with smooth muscle cells, collagen, and proteoglycan ground substance between them. These laminae store energy during systole and recoil in diastole, and their loss is what makes the media vulnerable to splitting.","whyNot":["This describes the tunica intima. The intimal tear is the entry point, but the false lumen runs in the media.","Correct.","Longitudinal smooth muscle bundles are typical of the adventitia of large veins such as the inferior vena cava, not the aortic wall.","This describes the tunica adventitia, the outer layer that carries vasa vasorum and nerves to the outer wall."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of elastic arteries","Layers of the aortic wall"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I2"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q3","stem":"Two hours later, he becomes confused. Pulse is 128/min, and blood pressure is 84/60 mm Hg. Jugular venous distention is present, and heart sounds are muffled. Systolic pressure decreases by 18 mm Hg with each inspiration. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the decrease in systolic pressure during inspiration?","options":["Inspiration lowers venous return to the right heart, reducing output","Lung expansion compresses the false lumen and narrows the true lumen","Inspiration raises vagal tone, slowing the heart and lowering output","Right ventricular filling rises and bows the septum to the left"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-15","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize cardiac tamponade: hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds after a type A dissection ruptures into the pericardium.","Recall the effect of inspiration: lower intrathoracic pressure increases venous return to the right ventricle.","Apply the fixed pericardial volume: the right ventricle can only expand by bowing the septum left, so left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall."],"why":"A type A dissection can rupture back into the pericardial sac, causing hemopericardium and tamponade. The tight pericardium fixes the total volume of the heart. During inspiration, falling intrathoracic pressure draws more blood into the right ventricle, which can only enlarge by pushing the interventricular septum into the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, so systolic pressure drops by more than 10 mm Hg with inspiration (pulsus paradoxus). Pooling of blood in the expanding pulmonary veins adds to the fall.","whyNot":["Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. It does not reduce it.","There is no recognized mechanism by which lung expansion compresses the false lumen to cause a cyclic fall in pressure.","Inspiration lowers vagal tone and slightly speeds the heart (sinus arrhythmia), the opposite of this option.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pulsus paradoxus","Cardiac tamponade physiology","Effects of respiration on venous return"],"comps":["CV-15","CV-18-F"]}},{"id":"CV-C44-Q4","stem":"A resected segment of his ascending aorta is examined microscopically. Which of the following findings in the media is most characteristic of the process that predisposed him to dissection?","options":["Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells throughout the media","Elastic fiber breakup, smooth muscle loss, and mucoid pools","Plasma cells around narrowed, thickened vasa vasorum","Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils across the vessel wall"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the predisposing condition: Marfan syndrome weakens the aortic media.","Name the lesion: cystic medial degeneration.","Describe it: elastic fiber fragmentation, smooth muscle loss, and pools of proteoglycan-rich ground substance, with little inflammation."],"why":"Marfan syndrome and long-standing hypertension both predispose to dissection through cystic medial degeneration. The media shows fragmented and separated elastic fibers, loss of smooth muscle cells, and small cleftlike or cystic spaces filled with proteoglycan-rich (mucoid) ground substance. Inflammation is characteristically absent. The weakened media splits easily once blood enters through an intimal tear.","whyNot":["Granulomas with giant cells describe giant cell arteritis or Takayasu arteritis.","Correct.","Plasma cells around narrowed vasa vasorum describe syphilitic aortitis, which causes aneurysms of the ascending aorta but rarely dissection.","Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils describes necrotizing vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa, which affects medium-sized arteries."],"study":{"topics":["Cystic medial degeneration","Risk factors for aortic dissection"],"comps":["CV-18-I2","CV-14"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C45","system":"CV","dx":"Atherosclerotic carotid artery disease with amaurosis fugax","codes":["CV-18-I3"],"case":"A 66-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an episode of painless vision loss in her right eye yesterday. She describes a shade coming down over the eye that lasted about 5 minutes and then lifted. She has type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension and smoked for 30 years. Blood pressure is 148/90 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). A bruit is heard over the right side of the neck near the angle of the mandible. Serum LDL cholesterol is 168 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL). Duplex ultrasonography shows an ulcerated plaque at the origin of the right internal carotid artery that narrows the lumen diameter by about 50%. The diagnosis is atherosclerotic carotid artery disease with amaurosis fugax, a warning sign of high stroke risk.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C45-Q1","stem":"Her transient vision loss was most likely caused by a small embolus from the plaque that lodged in a branch of which of the following arteries?","options":["Right posterior cerebral artery","Right superficial temporal artery","Left middle cerebral artery","Right ophthalmic artery"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Note that vision was lost in one eye only, which points to the retina rather than the visual cortex or optic radiations.","Trace the path: plaque at the right internal carotid origin, up the internal carotid, into its first major intracranial branch.","That branch is the ophthalmic artery, which gives off the central retinal artery."],"why":"Monocular vision loss means the retina or optic nerve of that eye lost blood flow. The internal carotid artery has no branches in the neck. Its first major branch inside the skull is the ophthalmic artery, which gives off the central retinal artery. An embolus from an ulcerated plaque at the internal carotid origin can travel into this branch and cause transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax).","whyNot":["The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital cortex. Its ischemia causes a homonymous hemianopia that affects the visual field of both eyes, and it usually arises from the basilar artery, not the carotid.","The superficial temporal artery is a branch of the external carotid artery and does not supply the retina. It is the classic site of giant cell arteritis.","The left middle cerebral artery cannot receive an embolus from a right carotid plaque, and middle cerebral territory loss affects both eyes' visual fields, not one eye.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the internal carotid artery","Amaurosis fugax"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q2","stem":"At the plaque, the lumen radius is about half of normal. If flow is laminar and blood viscosity and segment length are unchanged, the resistance of the narrowed segment has increased by approximately which of the following factors?","options":["About 2 times","About 4 times","About 16 times","About 256 times"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Recall Poiseuille's law: resistance is proportional to viscosity times length divided by radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius divides the denominator by 2 to the fourth power, which is 16.","So resistance in that segment rises about 16 times."],"why":"Poiseuille's law states that resistance equals 8 times viscosity times length, divided by pi times the radius to the fourth power. Because radius is raised to the fourth power, halving the radius raises resistance 16 times. A 50% diameter narrowing is the same as halving the radius. This steep relationship explains why modest further plaque growth or a thrombus on an ulcerated plaque can sharply cut flow.","whyNot":["About 2 times would be true only if resistance were inversely proportional to radius itself.","About 4 times would be true if resistance depended on radius squared (cross-sectional area).","Correct.","About 256 times is the increase when the radius falls to one quarter of normal (4 to the fourth power)."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Vascular resistance and radius"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q3","stem":"Macrophages in her plaque become foam cells by taking up oxidized LDL. Which of the following best explains why these cells continue to take up cholesterol after they are heavily loaded?","options":["LDL receptor numbers rise as the cell's cholesterol increases","Scavenger receptors are not downregulated by cholesterol","HDL delivers extra stored cholesterol into macrophages","SREBP-2 is activated by high levels of cell cholesterol"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the receptor: oxidized LDL enters macrophages through scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36.","Contrast with the LDL receptor, which is shut down by high intracellular cholesterol through SREBP-2.","Conclude that scavenger receptor uptake has no feedback brake, so lipid keeps accumulating."],"why":"Oxidized LDL in the intima is recognized by macrophage scavenger receptors, such as SR-A and CD36, which are pattern recognition receptors of innate immunity. Unlike the LDL receptor, these receptors are not downregulated when the cell fills with cholesterol. Uptake continues, cholesteryl ester droplets build up, and the cell becomes a foam cell. Loaded macrophages also release cytokines that recruit more inflammatory cells.","whyNot":["LDL receptor expression falls when cell cholesterol rises, because SREBP-2 is held inactive. This feedback is the reason native LDL does not overload cells.","Correct.","HDL removes cholesterol from macrophages (reverse cholesterol transport). It does not load them.","SREBP-2 is activated when cell cholesterol is low and suppressed when it is high."],"study":{"topics":["Scavenger receptors and foam cells","Macrophages in atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C45-Q4","stem":"Her plaque formed at the carotid bifurcation, a common site of atherosclerosis. Which of the following local conditions best explains why arterial branch points are prone to plaque formation?","options":["High, steady laminar shear stress on endothelial cells","Lower pressure at branches that draws plasma lipids inward","Absent vasa vasorum causing ischemia of the inner wall","Low and oscillating shear stress on endothelial cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall where plaques form: branch points, ostia, and the outer wall of the carotid bulb.","Link flow pattern to endothelial behavior: steady laminar flow is protective, disturbed flow is not.","Choose low, oscillating shear stress, which turns on proinflammatory, adhesive endothelial genes."],"why":"Steady laminar flow with high shear stress induces protective endothelial genes, including endothelial nitric oxide synthase, which keep the surface anti-inflammatory and less permeable. At branch points such as the carotid bulb, flow separates and recirculates, so shear stress is low and changes direction. Endothelium there makes less nitric oxide, expresses adhesion molecules such as VCAM-1, and lets more LDL into the intima. This is why plaques cluster at bifurcations and ostia.","whyNot":["High, steady laminar shear stress protects the endothelium and is found in straight segments where plaques are less common.","Pressure differences between branches do not draw lipids into the wall. LDL entry depends on endothelial permeability.","The inner wall of all arteries is nourished by diffusion from the lumen, so lack of vasa vasorum there is not specific to branch points.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shear stress and endothelial function","Sites of atherosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-11"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C46","system":"CV","dx":"Atherosclerosis risk in metabolic syndrome with atherogenic dyslipidemia","codes":["CV-18-I3"],"case":"A 48-year-old man is evaluated at a workplace health screening. He has a sedentary lifestyle and a diet high in fast food. Waist circumference is 108 cm (risk threshold above 102 cm in men). Blood pressure is 142/90 mm Hg (normal below 120/80 mm Hg). Fasting laboratory studies show glucose 118 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (normal below 150 mg/dL), HDL cholesterol 34 mg/dL (desired above 40 mg/dL in men), and LDL cholesterol 138 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL). High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) is 4.2 mg/L (low risk below 1.0 mg/L). Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 45 mg/g (normal below 30 mg/g). The diagnosis is metabolic syndrome with atherogenic dyslipidemia, which raises his risk of atherosclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C46-Q1","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains this patient's elevated fasting triglyceride concentration?","options":["Increased hepatic VLDL secretion from excess fatty acids","Reduced hepatic secretion of apoB-100 particles","Loss of hepatic LDL receptors slowing the clearance of LDL","Increased intestinal absorption of dietary cholesterol"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the driver: central obesity with high glucose points to insulin resistance.","Follow the fatty acids: insulin normally suppresses adipocyte lipolysis, so resistant fat cells release more free fatty acids to the liver.","The liver packages the extra fatty acids as triglyceride in VLDL, so fasting triglycerides rise."],"why":"In insulin resistance, insulin fails to suppress hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, so more free fatty acids flow to the liver. The liver re-esterifies them into triglyceride and secretes more VLDL with apoB-100. Reduced lipoprotein lipase activity slows VLDL clearance and adds to the rise. Through cholesteryl ester transfer protein, triglyceride then moves from VLDL into HDL and LDL, which explains his low HDL and the small, dense LDL typical of this pattern.","whyNot":["Correct.","Reduced apoB-100 secretion would lower VLDL and triglycerides. Insulin resistance does the opposite.","Loss of LDL receptors raises LDL cholesterol, as in familial hypercholesterolemia, but does not by itself raise fasting triglycerides.","Dietary cholesterol absorption mainly affects LDL cholesterol. Fasting triglycerides come from hepatic VLDL, not from chylomicrons."],"study":{"topics":["Insulin resistance and VLDL","Atherogenic dyslipidemia"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q2","stem":"In a research study, infusion of acetylcholine into his brachial artery causes much less forearm vasodilation than in healthy controls. Infusion of sodium nitroprusside, which releases nitric oxide directly, causes a normal response. Which of the following steps is most likely impaired?","options":["Release of nitric oxide by the endothelium","Smooth muscle guanylyl cyclase response to nitric oxide","Alpha-1 receptor signaling in forearm arterioles","Smooth muscle beta-2 receptor signaling through cAMP"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the acetylcholine pathway: acetylcholine acts on endothelial muscarinic receptors to make nitric oxide.","Use the control: nitroprusside bypasses the endothelium and relaxes smooth muscle normally, so the smooth muscle response is intact.","Locate the defect in the endothelium: reduced release and availability of nitric oxide (endothelial dysfunction)."],"why":"Acetylcholine binds endothelial M3 receptors, raises endothelial calcium, and activates endothelial nitric oxide synthase. Nitric oxide diffuses into smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raises cGMP, and relaxes the vessel. Nitroprusside supplies nitric oxide without the endothelium, so a normal response to it proves the smooth muscle pathway works. A blunted acetylcholine response with a normal nitroprusside response is the standard test result for endothelial dysfunction, an early step in atherosclerosis that is common in insulin resistance.","whyNot":["Correct.","A smooth muscle guanylyl cyclase defect would also blunt the response to nitroprusside, which was normal.","Alpha-1 receptors cause vasoconstriction. Acetylcholine-induced dilation does not depend on them.","Beta-2 receptors respond to epinephrine, not acetylcholine, and a beta-2 defect would not explain this test result."],"study":{"topics":["Endothelial nitric oxide","Endothelial dysfunction"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q3","stem":"Visceral adipose tissue in central obesity contains many activated macrophages. Which of the following cytokines released from this tissue most directly stimulates hepatic synthesis of C-reactive protein in this patient?","options":["Interleukin 4","Interleukin 10","Interleukin 6","Interleukin 2"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize CRP as an acute phase protein made by hepatocytes.","Recall the main cytokine that induces acute phase protein synthesis: interleukin 6.","Link visceral fat macrophages and adipocytes as an ongoing source of interleukin 6 in obesity."],"why":"C-reactive protein is an acute phase protein made by hepatocytes. Interleukin 6 is the main signal that drives its synthesis, with help from interleukin 1 and tumor necrosis factor. In central obesity, activated macrophages and adipocytes in visceral fat release interleukin 6 steadily, producing low-grade chronic inflammation. An hs-CRP above about 3 mg/L marks higher cardiovascular risk.","whyNot":["Interleukin 4 is a Th2 cytokine that drives IgE class switching and does not induce CRP.","Interleukin 10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that dampens acute phase responses.","Correct.","Interleukin 2 is a T-cell growth factor and does not drive hepatic acute phase protein synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["Acute phase response","Inflammation in obesity"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C46-Q4","stem":"Two years later, a kidney biopsy is performed because of increasing albuminuria. It shows small arterioles with walls thickened by homogeneous, glassy, eosinophilic material and narrowed lumens. Which of the following processes most likely produced this arteriolar change?","options":["Lipid-laden macrophages collecting beneath the endothelium","Concentric smooth muscle growth with duplicated basement membrane","Calcium deposition in the media of muscular arteries","Plasma proteins leaking into the wall, plus excess matrix"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","dok":2,"approach":["Name the lesion: glassy pink arteriolar wall thickening is hyaline arteriolosclerosis.","Link it to his conditions: chronic hypertension and diabetes or prediabetes.","Recall the mechanism: plasma protein leakage through injured endothelium plus extra matrix from smooth muscle cells."],"why":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is a form of arteriosclerosis seen with long-standing hypertension, diabetes, and aging. Chronic hemodynamic and metabolic stress injures the endothelium, so plasma proteins leak into the arteriolar wall. Smooth muscle cells also lay down extra extracellular matrix. The result is homogeneous pink wall thickening that narrows the lumen and causes ischemic injury to the kidney (nephrosclerosis).","whyNot":["Lipid-laden macrophages beneath the endothelium form fatty streaks and atheromas in large and medium arteries, not arterioles.","Concentric, onion-skin smooth muscle growth with duplicated basement membrane is hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, seen in severe (malignant) hypertension.","Medial calcification is Monckeberg medial sclerosis, which affects muscular arteries rather than arterioles and does not narrow the lumen.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Types of arteriosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-18-A"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C47","system":"CV","dx":"Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","codes":["CV-18-I4"],"case":"A 31-year-old man comes to the physician for evaluation after his father died of a myocardial infarction at age 46. He does not smoke and is not obese. Physical examination shows nodular thickening of both Achilles tendons and bilateral corneal arcus. Fasting serum LDL cholesterol is 292 mg/dL (optimal below 100 mg/dL), triglycerides are 120 mg/dL (normal below 150 mg/dL), and HDL cholesterol is 48 mg/dL (desired above 40 mg/dL in men). Thyroid and kidney function test results are normal. Genetic testing shows one pathogenic variant in the LDL receptor gene (LDLR). The diagnosis is heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C47-Q1","stem":"His wife also has heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia caused by a different pathogenic LDLR variant. Which of the following is the probability that their first child inherits a pathogenic variant from each parent and has the severe form of the disease?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize the inheritance: familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant, and having two pathogenic alleles causes a more severe disease.","Each heterozygous parent passes the variant to a child with a probability of 1/2.","Multiply: 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4, or 25%."],"why":"Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant with a gene dosage effect. Each heterozygous parent passes the pathogenic allele half the time, so the chance that a child inherits both is 1/2 x 1/2, or 25%. A child with two pathogenic alleles (homozygous or compound heterozygous) has very little LDL receptor function, LDL cholesterol often above 500 mg/dL, and coronary disease that can begin in childhood. Each child also has a 50% chance of being heterozygous and a 25% chance of being unaffected.","whyNot":["Correct.","50% is the chance that a child is heterozygous, carrying exactly one pathogenic allele.","75% is the chance that a child has at least one pathogenic allele (affected in either form).","100% would apply only if a parent had two pathogenic alleles and could pass nothing else."],"study":{"topics":["Autosomal dominant inheritance with gene dosage","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q2","stem":"In his hepatocytes, fewer functional LDL receptors means less LDL-derived cholesterol reaches the cell interior. Which of the following best describes the hepatocyte response to the decreased intracellular cholesterol?","options":["ACAT activity rises, storing more cholesteryl ester","HMG-CoA reductase is ubiquitinated and degraded faster","SREBP-2 is activated, increasing HMG-CoA reductase","SREBP-2 stays in the ER, lowering HMG-CoA reductase"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the sensor: SCAP in the endoplasmic reticulum senses membrane cholesterol.","Low cholesterol lets SCAP carry SREBP-2 to the Golgi, where it is cleaved and released.","The released SREBP-2 enters the nucleus and raises transcription of HMG-CoA reductase and the LDL receptor."],"why":"When cholesterol in the endoplasmic reticulum membrane is low, SCAP escorts SREBP-2 to the Golgi. Two proteases (S1P and S2P) cleave it, and the freed transcription factor enters the nucleus and binds sterol regulatory elements. This raises synthesis of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis, and of the LDL receptor. In heterozygotes, the normal allele is upregulated this way, which is why they respond to statins.","whyNot":["ACAT esterifies cholesterol for storage when the cell has excess cholesterol. Low cholesterol reduces its activity.","Faster ubiquitination and degradation of HMG-CoA reductase is triggered by high sterol levels, not low ones.","Correct.","SREBP-2 stays in the endoplasmic reticulum when cell cholesterol is high, not low."],"study":{"topics":["SREBP-2 regulation","HMG-CoA reductase control","LDL receptor pathway"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q3","stem":"An elastic-stained section of his father's left anterior descending artery shows a plaque with foam cells and a lipid core. The plaque lies between the endothelium and a dark, wavy band, and beneath the band are layers of smooth muscle cells. Which of the following correctly identifies the layer containing the plaque and the band?","options":["Media; the band is the external elastic lamina","Intima; the band is the internal elastic lamina","Adventitia; the band is the external elastic lamina","Media; the band is the internal elastic lamina"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the layers of a muscular artery: intima, internal elastic lamina, media, external elastic lamina, adventitia.","Place the plaque: it sits between the endothelium and the first elastic band, so it is in the intima.","Identify the band: the elastic layer just under the intima, above the smooth muscle media, is the internal elastic lamina."],"why":"Coronary arteries are muscular arteries. From the lumen outward they have an intima (endothelium and subendothelial connective tissue), a wavy internal elastic lamina, a media of circular smooth muscle, an external elastic lamina, and an adventitia. Atherosclerotic plaques form in the intima, above the internal elastic lamina. As a plaque grows, the internal elastic lamina can fragment and the underlying media thins.","whyNot":["The external elastic lamina lies outside the media, so it cannot be the band directly beneath the plaque with smooth muscle below it.","Correct.","The adventitia is the outermost layer, and atheromas do not form there.","The media is the smooth muscle layer beneath the band. The plaque is above the band, so it is in the intima."],"study":{"topics":["Layers of a muscular artery","Location of atherosclerotic plaque"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C47-Q4","stem":"His father's fatal infarction most likely followed rupture of a coronary plaque with a thin fibrous cap. T-helper 1 cells in such plaques release a cytokine that decreases collagen synthesis by intimal smooth muscle cells and activates macrophages. Which of the following cytokines is most likely responsible?","options":["Interferon gamma","Interleukin 5","Interleukin 10","Transforming growth factor beta"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the cell: T-helper 1 cells.","Recall their signature cytokine: interferon gamma, which activates macrophages.","Link it to cap thinning: interferon gamma lowers smooth muscle collagen production while activated macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases."],"why":"The fibrous cap is made of collagen laid down by intimal smooth muscle cells. Th1 cells in the plaque release interferon gamma, which reduces smooth muscle collagen synthesis and activates macrophages. Activated macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases that digest the cap. A thin cap over a large lipid core is the typical vulnerable plaque that ruptures and triggers thrombosis and myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Correct.","Interleukin 5 is a Th2 cytokine that drives eosinophil growth and does not reduce cap collagen.","Interleukin 10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that dampens macrophage activation.","Transforming growth factor beta stimulates collagen synthesis and tends to stabilize plaques."],"study":{"topics":["Vulnerable plaque","Th1 cells and interferon gamma"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-C"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C48","system":"CV","dx":"Peripheral arterial disease with intermittent claudication","codes":["CV-18-I6"],"case":"A 63-year-old woman comes to the physician because of cramping pain in her right calf that begins after she walks about two blocks and resolves within a few minutes of standing still. She has smoked for 40 years. Physical examination shows normal femoral pulses bilaterally. The right popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibial pulses are diminished. The skin of the right lower leg is shiny with sparse hair. Resting ankle-brachial index (ABI) is 0.62 on the right (normal 1.0 to 1.4) and 1.05 on the left. The diagnosis is peripheral arterial disease of the right leg with intermittent claudication.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C48-Q1","stem":"Based on the pulse findings and the location of her pain, stenosis of which of the following arteries is most likely?","options":["Superficial femoral artery","Deep artery of the thigh (profunda femoris)","Anterior tibial artery","Common iliac artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Use the pulses: a normal femoral pulse places the lesion below the common femoral artery, and a weak popliteal pulse places it above the popliteal artery.","The artery between those two points that continues to the popliteal is the superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal.","Confirm with symptoms: calf claudication fits a femoropopliteal lesion, one level above the painful muscles."],"why":"The femoral artery continues down the thigh as the superficial femoral artery, passes through the adductor canal, and becomes the popliteal artery at the adductor hiatus. A normal femoral pulse with a weak popliteal pulse localizes the narrowing to this segment. The superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal is the most common site of symptomatic atherosclerosis in the leg, and it typically causes calf claudication.","whyNot":["Correct.","The deep artery of the thigh supplies the thigh muscles and does not continue to the popliteal artery, so narrowing there would not weaken the popliteal pulse.","The anterior tibial artery arises below the popliteal artery, so narrowing there would not weaken the popliteal pulse.","A common iliac lesion would weaken the femoral pulse and usually causes buttock and thigh claudication."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the lower limb","Localizing PAD by pulses and symptoms"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q2","stem":"Her calf blood flow at rest is normal, yet pain develops with walking. Which of the following best explains this pattern?","options":["Walking triggers sympathetic constriction of the calf arterioles","Arterioles offset the stenosis at rest but not during exercise","Exercise lowers arterial pressure, so less blood reaches the calf","Calf contraction collapses the stenosis and stops flow completely"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-12","dok":3,"approach":["Think of the circuit as two resistances in series: the fixed stenosis and the adjustable arterioles downstream.","At rest, the arterioles dilate enough to keep total resistance and flow normal.","During exercise the arterioles are already dilated, so total resistance cannot fall enough, and flow cannot rise to meet demand."],"why":"The stenosis adds a fixed resistance in series with the calf arterioles. At rest, the arterioles dilate to compensate, so resting flow stays normal. During walking, muscle metabolites call for flow several times higher, but the arterioles have little dilation left and the stenosis caps the flow. The pressure drop across the stenosis also grows as flow increases. Oxygen supply falls short of demand, anaerobic metabolites build up, and pain begins. It eases when demand drops with rest.","whyNot":["During exercise, local metabolic vasodilation in working muscle overrides sympathetic constriction.","Correct.","Arterial systolic pressure rises during walking. It does not fall.","Muscle contraction does not close a proximal arterial stenosis. The problem is limited flow reserve, not complete collapse."],"study":{"topics":["Flow reserve in claudication","Exercise hyperemia"],"comps":["CV-12","CV-11"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q3","stem":"She drinks alcohol heavily and eats few vegetables. Fasting plasma homocysteine is 24 µmol/L (normal 5 to 15 µmol/L), mean corpuscular volume (MCV) is 106 fL (normal 80 to 100 fL), and serum methylmalonic acid is within the reference range. Deficiency of which of the following vitamins most likely explains her elevated homocysteine concentration?","options":["Cobalamin (vitamin B12)","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Thiamine (vitamin B1)","Folate (vitamin B9)"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by methionine synthase, which needs N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate and vitamin B12.","Use methylmalonic acid to separate the two: it rises in B12 deficiency and stays normal in folate deficiency.","Combine with the large red cells and poor diet with heavy drinking: folate deficiency."],"why":"Methionine synthase moves a methyl group from N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, through methylcobalamin, to homocysteine to make methionine. A lack of folate or B12 therefore raises homocysteine and causes a macrocytic anemia. Only B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid, because B12 is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. High homocysteine injures endothelium and promotes thrombosis, and it is a recognized risk factor for atherosclerosis.","whyNot":["B12 deficiency would raise both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid. Her methylmalonic acid is normal.","B6 deficiency can raise homocysteine through cystathionine beta-synthase, but it causes a microcytic (sideroblastic) anemia, not a high MCV.","Thiamine is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase and does not affect homocysteine metabolism.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Homocysteine metabolism","Folate versus B12 deficiency"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"CV-C48-Q4","stem":"Which of the following serious outcomes is she most likely to have over the next 5 years?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Major amputation of the right leg","Myocardial infarction or stroke","Rupture of an abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I6","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize that PAD is a marker of widespread atherosclerosis.","Recall that most patients with claudication stay stable in the leg, and few need amputation.","Conclude that coronary and cerebrovascular events are the most common serious outcomes and the leading cause of death."],"why":"Peripheral arterial disease reflects atherosclerosis throughout the arterial tree, so patients with it usually also have coronary and carotid disease. In people with claudication, myocardial infarction and stroke are far more common than limb loss and are the main causes of death. Only a small minority progress to critical limb ischemia or major amputation over five years. This is why PAD is treated as a coronary risk equivalent.","whyNot":["Pulmonary embolism arises from venous thrombosis and is not a typical consequence of arterial atherosclerosis.","Major amputation occurs in only a small minority of patients with claudication over five years, and the risk is lower in those who stop smoking.","Correct.","PAD raises the chance of having an AAA, but rupture is much less common than myocardial infarction or stroke in this group."],"study":{"topics":["Prognosis of peripheral arterial disease","Atherosclerosis as a systemic disease"],"comps":["CV-18-I6","CV-18-C"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C49","system":"CV","dx":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis with large-vessel involvement","codes":["CV-18-I5"],"case":"A 74-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of new right-sided headache, jaw claudication, and scalp tenderness. Twice this week she has had transient blurred vision in the right eye. She also has aching and morning stiffness of both shoulders. Physical examination shows a thickened, tender right superficial temporal artery with a diminished pulse. Blood pressure is 142/80 mm Hg in the right arm and 118/74 mm Hg in the left arm. Laboratory studies show an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 88 mm/h (less than 42 for her age and sex) and a C-reactive protein concentration of 64 mg/L (less than 10). Temporal artery biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation of the media with multinucleated giant cells and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina. Diagnosis: giant cell (temporal) arteritis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C49-Q1","stem":"Permanent vision loss in this disease most often results from occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, which supply the optic nerve head. These arteries are branches of an artery that arises from which of the following vessels?","options":["Internal carotid artery","External carotid artery","Superficial temporal artery","Basilar artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: one-eye vision loss from ischemia of the optic nerve head.","The posterior ciliary arteries are branches of the ophthalmic artery.","The ophthalmic artery is a branch of the internal carotid artery, so the eye is fed by a different carotid system than the scalp and jaw."],"why":"Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is the usual cause of blindness in giant cell arteritis. It results from inflammatory occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries, which come off the ophthalmic artery. The ophthalmic artery is the first major branch of the internal carotid artery after it leaves the cavernous sinus. The biopsied superficial temporal artery belongs to the external carotid system, so this disease can threaten both carotid territories at once.","whyNot":["Correct.","The external carotid artery supplies the scalp, face, and chewing muscles through branches such as the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries. It explains her scalp tenderness and jaw claudication, while the optic nerve head is supplied from the internal carotid system.","The superficial temporal artery is the inflamed artery that was biopsied. It is a terminal branch of the external carotid and does not give rise to the vessels that feed the eye.","The basilar artery supplies the brainstem and, through the posterior cerebral arteries, the occipital cortex. Vision loss from that system affects the visual fields of both eyes, while her symptoms are in one eye."],"study":{"topics":["Ophthalmic artery and its branches","Arteritic ischemic optic neuropathy"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I5"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q2","stem":"Which of the following best explains her increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate?","options":["Hemoglobin polymerizes and makes the red cells rigid and sickle shaped","Red cells gain water and swell into spheres that resist stacking","Fibrinogen masks red cell surface charge, letting cells form rouleaux","Albumin binds red cells and adds to their mutual surface repulsion"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: ESR rises in inflammation, so the change must come from acute-phase proteins.","IL-6 drives the liver to make fibrinogen, the main plasma protein that speeds sedimentation.","Fibrinogen lowers the negative-charge repulsion between red cells, so they stack and fall faster."],"why":"IL-6 from the inflamed arteries signals hepatocytes to make acute-phase proteins, chiefly fibrinogen. Red cells normally carry a negative surface charge (the zeta potential) that keeps them apart. Large, asymmetric plasma proteins such as fibrinogen reduce this repulsion, so red cells stack into rouleaux. A stack has more mass for its surface area than a single cell, so it falls faster and the ESR rises.","whyNot":["Sickled cells are rigid and cannot form rouleaux, so sickle cell anemia tends to give a low ESR. Polymerization of hemoglobin S also has no link to inflammation.","Spherocytes stack poorly, so swollen spherical cells lower the ESR, as in hereditary spherocytosis.","Correct.","Albumin opposes rouleaux formation, so a high albumin tends to slow the ESR and a low albumin speeds it. It cannot explain a high ESR in active inflammation."],"study":{"topics":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Acute-phase proteins and IL-6"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q3","stem":"She also reports aching and fatigue of the left arm when she carries groceries. Imaging shows narrowing of the artery that continues as the left axillary artery. This narrowed artery arises from which of the following?","options":["Brachiocephalic trunk","Arch of the aorta","Left common carotid artery","Thyrocervical trunk"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: lower left arm pressure plus arm claudication means a stenosis upstream of the left axillary artery.","The artery that becomes the axillary artery is the subclavian artery.","The left subclavian is the third branch of the aortic arch; the right subclavian comes from the brachiocephalic trunk."],"why":"The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the outer border of the first rib. On the left, the subclavian artery arises directly from the arch of the aorta as its third branch, after the brachiocephalic trunk and the left common carotid artery. Giant cell arteritis involves the aorta and its main branches in a sizable share of patients, and subclavian narrowing gives arm claudication and unequal arm pressures, as in this woman.","whyNot":["The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries. It would be the answer for a narrowing that lowered pressure in the right arm.","Correct.","The left common carotid artery is the second arch branch and supplies the head and neck. It does not give rise to the artery of the arm.","The thyrocervical trunk is a branch of the subclavian artery, so it lies downstream of the narrowed vessel."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the aortic arch","Large-vessel giant cell arteritis"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-I5"]}},{"id":"CV-C49-Q4","stem":"In this disease, adventitial dendritic cells present an unidentified antigen to CD4+ T lymphocytes, which then drive inflammation in the media. Which of the following T-lymphocyte responses is mainly responsible for the granulomatous inflammation in her artery wall?","options":["Th2 cells releasing IL-4 and IL-5 that recruit eosinophils","CD8 T cells releasing perforin and granzymes that kill endothelial cells","Regulatory T cells releasing IL-10 that quiets macrophages","Th1 cells releasing interferon gamma that activates macrophages"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: the biopsy shows granulomas with multinucleated giant cells and no mention of eosinophils or immune complexes.","Granulomas are a delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity pattern driven by CD4 T cells.","Th1 cells release interferon gamma, which activates macrophages that gather into granulomas and fuse into giant cells."],"why":"Giant cell arteritis is a T cell-mediated (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction in the artery wall. Activated dendritic cells in the adventitia prime CD4 T cells, which polarize mainly to Th1 and Th17 cells. Interferon gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages, which form granulomas, fuse into multinucleated giant cells, and release enzymes and growth factors. These damage the elastic laminae and cause intimal thickening that narrows the lumen and causes ischemia, as in her jaw and eye.","whyNot":["Th2 cells and eosinophils drive allergic inflammation and the eosinophil-rich vasculitis of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which occurs with asthma. Her biopsy does not describe eosinophils.","CD8 T cells kill cells that display foreign peptide on MHC class I, as in viral infection and transplant rejection. Cytotoxic killing does not build granulomas.","Regulatory T cells and IL-10 suppress inflammation. Lack of them can allow autoimmunity, but they do not produce granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Type IV hypersensitivity","Th1 cells and granuloma formation"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I5"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C50","system":"CV","dx":"Secondary Raynaud phenomenon caused by systemic sclerosis","codes":["CV-18-I8"],"case":"A 46-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 10-year history of episodes in which her fingers turn white, then blue, then red on exposure to cold or emotional stress. Over the past 3 years, the skin of her fingers and face has become thick and tight. She also has heartburn and a sensation of solid food sticking behind the sternum. Physical examination shows tight, shiny skin over the fingers distal to the metacarpophalangeal joints, with normal skin over the upper arms and trunk. There is a healing pitted ulcer on the right index fingertip, firm subcutaneous calcium deposits in two finger pads, and telangiectasias on the face and lips. Nailfold capillaroscopy shows enlarged capillary loops with areas of capillary dropout. Antinuclear antibody assay is positive. Diagnosis: secondary Raynaud phenomenon caused by systemic sclerosis.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C50-Q1","stem":"During an attack, her fingers turn red as they rewarm after the blue phase. Which of the following best explains the red phase?","options":["Reactive hyperemia from local vasodilator metabolites","Increased sympathetic alpha-adrenergic tone in the digital arteries","Rising venous pressure that traps deoxygenated blood in the skin","Polycythemia that raises the hemoglobin content of skin capillaries"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Match each color to blood flow: white is no flow, blue is slow flow with deoxygenated blood, red is flow returning.","During ischemia, metabolites build up in the tissue.","When the spasm ends, these metabolites dilate arterioles and flow overshoots: reactive hyperemia."],"why":"The white phase is vasospasm of the digital arteries that stops flow. The blue phase follows as a small amount of slowly moving blood gives up its oxygen. While flow is low, vasodilator metabolites such as adenosine, carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions, and potassium build up in the tissue. When the spasm relaxes, these metabolites hold the arterioles open, and flow rises above its resting level. This overshoot, called reactive hyperemia, makes the fingers red and often throbbing.","whyNot":["Correct.","Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction triggered by cold or stress causes the white phase, not the red one.","Slow flow with deoxygenated blood in the skin explains the blue phase. The red phase means fresh arterial blood is flowing in again.","Polycythemia can make skin look ruddy all the time, but it would not appear in a pattern that follows each cold attack. Her color change comes from a change in flow."],"study":{"topics":["Raynaud color sequence","Reactive hyperemia and local metabolic control"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q2","stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies is most likely to be found in her serum?","options":["Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody","Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) antibody","Anticentromere antibody","Anti-Jo-1 antibody"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: skin thickening only on the fingers and face, with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, and telangiectasias.","This pattern is the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis (CREST).","Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is most strongly linked to anticentromere antibody."],"why":"Her skin changes stop at the fingers and face, with calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasias. These five features make up CREST syndrome, the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis. Raynaud phenomenon often comes years before skin changes in this form, as it did for her. Anticentromere antibody is the autoantibody most closely tied to this form, which carries a later risk of pulmonary arterial hypertension.","whyNot":["Anti-double-stranded DNA is specific for systemic lupus erythematosus and tracks with lupus nephritis. It is not typical of systemic sclerosis.","Anti-Scl-70 is linked to diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis, in which skin thickening spreads to the upper arms and trunk and interstitial lung disease is common. Her trunk and upper arms are spared.","Correct.","Anti-Jo-1 is a marker of inflammatory myositis (polymyositis or dermatomyositis), often with interstitial lung disease. She has no muscle weakness."],"study":{"topics":["Limited vs diffuse systemic sclerosis","Autoantibodies in connective tissue disease"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q3","stem":"The fibrosis in her skin and esophagus is driven largely by transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta), which stimulates fibroblasts to deposit excess collagen. Which of the following best describes how the TGF-beta receptor transmits this signal into the cell?","options":["Activation of Gs, which raises cyclic AMP","Tyrosine autophosphorylation that recruits RAS","Associated JAK kinases that phosphorylate STAT proteins","Serine/threonine phosphorylation of SMAD proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the mediator: TGF-beta is the main profibrotic cytokine in systemic sclerosis.","Recall its receptor class: TGF-beta receptors are serine/threonine kinases.","The activated receptor phosphorylates SMAD proteins, which carry the signal to the nucleus."],"why":"TGF-beta binds the type II receptor, a serine/threonine kinase, which then phosphorylates and activates the type I receptor. The type I receptor phosphorylates SMAD2 and SMAD3. These join SMAD4, move into the nucleus, and switch on genes for collagen and other matrix proteins. In systemic sclerosis, persistent TGF-beta signaling keeps fibroblasts producing matrix, which thickens the skin and stiffens the esophageal wall.","whyNot":["Gs coupling and cyclic AMP are used by receptors such as the beta-adrenergic receptors and the glucagon receptor, not by TGF-beta receptors.","Tyrosine autophosphorylation and RAS recruitment describe receptor tyrosine kinases such as the receptors for insulin, EGF, and PDGF.","JAK-STAT signaling is used by many cytokine receptors, such as those for IL-6, interferons, and erythropoietin. TGF-beta uses SMAD proteins instead.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["TGF-beta and SMAD signaling","Receptor classes and second messengers"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I8"]}},{"id":"CV-C50-Q4","stem":"Fingertip ulceration is rare in primary Raynaud phenomenon. Which of the following changes in her digital arteries best explains why her attacks cause tissue loss?","options":["Immune complex leukocytoclastic vasculitis of postcapillary venules","Intimal proliferation and fibrosis that keep the lumen narrow","Cholesterol-rich atheroma with a thin fibrous cap","Cold-reacting IgM antibodies that agglutinate red cells in the fingers"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I8","dok":3,"approach":["Compare primary and secondary Raynaud: primary is pure vasospasm in a normal vessel; secondary adds structural vessel disease.","In systemic sclerosis, endothelial injury leads to intimal proliferation and fibrosis of small arteries, and capillaries are lost (seen on nailfold capillaroscopy).","A vessel that is already narrow closes completely during spasm, so ischemia is deeper and lasts longer, causing ulcers."],"why":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon the digital arteries are structurally normal, so blood flow returns fully after each spasm and ulcers are rare. In systemic sclerosis, endothelial injury triggers intimal proliferation and fibrosis of small arteries, and capillaries drop out, as her nailfold capillaroscopy shows. The lumen stays narrow between attacks, so each spasm shuts the vessel completely and ischemia lasts longer. The result is pitting scars, ulcers, and sometimes gangrene of the fingertips.","whyNot":["Leukocytoclastic vasculitis from immune complexes affects postcapillary venules and gives palpable purpura, as in IgA vasculitis or cryoglobulinemia. It does not explain cold-triggered digital ischemia.","Correct.","Atheroma forms mainly in large and medium arteries, not in small digital arteries, and it is not the lesion of systemic sclerosis.","Cold agglutinins cause acrocyanosis and hemolysis in cold exposure. They are not part of systemic sclerosis, and her antibody findings point to an autoimmune connective tissue disease."],"study":{"topics":["Primary vs secondary Raynaud phenomenon","Vasculopathy of systemic sclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-I8","CV-16"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C51","system":"CV","dx":"Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)","codes":["CV-18-I9"],"case":"A 36-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of cramping pain in the arch of his right foot when he walks. For the past 2 weeks he has had pain in his right toes at rest that is worse at night. He has smoked about 30 cigarettes daily since the age of 15 years. Last year he had tender, erythematous cords along superficial veins of both forearms that resolved spontaneously. He does not have diabetes mellitus. Physical examination shows a small dry ulcer at the tip of the right great toe. Femoral and popliteal pulses are normal; the right dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are absent. Serum LDL cholesterol concentration is 96 mg/dL (less than 100). Angiography shows segmental occlusions of the tibial arteries with corkscrew-shaped collateral vessels. Diagnosis: thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease).","questions":[{"id":"CV-C51-Q1","stem":"A biopsy specimen of an involved segment of his tibial artery obtained during the acute phase is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Inflamed thrombus with microabscesses that spreads to the nearby vein and nerve","Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells centered on the internal elastic lamina","Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with lesions at different stages of healing","Lipid-rich atheroma with cholesterol clefts beneath a fibrous cap"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I9","dok":2,"approach":["Key clues: young heavy smoker, distal small and medium arteries, migratory superficial thrombophlebitis, and normal lipids.","Buerger disease is a segmental inflammation of arteries, veins, and nerves of the limbs.","Its acute lesion is a cellular, inflamed thrombus with microabscesses, with inflammation that extends to the neighboring vein and nerve."],"why":"Thromboangiitis obliterans is a segmental, thrombosing inflammation of small and medium arteries of the hands and feet that nearly always occurs in heavy tobacco users. In the acute phase, the lumen holds a highly cellular thrombus with small microabscesses, sometimes with giant cells, and the inflammation spreads to the adjacent vein and nerve. The internal elastic lamina is usually preserved. Later the thrombus organizes and the artery, vein, and nerve are bound together by fibrosis. Nerve involvement helps explain his severe rest pain.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomas with giant cells centered on a fragmented internal elastic lamina describe giant cell arteritis, which affects older adults and the carotid branches and aorta.","Transmural fibrinoid necrosis with lesions of different ages is the pattern of polyarteritis nodosa. That disease does not involve veins and is not tied to smoking.","Atheroma is the lesion of atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease, usually in older patients with risk factors such as diabetes and high LDL. His age, normal lipids, and phlebitis point elsewhere."],"study":{"topics":["Histology of thromboangiitis obliterans","Comparing vasculitis lesions"],"comps":["CV-18-I9","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q2","stem":"Angiography shows that the longest occlusion is in the artery that descends in the anterior compartment of the leg with the deep fibular nerve. Flow in which of the following arteries is most directly reduced as a result?","options":["Fibular artery","Dorsalis pedis artery","Medial plantar artery","Posterior tibial artery"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the occluded vessel: the artery of the anterior compartment, running with the deep fibular nerve, is the anterior tibial artery.","Follow it distally: it crosses the front of the ankle and continues on the dorsum of the foot.","Its continuation is the dorsalis pedis artery."],"why":"The popliteal artery divides into the anterior tibial artery and the tibioperoneal trunk. The anterior tibial artery passes into the anterior compartment, runs down with the deep fibular nerve, and crosses the front of the ankle midway between the malleoli. On the dorsum of the foot it continues as the dorsalis pedis artery, whose pulse is felt just lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon. An occlusion of the anterior tibial artery therefore removes direct flow to the dorsalis pedis.","whyNot":["The fibular artery also comes from the tibioperoneal trunk and runs in the posterior compartment near the fibula. It is not downstream of the anterior tibial artery.","Correct.","The medial plantar artery is a terminal branch of the posterior tibial artery in the sole, so it lies in a different arterial line.","The posterior tibial artery is a branch of the tibioperoneal trunk in the posterior compartment. It runs with the tibial nerve and is felt behind the medial malleolus."],"study":{"topics":["Arteries of the leg and foot","Peripheral pulse sites"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I9"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q3","stem":"One segment of a tibial artery has its radius reduced by half by thrombus and wall inflammation. If the pressure difference across that segment and blood viscosity remain constant, which of the following best approximates flow through the segment compared with baseline?","options":["About 12 percent of baseline","About 25 percent of baseline","About 6 percent of baseline","About 50 percent of baseline"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Name the law: Poiseuille's law says resistance is inversely proportional to the radius to the fourth power.","Halving the radius gives (1/2) to the fourth power, which is 1/16.","Resistance rises 16-fold, so flow at the same pressure difference falls to 1/16, about 6 percent."],"why":"By Poiseuille's law, flow equals the pressure difference times pi times the radius to the fourth power, divided by 8 times viscosity times length. Because radius enters to the fourth power, halving it raises resistance 16-fold. With the same pressure difference, flow falls to 1/16 of baseline, which is about 6 percent. This steep relationship is why modest narrowing of small distal arteries can leave toes without enough blood at rest.","whyNot":["About 12 percent (1/8) would follow if flow depended on the radius cubed. Poiseuille's law uses the fourth power.","About 25 percent (1/4) would follow if flow depended on cross-sectional area (radius squared). That underestimates the effect of narrowing on resistance.","Correct.","About 50 percent would follow if flow changed in direct proportion to radius. Resistance depends far more steeply on radius than that."],"study":{"topics":["Poiseuille's law","Resistance and vessel radius"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-I6"]}},{"id":"CV-C51-Q4","stem":"His carboxyhemoglobin concentration is 8 percent (less than 2 percent in nonsmokers). Which of the following best describes how carbon monoxide further reduces oxygen delivery to his ischemic toes?","options":["It oxidizes heme iron to the ferric form, producing methemoglobin","It shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve to the right, so oxygen unloads too early","It takes up heme sites and shifts the curve left, so less oxygen unloads","It lowers the dissolved oxygen in plasma, reducing the arterial PO2"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Key fact: carbon monoxide binds the iron of heme with an affinity about 200 times or more that of oxygen.","Bound CO lowers oxygen content by taking heme sites.","It also makes the remaining heme groups hold oxygen more tightly (a left shift), so less oxygen is released in tissues."],"why":"Carbon monoxide binds ferrous heme far more tightly than oxygen does, so each CO molecule takes a site that oxygen could have used, lowering oxygen content. CO also changes the shape of the hemoglobin molecule so the remaining heme groups hold their oxygen more tightly, shifting the dissociation curve to the left. Tissues with already poor flow, such as his toes, then get even less oxygen. The arterial PO2 stays normal, because it reflects only dissolved oxygen.","whyNot":["Oxidizing heme iron to the ferric (Fe3+) form produces methemoglobin, as with nitrites or dapsone. Carbon monoxide binds ferrous (Fe2+) heme without oxidizing it.","A right shift helps oxygen unload in tissues. Carbon monoxide shifts the curve in the opposite direction.","Correct.","The arterial PO2 depends on alveolar oxygen and lung function, which carbon monoxide does not change. Oxygen content falls because hemoglobin sites are occupied."],"study":{"topics":["Carbon monoxide and hemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I9"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C52","system":"CV","dx":"Polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B","codes":["CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of fever, myalgias, and a 6-kg weight loss. For 3 weeks he has had crampy abdominal pain that begins about 30 minutes after meals. One week ago he developed numbness of the left ring and little fingers, and yesterday he awoke with a right foot drop. Blood pressure is 168/98 mm Hg; 1 year ago it was 124/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows tender erythematous nodules and livedo reticularis over both legs. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.9 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3). Urinalysis shows mild proteinuria with no red blood cell casts. Hepatitis B surface antigen is positive, and ANCA is negative. A chest x-ray shows no abnormalities. Angiography shows multiple small aneurysms and segmental narrowing of the renal and mesenteric arteries. Diagnosis: polyarteritis nodosa associated with hepatitis B.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C52-Q1","stem":"His arterial injury is attributed to a viral protein circulating in his blood. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains the damage to his arteries?","options":["IgE bound to mast cells triggers histamine release within the vessel wall","Antibodies bind antigens fixed on the surface of normal endothelial cells","Sensitized T cells form granulomas around viral antigen in the arterial media","Antigen-antibody complexes deposit in vessel walls and activate complement"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: hepatitis B surface antigen circulates in large amounts in his blood.","Soluble antigen plus antibody forms circulating immune complexes.","Complexes that lodge in vessel walls fix complement and recruit neutrophils: type III hypersensitivity."],"why":"In hepatitis B-associated polyarteritis nodosa, circulating hepatitis B surface antigen combines with antibody to form immune complexes. These deposit in the walls of medium-sized arteries, activate complement, and draw in neutrophils, whose enzymes and oxidants cause fibrinoid necrosis. This is type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity. The weakened wall then forms the small aneurysms and narrowings seen on his angiogram.","whyNot":["IgE on mast cells is type I hypersensitivity, which causes allergy and anaphylaxis. It does not cause necrotizing arteritis.","Antibody against an antigen fixed on cells or matrix is type II hypersensitivity, as in anti-glomerular basement membrane disease. Here the antigen circulates in the blood.","T cell granulomas are type IV hypersensitivity, the mechanism of giant cell arteritis. Polyarteritis nodosa shows neutrophils and fibrinoid necrosis without granulomas.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Type III hypersensitivity","Hepatitis B and vasculitis"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q2","stem":"Three years ago, after donating blood, he was told that he tested positive for hepatitis B surface antigen. Today, tests for IgM antibody to hepatitis B core antigen and for antibody to hepatitis B surface antigen are negative. Which of the following best describes the status of his hepatitis B infection?","options":["Window period of a resolving infection","Acute infection in its early phase","Past infection with lasting immunity","Chronic infection"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: surface antigen has been present for years, far longer than 6 months.","IgM anti-core is a marker of recent infection, and it is negative.","Persistent surface antigen without anti-surface antibody means chronic infection."],"why":"Hepatitis B surface antigen that persists for more than 6 months defines chronic infection. IgM antibody to core antigen appears in acute infection and fades over months, so a negative result fits long-standing infection. Antibody to surface antigen appears when the infection clears or after vaccination, and he has none. Ongoing antigen production in chronic infection supplies the antigen for the immune complexes in his arteries.","whyNot":["In the window period surface antigen has already disappeared and antibody to surface antigen has not yet appeared, with IgM anti-core as the main marker. His surface antigen is positive.","Acute infection would show IgM antibody to core antigen, and surface antigen would not have been present 3 years ago.","Past infection with immunity shows antibody to surface antigen and IgG anti-core, with no surface antigen in the blood.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis B serology","Acute vs chronic hepatitis B"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q3","stem":"His postprandial abdominal pain is caused by narrowing of the artery that supplies the jejunum and ileum. This artery arises from which of the following locations?","options":["Anterior aorta at about the T12 vertebra","Anterior aorta at about the L1 vertebra","Lateral aorta at about the L1 to L2 vertebrae","Anterior aorta at about the L3 vertebra"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-9","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the artery: the jejunum and ileum are midgut, supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","Recall the unpaired anterior branches of the abdominal aorta and their levels: celiac trunk T12, superior mesenteric L1, inferior mesenteric L3.","The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L1."],"why":"The jejunum and ileum are midgut structures supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. It arises from the anterior surface of the abdominal aorta at about the L1 vertebral level, just below the celiac trunk, and passes behind the neck of the pancreas. Narrowing of its branches limits the increase in intestinal blood flow that normally follows a meal, which causes postprandial pain and food avoidance with weight loss, a pattern called intestinal angina.","whyNot":["About T12 is the level of the celiac trunk, which supplies the foregut (stomach, liver, spleen, and proximal duodenum).","Correct.","Paired lateral branches at about L1 to L2 are the renal arteries. They are also often involved in polyarteritis nodosa, but they do not supply the small bowel.","About L3 is the level of the inferior mesenteric artery, which supplies the hindgut from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum."],"study":{"topics":["Branches of the abdominal aorta","Midgut blood supply"],"comps":["CV-9","CV-18-I12"]}},{"id":"CV-C52-Q4","stem":"His blood pressure has increased as aneurysms and stenoses have developed in his renal arteries. Which of the following is the initiating event in this increase in blood pressure?","options":["Juxtaglomerular cells sense a lower afferent arteriolar pressure and release renin","Macula densa cells sense a rise in tubular sodium chloride and trigger renin release","Atrial myocytes release natriuretic peptide in response to a larger blood volume","Carotid baroreceptors increase their firing and withdraw sympathetic tone"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: narrowed renal arteries lower the pressure reaching the glomeruli.","Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole act as pressure sensors and release renin when pressure falls.","Renin starts the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which raises vascular resistance and sodium retention."],"why":"Narrowing of renal arteries lowers the pressure in the afferent arterioles downstream. The juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole wall sense this lower stretch and release renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which angiotensin-converting enzyme turns into angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone release, so sodium retention and vasoconstriction raise systemic blood pressure. This renovascular hypertension is common in polyarteritis nodosa.","whyNot":["Correct.","A fall in sodium chloride delivery to the macula densa stimulates renin release. A rise in sodium chloride delivery suppresses renin.","Atrial natriuretic peptide promotes sodium excretion and vasodilation, so it lowers blood pressure.","Increased baroreceptor firing withdraws sympathetic tone, which lowers blood pressure. It is a response to high pressure, not its cause."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Renovascular hypertension"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-A2","CV-18-I12"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C53","system":"CV","dx":"Kawasaki disease with coronary artery dilation","codes":["CV-18-I12"],"case":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of fever for 6 days and marked irritability. His temperature is 39.8 °C (103.6 °F). Physical examination shows bilateral nonexudative conjunctival injection with limbal sparing, red cracked lips, and a strawberry tongue. There is a polymorphous erythematous rash over the trunk, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and a single firm 2-cm left cervical lymph node. Laboratory studies show a C-reactive protein concentration of 110 mg/L (less than 10) and a leukocyte count of 19,600/µL (about 6,000 to 17,000 at his age). A rapid streptococcal antigen test and throat culture are negative. Echocardiography shows dilation of the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery. Diagnosis: Kawasaki disease.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C53-Q1","stem":"Kawasaki disease targets medium-sized muscular arteries such as the coronary arteries. Histologic examination of a normal segment of his left anterior descending artery is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["A thin media, wide lumen, and valves formed by folds of intima","Endothelium with fenestrations and a basal lamina, with no media at all","A smooth muscle media bounded by a distinct internal elastic lamina","A media built mostly of many fenestrated elastic lamellae"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":2,"approach":["Classify the vessel: coronary arteries are medium-sized muscular (distributing) arteries.","Recall the muscular artery wall: a thick media of smooth muscle between a prominent internal elastic lamina and an external elastic lamina.","Rule out the elastic artery, vein, and capillary patterns."],"why":"Muscular arteries, such as the coronary, renal, and mesenteric arteries, have a media made mostly of layered smooth muscle. A distinct, wavy internal elastic lamina separates the intima from the media, and an external elastic lamina often marks the outer edge of the media. In Kawasaki disease, inflammation damages this smooth muscle and elastic tissue, and the weakened segment balloons into an aneurysm.","whyNot":["A thin media, a wide lumen, and valves describe a medium-sized vein, especially in the limbs.","Endothelium with pores (fenestrations) on a basal lamina describes a fenestrated capillary, found in places such as the kidney and endocrine glands.","Correct.","Many fenestrated elastic lamellae in the media mark an elastic (conducting) artery such as the aorta or pulmonary trunk. Kawasaki disease usually spares these."],"study":{"topics":["Muscular vs elastic artery histology","Vessel wall layers"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-8"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q2","stem":"Scarlet fever was considered in the differential diagnosis because it can also cause a red tongue, rash, and later desquamation. The rash of scarlet fever is caused by a streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin that acts as a superantigen. Which of the following best describes how a superantigen activates T lymphocytes?","options":["It links MHC class II to the T cell receptor beta chain outside the groove","It is processed and presented in the MHC class II groove like a normal peptide","It cross-links IgE on mast cells and basophils, releasing histamine","It binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages in the same way as endotoxin"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-17","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the toxin: streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin A of Streptococcus pyogenes is a superantigen.","A superantigen skips antigen processing and binds the outside of MHC class II and the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain.","Because many T cells share beta chain variable regions, a huge number are activated at once, releasing large amounts of cytokines."],"why":"A normal peptide antigen is processed, loaded into the groove of an MHC molecule, and recognized only by the rare T cell clones whose receptors fit it. A superantigen is not processed. It binds intact to the outer surface of MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and to the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain. This links any T cell with a matching beta chain, activating a large fraction of all T cells. The surge of cytokines such as IL-2, interferon gamma, and TNF causes fever, rash, and, with toxic shock toxins, shock.","whyNot":["Correct.","Processing and presentation in the MHC groove is how conventional peptide antigens work. Only a tiny fraction of T cells respond to any one peptide.","Cross-linking IgE on mast cells and basophils is type I hypersensitivity, triggered by allergens, not streptococcal toxins.","CD14 and TLR4 recognize lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) from gram-negative bacteria and activate innate immunity. Streptococci are gram-positive and lack lipopolysaccharide."],"study":{"topics":["Superantigens","Streptococcus pyogenes exotoxins"],"comps":["CV-17","CV-16"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q3","stem":"Over the next 2 weeks, the dilated segment enlarges into an aneurysm with an internal diameter 3 times that of the normal segment just upstream. Total blood flow through the artery is unchanged. Which of the following best describes the mean blood velocity in the aneurysm compared with the velocity upstream?","options":["About the same as upstream","About one third as fast","About three times as fast","About one ninth as fast"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["Apply the continuity principle: flow equals velocity times cross-sectional area, and flow is the same in both segments.","Area depends on the diameter squared, so tripling the diameter makes the area 9 times larger.","Velocity falls to 1/9, and slow flow in the aneurysm favors thrombus formation."],"why":"When the same volume of blood per minute passes through segments in series, flow equals velocity times cross-sectional area in each segment. Cross-sectional area depends on the radius (or diameter) squared, so a 3-fold wider lumen has 9 times the area. Average velocity there drops to about one ninth of the upstream value. Slow, swirling flow in the aneurysm, together with injured endothelium and the high platelet counts of later Kawasaki disease, promotes thrombus that can block the artery and cause myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Velocity stays the same only if the cross-sectional area is unchanged. Here the area is much larger.","One third as fast would be true if velocity depended on diameter directly. Area, and so velocity, depends on diameter squared.","Velocity rises, not falls, where a vessel narrows. Three times faster would fit a segment whose area was one third of normal.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Continuity of flow and velocity","Thrombosis in coronary aneurysms"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"CV-C53-Q4","stem":"In the third week of illness, the skin of his fingertips and toes begins to desquamate. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely at this stage?","options":["Positive antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA)","Platelet count well above the reference range","Rising antistreptolysin O titer","Low complement C3 and C4 levels"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I12","dok":2,"approach":["Place him on the timeline: peeling of the fingers and toes marks the subacute phase, about weeks 2 to 4.","Recall the hallmark laboratory change of this phase: reactive thrombocytosis.","Rule out markers of other diseases: ANCA vasculitis, streptococcal infection, and immune complex disease."],"why":"Kawasaki disease runs in phases. In the acute febrile phase, platelet counts are usually normal. In the subacute phase, about weeks 2 to 4, the fingers and toes peel and the platelet count rises, often to 500,000 to more than 1,000,000/µL (reference about 150,000 to 450,000). Platelet counts then fall back toward normal during convalescence. The high platelet count adds to the risk of thrombosis inside coronary aneurysms in this phase.","whyNot":["ANCA is a marker of small-vessel vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis. It is negative in Kawasaki disease.","Correct.","A rising antistreptolysin O titer points to a recent Streptococcus pyogenes infection, as in rheumatic fever. His streptococcal tests were negative.","Low C3 and C4 suggest complement consumption by immune complexes, as in lupus or cryoglobulinemia. Complement is not low in Kawasaki disease."],"study":{"topics":["Phases of Kawasaki disease","Laboratory findings in vasculitis"],"comps":["CV-18-I12","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"CV-C54","system":"CV","dx":"Multifocal infantile hemangiomas with liver involvement","codes":["CV-18-J1"],"case":"A 10-week-old girl is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. She was born at 31 weeks' gestation. Two weeks after birth, her parents noticed small red marks on her scalp, back, and right thigh, which have since grown rapidly. She is feeding well and gaining weight. Physical examination shows seven bright red, raised, lobulated lesions 0.5 to 2 cm in diameter that partially blanch with pressure. Because she has five or more cutaneous lesions, abdominal ultrasonography is performed and shows several small, well-defined, highly vascular lesions scattered throughout the liver. Diagnosis: multifocal infantile hemangiomas of the skin with liver involvement.","questions":[{"id":"CV-C54-Q1","stem":"The skin lesions consist of tightly packed, capillary-sized channels lined by plump endothelial cells. In a normal continuous capillary, which of the following cells surrounds the endothelium and lies within the same basal lamina?","options":["Pericyte","Fibroblast","Smooth muscle cell","Myoepithelial cell"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-2","dok":2,"approach":["Key clue: the channels are capillary-sized, so compare them with the normal capillary wall.","A capillary wall has endothelium, a basal lamina, and scattered contractile cells with long processes.","These cells, enclosed by the endothelial basal lamina, are pericytes."],"why":"A continuous capillary is a tube of endothelial cells joined by tight junctions and resting on a basal lamina. Pericytes wrap their long processes around the endothelium and sit inside a split in that basal lamina. They have contractile proteins, help stabilize the vessel, and can give rise to smooth muscle cells and fibroblasts after injury. Proliferating infantile hemangiomas contain both endothelial cells and pericytes arranged in capillary-sized lobules.","whyNot":["Correct.","Fibroblasts lie in the surrounding connective tissue outside the basal lamina and do not wrap the endothelium.","Smooth muscle cells form the media of arteries, arterioles, and veins. Capillaries have no muscular media.","Myoepithelial cells wrap glandular secretory units, such as sweat and breast glands, not blood vessels."],"study":{"topics":["Capillary structure","Pericytes"],"comps":["CV-2","CV-18-J1"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q2","stem":"At 14 weeks of age, the liver lesions have increased in size and number. Her serum TSH concentration is now increased and her free T4 concentration decreased for her age, although her newborn thyroid screen was normal. An enzyme produced by the hemangioma tissue is inactivating thyroid hormone faster than her thyroid gland can replace it. Which of the following reactions does this enzyme catalyze?","options":["Blocking thyroid peroxidase coupling of iodotyrosines","Removing an outer-ring iodine, converting T4 into active T3","Blocking iodide uptake by the sodium-iodide symporter","Removing an inner-ring iodine, converting T4 to reverse T3"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-J1","dok":3,"approach":["Key clue: normal newborn screen, then low free T4 with high TSH once large liver hemangiomas appear, so the hormone is being destroyed outside the thyroid.","Hemangioma tissue can express type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase.","Type 3 deiodinase removes an inner-ring iodine, turning T4 into inactive reverse T3 (and T3 into inactive T2)."],"why":"Infantile hemangiomas, especially large or numerous liver lesions, can express high levels of type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase. This enzyme removes an iodine from the inner ring of thyroid hormone, converting T4 into inactive reverse T3 and T3 into inactive T2. When the lesions break down hormone faster than the thyroid can make it, free T4 falls and TSH rises. This is called consumptive hypothyroidism, and it matters in infancy because thyroid hormone is needed for brain development.","whyNot":["Blocking thyroid peroxidase reduces hormone synthesis within the gland, as thionamide drugs do. Her gland works normally, as shown by her normal newborn screen.","Removing an outer-ring iodine is done by type 1 and type 2 deiodinases and activates the hormone by making T3. It would not lower her thyroid hormone activity.","Blocking the sodium-iodide symporter reduces hormone synthesis within the gland, as perchlorate or thiocyanate can. The problem here is breakdown of hormone outside the thyroid.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Thyroid hormone deiodinases","Consumptive hypothyroidism"],"comps":["CV-18-J1","CV-13"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q3","stem":"Over the next month the liver lesions enlarge. Her pulse is 180/min (about 100 to 160 at her age). Physical examination shows bounding pulses, a wide pulse pressure, and hepatomegaly. Which of the following is the initiating change in this high-output state?","options":["Higher systemic vascular resistance from a surge of catecholamine release","Shunting through the lesions that lowers systemic vascular resistance","Lower contractility that reduces stroke volume and causes reflex tachycardia","Reduced venous return as the enlarged liver presses on the vena cava"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J1","dok":3,"approach":["Key clues: bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, and tachycardia point to high cardiac output with low resistance.","Large vascular liver lesions act as low-resistance shunts between arteries and veins.","Lower systemic vascular resistance lowers diastolic pressure and increases venous return, so the heart must pump more blood each minute."],"why":"Large or numerous liver hemangiomas can contain arteriovenous and portovenous shunts. Blood takes this low-resistance path, so systemic vascular resistance falls. Diastolic pressure drops, which widens the pulse pressure and makes pulses bounding. More blood returns quickly to the heart, and cardiac output rises with tachycardia to keep arterial pressure up. If the demand outstrips the heart, she develops high-output heart failure with an enlarged liver.","whyNot":["Higher systemic vascular resistance would narrow the pulse pressure and lower cardiac output. Her findings show the opposite.","Correct.","Lower contractility reduces stroke volume and cardiac output. It would give weak pulses and a narrow pulse pressure, not bounding pulses with a wide pulse pressure.","Reduced venous return lowers cardiac output by the Frank-Starling mechanism. A high-output state needs more venous return, not less."],"study":{"topics":["High-output heart failure","Systemic vascular resistance and pulse pressure"],"comps":["CV-18-J1","CV-11","CV-18-B1"]}},{"id":"CV-C54-Q4","stem":"Her parents ask what the skin lesions will do over time if they cause no complications. Which of the following is the most likely course?","options":["Steady shrinking that is complete within a few more weeks","Slow conversion into angiosarcoma during early childhood","More growth for a few months, then slow regression over several years","Growth in proportion to her body throughout life, without any regression"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-J1","dok":2,"approach":["Key clues: lesions absent at birth, appeared in the first weeks, and are growing fast.","This is the natural history of infantile hemangioma: rapid growth in early infancy, then slow involution.","Rule out the patterns of vascular malformations and malignant vascular tumors."],"why":"Infantile hemangiomas are absent or faint at birth and appear in the first weeks of life. Most of their growth happens in the first 3 to 5 months. Growth then slows and stops, and the lesions involute slowly over several years, with most regression finished by about age 4. Some leave behind thin, wrinkled skin, fibrofatty tissue, or telangiectasias. Female sex and preterm birth, both present here, are known risk factors.","whyNot":["Infantile hemangiomas do not disappear within weeks. Involution takes years.","Infantile hemangiomas are benign and do not turn into angiosarcoma. Angiosarcoma is a rare malignant tumor, usually of older adults.","Correct.","Growth in proportion to the child with no regression describes a vascular malformation, such as a capillary malformation (port-wine stain), which is present at birth."],"study":{"topics":["Natural history of infantile hemangioma","Hemangioma vs vascular malformation"],"comps":["CV-18-J1"]}}]}];
window.NPLEX_DIAGNOSTIC = [{"id":"DX-CV-C01","system":"CV","dx":"Acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction from left anterior descending artery occlusion","codes":["CV-18-C3","CV-8"],"case":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension and a 40 pack-year smoking history comes to the emergency department 4 hours after crushing substernal chest pain began. The pain radiates to his left arm, and he is diaphoretic. Pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. The ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4. Serum troponin I is 8.2 ng/mL (reference below 0.04 ng/mL). Echocardiography shows no motion of the anterior wall, anterior septum, and apex of the left ventricle, with an ejection fraction of 40% (normal 55 to 70%). Diagnosis: acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q1","from":[4],"stem":"Occlusion of which of the following arteries most likely explains the ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V4 and the loss of anterior wall, septal, and apical motion?","options":["Left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Posterior interventricular artery"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-8","dok":2,"approach":["Match the leads to the wall: V1 and V2 face the septum, and V3 and V4 face the anterior wall.","Recall which artery runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex.","That artery, the LAD, supplies the anterior left ventricle, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the septum."],"why":"The left anterior descending artery is a branch of the left main coronary artery that runs in the anterior interventricular groove toward the apex. It supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the apex, and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum through its septal branches. ST elevation in V1 to V4 with anterior and apical wall motion loss is the classic pattern of LAD occlusion, the most common site of coronary occlusion in MI.","whyNot":["Correct.","The right coronary artery supplies the right ventricle and, in most people, the inferior wall, the SA node, and the AV node. Its occlusion causes ST elevation in the inferior leads II, III, and aVF.","The left circumflex artery runs in the left atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior left ventricle. Its occlusion shows up in the lateral leads I, aVL, V5, and V6.","The posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery usually arises from the right coronary artery and supplies the inferior wall and the posterior third of the septum, which leads V1 to V4 do not face."],"study":{"topics":["Coronary artery territories","ECG leads and the walls they face"],"comps":["CV-8","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q2","from":[17],"stem":"Troponin I is a structural protein of the myocyte contractile apparatus. Which of the following processes is most likely responsible for its increased serum concentration in this patient?","options":["Caspase-driven apoptosis of myocytes in the ischemic zone","Hepatic synthesis of troponin as an acute phase reactant","Exocytosis of troponin by viable myocytes under stress","Membrane rupture in myocytes that have died by necrosis"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","dok":2,"approach":["Note that troponin normally sits inside the myocyte, bound to the thin filaments.","Ask what lets an intracellular protein reach the blood: loss of plasma membrane integrity.","Membrane breakdown is the hallmark of irreversible injury and necrosis."],"why":"After about 20 to 40 minutes of severe ischemia, myocyte injury becomes irreversible. ATP loss, calcium influx, and activation of phospholipases and proteases damage the plasma membrane, so the cell contents leak out. Troponin I and troponin T are cardiac-specific, so their leakage into blood marks myocyte necrosis. Troponin begins to rise within about 2 to 4 hours and stays elevated for about 7 to 10 days.","whyNot":["Apoptosis keeps the plasma membrane intact, and the cell fragments are eaten by macrophages without spilling their contents. The cell death of an infarct is mainly coagulative necrosis.","The liver does not make cardiac troponin. Acute phase reactants made by the liver include C-reactive protein and fibrinogen.","Myocytes do not package troponin into vesicles for secretion. A large rise like his reflects leakage from dead cells.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Reversible and irreversible cell injury","Cardiac biomarkers after MI"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q3","from":[26],"stem":"Two days after the onset of pain, he dies of ventricular fibrillation. Which of the following microscopic findings is most likely in the infarcted anterior wall at autopsy?","options":["Granulation tissue with new capillaries and fibroblasts","Dense collagen scar replacing the lost muscle fibers","Macrophages clearing dead myocytes at the infarct border","Coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-C3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the timing: about 48 hours after the infarct began.","Recall the Robbins sequence: neutrophils peak at about 1 to 3 days, macrophages at about 3 to 7 days, granulation tissue at about 7 to 14 days, then scar.","At 2 days, expect dead myocytes with preserved outlines and many neutrophils."],"why":"Myocardial infarcts undergo coagulative necrosis: dead myocytes keep their outlines while their nuclei fade. Neutrophils are drawn into the dead tissue and peak at about 1 to 3 days. Macrophages take over at about 3 to 7 days, granulation tissue forms at about 7 to 14 days, and a collagen scar matures over the following weeks. At 2 days the expected picture is coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate.","whyNot":["Granulation tissue forms at the infarct margins at about 7 to 14 days.","A collagen scar replaces the infarct over several weeks and is largely complete by about 2 months.","Macrophages arrive and begin removing dead myocytes at about 3 to 7 days. At 2 days the infiltrate is still mainly neutrophils.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Timeline of changes after MI","Coagulative necrosis"],"comps":["CV-18-C3","CV-18-H3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C01-Q4","from":[8],"stem":"During the first hours, potassium released from injured cells depolarizes the resting membrane potential of myocytes at the infarct border from about -90 mV to about -65 mV. Which of the following best explains the slowed impulse conduction through these ventricular myocytes?","options":["L-type calcium channels close early, so the phase 2 plateau is lost","Many fast sodium channels stay inactivated, so phase 0 is slower","Funny current channels open, so phase 4 depolarization speeds up","Delayed rectifier potassium channels fail, so phase 3 is prolonged"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-5","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the ventricular myocyte upstroke (phase 0): rapid sodium influx through voltage-gated fast sodium channels.","Recall that these channels inactivate with sustained depolarization and recover only at a negative resting potential.","A less negative resting potential leaves fewer channels available, so the upstroke is smaller and slower, and conduction slows."],"why":"The rapid upstroke of the ventricular myocyte action potential comes from sodium rushing in through voltage-gated fast sodium channels. These channels close an inactivation gate when the membrane stays depolarized and need a negative resting potential to reset. When extracellular potassium rises in ischemic tissue, the resting potential becomes less negative, many sodium channels stay inactivated, and phase 0 becomes smaller and slower. Slow conduction in the border zone sets up reentry, which is why ventricular fibrillation is a common cause of early death after MI.","whyNot":["Calcium entry through L-type channels holds up the plateau and triggers contraction. It does not set how fast the upstroke spreads in ventricular myocytes.","Correct.","The funny current drives spontaneous phase 4 depolarization in SA node cells. Working ventricular myocytes have a stable phase 4 and rely on sodium channels for their upstroke.","Potassium efflux in phase 3 repolarizes the cell and sets the refractory period. It has no role in the speed of the upstroke."],"study":{"topics":["Ventricular myocyte action potential phases","Sodium channel inactivation"],"comps":["CV-5","CV-7","CV-18-C3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C02","system":"CV","dx":"Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with coronary atherosclerosis and stable angina","codes":["CV-18-I4","CV-18-I3","CV-13"],"case":"A 32-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 3-month history of chest pressure that occurs when he climbs stairs and resolves with rest within 5 minutes. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 44 years. Physical examination shows firm nodules on both Achilles tendons. Fasting serum studies show LDL cholesterol 310 mg/dL (desirable below 100 mg/dL), HDL cholesterol 44 mg/dL (40 or higher), and triglycerides 120 mg/dL (below 150 mg/dL). An exercise stress test shows ischemic ST-segment depression. Coronary angiography shows 75% atherosclerotic narrowing of the proximal right coronary artery. Genetic testing shows one pathogenic variant in the LDL receptor gene and one normal copy. Diagnosis: heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with coronary atherosclerosis and stable angina.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q1","from":[16],"stem":"His LDL cholesterol is markedly increased, but his triglyceride concentration is normal. Which of the following is the direct consequence of his LDL receptor defect?","options":["The liver makes and secretes more VLDL than usual","Lipoprotein lipase in capillaries is less active","Less LDL is taken up and cleared by hepatocytes","HDL can no longer carry cholesterol back to the liver"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-18-I4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the pattern: isolated high LDL with normal triglycerides.","Recall that most LDL leaves the blood by receptor-mediated endocytosis into hepatocytes through the LDL receptor.","A defective receptor means LDL stays in the blood longer and its level rises."],"why":"Hepatocytes remove most LDL from the blood by binding its apolipoprotein B-100 to the LDL receptor and taking it in by receptor-mediated endocytosis. In familial hypercholesterolemia, an autosomal dominant defect in the LDL receptor slows this clearance, so plasma LDL rises, often 2 to 3 times normal in heterozygotes. The excess LDL deposits in tendons as xanthomas and in arteries as atheroma, which explains his early coronary disease.","whyNot":["More VLDL output would raise fasting triglycerides as well as LDL. His triglycerides are normal, and an LDL receptor defect does not drive VLDL synthesis.","Low lipoprotein lipase activity causes high triglycerides from retained chylomicrons and VLDL (familial chylomicronemia). It does not raise LDL alone.","Correct.","Reverse cholesterol transport by HDL uses other receptors, such as scavenger receptor B1. His HDL is normal."],"study":{"topics":["LDL receptor and receptor-mediated endocytosis","Familial hypercholesterolemia"],"comps":["CV-18-I4","CV-13","CV-14"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q2","from":[14],"stem":"He begins statin therapy, and 8 weeks later his LDL cholesterol concentration has decreased by 45%. Which of the following best explains this decrease?","options":["It blocks 7-alpha-hydroxylase, so the liver converts more cholesterol to bile","It blocks HMG-CoA reductase, so his normal allele makes more LDL receptors","It blocks cholesterol esterase in the gut, so less dietary cholesterol is absorbed","It blocks lipoprotein lipase, so less LDL is formed from VLDL in the blood"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Name the statin target: HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA to mevalonate).","Follow the effect in the hepatocyte: less cholesterol inside the cell activates SREBP, which turns on LDL receptor gene transcription.","Apply it to him: his one normal LDL receptor allele can still respond, so LDL clearance rises."],"why":"Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate in hepatic cholesterol synthesis. When hepatocyte cholesterol falls, the transcription factor SREBP increases expression of the LDL receptor gene, so the liver pulls more LDL out of the blood. In heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia the normal allele can be upregulated, so statins work well. Patients with two nonfunctional LDL receptor alleles respond poorly for this reason.","whyNot":["7-alpha-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis. Blocking it would leave more cholesterol in the liver, and statins do not target it.","Correct.","Reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption is how ezetimibe works, through the NPC1L1 transporter. Statins act on hepatic cholesterol synthesis.","Blocking lipoprotein lipase would raise triglycerides and is not how statins work. Statins inhibit the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis."],"study":{"topics":["HMG-CoA reductase and SREBP regulation","How statins lower LDL"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-I4"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q3","from":[15],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the lipoprotein particle and the route by which dietary triglyceride leaves his small intestine after a fatty meal?","options":["Chylomicrons, through lacteals and the thoracic duct","Chylomicrons, through the portal vein to the liver","VLDL particles, through lacteals and the thoracic duct","LDL particles, through the portal vein to the liver"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-10","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the particle the enterocyte builds for dietary triglyceride: the chylomicron, with apolipoprotein B-48.","Recall that chylomicrons are too large for blood capillaries and enter the central lacteal of each villus.","Trace the lymph: lacteals, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, and the left venous angle."],"why":"Enterocytes package absorbed dietary triglyceride and cholesterol into chylomicrons containing apolipoprotein B-48. Chylomicrons are too large to pass into blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. In the blood, lipoprotein lipase removes their triglyceride for muscle and fat, and the remnants are taken up by the liver.","whyNot":["Correct.","Chylomicrons are too large to enter intestinal capillaries, so they do not travel in the portal vein. Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are the dietary fats that go by the portal route.","VLDL is made by the liver to export triglyceride the liver itself synthesizes. It does not carry dietary fat out of the intestine.","LDL is formed in the blood from VLDL after its triglyceride is removed. It does not carry dietary fat from the gut."],"study":{"topics":["Chylomicron formation and lymphatic transport","Lipoprotein classes"],"comps":["CV-10","CV-13"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C02-Q4","from":[25],"stem":"Macrophages in his coronary plaque are filled with cholesterol despite his defective LDL receptors. Which of the following best explains how cholesterol accumulated in his plaque?","options":["Oxidized intimal LDL is taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors","Adventitial fibroblasts multiply, and LDL enters the wall from the vasa vasorum","Medial smooth muscle calcifies, and the stiff wall traps LDL from the plasma within it","Fibrin forms on normal endothelium, and LDL is trapped in the clot as it organizes"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that atherosclerosis begins with endothelial injury or dysfunction in the intima.","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized; monocytes enter and become macrophages.","Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which are separate from the LDL receptor, so foam cells form even in familial hypercholesterolemia."],"why":"Atherosclerosis starts when injured or dysfunctional endothelium lets LDL into the intima, where it is oxidized. Recruited monocytes become macrophages that take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which do not depend on the LDL receptor and are not turned off by cholesterol in the cell. Engorged macrophages become foam cells, and smooth muscle cells migrate from the media into the intima and lay down collagen. The result is a fibrous cap over a lipid core. High LDL speeds up every step.","whyNot":["Correct.","Atherosclerosis is a disease of the intima. LDL enters from the lumen through injured endothelium, and the adventitia is not the site of plaque.","Calcification of the media without lipid or inflammation is Monckeberg medial sclerosis. It does not narrow the lumen or form foam cells.","Thrombus usually forms on a plaque after its cap is disrupted. It does not start atherosclerosis on healthy endothelium."],"study":{"topics":["Atherosclerosis pathogenesis","Scavenger receptors and foam cells"],"comps":["CV-18-I3","CV-2","CV-18-I4"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C03","system":"CV","dx":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis with a past subacute endocarditis and current acute Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis","codes":["CV-18-D5","CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1"],"case":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of a 4-day history of fever. At age 9 years, she had fever, migratory polyarthritis, and a new heart murmur about 3 weeks after an untreated sore throat. She has known mitral stenosis with a valve area of 1.3 cm2 (normal 4 to 6 cm2). Three years ago, 2 weeks after a tooth extraction, she had 6 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and a vegetation was found on her mitral valve; that infection was cured. She currently injects heroin. Her temperature is 39.4 °C (102.9 °F). Physical examination shows a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases in intensity with inspiration. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Diagnosis: rheumatic mitral stenosis, a past subacute endocarditis, and acute S. aureus endocarditis.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q1","from":[19],"stem":"Which of the following organisms most likely caused the pharyngitis that preceded her childhood illness?","options":["Streptococcus agalactiae (group B)","Streptococcus pyogenes (group A)","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-D5","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the childhood picture as acute rheumatic fever: carditis and migrating polyarthritis a few weeks after pharyngitis.","Recall that rheumatic fever follows throat infection with group A beta-hemolytic streptococci.","Name the organism: Streptococcus pyogenes."],"why":"Acute rheumatic fever is a delayed, nonsuppurative complication of pharyngitis caused by group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes). It usually starts about 2 to 4 weeks after the sore throat, most often in children aged 5 to 15. Repeated or severe attacks scar the valves, and the mitral valve is affected most often. Prompt antibiotic treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis prevents rheumatic fever.","whyNot":["Group B streptococci colonize the vagina and cause sepsis and meningitis in newborns. They do not cause pharyngitis followed by rheumatic fever.","Correct.","Pneumococcus causes pneumonia, otitis media, and meningitis. It is not linked to rheumatic fever.","S. aureus can cause many infections, but it does not trigger rheumatic fever. Her current endocarditis is a separate, later problem."],"study":{"topics":["Acute rheumatic fever","Group A streptococcus"],"comps":["CV-18-D5","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q2","from":[22],"stem":"No bacteria were present in her heart during the childhood illness, yet her mitral valve was damaged and later scarred. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this valve damage?","options":["Circulating immune complexes deposit in the valve and fix complement","IgE on mast cells triggers degranulation within the valve tissue","Streptococci invade the valve leaflets and form a small abscess","Antibodies to streptococcal M protein bind valve proteins"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-16","dok":2,"approach":["Note that the valve is sterile, so the damage is immune mediated.","Recall molecular mimicry: streptococcal M protein resembles proteins in heart valves and myocardium.","Antibodies made against the bacterium bind the patient's own tissue, which is type II hypersensitivity."],"why":"Rheumatic fever is an example of molecular mimicry. Antibodies made against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with similar proteins in the heart, including valve glycoproteins and myosin, which is classified as type II (antibody-mediated) hypersensitivity. CD4 T cells that react to the same antigens add to the inflammation. Aschoff bodies in the myocardium are the characteristic lesion, and repeated attacks leave the mitral valve thick, fused, and stenotic.","whyNot":["Immune complex deposition is type III hypersensitivity. After streptococcal infection it explains poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, not rheumatic carditis.","IgE and mast cell degranulation is type I hypersensitivity, as in anaphylaxis and allergic asthma. It does not scar heart valves.","Rheumatic fever is nonsuppurative: the valve is sterile. Bacteria growing on a valve would be infective endocarditis.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Molecular mimicry","Hypersensitivity types I to IV"],"comps":["CV-16","CV-18-D5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q3","from":[20],"stem":"Her endocarditis 3 years ago began 2 weeks after a tooth extraction and followed an indolent 6-week course on her scarred mitral valve. Which of the following best explains that infection?","options":["Enterococcus faecalis from the bowel reached the valve after a colonoscopy","Candida albicans from a central line grew on the surface of the valve","Oral viridans streptococci adhered to fibrin on the scarred valve","Staphylococcus aureus from the skin invaded the normal leaflets of the valve"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-D3","dok":3,"approach":["Note the clues: a slow, low-grade illness (subacute), a previously damaged valve, and recent dental work.","Recall that oral viridans streptococci enter the blood during dental procedures.","They adhere to the sterile fibrin and platelet deposits that form on damaged valve endothelium, using surface dextran and adhesins."],"why":"Viridans streptococci, such as Streptococcus sanguinis and Streptococcus mutans, are normal mouth flora that enter the blood during dental work. They are low in virulence, so they usually infect valves that are already damaged, where small sterile fibrin and platelet deposits give them a place to attach. Dextran on their surface helps them stick. The illness develops over weeks to months, which is the classic subacute endocarditis pattern.","whyNot":["Enterococci cause subacute endocarditis after gastrointestinal or genitourinary procedures. Her infection followed dental work.","Fungal endocarditis is linked to central lines, prosthetic valves, and injection drug use. She had none of these at that time, and the trigger was a tooth extraction.","Correct.","S. aureus is highly virulent, can infect normal valves, and causes an acute illness over days. Her infection was slow and sat on a valve that was already damaged."],"study":{"topics":["Acute and subacute endocarditis","Viridans streptococci"],"comps":["CV-18-D3","CV-18-K1","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C03-Q4","from":[24],"stem":"Which of the following valves is most likely the site of her current vegetation?","options":["Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-18-K1","dok":3,"approach":["Note that injected bacteria enter the venous blood and reach the right heart valves first.","A holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration is right-sided regurgitation.","Inspiration raises venous return to the right heart, so right-sided murmurs get louder. The valve is the tricuspid."],"why":"In people who inject drugs, bacteria and particles enter the veins and strike the tricuspid valve first, and S. aureus is the most common organism. Damage to the leaflets causes tricuspid regurgitation, a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border. Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, which increases venous return to the right heart and makes right-sided murmurs louder. Pieces of a tricuspid vegetation travel to the lungs as septic emboli.","whyNot":["Pulmonary valve endocarditis is rare. Its murmurs are heard at the left upper sternal border.","Correct.","Aortic regurgitation gives a diastolic murmur along the left sternal border, and left-sided murmurs do not get louder with inspiration.","The mitral valve is the most common site of endocarditis in people who do not inject drugs. A mitral regurgitation murmur is loudest at the apex and does not increase with inspiration."],"study":{"topics":["Endocarditis in people who inject drugs","Murmurs and their locations"],"comps":["CV-18-K1","CV-4"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C04","system":"CV","dx":"Hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and an aneurysm of the ascending aorta and arch","codes":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I1"],"case":"A 71-year-old man with 30 years of poorly controlled hypertension comes to the physician because of a 4-month history of hoarseness and difficulty swallowing solid food. Blood pressure is 172/98 mm Hg. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.7 mg/dL (0.7 to 1.3 mg/dL) with mild proteinuria. Echocardiography shows a non-dilated left ventricle with a wall thickness of 15 mm (normal 6 to 11 mm), an ejection fraction of 62% (normal 55 to 70%), and impaired relaxation in diastole. CT shows a 6.2 cm aneurysm of the ascending aorta and aortic arch. Diagnosis: hypertensive heart disease with concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and an aneurysm of the ascending aorta and arch.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q1","from":[11],"stem":"Which of the following processes best explains his echocardiographic findings?","options":["Dilated cardiomyopathy with failing contraction","Eccentric hypertrophy from chronic volume overload","Concentric hypertrophy from pressure overload","Constrictive pericarditis that limits filling"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-18-A2","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the load: long-standing hypertension raises afterload, which is a pressure overload.","Apply the Laplace relationship: wall stress rises with pressure and falls as the wall thickens.","Myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel, the wall thickens without dilation, and the stiff wall relaxes poorly."],"why":"Chronic hypertension forces the left ventricle to generate high pressure. By the Laplace relationship, wall stress rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness, so myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel and the wall thickens to bring stress back down. This is concentric hypertrophy. The thick, stiff wall fills poorly in diastole, so ejection fraction stays normal at first while relaxation is impaired. Over many years, the hypertrophied heart can dilate and fail.","whyNot":["Dilated cardiomyopathy gives a large, thin-walled ventricle with a low ejection fraction. His ejection fraction is normal and the cavity is not dilated.","Volume overload, as in mitral or aortic regurgitation, adds sarcomeres in series. The chamber dilates and the wall is not much thicker than normal.","Correct.","A stiff pericardium limits filling, but the myocardium itself is not thickened. His problem is in the ventricular wall."],"study":{"topics":["Concentric and eccentric hypertrophy","Law of Laplace"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-6"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q2","from":[6],"stem":"Compression of which of the following pairs of structures by the aneurysm most likely explains his hoarseness and dysphagia?","options":["Thoracic duct and left sympathetic trunk","Left brachiocephalic vein and left vagus nerve","Left phrenic nerve and trachea","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve and esophagus"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":2,"approach":["Link hoarseness to the vocal cords, which are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerves.","Recall that the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch next to the ligamentum arteriosum.","Link trouble swallowing to the esophagus, which lies just to the right of the arch."],"why":"The left vagus nerve gives off the left recurrent laryngeal nerve as it crosses the aortic arch. The nerve hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum and climbs back up in the groove between the trachea and esophagus to supply most of the muscles of the larynx. An arch aneurysm can stretch or compress it, causing left vocal cord paralysis and hoarseness. The esophagus lies right next to the arch and the descending aorta, so a large aneurysm can also compress it and cause dysphagia.","whyNot":["Compression of the sympathetic trunk can cause Horner syndrome, and duct injury can cause chylothorax. Neither causes hoarseness or trouble swallowing.","A compressed brachiocephalic vein causes swelling of the left arm and face. It does not explain trouble swallowing solid food.","The phrenic nerve controls the diaphragm, so compressing it paralyzes a hemidiaphragm. It does not cause hoarseness, and tracheal compression causes cough and stridor, not trouble swallowing.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Relations of the aortic arch","Recurrent laryngeal nerve course"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-I1"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q3","from":[27],"stem":"His increased serum creatinine concentration and mild proteinuria are attributed to long-standing hypertension without a malignant phase. Which of the following changes is most likely present in the walls of his small renal arterioles?","options":["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis","Hyperplastic (onion-skin) arteriolosclerosis","Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-A2","dok":2,"approach":["Note the setting: years of moderate (benign) hypertension, not a hypertensive emergency.","Recall that chronic pressure pushes plasma proteins into arteriolar walls and stimulates extra matrix.","The result is pink, glassy wall thickening that narrows the lumen: hyaline arteriolosclerosis, which causes nephrosclerosis."],"why":"In long-standing benign hypertension, and also in diabetes, plasma proteins leak through injured endothelium into the walls of small arterioles, and smooth muscle cells lay down extra basement membrane. The wall becomes thick, pink, and glassy on H&E stain, and the lumen narrows. In the kidney, the resulting ischemia shrinks glomeruli and tubules. This is benign nephrosclerosis, which causes a slow rise in creatinine and mild proteinuria.","whyNot":["Correct.","Granulomatous inflammation that destroys vessel walls is seen in vasculitis, such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis. It is not caused by hypertension.","Concentric layers of smooth muscle and basement membrane, often with fibrinoid necrosis, are seen in malignant hypertension, usually with pressures above 180/120 mm Hg and acute organ damage.","Monckeberg sclerosis is calcification in the media of medium-sized muscular arteries in older people. It does not narrow the lumen or damage the kidney."],"study":{"topics":["Arteriolosclerosis types","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis"],"comps":["CV-18-A2","CV-18-I3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C04-Q4","from":[3],"stem":"Three weeks later, he develops sudden tearing chest pain and collapses. Blood pressure is 76/50 mm Hg. Physical examination shows jugular venous distention and muffled heart sounds. The ascending aorta has dissected and ruptured. Into which of the following spaces is blood most likely accumulating?","options":["Left pleural cavity","Superior mediastinum","Posterior mediastinum","Pericardial cavity"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-3","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize Beck triad: low blood pressure, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds point to cardiac tamponade.","Recall that the ascending aorta begins inside the pericardial sac.","Blood from an ascending aortic rupture fills the pericardial cavity, between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium."],"why":"The pericardial cavity is the thin space between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium. Because the fibrous pericardium does not stretch quickly, sudden bleeding into this space raises its pressure and squeezes the heart, especially the thin-walled right side, so the ventricles cannot fill in diastole. Stroke volume and blood pressure fall, venous pressure rises, and the fluid muffles the heart sounds. The same picture follows a stab wound to the heart or rupture of the free wall after a myocardial infarction.","whyNot":["Rupture of the descending thoracic aorta often bleeds into the left pleural cavity. That causes hypotension and a hemothorax, but not distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds.","The aortic arch and great vessels lie in the superior mediastinum, outside the pericardial sac. Bleeding there does not produce Beck triad.","Bleeding there collects around the descending aorta and esophagus, outside the pericardial sac, so it does not cause tamponade. His rupture is in the ascending aorta, which lies inside the sac.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Pericardium layers","Cardiac tamponade and Beck triad"],"comps":["CV-3","CV-18-F2","CV-18-I2"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C05","system":"CV","dx":"Tetralogy of Fallot","codes":["CV-18-G4","CV-1"],"case":"A 4-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of episodes of perioral cyanosis during crying and feeding. She was born at term after a normal pregnancy. Physical examination shows a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border. Oxygen saturation is 82% on room air (normal 95 to 100%). Chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with reduced lung markings. Echocardiography shows a large ventricular septal defect, an aorta that sits over the defect, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract below the pulmonary valve, and right ventricular hypertrophy. Diagnosis: tetralogy of Fallot.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q1","from":[29],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains her cyanosis?","options":["Two parallel circuits with no mixing because the great arteries are switched","Right-to-left shunt across the VSD because flow to the lungs is obstructed","Left-to-right shunt across the VSD because left ventricular pressure is higher","Left-to-right shunt across a patent ductus because aortic pressure is higher"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-G4","dok":2,"approach":["Note the low saturation with reduced lung markings: less blood is reaching the lungs.","Identify the obstruction: right ventricular outflow narrowing raises right ventricular pressure.","With a large VSD, deoxygenated blood takes the easier path into the overriding aorta, a right-to-left shunt."],"why":"In tetralogy of Fallot, narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract makes it hard for blood to reach the lungs. Right ventricular pressure rises to match or exceed left ventricular pressure, so deoxygenated blood crosses the large VSD into the overriding aorta. This right-to-left shunt sends unoxygenated blood to the body and causes cyanosis. The more severe the outflow obstruction, the larger the shunt and the worse the cyanosis.","whyNot":["Parallel circuits are the problem in transposition of the great arteries, where the aorta arises from the right ventricle. Her echo shows an overriding aorta, not transposition.","Correct.","A left-to-right shunt sends oxygenated blood back to the lungs. It increases lung markings and does not cause cyanosis early on, as in an isolated VSD.","Flow from the aorta into the pulmonary artery through a patent ductus returns oxygenated blood to the lungs. It does not cause cyanosis unless pulmonary hypertension later reverses it."],"study":{"topics":["Tetralogy of Fallot","Right-to-left and left-to-right shunts"],"comps":["CV-18-G4","CV-18-G3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q2","from":[],"stem":"During an episode of severe cyanosis, her mother draws the infant's knees up to her chest, and the cyanosis improves. Older children with this defect squat for the same reason. Which of the following best explains this improvement?","options":["It relaxes the outflow tract muscle, so pulmonary resistance falls","It raises systemic resistance, so less blood crosses the VSD","It lowers venous return, so the right ventricle ejects less blood","It lowers systemic vascular resistance, so more blood enters the aorta"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":3,"approach":["See the VSD as a fork: right ventricular blood goes to the lungs or into the aorta, whichever path resists less.","Flexing the legs kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance.","Higher aortic resistance pushes more right ventricular blood through the narrowed outflow tract to the lungs, so oxygenation improves."],"why":"The direction of flow across her VSD depends on the balance between resistance to pulmonary flow and resistance to systemic flow. Pulling the knees to the chest, or squatting, compresses the femoral arteries and raises systemic vascular resistance. With a higher pressure in the aorta, less deoxygenated blood crosses from the right ventricle into the aorta and more goes to the lungs. Anything that lowers systemic resistance, such as exercise or a hot bath, does the opposite.","whyNot":["Spasm of the outflow tract can trigger a spell, but leg position does not relax it. The benefit comes from the systemic side of the circulation.","Correct.","Squatting tends to increase venous return from the legs. Less right ventricular filling would also not send more blood to the lungs.","Lower systemic resistance makes the aorta an easier path for right ventricular blood, which increases the right-to-left shunt and worsens cyanosis."],"study":{"topics":["Tet spells and squatting","Systemic vascular resistance and shunt direction"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-G4"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q3","from":[1],"stem":"Before surgery, a catheter is advanced from the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and then directly into the hypertrophied ventricle with the narrowed outflow tract. Which of the following valves does the catheter cross?","options":["Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-4","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the chamber: the hypertrophied chamber with a narrowed outflow tract in tetralogy of Fallot is the right ventricle.","The catheter is moving from the right atrium to the right ventricle.","Name the right atrioventricular valve: the tricuspid valve."],"why":"The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, with three leaflets held by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles of the right ventricle. A venous catheter passes from the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. In tetralogy of Fallot, right ventricular pressure equals left ventricular and aortic pressure because the large VSD joins the two ventricles and the outflow tract is narrow.","whyNot":["Correct.","The aortic valve guards the exit from the left ventricle. It is reached from the arterial side or by crossing the septum.","The mitral valve lies between the left atrium and left ventricle. A catheter from the inferior vena cava would reach it only by crossing the atrial septum.","The pulmonary valve guards the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. The catheter would cross it only after leaving the right ventricle."],"study":{"topics":["Heart valves and chambers","Right heart catheterization path"],"comps":["CV-4","CV-3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C05-Q4","from":[5],"stem":"Before birth, oxygenated blood from the placenta entered her right atrium through the inferior vena cava and passed directly to the left atrium. Which of the following correctly names this structure and the reason it normally closes after birth?","options":["Ductus arteriosus; rising oxygen and falling prostaglandin E2 constrict it","Foramen ovale; falling oxygen levels constrict the septum primum","Foramen ovale; left atrial pressure rises above right atrial pressure","Ductus venosus; flow in the umbilical vein stops when the cord is clamped"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-1","dok":2,"approach":["Trace fetal blood: inferior vena cava to right atrium, then across the atrial septum to the left atrium.","Name the opening in the atrial septum: the foramen ovale.","At birth, lung blood flow rises, more blood returns to the left atrium, and left atrial pressure pushes the septum primum flap shut."],"why":"In the fetus, the crista dividens (the lower edge of the septum secundum) directs oxygen-rich inferior vena cava blood through the foramen ovale into the left atrium, so it bypasses the airless lungs. At birth the lungs expand, pulmonary resistance falls, pulmonary venous return to the left atrium rises, and placental flow into the right side stops. Left atrial pressure then exceeds right atrial pressure and presses the septum primum against the septum secundum. The opening later seals to form the fossa ovalis.","whyNot":["This is how the ductus arteriosus closes, but the ductus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. It does not carry blood between the atria.","The foramen ovale is the correct structure, but it closes by pressure, with no muscle to constrict. Oxygen level is the trigger for closing the ductus arteriosus.","Correct.","The ductus venosus carries umbilical vein blood past the liver into the inferior vena cava. It does not connect the two atria."],"study":{"topics":["Fetal circulation","Changes in the circulation at birth"],"comps":["CV-1","CV-18-G3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C06","system":"CV","dx":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy (dilated) with left and then right heart failure","codes":["CV-18-K2","CV-18-E1","CV-18-B"],"case":"A 47-year-old woman who grew up in a mud-walled house in rural Bolivia comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of worsening dyspnea with paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and a 1-month history of leg swelling. Physical examination shows bibasilar crackles, an S3 gallop, jugular venous distention, tender hepatomegaly, and pitting edema of the legs. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25% (normal 55 to 70%) and an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 58 mm Hg (normal 15 to 30 mm Hg). Coronary angiography is normal. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0 g/dL). Two serologic tests for Trypanosoma cruzi are positive. Diagnosis: chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy with heart failure.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q1","from":[21],"stem":"By which of the following routes was Trypanosoma cruzi most likely transmitted to her during childhood?","options":["Tsetse fly saliva injected during a bite","Sand fly saliva injected during a bite","Triatomine bug feces rubbed into a bite","Anopheles mosquito saliva injected during a bite"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-K2","dok":2,"approach":["Link the setting, a rural home with mud walls in Latin America, to the insects that live in wall cracks.","Name the vector of Trypanosoma cruzi: triatomine (reduviid, kissing) bugs.","Recall the route: the bug defecates while feeding, and the person rubs infected feces into the bite or the conjunctiva."],"why":"Trypanosoma cruzi is carried by triatomine (reduviid or kissing) bugs that hide in cracks in mud walls and thatched roofs and feed at night. The parasite is in the bug's feces, not its saliva, and enters through the bite wound or mucous membranes when the person scratches or rubs the area. Years later, about 20 to 30% of infected people develop chronic disease, most often a dilated cardiomyopathy, with megaesophagus or megacolon in some.","whyNot":["Tsetse flies in Africa carry Trypanosoma brucei, which causes African sleeping sickness. They do not live in the Americas.","Sand flies transmit Leishmania species, which cause cutaneous and visceral leishmaniasis.","Correct.","Female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium species, which cause malaria."],"study":{"topics":["Chagas disease","Arthropod vectors of protozoa"],"comps":["CV-18-K2","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q2","from":[12],"stem":"Her dyspnea preceded the leg edema, jugular venous distention, and hepatomegaly by several months. Which of the following mechanisms best explains how her left ventricular failure led to these right-sided findings?","options":["Pulmonary venous congestion raised right ventricular afterload","Occlusion of the right coronary artery caused a right ventricular infarct","Low plasma oncotic pressure from protein loss let fluid leave the capillaries","A stiff, thickened pericardium limited filling of the right ventricle"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-B","dok":3,"approach":["Start on the left: a failing left ventricle raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, which causes her breathlessness and crackles.","Follow the pressure backward: pulmonary artery pressure rises (58 mm Hg), raising right ventricular afterload.","The right ventricle fails, so systemic venous pressure rises, causing raised jugular venous pressure, liver congestion, and leg edema."],"why":"Left ventricular failure is the most common cause of right ventricular failure. When the left ventricle cannot empty, left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures rise, and the pulmonary arteries must work at higher pressure to push blood through the lungs. The thin-walled right ventricle tolerates this afterload poorly and eventually fails. Systemic venous pressure then rises, causing raised jugular venous pressure, a congested liver, and dependent edema.","whyNot":["Correct.","Her coronary angiogram is normal, so there was no right coronary occlusion.","Her albumin is normal. Low oncotic pressure, as in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis, causes edema without raised jugular venous pressure.","Constrictive pericarditis limits filling of both ventricles and would not explain a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 25%."],"study":{"topics":["Left-sided and right-sided heart failure","Pulmonary hypertension from left heart disease"],"comps":["CV-18-B","CV-18-A1","CV-15"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q3","from":[7],"stem":"In a healthy heart, an increase in end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume, but her dilated ventricle gains little stroke volume from the same increase in filling. Which of the following is the main cellular basis of this length-tension relationship in a normal ventricle?","options":["Stretch shortens the sarcomeres, so more myosin heads bind to the thin filaments","Stretch releases norepinephrine from cardiac nerve endings, which raises contractility","Stretch lowers wall tension, so afterload falls and the ventricle empties more fully","Stretch sets better filament overlap and makes troponin C more sensitive to calcium"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":3,"approach":["Name the principle: the Frank-Starling mechanism, where preload sets stroke volume.","Go to the sarcomere: stretch moves resting sarcomere length toward the optimum and increases the calcium sensitivity of the myofilaments.","Apply it to her: a failing ventricle has a flattened curve, so extra filling mostly raises filling pressure and congestion."],"why":"When the ventricle fills more, each myocyte is stretched. In the normal working range this brings the sarcomere closer to its optimal length, and it increases the calcium sensitivity of troponin C, so the same calcium transient produces more force. The ventricle therefore ejects more, matching output to venous return beat by beat. In a dilated, failing ventricle the curve is flat, so extra preload adds congestion with little gain in stroke volume.","whyNot":["Filling stretches the sarcomeres, it does not shorten them. In the normal working range, stretch brings sarcomere length closer to the optimum for force.","Norepinephrine raises contractility through beta-1 receptors, but stretch does not release it. The Frank-Starling effect works in a denervated, transplanted heart.","By the Laplace relationship, a larger chamber has higher wall tension for the same pressure, so afterload on each fiber rises with dilation.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Frank-Starling mechanism","Length-dependent activation"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-5"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C06-Q4","from":[30],"stem":"Her low cardiac output decreases perfusion pressure in the renal afferent arterioles, and juxtaglomerular cells release renin. Which of the following sequences correctly describes the subsequent steps leading to aldosterone release?","options":["Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, then ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II","Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin II, then ACE converts angiotensin II to aldosterone","Renin converts angiotensin I to angiotensinogen, then ACE converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin II","ACE converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, then renin converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-11","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the trigger: low afferent arteriolar pressure and less sodium chloride at the macula densa cause renin release.","Place each enzyme: renin cleaves liver-made angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, and ACE, mainly on lung endothelium, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II.","Angiotensin II acts on the zona glomerulosa to release aldosterone."],"why":"Juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole release renin when renal perfusion pressure falls, when the macula densa senses less sodium chloride, or with sympathetic stimulation through beta-1 receptors. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen, made by the liver, to angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme, abundant on pulmonary capillary endothelium, converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates the zona glomerulosa to release aldosterone, which increases sodium and water retention and worsens edema in heart failure.","whyNot":["Correct.","Renin makes angiotensin I, not angiotensin II. Aldosterone is a steroid made from cholesterol in the adrenal cortex, not a product of ACE.","Angiotensinogen is the starting substrate made by the liver. It is not a product of renin.","The enzymes are reversed. Renin acts first, on angiotensinogen, and ACE acts second."],"study":{"topics":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Neurohormonal activation in heart failure"],"comps":["CV-11","CV-18-B"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C07","system":"CV","dx":"Lyme carditis with complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block","codes":["CV-18-K3","CV-7"],"case":"A 26-year-old man from Connecticut comes to the emergency department in August because of a 2-day history of lightheadedness and one episode of syncope. Five weeks ago, after hiking, he had an expanding erythematous rash with central clearing on his thigh that resolved without treatment. Pulse is 42/min and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. The ECG shows regular P waves at 84/min and regular, narrow QRS complexes (0.08 s; normal below 0.12 s) at 42/min, with no fixed relationship between them. Serologic testing confirms Lyme disease. Diagnosis: Lyme carditis with complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q1","from":[23],"stem":"Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of his condition?","options":["Ehrlichia chaffeensis","Treponema pallidum","Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia rickettsii"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"CV-18-K3","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize erythema migrans: an expanding red rash with central clearing after a tick exposure.","Link it to Lyme disease, which in the northeastern United States is carried by Ixodes ticks.","Recall that early disseminated Lyme disease can cause carditis, most often as AV block."],"why":"Lyme disease is caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, carried by Ixodes ticks in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States. Erythema migrans marks early local infection. Weeks later the organism can spread to the heart, nerves, and joints. Lyme carditis most often causes AV block that can change quickly in degree, and it usually resolves with antibiotics, sometimes after temporary pacing.","whyNot":["Ehrlichia is spread by the lone star tick and causes fever, low white cell and platelet counts, and raised liver enzymes. It does not cause erythema migrans or heart block.","T. pallidum causes syphilis. Its main cardiovascular effect is tertiary syphilitic aortitis, which damages the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta years after infection.","Correct.","R. rickettsii causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever: fever, headache, and a rash that starts on the wrists and ankles and spreads to the trunk, including the palms and soles."],"study":{"topics":["Lyme disease stages","Spirochetes"],"comps":["CV-18-K3","CV-17"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q2","from":[2],"stem":"His P waves continue at a regular rate of 84/min although none are conducted to the ventricles. Which of the following correctly names the structure that sets this atrial rate and its location?","options":["AV node, in the interatrial septum near the coronary sinus opening","Bundle of His, in the membranous part of the interventricular septum","Purkinje fibers, under the endocardium of the right ventricle","SA node, in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava"],"answer":3,"sea":"A","comp":"CV-7","dok":2,"approach":["Note that each P wave is an atrial depolarization, so something above the block is firing at 84/min.","Recall the fastest intrinsic pacemaker: the SA node, at about 60 to 100/min.","Locate it: in the wall of the right atrium where the superior vena cava enters, at the top of the crista terminalis."],"why":"The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium where the superior vena cava enters, at the upper end of the crista terminalis. Its cells depolarize on their own faster than any other cardiac tissue, at about 60 to 100/min, so they set the rate of the whole heart in health. In complete heart block, the SA node keeps driving the atria normally, but its impulses cannot reach the ventricles.","whyNot":["The AV node is the structure most often blocked in Lyme carditis. It does not set the atrial rate, and its own rate is only about 40 to 60/min.","The bundle of His carries impulses from the AV node to the bundle branches. It does not drive atrial depolarization.","Purkinje fibers spread the impulse through the ventricles. Their intrinsic rate is about 20 to 40/min, and they do not drive the atria.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Conduction system anatomy","Intrinsic pacemaker rates"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-3"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q3","from":[9],"stem":"His QRS complexes are narrow (0.08 s) and occur at 42/min, independent of the P waves. Which of the following best describes the origin and spread of this escape rhythm?","options":["Atrial depolarization spreads backward from a focus in the AV node","Ventricular depolarization spreads from a focus above the bundle branch split","Ventricular depolarization spreads cell to cell from a Purkinje fiber focus","Ventricular repolarization spreads from a focus above the bundle branch split"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that the QRS complex records ventricular depolarization.","A narrow QRS means the impulse entered the ventricles through the His bundle and spread rapidly through both bundle branches and the Purkinje system.","A rate of about 40 to 60/min with a narrow QRS fits a junctional escape pacemaker in the AV junction or His bundle."],"why":"The QRS complex records depolarization of the ventricles. When the impulse starts at or above the His bundle, it spreads through the fast His-Purkinje system and the QRS stays narrow, below 0.12 s. An escape focus in the ventricular muscle or distal Purkinje fibers has to spread slowly from cell to cell and gives a wide QRS. His narrow QRS at 42/min points to a junctional escape rhythm below a block in the AV node, the usual site of block in Lyme carditis.","whyNot":["Atrial depolarization is shown by the P wave. His P waves come from the SA node and are unrelated to the QRS complexes.","Correct.","An escape beat from the Purkinje fibers or ventricular muscle spreads slowly through muscle, giving a wide QRS of 0.12 s or more and a rate of about 20 to 40/min.","Ventricular repolarization is shown by the T wave. The QRS complex records ventricular depolarization."],"study":{"topics":["ECG waves and intervals","Escape rhythms"],"comps":["CV-7"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C07-Q4","from":[10],"stem":"Intravenous atropine is administered, and his P-wave rate increases from 84/min to 112/min. Atropine blocked which of the following effects of vagal acetylcholine on SA node cells?","options":["Activation of nicotinic receptors that open sodium channels in nodal cells","Activation of M2 receptors that open potassium channels and slow phase 4","Activation of M3 receptors that raise IP3 and release stored calcium","Activation of beta-1 receptors that raise cAMP and speed up phase 4"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-7","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that atropine blocks muscarinic receptors, so the rate rise means vagal tone was slowing the SA node.","Name the cardiac muscarinic receptor: M2, coupled to Gi.","M2 activation opens acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels and lowers cAMP, which reduces the funny current and slows phase 4 depolarization."],"why":"Vagal fibers release acetylcholine onto M2 muscarinic receptors in the SA and AV nodes. Through Gi, the beta-gamma subunits open acetylcholine-sensitive potassium channels, which make the resting potential more negative, and the alpha subunit lowers cAMP, which reduces the funny current. Both effects slow phase 4 depolarization, so the node takes longer to reach threshold and the rate falls. Atropine blocks M2 receptors and removes this brake.","whyNot":["Nicotinic receptors are found at autonomic ganglia and the neuromuscular junction. Postganglionic vagal fibers act on muscarinic receptors at the SA node.","Correct.","M3 receptors act through Gq and IP3 in smooth muscle and glands. The heart uses M2 receptors, which act through Gi.","Beta-1 activation by norepinephrine is the sympathetic effect, and it speeds the heart. Atropine does not block beta-1 receptors."],"study":{"topics":["Autonomic control of heart rate","SA node action potential"],"comps":["CV-7","CV-6"]}}]},{"id":"DX-CV-C08","system":"CV","dx":"Wet beriberi (thiamine deficiency) with high-output heart failure, and deep vein thrombosis, in a woman with pancreatic cancer","codes":["CV-13","CV-18-I10","CV-14"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas has had poor oral intake and frequent vomiting for 3 months. She now has dyspnea, leg edema, and numbness and burning pain in both feet. Her pulse is 110/min, and blood pressure is 112/48 mm Hg. Physical examination shows warm skin and bounding peripheral pulses. Echocardiography shows an end-diastolic volume of 160 mL and an end-systolic volume of 60 mL. Serum lactate is 4.1 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2 mmol/L). She is admitted to the hospital and confined to bed. On hospital day 4, her left calf becomes swollen. Ultrasound shows a deep vein thrombosis in the left femoral and popliteal veins. She is later found to be heterozygous for factor V Leiden. Diagnosis: wet beriberi with high-output heart failure, and a deep vein thrombosis.","questions":[{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q1","from":[13],"stem":"Which of the following is closest to her cardiac output?","options":["1.1 L/min","6.6 L/min","11.0 L/min","17.6 L/min"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"CV-6","dok":2,"approach":["Find stroke volume: end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume, 160 mL minus 60 mL, which is 100 mL.","Multiply by heart rate: 100 mL times 110/min is 11,000 mL/min.","Convert and judge: 11.0 L/min is about twice the normal resting output of about 5 L/min, which fits high-output failure."],"why":"Cardiac output equals stroke volume times heart rate. Stroke volume is the end-diastolic volume minus the end-systolic volume, here 100 mL, so her output is 100 mL times 110/min, or 11.0 L/min. Her ejection fraction is 100 divided by 160, about 63%, so the pump itself is not weak. In wet beriberi, peripheral vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance and the heart must pump a very high output, which over time leads to failure with congestion.","whyNot":["This misplaces the decimal when converting units. Stroke volume is 100 mL, and 100 mL times 110/min is 11,000 mL/min, which is 11.0 L/min.","This multiplies the end-systolic volume (60 mL) by the heart rate. End-systolic volume is the blood left in the ventricle after the beat, not the amount ejected.","Correct.","This multiplies the end-diastolic volume (160 mL) by the heart rate. Not all of the end-diastolic volume is ejected."],"study":{"topics":["Cardiac output and stroke volume","High-output heart failure"],"comps":["CV-6","CV-11"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q2","from":[18],"stem":"Her increased serum lactate and her cardiac and neurologic findings result from deficiency of a cofactor required by several enzymes of energy metabolism. Which of the following reactions is most directly impaired?","options":["Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA","Alanine to pyruvate by transamination","Pyruvate to oxaloacetate","Methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-13","dok":3,"approach":["Name the missing cofactor in wet beriberi: thiamine pyrophosphate, made from vitamin B1.","List the enzymes that need it: pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase, and transketolase.","Link pyruvate dehydrogenase failure to her findings: pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and aerobic ATP production falls in heart and nerve."],"why":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, which converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA so it can enter the citric acid cycle. It is also needed by alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase. Without it, pyruvate builds up and is turned into lactate, and tissues that rely on glucose oxidation, such as nerves and heart, cannot make enough ATP. Poor intake, vomiting, and alcohol use are common causes of deficiency, and giving glucose before thiamine can make it worse.","whyNot":["Correct.","Transamination needs pyridoxal phosphate from vitamin B6. B6 deficiency causes neuropathy, cheilosis, and sideroblastic anemia.","Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin. Biotin deficiency is rare and causes dermatitis and hair loss.","This reaction needs vitamin B12 (adenosylcobalamin). B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and spinal cord disease, not high-output heart failure."],"study":{"topics":["Thiamine-dependent enzymes","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex"],"comps":["CV-13","CV-18-E1"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q3","from":[28],"stem":"Which of the following components of Virchow triad best explain the formation of her deep vein thrombosis?","options":["Endothelial injury and turbulent flow","Endothelial injury and venous stasis","Venous stasis and hypercoagulability","Turbulent flow and hypercoagulability"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"CV-18-I10","dok":2,"approach":["List the triad: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis or turbulence), and hypercoagulability.","Match her risks: bed rest causes venous stasis; pancreatic cancer and factor V Leiden cause hypercoagulability.","Pick the pair that matches the case."],"why":"Virchow triad names three factors that lead to thrombosis: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow, and hypercoagulability. In the deep leg veins, slow flow from immobility is the main flow problem, because the calf muscle pump stops working. Mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas such as pancreatic cancer release procoagulant factors, including tissue factor, and factor V Leiden adds an inherited hypercoagulable state. Her deep vein thrombosis therefore reflects stasis and hypercoagulability.","whyNot":["These two drive thrombosis in arteries and the heart, such as on a ruptured plaque or an aneurysm. In the deep leg veins of an immobile patient, slow flow is the main problem.","Stasis applies to her, but nothing in the case points to injury of the vein wall, such as trauma, surgery, or a catheter. This pair also leaves out her cancer and factor V Leiden.","Correct.","Her cancer and factor V Leiden make her blood hypercoagulable, but blood in the deep leg veins of a bedbound patient moves slowly, which is stasis, not turbulence."],"study":{"topics":["Virchow triad","Deep vein thrombosis risk factors"],"comps":["CV-18-I10","CV-18-H6"]}},{"id":"DX-CV-C08-Q4","from":[],"stem":"Factor V Leiden is a single amino acid substitution in factor V. Which of the following best explains how this variant increases her risk of thrombosis?","options":["Factor V is made in greater amounts by the liver","Factor V is no longer able to bind antithrombin","Factor Va binds tissue factor more tightly","Factor Va resists activated protein C"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"CV-14","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the normal brake: activated protein C, with protein S, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa.","Identify the variant: factor V Leiden changes arginine 506, the site where activated protein C cuts factor Va.","Factor Va stays active longer, so more thrombin is made and clots form more easily."],"why":"Factor V Leiden is a point mutation in the factor V gene that replaces arginine with glutamine at position 506, one of the sites where activated protein C cleaves factor Va. Factor Va therefore resists inactivation, keeps supporting the prothrombinase complex, and more thrombin is made. It is the most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European descent and is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Heterozygotes have a several-fold higher risk of venous thrombosis, which adds to risks such as cancer and immobility.","whyNot":["The Leiden variant does not raise the amount of factor V. Increased levels of a clotting factor describe other risks, such as high factor VIII.","Antithrombin mainly inhibits thrombin and factor Xa, and heparin speeds this up. Antithrombin deficiency is a separate inherited thrombophilia.","Tissue factor binds factor VII to start the extrinsic pathway. Factor V does not bind tissue factor.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Factor V Leiden","Protein C pathway"],"comps":["CV-14","CV-18-H6"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C01","system":"GI","dx":"MASH cirrhosis with portal hypertension, ascites, esophageal varices, and Barrett esophagus","codes":["GI-13-C2","GI-13-C5","GI-13-J1","GI-13-G2"],"case":"A 57-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of increasing abdominal girth. He has obesity (BMI 38 kg/m2), type 2 diabetes, 20 years of heartburn, and known cirrhosis from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH). He does not drink alcohol. Physical examination shows shifting dullness, spider angiomas, and a palpable spleen tip. Laboratory studies show albumin 2.6 g/dL (3.5 to 5.0), platelet count 82,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), and INR 1.8 (0.8 to 1.2). Paracentesis yields clear, straw-colored fluid with a serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) of 1.8 g/dL (1.1 or higher points to portal hypertension) and no increase in neutrophils. Screening endoscopy shows large distal esophageal varices and a tongue of salmon-colored mucosa extending 4 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. Diagnosis: MASH cirrhosis with portal hypertension, ascites, esophageal varices, and Barrett esophagus.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q1","from":[32],"stem":"His varices form where portal blood is diverted into systemic veins at the lower esophagus. Which of the following portal tributaries carries this blood backward into the esophageal submucosal veins?","options":["Left gastric vein","Superior rectal vein","Paraumbilical veins","Inferior mesenteric vein"],"answer":0,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-J1","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the varices: distal esophagus.","Name the anastomosis there: portal left gastric (coronary) vein meets systemic esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system.","With portal hypertension, blood flows backward through the left gastric vein into the thin-walled submucosal veins."],"why":"The left gastric (coronary) vein drains the lesser curvature of the stomach into the portal vein, and its esophageal branches connect with esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system. When portal pressure rises, blood is pushed backward through the left gastric vein into these submucosal esophageal veins. They dilate into varices that can rupture and cause massive hematemesis, a leading cause of death in cirrhosis.","whyNot":["Correct.","The superior rectal vein is the portal side of the anorectal anastomosis with the middle and inferior rectal veins. In portal hypertension it feeds anorectal varices, not esophageal ones.","The paraumbilical veins run in the falciform ligament and connect with superficial veins of the abdominal wall. When they dilate, they produce caput medusae around the umbilicus.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the left colon and upper rectum into the splenic vein. It does not reach the esophagus."],"study":{"topics":["Portosystemic anastomoses","Esophageal varices"],"comps":["GI-13-J1","GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q2","from":[56],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his ascites?","options":["Blocked thoracic duct releasing milky, fat-rich lymph","Tumor implants on the peritoneum leaking protein-rich fluid","Bacterial infection of the peritoneum producing an exudate","High sinusoidal pressure with low plasma albumin"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-I1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the SAAG: 1.1 g/dL or higher means portal hypertension drives the fluid.","Add the low albumin from reduced hepatic synthesis, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure.","Combine the two Starling forces: high hydrostatic pressure out, low oncotic pressure holding fluid in."],"why":"Scarring in cirrhosis raises resistance in the sinusoids, so hydrostatic pressure in the sinusoids and splanchnic capillaries rises and fluid leaks into the peritoneal cavity. The failing liver also makes less albumin, which lowers plasma oncotic pressure. Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, so the kidneys retain sodium and water that keep the ascites coming back. A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more confirms that portal hypertension is the driver.","whyNot":["Chylous ascites from lymphatic disruption (lymphoma, trauma, surgery) is milky and high in triglycerides. His fluid is clear and straw colored.","Peritoneal carcinomatosis produces protein-rich fluid with a SAAG below 1.1 g/dL. His SAAG of 1.8 g/dL points to portal hypertension instead.","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is an infection of ascites that is already present, and it raises the ascitic neutrophil count. His fluid has no increase in neutrophils, and infection does not explain why the ascites formed.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Ascites and SAAG","Starling forces"],"comps":["GI-13-I1","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q3","from":[46],"stem":"His INR of 1.8 reflects falling hepatic synthesis of clotting factors. Deficiency of which of the following factors prolongs the prothrombin time (PT) earliest as liver function declines?","options":["Factor VIII, made mostly by sinusoidal endothelium","Factor IX, which the prothrombin time measures directly","Factor VII, which has the shortest half-life","Factor XII, which begins the extrinsic coagulation pathway"],"answer":2,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C2","dok":3,"approach":["Recall what the PT measures: the extrinsic and common pathways (VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen).","Recall that the liver makes these factors, including the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X.","Pick the factor that falls first when synthesis drops: the one with the shortest half-life."],"why":"Hepatocytes make most clotting factors, including the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X and the anticoagulant proteins C and S. When synthesis fails, the factor with the shortest plasma half-life falls first. Factor VII lasts only about 4 to 6 hours, and because it starts the extrinsic pathway, the PT and INR rise early in liver failure. Vitamin K does not fully correct this, because the problem is synthesis, not vitamin supply.","whyNot":["Factor VIII is made mainly by sinusoidal and other endothelial cells, and its level is often normal or high in cirrhosis. It is measured by the aPTT, not the PT.","Factor IX belongs to the intrinsic pathway and is measured by the aPTT. A low factor IX level prolongs the aPTT, as in hemophilia B.","Correct.","Factor XII starts the intrinsic (contact) pathway, and its deficiency prolongs the aPTT without bleeding. The extrinsic pathway begins with tissue factor and factor VII."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatic synthesis of clotting factors","PT versus aPTT"],"comps":["GI-13-C2","GI-9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C01-Q4","from":[57],"stem":"Biopsy of the salmon-colored segment shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells where stratified squamous epithelium should be. This change is a precursor of which of the following tumors?","options":["Primary lymphoma of the esophageal wall","Small cell carcinoma of the esophagus","Adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus","Squamous cell carcinoma of the mid esophagus"],"answer":2,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G2","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize intestinal metaplasia (goblet cells) in the distal esophagus: Barrett esophagus from chronic reflux.","Follow the sequence: metaplasia, then dysplasia, then carcinoma.","A glandular precursor gives a glandular cancer: adenocarcinoma near the gastroesophageal junction."],"why":"Years of acid and bile reflux replace the distal esophageal squamous lining with intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing goblet cells. This metaplastic epithelium can become dysplastic and then progress to adenocarcinoma, which arises in the distal esophagus and gastroesophageal junction. Obesity adds to his risk because it promotes reflux. Patients with Barrett esophagus are followed with surveillance endoscopy to catch dysplasia early.","whyNot":["Primary esophageal lymphoma is very rare and does not arise from intestinal metaplasia.","Small cell carcinoma of the esophagus is a rare neuroendocrine tumor with no link to Barrett metaplasia.","Correct.","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma arises from the native squamous lining, usually in the middle third. Its main risks are tobacco, alcohol, very hot drinks, and achalasia, not columnar metaplasia."],"study":{"topics":["Barrett esophagus","Esophageal cancer types"],"comps":["GI-13-G2","GI-13-K1","GI-13-G8"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C02","system":"GI","dx":"Adenocarcinoma of the head of the pancreas with obstructive jaundice","codes":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 68-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of itching, dark urine, pale greasy stools, and a 7-kg weight loss. She has no abdominal pain. Physical examination shows jaundice and a smooth, nontender, pear-shaped mass below the right costal margin. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 9.4 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 7.9 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), alkaline phosphatase 610 U/L (40 to 120), and CA 19-9 1,250 U/mL (under 37). CT shows a 3-cm mass in the head of the pancreas, dilated common bile and pancreatic ducts, a distended gallbladder without stones, and tumor contact with the portal vein. Biopsy confirms ductal adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q1","from":[58],"stem":"Which of the following best explains why her gallbladder is enlarged and nontender?","options":["Slow tumor blockage of the distal bile duct stretches a healthy gallbladder","A stone lodged in the cystic duct inflames and swells the gallbladder wall","Years of stone-related inflammation have thickened and enlarged the gallbladder","Tumor has spread into the gallbladder wall and formed a bulky mass there"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-K6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the sign: painless jaundice with a palpable, nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign).","Ask what lets a gallbladder distend: a healthy, compliant wall and slowly rising pressure below it.","Match the cause: gradual distal bile duct blockage by a pancreatic head tumor."],"why":"The pancreatic head surrounds the distal common bile duct, so a tumor there slowly narrows the duct. Bile backs up gradually, and a gallbladder with a normal, compliant wall stretches until it can be felt. Because the process is slow and not inflammatory, the gallbladder is not tender. Gallstone disease scars the wall, so obstruction from a stone rarely produces a large palpable gallbladder.","whyNot":["Correct.","A stone impacted in the cystic duct causes acute cholecystitis, which makes the gallbladder tender (Murphy sign) and causes pain and fever. Her gallbladder is painless and has no stones.","Chronic stone disease scars the gallbladder wall, so it becomes small and stiff and cannot distend. This is why a palpable, painless gallbladder points away from stones.","Pancreatic head cancer blocks the bile duct; it does not usually invade the gallbladder. CT shows a distended gallbladder without a wall mass."],"study":{"topics":["Courvoisier sign","Obstructive jaundice"],"comps":["GI-13-K6","GI-13-C1"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q2","from":[34],"stem":"At surgery, the surgeon places a finger in the omental (epiploic) foramen and the thumb on its anterior border. Which of the following groups of structures runs in this free edge of the lesser omentum?","options":["Inferior vena cava, hepatic veins, and cystic duct","Hepatic artery proper, portal vein, and bile duct","Splenic artery, splenic vein, and pancreatic duct","Left gastric artery, portal vein, and thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-3","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the free edge of the lesser omentum: the hepatoduodenal ligament, the anterior border of the omental foramen.","Recall its contents, the portal triad: bile duct (right), hepatic artery proper (left), portal vein (posterior).","Check each option against that list."],"why":"The hepatoduodenal ligament is the free right edge of the lesser omentum and forms the front wall of the omental foramen. It carries the common bile duct on the right, the hepatic artery proper on the left, and the portal vein behind both. Surgeons use this relation to find the bile duct, and squeezing the ligament (the Pringle maneuver) stops inflow to the liver during bleeding.","whyNot":["The inferior vena cava lies behind the omental foramen, forming its posterior wall, and the hepatic veins drain into it above the liver. Neither runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","Correct.","The splenic artery and vein run along the upper and back surface of the pancreas, and the pancreatic duct runs inside the gland. None of them lies in the lesser omentum.","The left gastric artery runs in the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature, not in its free edge, and the thoracic duct is in the posterior mediastinum."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatoduodenal ligament","Omental foramen"],"comps":["GI-3","GI-4","GI-5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q3","from":[31],"stem":"If the tumor occludes the portal vein, venous pressure will rise in which of the following groups of organs?","options":["Lower limbs, pelvic walls, and bladder","Kidneys, adrenal glands, and gonads","Stomach, spleen, and intestines","Head, neck, and upper limbs"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-5","dok":2,"approach":["Recall how the portal vein forms: the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas.","List what those veins drain: the abdominal gut from stomach to upper rectum, plus the spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder.","Choose the option made only of portal territory."],"why":"The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas, where the superior mesenteric vein joins the splenic vein, so pancreatic head tumors often touch or block it. It carries blood from the stomach, small and large intestines, spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder to the liver, which processes absorbed nutrients and drugs before they reach the systemic circulation. Blockage raises pressure throughout this territory and can cause splenomegaly, bowel congestion, and gastric varices.","whyNot":["The lower limbs, pelvic walls, and bladder drain through the iliac veins into the inferior vena cava.","The kidneys, adrenal glands, and gonads drain into the inferior vena cava or the left renal vein, which are systemic veins.","Correct.","The head, neck, and upper limbs drain into the superior vena cava."],"study":{"topics":["Portal venous system","Portal vein formation"],"comps":["GI-5","GI-13-C5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C02-Q4","from":[38],"stem":"Her pale, greasy stools reflect blocked flow of bile and pancreatic juice. Normally, fat and protein entering the duodenum release a hormone that contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic acinar enzyme secretion. Which of the following cells releases this hormone?","options":["S cells of the duodenal mucosa","I cells of the duodenum and jejunum","G cells of the gastric antrum","K cells of the duodenum and upper jejunum"],"answer":1,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the hormone from its two actions: gallbladder contraction plus acinar enzyme secretion is cholecystokinin (CCK).","Recall its trigger: fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum.","Recall its source: I cells of the duodenum and jejunum."],"why":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is released by I cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa when fatty acids and amino acids arrive. It contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates acinar cells to secrete lipase, amylase, and proteases, and slows gastric emptying. In this patient the hormone still works, but the tumor blocks both ducts, so bile and enzymes cannot reach the gut and fat is poorly digested.","whyNot":["S cells release secretin when acid enters the duodenum. Secretin stimulates bicarbonate and water secretion from pancreatic duct cells, not gallbladder contraction.","Correct.","G cells release gastrin, which stimulates gastric acid secretion. Gastrin does not contract the gallbladder.","K cells release glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP), which raises insulin release after a meal. It does not drive gallbladder contraction or enzyme secretion."],"study":{"topics":["CCK","GI hormones and their cells"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-7"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C03","system":"GI","dx":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a duodenal gastrinoma","codes":["GI-13-K3","GI-13-G5"],"case":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of watery diarrhea with greasy, foul-smelling stools. He has had three duodenal ulcers in 2 years. Helicobacter pylori was found on gastric biopsy at his first endoscopy and treated, and a negative stool antigen test confirmed eradication, yet ulcers returned, including one in the proximal jejunum. Off acid-suppressing drugs, fasting serum gastrin is 1,150 pg/mL (under 100) with a gastric pH of 1.2. Endoscopy shows prominent, thickened gastric folds. Imaging finds a 1.5-cm mass in the duodenal wall. Diagnosis: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a duodenal gastrinoma.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q1","from":[39,51],"stem":"His first gastric biopsy showed curved organisms in the mucus layer of the antrum. Which of the following best describes this organism and how it survives gastric acid?","options":["Gram-positive, facultative anaerobic rod; catalase removes peroxide","Gram-positive coccus in chains; a toxin increases acid output","Gram-negative, strictly anaerobic rod; spores resist the acid","Gram-negative, microaerophilic spiral rod; urease makes ammonia"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-12","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the organism: curved rods in the antral mucus layer of an ulcer patient are Helicobacter pylori.","Recall its features: gram negative, microaerophilic, helical or spiral rod with flagella.","Recall its acid survival tool: urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, buffering the acid around it."],"why":"Helicobacter pylori is a gram-negative, microaerophilic, flagellated spiral rod that lives in the mucus layer over the gastric epithelium, mostly in the antrum. Its urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia neutralizes acid in its immediate surroundings while also injuring epithelial cells. Flagella let it swim through mucus, and the CagA and VacA proteins add to tissue damage. The same urease is the basis of the urea breath test and the rapid urease biopsy test.","whyNot":["Helicobacter pylori is gram negative, not gram positive. It does make catalase, but catalase protects against neutrophil peroxide, not against gastric acid.","Helicobacter pylori is a curved rod, not a coccus, and it does not survive by raising acid output. Gram-positive cocci in chains are streptococci.","Helicobacter pylori is microaerophilic, not a strict anaerobe, and it does not form spores. Spore formation is a feature of gram-positive rods such as Clostridium and Bacillus.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Helicobacter pylori virulence","Gram-negative spiral organisms"],"comps":["GI-12","GI-13-G5","GI-13-G7"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q2","from":[43],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his greasy, foul-smelling stools?","options":["Excess acid in the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase","Gastrin acts on enterocytes and drives chloride secretion","Gastrin overstimulates the gallbladder and causes bile acid loss","Gastrin blocks CCK receptors on pancreatic acinar cells"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-K3","dok":3,"approach":["Start with the cause: autonomous gastrin secretion drives huge acid output.","Follow the acid: it overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate, so the duodenal pH stays low.","Apply enzyme pH limits: lipase is inactivated in acid, and bile acids precipitate, so fat is not digested."],"why":"The gastrinoma releases gastrin continuously, so parietal cells secrete large volumes of acid. This acid overwhelms the bicarbonate from the pancreas, and the duodenal pH stays low. Pancreatic lipase is inactivated at low pH, and bile acids precipitate, so fewer micelles form and fat is poorly digested and absorbed. The result is steatorrhea, alongside the watery diarrhea caused by the sheer volume of gastric secretion.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gastrin does not stimulate intestinal chloride secretion. Secretory diarrhea from chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera toxin and VIP-secreting tumors, and it does not cause fat malabsorption.","Gastrin is a weak stimulus for gallbladder contraction at most. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease or resection, which he does not have.","Gastrin and CCK share a receptor family, but gastrin does not block acinar CCK receptors. Enzyme secretion is still present; the enzymes are inactivated after they reach the acidic duodenum."],"study":{"topics":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Fat digestion and pH"],"comps":["GI-13-K3","GI-7"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q3","from":[37],"stem":"Gastrin is the main hormonal stimulus of gastric acid secretion. Besides acting on parietal cells directly, it raises acid output by making a neighboring cell release a paracrine mediator that binds H2 receptors on parietal cells. Which of the following cells releases this mediator?","options":["D cell of the oxyntic mucosa","Chief cell at the gland base","Mucous neck cell of the gland","Enterochromaffin-like cell"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","dok":3,"approach":["Identify the mediator from the receptor: H2 receptors bind histamine.","Name the gastric cell that releases histamine: the enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cell.","Link it to gastrin: gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on ECL cells and triggers histamine release."],"why":"Gastrin from antral G cells, or here from the tumor, binds CCK2 (CCK-B) receptors on parietal cells and on enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells. ECL cells respond by releasing histamine, which binds H2 receptors on nearby parietal cells and raises cAMP, strongly increasing H+/K+-ATPase activity. This is why H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid secretion. Long-term high gastrin also makes ECL cells and parietal cells multiply.","whyNot":["D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release and acid secretion. Somatostatin does not act on H2 receptors.","Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. They do not release histamine.","Mucous neck cells secrete mucus and are stem-like precursors. They do not release histamine.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Regulation of gastric acid secretion","Gastrin and ECL cells"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-2"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C03-Q4","from":[33],"stem":"To confirm that his thickened folds come from hyperplasia of the acid-secreting cells, the endoscopist biopsies the region where those cells are concentrated. Which of the following regions is most likely biopsied?","options":["Antrum near the pylorus","Gastric body and fundus","Cardia near the esophagus","First part of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-2","dok":2,"approach":["Name the acid-secreting cell: the parietal cell.","Recall where parietal cells live: the oxyntic glands of the gastric body and fundus.","Remember that gastrin is trophic to this mucosa, so chronic hypergastrinemia thickens its folds."],"why":"Parietal cells, which secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, are concentrated in the oxyntic glands of the gastric body and fundus, along with chief cells that secrete pepsinogen. The antrum holds G cells that make gastrin, and the cardia holds mainly mucous glands. Constant high gastrin from a gastrinoma makes parietal cells and ECL cells multiply, so the body and fundus mucosa thickens into prominent folds.","whyNot":["The antral glands contain G cells and mucous cells and have few parietal cells. Gastrin from the antrum acts on the body and fundus.","Correct.","The cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction are mostly mucus secreting and contain few parietal cells.","The duodenum has no parietal cells. Its Brunner glands secrete alkaline mucus."],"study":{"topics":["Gastric gland cell types","Regions of the stomach"],"comps":["GI-2","GI-3"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C04","system":"GI","dx":"Celiac disease with iron deficiency anemia and coexisting primary biliary cholangitis","codes":["GI-13-E2","GI-13-C1"],"case":"A 41-year-old woman comes to the physician because of an 8-month history of bloating, loose bulky stools, fatigue, and a 5-kg weight loss, and a 1-year history of generalized itching. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (12.0 to 16.0), MCV 71 fL (80 to 100), and ferritin 5 ng/mL (15 to 150). Tissue transglutaminase IgA is strongly positive. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Alkaline phosphatase is 390 U/L (40 to 120) with normal bilirubin, antimitochondrial antibody (AMA) is positive, and ultrasound shows no bile duct dilation. Diagnosis: celiac disease with iron deficiency anemia and coexisting primary biliary cholangitis (PBC), an autoimmune liver disease that is more common in people with celiac disease.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q1","from":[41],"stem":"Because of where celiac disease damages the bowel, which of the following is she most likely to malabsorb early in her disease?","options":["Fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K","Vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor","Short-chain fatty acids from colonic fermentation","Conjugated bile acids returning to the liver"],"answer":0,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-E2","dok":2,"approach":["Locate the injury: celiac disease is worst in the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Recall what those segments absorb: most dietary fat and fat-soluble vitamins, plus iron, calcium, and folate.","Exclude options absorbed in the terminal ileum or colon."],"why":"Gluten-driven immune injury flattens the villi most severely in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, where most dietary fat and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K are absorbed. The loss of surface area causes bulky, fatty stools and deficiencies such as low vitamin D with bone loss and low vitamin K with easy bruising. Her PBC can add to fat malabsorption by reducing bile flow late in its course. The terminal ileum and colon are usually spared early in celiac disease.","whyNot":["Correct.","The vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum, which celiac disease usually spares early on. B12 deficiency suggests ileal disease, as in Crohn disease.","Short-chain fatty acids are made and absorbed in the colon, which celiac disease does not damage.","Conjugated bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Their loss follows ileal disease or resection."],"study":{"topics":["Celiac disease malabsorption","Fat-soluble vitamins"],"comps":["GI-13-E2","GI-6","GI-8"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q2","from":[40],"stem":"Her iron deficiency persists even though her diet contains enough iron. Which of the following best explains why celiac disease often causes iron deficiency?","options":["Iron is absorbed mostly in the terminal ileum, where gluten acts","Iron absorption needs intrinsic factor made by parietal cells","Iron is absorbed mostly in the duodenum, where damage is worst","Iron absorption needs bile acid micelles to cross the mucosa"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the absorption site for iron (and calcium): the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Recall the site of maximal celiac injury: the same proximal segment.","Connect them: proximal villous atrophy causes iron deficiency that does not respond well to oral iron."],"why":"Dietary nonheme iron is reduced from Fe3+ to Fe2+ at the brush border and enters duodenal enterocytes through DMT1, then leaves through ferroportin. Active, calcitriol-driven calcium absorption also happens mainly in the duodenum. Because celiac disease damages the duodenum most, iron deficiency anemia is one of its most common presentations, sometimes without diarrhea. Unexplained iron deficiency that does not respond to oral iron is a classic reason to test for celiac disease.","whyNot":["The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 and bile acids, not most of the iron. Celiac damage is also worst proximally.","Intrinsic factor is needed for vitamin B12 absorption, not iron absorption.","Correct.","Bile acid micelles carry fats and fat-soluble vitamins. Iron is taken up by a membrane transporter without micelles."],"study":{"topics":["Iron absorption","Nutrient absorption sites"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-8","GI-13-E2"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q3","from":[47],"stem":"Her liver adjusts iron absorption to her depleted iron stores by changing its output of a peptide hormone. Which of the following changes occurs, and what is its effect?","options":["Hepcidin rises, so ferroportin is degraded and absorption falls","Hepcidin falls, so more ferroportin stays in cell membranes","Transferrin falls, so less iron is carried away from enterocytes","Erythropoietin falls, so the marrow's demand for iron decreases"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-8","dok":3,"approach":["Name the liver hormone that controls iron: hepcidin.","Recall its action: it binds ferroportin and causes its internalization and degradation.","Apply to low stores: hepcidin falls, ferroportin stays, and enterocytes and macrophages release more iron into blood."],"why":"Hepcidin, made by hepatocytes, is the main regulator of body iron. It binds ferroportin, the only known iron exporter, on enterocytes and macrophages and causes it to be degraded, which traps iron inside those cells. When iron stores are low, hepcidin falls, ferroportin stays in the membrane, and absorption rises. In hereditary hemochromatosis, hepcidin is inappropriately low despite iron overload, so absorption continues unchecked.","whyNot":["Hepcidin rises when iron stores are full or with inflammation, which lowers iron absorption. With a ferritin of 5 ng/mL, her stores are empty, so hepcidin is suppressed.","Correct.","Transferrin is a carrier protein, not a regulating hormone, and its level rises in iron deficiency (high TIBC).","Erythropoietin is made mainly by the kidney, not the liver, and anemia raises it."],"study":{"topics":["Hepcidin and ferroportin","Iron homeostasis"],"comps":["GI-8","GI-9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C04-Q4","from":[60],"stem":"Her itching and high alkaline phosphatase come from autoimmune injury in the liver. Which of the following cells are the main target of this injury?","options":["Kupffer cells lining the hepatic sinusoids","Stellate cells in the space of Disse","Hepatocytes around the central veins","Small intrahepatic bile duct cells"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-C1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the pattern: itching and high alkaline phosphatase with normal duct imaging is intrahepatic cholestasis.","Add the AMA: this marks primary biliary cholangitis.","Name the target: epithelial cells (cholangiocytes) of small intrahepatic bile ducts."],"why":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune disease in which T cells destroy the epithelial cells (cholangiocytes) of small intrahepatic bile ducts. Bile backs up, causing itching, high alkaline phosphatase, and later jaundice, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, and cirrhosis. Antimitochondrial antibodies against the E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex are found in more than 90% of patients. It mainly affects middle-aged women and often occurs with other autoimmune diseases.","whyNot":["Kupffer cells are resident macrophages. They take part in liver inflammation but are not the target of PBC.","Stellate cells store vitamin A and become collagen-producing myofibroblasts in fibrosis. They cause scarring but are not the target of the autoimmune attack.","Hepatocytes are the main target in autoimmune and viral hepatitis, which raise ALT and AST far more than alkaline phosphatase.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Primary biliary cholangitis","Cholestasis patterns"],"comps":["GI-13-C1","GI-11"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C05","system":"GI","dx":"Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon","codes":["GI-13-G9","GI-13-K7"],"case":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 6-month history of right lower quadrant cramping, nonbloody diarrhea, and an 8-kg weight loss. Her temperature is 37.9 °C (100.2 °F). Physical examination shows a tender mass in the right lower quadrant. Colonoscopy shows deep linear ulcers and a cobblestone pattern in the terminal ileum and in patches of the ascending colon, with normal mucosa between them and a normal rectum. A 1.4-cm tubular adenoma is removed from her sigmoid colon, which is not inflamed. Ileal biopsies show transmural inflammation with non-caseating granulomas. Diagnosis: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum and colon.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q1","from":[53],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the main cells in her ileal granulomas and the cytokine that drives their formation?","options":["Plasma cells and B cells, activated by IL-4 from Th2 cells","Neutrophils and mast cells, activated by IL-5 from Th2 cells","Epithelioid macrophages, driven by IFN-gamma from Th1 cells","Eosinophils and NK cells, activated by IL-17 from Th17 cells"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-11","dok":3,"approach":["Recognize a granuloma: a collection of activated macrophages.","Recall the cell types: epithelioid macrophages, often with multinucleated giant cells, ringed by CD4 T cells.","Recall the signal: Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages (type IV hypersensitivity)."],"why":"Granulomas form through a type IV, cell-mediated response. Macrophages present antigen and release IL-12, which drives CD4 T cells toward the Th1 type. Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which turns macrophages into epithelioid cells that can fuse into multinucleated giant cells, surrounded by a cuff of lymphocytes. In Crohn disease the granulomas are non-caseating, which helps separate it from tuberculosis.","whyNot":["Plasma cells and B cells make antibodies, and IL-4 drives class switching to IgE. Antibody responses do not build granulomas.","Neutrophils form abscesses and mast cells drive immediate hypersensitivity. IL-5 recruits and activates eosinophils, and none of these form granulomas.","Correct.","Eosinophils and NK cells are not the core of granulomas. IL-17 recruits neutrophils and contributes to Crohn inflammation, but the granuloma itself is organized by Th1 cells."],"study":{"topics":["Granuloma formation","Type IV hypersensitivity"],"comps":["GI-11","GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q2","from":[55],"stem":"Six months later she has pneumaturia and recurrent urinary tract infections with enteric organisms. Which of the following features of her disease, in contrast to ulcerative colitis, best explains this complication?","options":["Continuous mucosal inflammation spreading up from the rectum","Crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa","Loss of haustra that leaves a short, tube-shaped colon","Transmural inflammation that tunnels into nearby organs"],"answer":3,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-G9","dok":3,"approach":["Read the new clue: pneumaturia with enteric organisms means a fistula from bowel to bladder.","Ask what lets inflammation reach the bladder: inflammation through the full thickness of the bowel wall.","Match to the disease: transmural, patchy (skip) inflammation is Crohn disease; mucosal, continuous inflammation from the rectum is ulcerative colitis."],"why":"Crohn disease inflames the full thickness of the bowel wall in patches (skip lesions) anywhere from mouth to anus, most often the terminal ileum. Deep fissuring ulcers can tunnel through the wall and form fistulas to bowel, bladder, vagina, or skin, and they can also cause abscesses and strictures. Ulcerative colitis involves only the mucosa and submucosa, starting at the rectum and spreading continuously, so fistulas are not a feature of it.","whyNot":["Continuous inflammation spreading upward from the rectum is the pattern of ulcerative colitis. Her rectum is normal and her disease is patchy.","Inflammation limited to the mucosa and submucosa is typical of ulcerative colitis, and it does not reach other organs.","A shortened colon without haustra (lead-pipe colon) is a late feature of ulcerative colitis and does not create tracts to the bladder.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","Fistulas in Crohn disease"],"comps":["GI-13-G9"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q3","from":[35],"stem":"Two years later, about 80 cm of her terminal ileum is removed because of a stricture. For which of the following vitamins will she most likely need lifelong monitoring and replacement?","options":["Vitamin B12, taken up with intrinsic factor by ileal receptors","Vitamin C, taken up only after binding gastric intrinsic factor","Folate, taken up mainly by the terminal ileum after digestion","Thiamine, taken up mainly by the terminal ileum with bile acids"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","dok":2,"approach":["Identify what was removed: the terminal ileum.","Recall what only the terminal ileum absorbs: the vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor complex (and bile acids).","Predict the result: B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia once liver stores run out over years."],"why":"Parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 in the duodenum after pancreatic proteases free it from haptocorrin. The B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed only in the terminal ileum through cubilin receptors. Removing a long stretch of terminal ileum, or severe ileal Crohn disease, therefore causes B12 malabsorption. Liver stores last years, so deficiency appears late, which is why monitoring and replacement are lifelong.","whyNot":["Correct.","Vitamin C does not bind intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is specific for vitamin B12.","Folate is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and jejunum, so it is not lost with ileal resection.","Thiamine is absorbed mainly in the jejunum and does not depend on bile acids."],"study":{"topics":["Vitamin B12 absorption","Ileal resection effects"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-8"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C05-Q4","from":[59],"stem":"The sigmoid tubular adenoma arose in a segment free of colitis, so it follows the usual sporadic pathway. Which of the following sequences of genetic changes drives that pathway from normal mucosa to carcinoma?","options":["TP53 loss early, with APC loss only late in the process","APC loss, then KRAS activation, then TP53 loss","MLH1 or MSH2 loss, causing microsatellite instability","BRCA2 loss, causing failed homologous recombination repair"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-K7","dok":2,"approach":["Recognize the lesion: a tubular adenoma in uninflamed colon follows the adenoma-carcinoma sequence.","Recall the order: APC loss starts the adenoma, KRAS activation enlarges it, and TP53 loss allows invasive cancer.","Separate it from the mismatch repair pathway and from colitis-associated cancer."],"why":"Most sporadic colorectal cancers follow the chromosomal instability pathway. Loss of both APC alleles turns on Wnt signaling and starts the adenoma, activating KRAS mutations let it grow, and loss of SMAD4 and TP53 allows the shift to invasive carcinoma. Adenomas take years to progress, which is why removing them prevents cancer. Cancers arising in colitis, as can happen in long-standing Crohn colitis, tend to lose TP53 early instead.","whyNot":["Early TP53 loss with late APC loss is the order seen in colitis-associated cancer, which arises in chronically inflamed mucosa without a preceding adenoma. Her adenoma arose outside the colitis.","Correct.","Loss of mismatch repair genes causes microsatellite instability. This happens in Lynch syndrome and in sporadic cancers with MLH1 promoter silencing, together about 15% of colorectal cancers, so it is not the usual pathway.","BRCA2 loss is linked to breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancers. It is not a step in the colorectal adenoma sequence."],"study":{"topics":["Adenoma-carcinoma sequence","Colorectal cancer pathways"],"comps":["GI-13-K7","GI-10"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C06","system":"GI","dx":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome from enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli O157:H7","codes":["GI-13-L1","GI-13-L3"],"case":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of pallor and decreased urine output. Six days ago he developed abdominal cramps and watery diarrhea that became bloody; the illness began 4 days after he ate an undercooked hamburger at a barbecue. He has had no fever. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL (11.5 to 15.5), platelet count 42,000/µL (150,000 to 400,000), creatinine 2.6 mg/dL (0.3 to 0.7), and a high LDH. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative, and the blood smear shows schistocytes. Stool testing detects Shiga toxin, and culture grows Escherichia coli O157:H7. Diagnosis: hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) caused by enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC).","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q1","from":[52],"stem":"The toxin in his stool binds the glycolipid Gb3 on endothelial cells of the colon and glomeruli and enters them. Which of the following is the action of its A subunit inside the cell?","options":["ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 in the cytosol","Links MHC class II to T-cell receptors outside the groove","Cleaves synaptobrevin and blocks vesicle release","Removes one adenine from 28S rRNA in the 60S subunit"],"answer":3,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L1","dok":2,"approach":["Identify the toxin: EHEC O157:H7 makes Shiga toxin (Shiga-like toxin, verotoxin).","Recall its enzyme action: the A subunit is an N-glycosidase that removes a single adenine from 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit.","Connect to disease: protein synthesis stops, endothelial cells die, and microthrombi form in glomeruli."],"why":"Shiga toxin is an AB5 toxin. Its B pentamer binds Gb3 (globotriaosylceramide), which is plentiful on glomerular and colonic endothelial cells. Inside the cell, the A subunit acts as an N-glycosidase that removes one specific adenine from the 28S rRNA of the 60S ribosomal subunit, so aminoacyl-tRNA can no longer bind and protein synthesis stops. Endothelial injury exposes a surface that triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi, producing HUS.","whyNot":["ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2 is how diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A stop protein synthesis.","Linking MHC class II to T-cell receptors outside the peptide groove is the action of superantigens such as TSST-1, which cause toxic shock.","Cleaving synaptobrevin is the action of tetanus toxin and some botulinum toxins, which block neurotransmitter release.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Shiga toxin mechanism","EHEC and HUS"],"comps":["GI-13-L1","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q2","from":[54],"stem":"While his culture was pending, the health department asked whether the family keeps backyard poultry or pet reptiles. That question screens for a different cause of bloody diarrhea. Which of the following identifies that organism and explains why the question fits?","options":["Shigella, which lives only in humans and spreads person to person","Vibrio cholerae, which lives in poultry and causes bloody stools","Salmonella, which has animal hosts and can enter the blood","Shigella, which has animal hosts and spreads mainly through eggs"],"answer":2,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L3","dok":3,"approach":["Spot the exposure clue: poultry, eggs, and reptiles are animal reservoirs.","Recall which enteric pathogen has those reservoirs: nontyphoidal Salmonella.","Contrast with Shigella: humans are the only reservoir, a very small dose causes infection, and spread is person to person."],"why":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella lives in animals, especially poultry, eggs, and reptiles, so public health teams ask about these exposures. It invades the intestinal mucosa and can enter the bloodstream, particularly in infants, older adults, and people with sickle cell disease or weak immunity. Shigella has no animal reservoir, spreads person to person with a very small infectious dose, and usually stays in the colon. Both are gram-negative rods that do not ferment lactose.","whyNot":["This statement about Shigella is true, but it explains why Shigella spreads in daycares and households. It does not explain a question about poultry and reptiles.","Vibrio cholerae lives in brackish and salt water and in shellfish, and it causes watery, not bloody, diarrhea.","Correct.","Shigella has no animal reservoir, so poultry and eggs are not its usual source."],"study":{"topics":["Salmonella versus Shigella","Reservoirs of enteric pathogens"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q3","from":[42],"stem":"Shiga toxin and cholera toxin are both AB5 toxins whose B pentamer binds a cell-surface glycolipid. Cholera toxin, however, causes profuse rice-water diarrhea without blood. Which of the following is the mechanism of its A subunit?","options":["ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha, so cAMP rises and CFTR secretes chloride","Activates guanylyl cyclase, so cGMP rises and NaCl absorption falls","ADP-ribosylates Gi alpha, so adenylyl cyclase loses its inhibition","Forms pores in enterocyte membranes, so the cells lyse"],"answer":0,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-13-L3","dok":3,"approach":["Recall the cholera toxin target: the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gs).","Follow the signal: ADP-ribosylation blocks GTP hydrolysis, so adenylyl cyclase stays on and cAMP rises.","Follow the effect: protein kinase A opens CFTR, chloride and water pour into the lumen, and the cells survive, so the stool is watery without blood."],"why":"Cholera toxin's B pentamer binds the ganglioside GM1 on enterocytes, and its A1 fragment ADP-ribosylates the Gs alpha subunit. This blocks GTP hydrolysis, so Gs stays active and adenylyl cyclase keeps making cAMP. Protein kinase A then opens CFTR chloride channels and reduces NaCl absorption, and water follows into the lumen. The enterocytes stay alive and intact, so the diarrhea is watery and noninflammatory, unlike the bloody diarrhea of EHEC.","whyNot":["Correct.","Raising cGMP through guanylyl cyclase is the action of the heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli, not cholera toxin.","ADP-ribosylation of Gi alpha is the action of pertussis toxin. It raises cAMP in airway cells and lymphocytes, not by locking Gs on.","Forming pores in enterocyte membranes is the action of Clostridium perfringens enterotoxin, which damages the cells. Cholera toxin leaves enterocytes alive and intact."],"study":{"topics":["Cholera toxin mechanism","Secretory diarrhea"],"comps":["GI-13-L3","GI-12","GI-6"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C06-Q4","from":[],"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains his anemia?","options":["IgG coats red cells, which splenic macrophages then remove","Red cells are sliced by microthrombi in small vessels","Toxin in the blood suppresses red cell production in marrow","Complement lyses red cells that lack CD55 and CD59"],"answer":1,"sea":"X","comp":"GI-13-L1","dok":2,"approach":["Read the clues: schistocytes, high LDH, low platelets, negative Coombs test.","Name the pattern: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.","Link to the toxin: endothelial injury triggers platelet-fibrin microthrombi that shear red cells, mainly in glomeruli."],"why":"Shiga toxin injures endothelial cells, especially in the glomeruli. Platelets stick to the damaged surface and form platelet-fibrin microthrombi, which use up platelets and narrow small vessels. Red cells are sliced as they squeeze past these thrombi, producing schistocytes, high LDH, and anemia, while blocked glomerular capillaries cause acute kidney injury. This triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury defines HUS.","whyNot":["IgG-coated red cells cause warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which gives a positive direct Coombs test and spherocytes. His Coombs test is negative.","Correct.","Marrow suppression lowers red cell production without fragmented cells or a high LDH. His schistocytes and high LDH point to destruction in the circulation.","Red cells lacking CD55 and CD59 are found in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, an acquired stem cell disorder unrelated to EHEC."],"study":{"topics":["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","HUS pathogenesis"],"comps":["GI-13-L1"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C07","system":"GI","dx":"Acute hepatitis A in a man with Gilbert syndrome","codes":["GI-13-L7","GI-13-C3"],"case":"A 23-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 5-day history of fever, nausea, right upper quadrant pain, and dark urine. Three weeks ago he ate raw oysters on a trip, and two friends from the trip are also ill. His temperature is 38.2 °C (100.8 °F). Physical examination shows jaundice and a tender liver edge. Laboratory studies show ALT 2,150 U/L (7 to 56), total bilirubin 6.8 mg/dL (0.1 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 4.1 mg/dL (0 to 0.3), and INR 1.1 (0.8 to 1.2). IgM anti-HAV is positive. HBsAg and anti-HBc are negative, and anti-HBs is positive. His records show three episodes of mild jaundice since age 19 during fasting or illness, each with unconjugated bilirubin of 2.4 to 3.0 mg/dL, normal ALT, and normal hemoglobin and reticulocyte counts. Diagnosis: acute hepatitis A.","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q1","from":[49],"stem":"Which of the following is the most likely course of his hepatitis A infection?","options":["Full recovery with lasting immunity and no carrier state","Chronic infection in most patients, with slow scarring over decades","Chronic infection in about 5% of adults, with later cirrhosis","Latent virus in hepatocytes that reactivates during later stress"],"answer":0,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","dok":2,"approach":["Use the clues: raw shellfish and a cluster of ill travelers point to fecal-oral spread, and IgM anti-HAV confirms acute hepatitis A.","Recall the natural history of HAV: acute, self-limited illness.","Exclude options describing chronic hepatitis B or C or latent viruses."],"why":"Hepatitis A is a nonenveloped single-stranded RNA picornavirus spread by the fecal-oral route through contaminated food, water, and shellfish. It causes an acute illness that resolves in nearly all patients, with no chronic infection and no carrier state, and recovery gives lifelong immunity (IgG anti-HAV). Rare patients develop fulminant liver failure, mostly older adults or people with existing liver disease. Hepatitis E is also spread fecal-orally but can become chronic in people with weak immunity.","whyNot":["Correct.","Chronic infection in most patients describes hepatitis C, which spreads mainly through blood.","Chronic infection in about 5% of adults infected in adulthood describes hepatitis B, which spreads through blood and sexual contact.","Hepatitis A, an RNA virus, does not establish latency. Latency and reactivation are features of herpesviruses."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis A natural history","Comparison of hepatitis viruses"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-12"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q2","from":[45],"stem":"Which of the following defects best explains the jaundice episodes recorded since age 19?","options":["Short red cell survival from a membrane skeleton defect","Promoter variant lowering UGT1A1 activity to about 30%","Defective MRP2 transport of conjugated bilirubin into bile","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity from a coding mutation"],"answer":1,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-13-C3","dok":3,"approach":["Characterize the episodes: mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia triggered by fasting or illness, with normal ALT.","Exclude hemolysis: normal hemoglobin and reticulocytes.","Name the condition and defect: Gilbert syndrome, reduced (not absent) UGT1A1 activity, most often from an extra TA repeat in the promoter."],"why":"Gilbert syndrome is the most common inherited cause of hyperbilirubinemia. In most people of European descent it comes from an extra TA repeat in the UGT1A1 promoter, which lowers expression of bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase to about 30% of normal. Bilirubin stays normal or mildly high and rises with fasting, illness, stress, or exertion. Liver enzymes and liver structure are normal, and no treatment is needed.","whyNot":["A red cell membrane defect such as hereditary spherocytosis causes hemolysis with anemia and a high reticulocyte count. His hemoglobin and reticulocytes were normal.","Correct.","An MRP2 defect is Dubin-Johnson syndrome, which raises conjugated bilirubin and darkens the liver. His past episodes were unconjugated.","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity is Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I, which causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus in the newborn period."],"study":{"topics":["Gilbert syndrome","Inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"comps":["GI-13-C3","GI-9","GI-10"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q3","from":[44],"stem":"During his earlier episodes his urine stayed normal in color, but now it is dark with bilirubin. Which of the following changes allows bilirubin to appear in his urine now?","options":["Albumin binding increased in plasma, so it passes the glomerulus","Glycine was attached in hepatocytes, so it became lipid soluble","Glucuronic acid was added in hepatocytes, so it dissolves in water","Sulfate was attached by gut bacteria, so it is reabsorbed from bowel"],"answer":2,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-9","dok":3,"approach":["Compare the episodes: before, unconjugated bilirubin; now, a large conjugated (direct) fraction.","Recall the change: UGT1A1 attaches glucuronic acid, making bilirubin water soluble.","Apply to the kidney: conjugated bilirubin that leaks back into blood from injured hepatocytes is filtered and appears in urine."],"why":"Unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and tightly bound to albumin, so it is not filtered and never appears in urine, which is why his Gilbert episodes left his urine normal. In the hepatocyte, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase attaches glucuronic acid, making conjugated (direct) bilirubin that is water soluble and normally secreted into bile. Hepatitis injures hepatocytes and bile canaliculi, so conjugated bilirubin leaks back into blood, is filtered by the kidney, and darkens the urine.","whyNot":["Albumin binding keeps unconjugated bilirubin out of the urine, because the albumin complex is too large to pass the glomerular barrier.","Glycine and taurine are attached to bile acids, not bilirubin, and conjugation makes molecules more water soluble, not lipid soluble.","Correct.","Gut bacteria turn conjugated bilirubin into urobilinogen; they do not sulfate it. Urobilinogen reabsorbed from the gut is a small, normal urine component that does not make urine dark with bilirubin."],"study":{"topics":["Bilirubin conjugation","Bilirubinuria"],"comps":["GI-9","GI-13-C3"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C07-Q4","from":[50],"stem":"He asks whether he could also acquire hepatitis D. Based on his serologic results, which of the following best describes his risk?","options":["At risk, because HDV replicates on its own once inside hepatocytes","Protected, because HDV needs HBsAg made by HBV for its envelope","Protected, because HDV spreads only by the fecal-oral route","At risk, because antibodies against HBsAg do not neutralize HDV"],"answer":1,"sea":"M","comp":"GI-13-L7","dok":3,"approach":["Read the serology: anti-HBs positive with HBsAg and anti-HBc negative means vaccinated against HBV.","Recall what HDV needs: HBsAg from an active HBV infection to build its envelope.","Conclude: no HBV infection, so no HDV infection."],"why":"Hepatitis D virus is a small, defective RNA virus. It can replicate its genome inside hepatocytes, but it uses HBsAg made by hepatitis B virus as its envelope, so it can only infect people who also have HBV. It can arrive at the same time as HBV (coinfection, which usually clears) or infect someone with chronic HBV (superinfection, which causes the most severe and rapidly progressive hepatitis). Because he is immune to HBV from vaccination, he is protected from HDV as well.","whyNot":["HDV can copy its own RNA genome using host RNA polymerase, but it cannot make an envelope or leave the cell without HBsAg from HBV. Without HBV it cannot spread.","Correct.","HDV spreads like HBV, through blood, sexual contact, and from mother to child. The fecal-oral route belongs to hepatitis A and E.","The HDV envelope is made of HBsAg, so anti-HBs neutralizes HDV particles. HBV vaccination therefore also prevents HDV infection."],"study":{"topics":["Hepatitis D","Hepatitis B serology"],"comps":["GI-13-L7","GI-12"]}}]},{"id":"DX-GI-C08","system":"GI","dx":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon)","codes":["GI-13-F5","NE-20-E3"],"case":"A 2-day-old boy born at term is evaluated because he has not passed meconium. He now has abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. His mother has phenylketonuria (PKU) and kept a phenylalanine-restricted diet before and during the pregnancy; his father has no family history of PKU. Rectal examination shows a tight rectum, and withdrawal of the finger releases an explosive gush of stool and gas. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectum and sigmoid colon below a cone-shaped transition zone and a dilated colon above it. Suction rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosal plexus and thickened nerve fibers. Diagnosis: Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon).","questions":[{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q1","from":[36],"stem":"Which of the following developmental failures best explains the missing ganglion cells?","options":["Splanchnic mesoderm did not form the muscularis externa","Endoderm did not recanalize the gut lumen after its solid stage","Neural crest cells did not migrate all the way to the distal colon","The midgut loop did not rotate around the superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":2,"sea":"A","comp":"GI-13-F5","dok":2,"approach":["Identify what is missing: ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system.","Recall their origin: neural crest cells that enter the foregut early in development and migrate in a head-to-tail wave toward the end of the hindgut.","Apply: migration that stops early leaves the most distal segment (rectum and sigmoid) without ganglia."],"why":"The enteric nervous system comes from neural crest cells, mainly from the vagal region, that enter the foregut and migrate down the gut toward the anus. In Hirschsprung disease this migration stops short, so a continuous distal segment starting at the rectum has no ganglion cells in the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. That segment cannot relax, so it acts as a functional obstruction, and the normal bowel above it dilates. Risk is higher in boys and in Down syndrome.","whyNot":["Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue. A muscle defect does not explain absent ganglion cells.","Failed recanalization of the gut lumen, best known in the duodenum, causes stenosis or atresia. That is a structural blockage, and ganglion cells are normal.","Correct.","Failed midgut rotation causes malrotation with a risk of volvulus. It does not affect ganglion cells in the rectum."],"study":{"topics":["Enteric nervous system development","Hirschsprung disease"],"comps":["GI-13-F5","GI-1"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q2","from":[],"stem":"The aganglionic segment stays tightly contracted. Loss of which of the following neurons best explains why it cannot relax?","options":["Sympathetic fibers that release norepinephrine onto the bowel","Excitatory motor neurons that release acetylcholine and substance P","Vagal preganglionic fibers that release acetylcholine onto ganglia","Inhibitory motor neurons that release nitric oxide and VIP"],"answer":3,"sea":"P","comp":"GI-6","dok":3,"approach":["Recall that gut smooth muscle relaxes in front of a bolus by an enteric reflex.","Identify the neurons that make it relax: intrinsic inhibitory motor neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP.","Apply: with no ganglion cells, these neurons are absent, so the segment cannot relax and stays narrow."],"why":"Peristalsis needs the muscle ahead of a bolus to relax while the muscle behind it contracts. Relaxation comes from inhibitory motor neurons in the myenteric plexus that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). Without ganglion cells, these neurons are missing, so the segment cannot relax, and thick extrinsic cholinergic nerve fibers in the area add to its tone. The internal anal sphincter also fails to relax when the rectum fills.","whyNot":["Sympathetic fibers come from outside the gut and are still present in the aganglionic segment, so their loss does not explain the problem.","Losing excitatory motor neurons would reduce contraction and cause a flaccid segment, not a tightly contracted one.","Vagal fibers do not supply the rectum or sigmoid colon; pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic input there.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Enteric motor neurons","Peristaltic reflex"],"comps":["GI-6","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q3","from":[],"stem":"Genetic testing finds a heterozygous variant in the gene most often mutated in this disease. Which of the following variants is most likely?","options":["Loss-of-function variant in RET","Gain-of-function variant in RET","Loss-of-function variant in APC","Gain-of-function variant in KRAS"],"answer":0,"sea":"B","comp":"GI-10","dok":2,"approach":["Recall the receptor that guides enteric neural crest cells: the RET receptor tyrosine kinase, activated by GDNF.","Ask which direction of change blocks migration: loss of function.","Separate it from the gain-of-function RET variants of MEN2."],"why":"Enteric neural crest cells need signaling through the RET receptor tyrosine kinase, activated by glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), to survive, multiply, and migrate along the gut. Loss-of-function RET variants are the most common genetic finding in Hirschsprung disease, especially in familial and long-segment cases, and penetrance is incomplete. Gain-of-function RET variants have the opposite effect and cause MEN2.","whyNot":["Correct.","Gain-of-function RET variants cause multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 and medullary thyroid cancer. A few MEN2A variants occur with Hirschsprung disease, but the usual cause is loss of RET signaling, which impairs neural crest migration into the gut.","Loss-of-function APC variants cause familial adenomatous polyposis and start the colorectal adenoma sequence.","Gain-of-function KRAS variants drive growth in many cancers, including colorectal and pancreatic cancer, and are not a cause of Hirschsprung disease."],"study":{"topics":["RET and Hirschsprung disease","Loss versus gain of function"],"comps":["GI-10","GI-13-F5"]}},{"id":"DX-GI-C08-Q4","from":[48],"stem":"His newborn screen shows a normal phenylalanine level. Which of the following best describes him with respect to his mother's disorder?","options":["Obligate carrier of one tyrosine hydroxylase variant; PKU is X-linked","Obligate carrier of one homogentisate oxidase variant; PKU is autosomal recessive","50% chance of carrying one PAH variant; PKU is autosomal recessive","Obligate carrier of one PAH variant; PKU is autosomal recessive"],"answer":3,"sea":"B","comp":"NE-20-E3","dok":3,"approach":["Name the defect: PKU is deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which converts phenylalanine to tyrosine.","Recall the inheritance: autosomal recessive, so his affected mother has two PAH variants.","Apply: he must inherit one maternal variant, and his normal screen means he is not affected, so he is a carrier."],"why":"Classic PKU is an autosomal recessive deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which needs tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine. An affected mother passes one PAH variant to every child, so this baby is an obligate carrier, and his normal screen shows he is not affected. Untreated PKU causes intellectual disability, seizures, light skin and hair, and a musty odor, and tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid. Keeping maternal phenylalanine low before and during pregnancy prevents maternal PKU embryopathy (microcephaly, heart defects, growth restriction).","whyNot":["Tyrosine hydroxylase makes L-DOPA from tyrosine and is not the PKU enzyme, and PKU is autosomal, not X-linked.","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency causes alkaptonuria, not PKU.","His mother has two PAH variants, so every child inherits one of them. His chance of carrying one is 100%, not 50%.","Correct."],"study":{"topics":["Phenylketonuria","Autosomal recessive inheritance"],"comps":["NE-20-E3","GI-8"]}}]}];
